INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0deea3f07002N8BbbxBLjAVHCT','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',284,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0deea2fc4001crHvDyVlUDa4y3","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0deea2b0a001Hx8rYhaERrm5VP","type":"reasoning","text":"The user says the SSH key is uploaded and asks me to scan their repos. But I still don''t know the account username or repo names. However, I can now test the SSH auth, and if it works, I can use the GitHub API to list repos — the GitHub API over HTTPS supports SSH-authenticated... no, actually the REST API doesn''t accept SSH keys. But there are tricks: you can''t list repos via SSH directly.\n\nActually, you CAN list repositories via SSH if the GitHub account has the repos, using the git protocol. GitHub supports the \"git\" SSH service for fetch/push only, not repo listing.\n\nSo: I can test SSH auth, and SSH auth success tells me the username (`Hi username! You''ve successfully authenticated...`). That gives me the account name. Then I can list public repos via the unauthenticated API, or list repos with `gh` if installed.\n\nLet me first test the SSH auth. That''s the concrete step.\n\nIf SSH works, I get the username from the greeting. Then I can try the GitHub API for that user''s public repos. Private repos won''t show without a token — but I could try cloning by guessing... no, that''s bad.\n\nActually there''s a neat approach: once I have the username, I can attempt `git ls-remote git@github.com:username/repo.git` for candidate names. But I don''t know the names. However, I could use the GitHub API to list public repos for that user: `https://api.github.com/users/<username>/repos`.\n\nLet me start by testing SSH.\n\nLet me be efficient: test SSH, capture username.","time":{"start":1790446284740,"end":1790446288647}},"time":1790446288647}');
