INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df0104bc001GfTTIYOPh5Pi74','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1347,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df00f521001kLYZndfiHZMqYX","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df00edcb001SoZ9qqNQmfBjOQ","type":"text","text":"I don''t have it. Nothing arrived — no token file, no `gh`, no `hosts.yml`.\n\nTo be clear about what did and didn''t happen: I opened the GitHub page, and you created a token **in your browser**. That token lives in GitHub''s vault and on your clipboard. It never reached this machine, so there''s nothing here for me to read. Creating it and granting me access are two separate steps, and only the first one has happened.\n\nAlso worth catching: you said **30 days**, but the guidance I gave was 7. If you set 30, that''s your call — just remember it expires then, and nothing here will warn you before it does.\n\nThe `read -rs` command is what bridges the gap. Run it in your own terminal — it won''t echo the token, won''t enter shell history, and won''t show up in `ps`:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.config/gh\nread -rs -p \"token: \" T && printf ''%s'' \"$T\" > ~/.config/gh/token && chmod 600 ~/.config/gh/token && echo saved\n```\n\nPaste the token at the `token:` prompt, press Enter, and it lands in `~/.config/gh/token` with 600 perms. Tell me \"saved\" and I''ll read it from there, enumerate all 120 repos, and delete the file when the audit''s done.\n\nWhile you''re in that terminal, two other things are still outstanding:\n\n```bash\nsudo pacman -U /home/livings/.cache/paru/clone/hermes-agent/hermes-agent-0.21.5-1-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst\n```\n\nThe 126 MB hermes package is built and waiting on that one command.\n\nAnd **supercode** — still zero leads. Not in the AUR, not in the repos, not on disk. I need a repo URL or package name, and I won''t guess one and clone something arbitrary onto this machine.","time":{"start":1790447777057,"end":1790447781052}},"time":1790447781052}');
