INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df05763c001t1iGGX710iAyoc','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1619,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df055ea10016XabIUeWsHHKvj","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df0556b7001Ex2xeXzeqM22wG","type":"text","text":"Conclusive: the token gets **404 on `hermes-blood`** — a private repo I know exists, because I cloned it over SSH. Same account, confirmed both identities match, no orgs.\n\nSo the token definitively has **zero** private repo access. The scope edit isn''t taking effect. Likely causes, in order of likelihood:\n\n1. **You edited a different token.** You''ve made several in this session. Easy to edit the wrong one.\n2. **The edit saved but the old token value is what I have.** Fine-grained tokens keep the same string on edit, so this shouldn''t break — but if you regenerated, my copy is dead and a *new* token exists that I don''t have.\n3. **The permission change needs the token re-selected.** GitHub occasionally needs a re-pick of \"All repositories\" rather than just changing the dropdown.\n\nCheck the token list at [github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens](https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens) and confirm the **most recently modified** one shows \"All repositories\" and that the token I hold is that one. If you regenerated, the simplest path is to revoke everything and create one clean token:\n\n- Resource owner: `intricos777-dot`\n- **Repository access: All repositories**\n- Permissions: `Contents: Read-only` + `Metadata: Read-only`\n- Expiration: whatever you prefer\n\nThen save it to the file again and tell me. I''ll verify the fix instantly by hitting `/repos/intricos777-dot/hermes-blood` and checking for a 200 before doing anything else.\n\n**Alternative that skips tokens entirely** — since SSH already reaches every private repo, I can enumerate them by probing candidate names over `git ls-remote`, like I did to find `hermes-blood`. It''s slower and needs name guesses, but it needs no credential at all. Given the token trouble, worth considering if you''d rather not keep minting scoped credentials.\n\nOne correction to something I said earlier, so it''s on the record: I twice claimed Hermes had no session history, and I''ve since found `~/.hermes` scaffolding that looked like proof of prior use. It wasn''t — every file in it was created at 11:22 by the AUR package''s `check()` step importing `hermes_cli.main`. Zero real sessions, before or after.\n\nStill parked: **hermes** needs `sudo pacman -U` on the built package, and **supercode** I still cannot locate.","time":{"start":1790448066209,"end":1790448072251}},"time":1790448072251}');
