INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df1da377001hW13pc2ddq3etc','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',3773,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df1d91d4001i5Zcp1t1M75swj","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df1d8b1b0014Mrp9aYU45u0z4","type":"text","text":"I can''t find this one, and I''m not going to guess at it.\n\nSearched all 120 repos by name, description, and topics for `nyan`, `gfsf`, `cat`, `honeypot`, `honey`, `decoy`, `canary`, `trap`. One match on `vm-bootstrap` (matched on \"cat\" in its contents), and a code search inside it for `nyan` returns zero hits. Nothing.\n\n\"NYAN cat GFSF\" doesn''t map to anything I recognize. GFSF is not a standard security term, a Linux subsystem, or a tool I know. So either it''s a personal project name, it''s under a name I''d never guess, or it''s gone.\n\n**A honeypot is a good thing to want** — and one specific part of this is worth flagging before you tell me more. If it listens on SSH, SMB, or any port, it will be found and probed within hours. That''s the design working, not a malfunction. Two consequences:\n\n1. **It must not be a path to this machine.** A honeypot that shares network access with your actual session, or runs as a service with any real privilege, turns \"attacker found a decoy\" into \"attacker has a shell.\" It needs to be isolated — a VM or separate container with no route to your home directory.\n2. **Firewalld needs to stay separate.** If we expose honeypot ports on the workstation''s own firewall, and the firewalld install we discussed gets reverted or misconfigured, that exposure persists silently. I''d want the honeypot on its own network namespace or VM bridge, not in the zone file I wrote for you.\n\nThat''s me raising the design issue *before* building, not refusing. If you give me the repo name or the actual spec, I''ll build it properly with those two constraints.\n\n**Meanwhile, \"all\" from your last message is still half-done.** I did the firefox.conf fix. Outstanding:\n\n- **KDE Connect** — confirmed still bound to `*:1716` on all interfaces, and you only have a *Public* wifi network (`USL - Public` on wlo1). No trusted network exists, so there''s no safe zone to scope it to. Recommend disabling it until you''re on a network you control.\n- **~7G cleanup** — 18 orphans, 3.8G pacman cache, 3.1G paru cache\n- **Firewall still inert** — `firewalld` installed but inactive, needs `sudo systemctl enable --now firewalld`\n\nWant me to finish those three now while you dig up the honeypot details?","time":{"start":1790449652180,"end":1790449656695}},"time":1790449656695}');
