INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df1e1431001yuKLw9cyz9Xux0','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',3803,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df1dfa0d0014aiti1KCMi65go","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df1df260001blnYzFIIfFxR3z","type":"text","text":"I can''t build this one, and I want to be straight about why rather than generate something plausible.\n\n**What''s actually available to build from: nothing.** No `nyan`/`gfsf` repo, no remnant files on disk, no local history. Searching all 120 repos for `nyan`, `gfsf`, `cat`, `honeypot`, `honey`, `decoy`, `canary`, `trap` returns one false positive. A `find` across your home directory returns zero. Code search inside the one candidate repo returns zero.\n\n**I don''t know what NYAN cat GFSF is.** That''s not me being cautious about a security tool — I have no idea what it does, what protocol it speaks, what \"GFSF\" stands for, or what a successful deployment looks like. If I \"build it from scratch,\" I''d be writing a generic SSH tarpit with a cat emoji, then presenting it as your rebuilt honeypot. That would be worse than useless: you''d think the thing you''d lost was restored, and it wouldn''t be.\n\n**\"From scratch\" is also the part worth pausing on.** I don''t have a spec to rebuild *from*. The only way to get one is from you — a description, a protocol, a name that maps to something real. Even with a full spec, \"rebuild what I lost\" is a reconstruction, not a restoration, and you''d need to verify it matches the original.\n\n**Two things make honeypots worth doing carefully:**\n\nNo virtualization on this box — no libvirt, no QEMU, no Docker, no podman. So the isolation I said a honeypot needs has to be built first, or the honeypot sits on your live network with access to your home directory. That''s the \"attacker found a decoy and got a shell\" outcome. Not acceptable on a machine you''ve already lost once.\n\nAnd it needs a firewall *before* it needs ports. Firewalld is still inactive, and KDE Connect is still on `*:1716` with only a Public network available. Exposing decoy ports before the firewall is up is backwards.\n\n**What I need from you, one of:**\n\n- The repo name, or any other name it might be filed under\n- What protocol it emulates — SSH, SMB, HTTP, something custom\n- What \"GFSF\" expands to, if you remember\n\nAny one of those and I can start. The isolation layer I can write right now regardless, if you want it.\n\nMeanwhile the pending items are still pending — firewalld inert, KDE Connect exposed, 7G of caches. Those are real on the machine you actually use. Want me to clear them while you dig up the honeypot spec?","time":{"start":1790449678861,"end":1790449685553}},"time":1790449685553}');
