INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df1f92cb003xaonug0VkN06Mz','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',3885,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df1f87780016tyAP9khwfSM0r","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df1f80800016S19RglTqPZP1R","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the spec: it''s a visual alert — pixelated nyan cat with \"GFSF\" text in flames, pops up when outside systems attempt to access the device. So it''s a local intrusion-detection notifier, not a listening honeypot. That changes things significantly — no isolation concern about listening ports, since it doesn''t listen at all. It watches auth events and displays a fullscreen alert.\n\nLet me build it. Components:\n1. A watcher that tails the journal for failed auth / faillock / sudo failures\n2. A fullscreen PyQt6/QML display of pixelated nyan cat + \"GFSF\" text in flames\n3. systemd user service to run it\n\nLet me build the detector first, then the display. I need to make the pixel art myself since PIL is absent — I can do it in QML with a grid of rectangles, or generate SVG. Actually pixelated nyan cat — I can draw it in QML using Canvas or just a grid of Rectangles. Simplest robust approach: define the cat as a string-art bitmap and render as Rectangles in QML. Plus animated flames underneath with \"GFSF\" text.\n\nLet me be efficient. Write:\n- `~/Projects/nyan-gfsf-alert/watch.py` — journal tailer + debounce\n- `~/Projects/nyan-gfsf-alert/alert.qml` — fullscreen visual\n- systemd user units\n- install script\n\nLet me create the project.","time":{"start":1790449780600,"end":1790449783499}},"time":1790449783499}');
