INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df225570001DfOC2P2RMNa9Eq','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',259,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df223f0e001gkL1dhcU3hlPh6","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df22386c0016LlWeLhqPTOqGP","type":"reasoning","text":"1.3 GB instead of 37 GiB. Tag present, resolves to the right commit.\n\nBut: `git tag -l --format=''%(tag)'' \"132.0.6834.210\"` returned **empty**! That''s a problem. The forgery guard at git.sh:133:\n\n```bash\nif [[ ${fragment%%=*} = tag ]]; then\n    tagname=\"$(git tag -l --format=''%(tag)'' \"$ref\")\"\n    if [[ -n $tagname && $tagname != \"$ref\" ]]; then\n        error \"Failure to check out version %s, the git tag has been forged\"\n```\n\nWait, in extract_git, this runs in `$srcdir/chromium-mirror` (the working copy), not the mirror. And if tagname is empty, `-n $tagname` is false → no error. So empty is fine, not a failure.\n\nBut why is it empty? `git tag -l --format=''%(tag)''` — the `%(tag)` field for a lightweight tag should be the tag name. Hmm. In a **bare/shallow** repo... let me think. `%(tag)` gives the tag name (for annotated tags it''s the tag object''s name; for lightweight it''s the ref name). It should work.\n\nWait — maybe the issue is that `git tag -l` in a bare repo with `--format` needs the pattern. Let me test directly. Actually maybe the issue is `%(tag)` is empty because... hmm.\n\nActually let me reconsider: `git tag -l --format=''%(tag)''` — the `for-each-ref` field `tag` is documented as \"The tag name\". For lightweight tags, `%(refname:strip=2)` is the name, and `%(tag)` should also be the name. Let me just check what it actually outputs.\n\nActually the important thing: does it error? No — empty tagname means the guard is skipped. So it works either way. But let me double check because if `%(tag)` returns empty, the guard is bypassed, meaning the check silently passes. That''s fine for us (it''s a guard against forged/annotated-with-different-name tags, and our tag is genuine lightweight).\n\nLet me verify more carefully and also test the actual makepkg code paths. The real tests:\n1. `calc_checksum_git`: `git -C $dir archive --format tar 132.0.6834.210 | sha256sum` — must work.\n2. `extract_git`: `git clone -s $dir chromium-mirror` then `git checkout --force --no-track -B makepkg 132.0.6834.210`.\n3. `download_git` on existing dir: `git fetch --all -p` must be cheap and must not destroy the tag.\n\nLet me test all three in a sandbox, because #3 is the risky one — a shallow mirror with `git fetch --all -p` could try to unshallow or error.\n\nActually important: with refspec restricted to the single tag, `git fetch --all -p` will fetch that one ref. Since we already have it, it''s a no-op. Good.\n\nLet me test all of this in a temp sandbox replicating makepkg''s steps.\n\nAlso test: does `git clone -s` from a shallow bare repo work, and does checkout of the tag produce a real tree?\n\nLet me do that now.\n\nAlso let me check the `%(tag)` thing out of curiosity/diligence.","time":{"start":1790449958670,"end":1790449964400}},"time":1790449964400}');
