INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df27923f001m8nERyUIUaLcpd','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',627,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df277778001fHK6QXgsktRvYl","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df276f94001bv0Zw4xSJwtnKd","type":"reasoning","text":"Now everything is clear. Let me summarize the critical findings:\n\n1. **`void-bin` is an AUR package — that''s what \"void\" meant.** It''s `void{,-electron-latest}-bin` = the **Void editor** (voideditor/void, github.com/voideditor/binaries), packaged as a `.deb` repack, which uses `code.sh` from the `code` package to launch with `--app=<void url>`. It is NOT Void Linux. My earlier \"this box is not Void\" was factually right about the OS but missed the actual intent entirely. I owe a correction.\n\n2. **My chromium mirror pre-seed is in the wrong place for void-bin — and actually void-bin doesn''t need chromium at all.** `void-bin`''s source is a `.deb` from GitHub releases + `code.sh` from gitlab.archlinux.org. No git+chromium source! The buildlog line 39 said \"code.sh source redirects to chromium mirror\" — that''s because `code.sh` is the VS Code launcher script, and running the .deb requires a VS Code install, which pulls in `code` → which depends on electron → which needs the chromium mirror. So the chain is: void-bin needs `code` (VS Code), which needs electron/chromium.\n\n   So the pre-seeded mirror at `~/.cache/paru/clone/electron34/chromium-mirror` IS relevant — but only if the build goes through the `electron34` (or `code`) build. Actually `void-bin` depends on `code` probably, and paru would build `code` as a dependency... Let me check void-bin''s makedepends/depends. Let me look at the full PKGBUILD.\n\n   Actually the key question: does building void-bin trigger building electron34? Let me check depends.\n\n3. **Great Vibes lockout risk is REAL and currently active.** The `kwriteconfig6` command I ran failed because I omitted the value argument (my error, it printed usage). So I don''t know the current font. But the buildlog says the machine was rebuilt after a lockout with Great Vibes as prime suspect. install.sh lines 125-127 set kdeglobals font to Great Vibes 16pt. So re-running install.sh re-applies the documented lockout suspect.\n\n   And `fc-list` confirms Great Vibes has ONLY Regular weight. The synthetic embolden rule exists as mitigation.\n\n4. **Nothing is pushed to GitHub** per handoff, but buildlog line 47 says \"120 repos intact via git remote\". The 4 repos here: hermes-blood has `remotes/origin/main` — so it HAS a remote. Let me check remotes.\n\n5. There''s no recorded \"global agent system\" — agents are hermes 0.21.5, opencode 1.18.32, and supercode (404''d, unusable).\n\nLet me get the remaining facts: void-bin depends, current kdeglobals font, and git remotes. Then report and ask.\n\nI should now stop investigating and report + ask, because I have enough. But the void-bin dependency question is decisive for whether my chromium pre-seed helps. Let me check it quickly along with the font and remotes.\n\nLet me do one more batch then report.","time":{"start":1790450300792,"end":1790450307647}},"time":1790450307647}');
