INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df29680b0016LC4HGmWJkKeMJ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',4613,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df2957dc001znvZ6S93shuVo9","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df295108001s2LHA7eTmC50RE","type":"text","text":"Four tools installed and working. Note: `nyan-gfsf-alert` shows a commit I didn''t make — someone renamed it to `shield-kitty`. Leaving that alone.\n\n## Installed and verified\n\n| Tool | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `business-health` | wrapper works, scan ran, **0 critical / 0 high** |\n| `business-debug` | wrapper works, full analysis returns live data |\n| `lin-antimalware` | venv + 3 deps, CLI scans clean |\n| daily health scan | systemd timer armed for **tomorrow 07:00** |\n\n## Three real bugs I found and fixed\n\n**1. Both wrappers pointed at nonexistent paths.** `business-health` and `business-debug` both hardcoded `~/Projects/security/...`, but your repos live in `~/Projects/`. Both wrappers would have failed on first run. Now resolved from each install script''s own location.\n\n**2. `lin-antimalware/start.sh` was hardcoded to `/home/sin/`** — the old username. Now resolves its own path.\n\n**3. The cron job could never have run.** `install.sh` registered a `crontab` entry, but **cron isn''t installed on this system** — no cronie, no crond. The installer printed a success message from a command that failed. Replaced with a systemd user timer, which is the correct mechanism here and is now confirmed scheduled.\n\n## On PAM and faillock\n\nYou are already an admin — `livings` is in `wheel`, and the root commands you ran earlier all succeeded. PAM sees you correctly. Nothing to change.\n\nThe faillock exemption I''m still not adding, as before: there''s no such directive, and the alternatives (`even_deny`, `deny = 0`) would mean password guessing never locks out, on a machine with no firewall. Current state is `deny=10`, `unlock_time=1800`.\n\n## Still open\n\n**The firewall.** `firewalld` is installed and inactive — no packet filtering at all. This is the largest remaining exposure, and it''s the thing standing between you and closing the KDE Connect port too. One command:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl enable --now firewalld\n```\n\nAlso unstarted: the `nyan-gfsf-alert` watcher (code is tested and working, no service unit written yet), and the 7G of cache cleanup.\n\nWant the firewall next, or should I finish wiring the alert watcher into a service?","time":{"start":1790450423772,"end":1790450427915}},"time":1790450427915}');
