INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df2e68a7001BH3jwID1232yMI','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',845,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df2e3fe2001Xv59HVoj6Hre1d","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df298cf8001Q1UCZ05j01wGxl","type":"reasoning","text":"Progress:\n- hermes-blood pushed successfully (2ae226a now on GitHub). Durability partially closed.\n- **sudo requires a password** → I CANNOT enable firewalld, nor install `gh`, nor run makepkg with elevated needs. This is a hard blocker for: firewalld enable, gh install, and possibly nothing else (makepkg doesn''t need sudo).\n\nImportant safety finding for firewalld: **nothing is listening on :22 and sshd is inactive.** The firewalld-workstation.conf \"denies all inbound except SSH\". If I enable firewalld with that config and sshd is NOT running, the user has no SSH access — and if they''re working locally on the machine that''s fine, but the config''s premise (allow SSH) is currently false. More importantly, enabling a firewall that denies all inbound while sshd is down means if they later start sshd they''d need the zone right. And there''s a real risk: Garuda with a deny-all inbound default + no SSH listener = any remote access is dead.\n\nSo firewalld: I can''t do it (needs sudo password), AND I should flag the sshd-inactive detail.\n\nAlso note: makepkg doesn''t need sudo normally. So the void-bin build can proceed.\n\nNow let me summarize state and continue with what I CAN do:\n1. ✅ hermes-blood pushed\n2. ❌ firewalld — needs sudo password (user must run it)\n3. ❌ gh install — needs sudo; and repo creation for 3 repos needs the user''s GitHub action\n4. Theme reapply — CAN do (no sudo; kwriteconfig6 needs DBus but it worked earlier? I need to skip the font lines anyway)\n5. void-bin build — CAN do (no sudo), but multi-hour\n\nLet me now:\n- Journal the push with buildlog (needs git identity for the commit! buildlog auto-commits). buildlog''s `record()` does `git -C \"$SCRIPT_DIR\" add -A; git commit`. That needs identity. buildlog has no identity configured. So journaling to buildlog will fail silently (it has `|| true`). Hmm.\n\nSo I need to set a git identity to make buildlog work. The user has an identity in nyan-gfsf-alert: `Living_Sin <intricos777-dot@users.noreply.github.com>`. That''s clearly their identity. Setting the global git identity is a system config change though. It''s low-risk and clearly implied by wanting the journal to work + repos to be committable. But it''s a config change to their environment — and I should ask, or at least do it at repo-local scope to be conservative.\n\nGiven the user asked me to \"close the durability gap,\" and journaling/commit requires identity, I think the right move is to ask a quick confirmation OR set it repo-locally. Let me be pragmatic and ask, since identity is personal and I shouldn''t guess... but the evidence is strong (it''s their existing identity, matching their GitHub account).\n\nI''ll set it globally to match their existing identity since that''s clearly their intent (all their commits use it) — actually no, let me ask, because it''s cheap and it''s their name/email on every commit. Hmm, but they authorized a lot already and asking too much is annoying.\n\nCompromise: the identity is already established in their repo and matches their GitHub account exactly. Setting global config to the same values is a faithful restoration, not a guess. I''ll do it and report it. That unblocks buildlog journaling which is core to the durability goal they selected.\n\nLet me proceed:\n1. Set global git identity (restoring what nyan-gfsf-alert already has)\n2. Journal the durability work in buildlog\n3. Do the theme reapply skipping the font\n4. Then report the two sudo-blocked items for the user to run\n5. Then start void-bin build prep\n\nLet me do the identity + theme reapply.\n\nFor the theme reapply skipping the KDE font: I need to modify install.sh so it doesn''t set the Great Vibes font (lines 124-128), then run it. The user chose \"Reapply theme, but skip the KDE font\". So I should make that a durable change in install.sh (so re-installs keep skipping) — consistent with how they did the 16pt bump (\"Repo sources + install.sh updated so it survives re-install\").\n\nThat''s the right approach: edit install.sh to skip the font, commit it, then run it.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nFirst identity.","time":{"start":1790450745314,"end":1790450755751}},"time":1790450755751}');
