INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df2e7bd90017JsIjRhhZnvWOC','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',865,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df2e6d790013Roj0fiwCL2tKD","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df2e661e001CmRi9v1dX6w11N","type":"reasoning","text":"MAJOR findings. The rename is **broken in production**:\n\n1. **The installed systemd unit still points at the OLD path:**\n   - `Documentation=file://%h/Projects/nyan-gfsf-alert/` → path no longer exists\n   - `ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 %h/Projects/nyan-gfsf-alert/shield-kitty-watch.py` → **path no longer exists!**\n\n2. **The running process (PID 186750, running 143s) is the OLD in-memory process** started from the old path. It''s still running because it was started before the move. `Restart=on-failure` — if it dies, systemd will try to restart it from a path that **doesn''t exist**, and it''ll fail-loop.\n\n3. So: the service is `active` but running a zombie process from a deleted path. On next login (or if it restarts), it will break.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of thing that connects to the user''s request. But wait — the user asked \"lets also ensure shield kitty doesnt display against me.\"\n\nLet me now focus on the real request. The service is enabled+active. The user''s concern: SHIELD KITTY shouldn''t display alerts against them (false positives).\n\nLet me look at the watcher''s detection logic to understand what triggers the alert, and what \"against me\" means:\n- False positives: legitimate user activity being flagged as intrusion\n- Or: the alert displaying at all when there''s no threat\n\nLet me read shield-kitty-watch.py to understand the rules.\n\nAlso note: the handoff said \"Selftest re-verified after the rename: journal access True, 796 lines scanned, rules match, --demo exits 0.\" So it has a --demo mode.\n\nLet me read the watcher.\n\nAlso important: the unit file in the repo `~/Projects/shield-kitty/systemd/shield-kitty-watch.service` — does it have the corrected path? Let me check. The repo was updated (systemd dir has 52 bytes modified 12:25). The *installed* one at ~/.config/systemd/user/ is stale.\n\nLet me read both.\n\nLet me read the watch script to address false positives.\n\nThis is the substantive work. Let me read it.","time":{"start":1790450756985,"end":1790450760664}},"time":1790450760665}');
