INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df2ea3fa001pzjvsQIuKANpVd','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',899,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df2e94c80010k6hZkEXVVR7gr","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df2e8bf8001k7CMMUyY2coC3h","type":"reasoning","text":"Now let me find the actual false positives empirically. Let me run the selftest and also test the rules against known-benign journal lines that occur on this machine.\n\nKey candidate false positive I can see from reading: the `sudo_unattended` rule. The `is_local_admin_actor` only fires for `sudo_unattended`. But there''s an important bug: the actor regex `sudo:\\s*(?P<actor>\\w+)\\s*:` — journald sudo lines look like:\n`sudo: livings : a password is required` — actor = \"livings\" ✓\nBut ALSO: `sudo: pam_unix(sudo:session): session opened for user root(0) by user=livings` — doesn''t match \"a password is required\".\n\nAnd critically: **the service runs under systemd with a minimal environment. `os.environ.get(\"USER\")` may be UNSET in a systemd user service**, falling back to `getpass.getuser()` which returns the real username from /etc/passwd — that works. OK so LOCAL_USER should resolve.\n\nBUT — there''s a subtle and serious issue: `getpass.getuser()` checks LOGNAME, USER, LNAME, USERNAME env vars first, then falls back to pwd. Under systemd, USER is typically set for user services. So probably fine.\n\nNow the real question: what actually fires against the user? Let me just run the selftest and also scan a wider window.\n\nLet me run:\n1. `--selftest`\n2. Scan last 2000 lines over a longer window and see what classifies\n\nLet me also test specific known-benign lines:\n- `sudo: livings : a password is required` (own sudo — should be suppressed)\n- `sudo: root : a password is required`?\n\nLet me empirically run the classifier against real journal lines. Best: run a python snippet that imports the module and classifies a large window, printing the actual matching lines. That tells me exactly what would display.\n\nLet me do that — it''s the most direct evidence.\n\nLet me run the selftest first plus a wide classification dump.\n\nNote: I must be careful — running the watch script in foreground would launch alerts. `--selftest` does NOT launch alerts (it only calls classify). Good, safe.\n\nLet me run selftest and a wide scan.\n\nLet me write a small python harness that imports the classify function and dumps matches over a big window.\n\nThis is the key diagnostic. Let me do it.\n\nNote the module has `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` guard, so importing is safe.\n\nLet me do it.","time":{"start":1790450767048,"end":1790450770938}},"time":1790450770938}');
