INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df2f0bd9001rj4qDZOwNyn7Gk','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',989,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df2eef18001Ey2Mn8YG5qxACK","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df2ee8b00010nXkl1TwTDM1a7","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me diagnose precisely and report, then ask before making changes since this is a security tool and the user asked to \"ensure shield kitty doesnt display against me\".\n\nThe root cause: `fire_alert` is called for ANY event, including `warning` severity. The alert script then does `showFullScreen()` unconditionally. So a benign USB attach (which happens at every boot, plus every time you plug anything in) = fullscreen takeover.\n\nLet me check the classify/main loop to confirm there''s no severity gate.\n\nFrom the code I read:\n- `most_severe()` returns \"critical\" if any critical, else \"warning\"\n- `fire_alert(events)` is called whenever `events` is non-empty\n- No severity gating anywhere\n\nSo: warning events DO trigger fullscreen. That''s the bug.\n\nLet me verify the debounce and the exact fire path once more, and check if there''s any severity filter. I have that from the full read: lines 286-294, no severity check.\n\nSo the fix options:\n1. Make `warning` severity log-only (no fullscreen), only `critical` gets the fullscreen nyan cat. This is the cleanest and most correct fix.\n2. Fix the `usb_attach` regex to not match built-in xHCI root hub controllers.\n3. Add a cooldown/quiet-hours.\n\nThe user said \"ensure shield kitty doesnt display against me\" — so they want it to not fire on them. The correct fix is BOTH: (a) don''t fullscreen on warning, (b) tighten usb_attach so built-in controllers don''t count.\n\nLet me also note: the user might want it to still alert on real attacks. So we shouldn''t disable it entirely.\n\nLet me present the diagnosis and ask/propose. Actually, the user gave a direct instruction: \"lets also ensure shield kitty doesnt display against me\". I should fix it. But this is a security tool — I should be careful not to disable real protection. Let me propose the specific fix and confirm, since there''s a meaningful choice about how strict to be.\n\nActually, let me just be decisive but check one thing: is there a \"quiet hours\" or acknowledge mechanism? And let me verify the usb_attach false positive is the main daily source.\n\nLet me count how often usb_attach fires vs critical. 8 hits of usb in 6457 lines — all at boot. And these would have been debounced into 1 alert. But still a fullscreen takeover at every boot.\n\nI think the right move: present the diagnosis clearly, propose the fix (severity gate + tighten usb regex), and ask for confirmation on the policy choice since it affects security posture. Let me use the question tool.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. The user''s request is clear and low-risk to implement correctly. The fix that preserves security: only fullscreen on `critical`, and make `usb_attach` not fire on built-in root hubs. Let me implement it but confirm the policy choice first because \"don''t display against me\" could mean \"never display\" which would defeat the tool.\n\nLet me ask a focused question.\n\nLet me first gather the final piece of evidence — how many distinct usb_attach episodes per day and whether any were genuine, to justify tightening.\n\nActually I have enough. Let me ask the policy question.\n\nLet me be concise and decisive. I''ll present the fix and ask the one question that matters.","time":{"start":1790450790168,"end":1790450797529}},"time":1790450797529}');
