INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df302ba9001yBCso5wzg9vOXF','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',5252,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df301b67001ay7S7i1ALruan0","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df300b35001DKJFwy11FzXGuH","type":"tool","callID":"call_function_oci159xx9byn_1","tool":"bash","state":{"status":"running","input":{"command":"cd ~/Projects/shield-kitty-forensics\necho \"=== reset chain, then do a live end-to-end test through the WATCHER ===\"\nrm -f ~/.local/share/shield-kitty/evidence/chain.jsonl ~/.local/share/shield-kitty/evidence/anchor.json\nsystemctl --user restart shield-kitty-watch.service; sleep 3\nbefore=$(wc -l < ~/.local/share/shield-kitty/evidence/chain.jsonl 2>/dev/null || echo 0)\nlogger -p authpriv.warning \"sshd[88888]: Failed password for invalid user admin from 203.0.113.99 port 22 ssh2\"\necho \"  [+] attack logged to journal; waiting for watcher...\"\nsleep 9\nafter=$(wc -l < ~/.local/share/shield-kitty/evidence/chain.jsonl 2>/dev/null || echo 0)\necho\necho \"  chain records: $before -> $after\"\necho\n./evidence.py list -n 3 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\n./evidence.py verify 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''"},"metadata":{"output":"=== reset chain, then do a live end-to-end test through the WATCHER ===\n/usr/bin/bash: line 5: /home/livings/.local/share/shield-kitty/evidence/chain.jsonl: No such file or directory\n  [+] attack logged to journal; waiting for watcher...\n"},"time":{"start":1790450868188}}},"time":1790450871209}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df3047d7001Gt3a06YzK3KbKY','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1008,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df301978001UHJMF19KwWIRrK","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df300e5900135mxFQFJjC4psG","type":"reasoning","text":"The user chose \"Gate on severity (recommended)\". Let me now implement:\n\n1. Severity gate: only `critical` fires the fullscreen alert; `warning` goes to journal/log only.\n2. Tighten `usb_attach` to ignore built-in xHCI root hubs.\n3. Fix the stale systemd unit (this is required — otherwise the whole thing crash-loops and the fix is moot).\n4. I should also address #3 (sudo self-exemption) and #4 (narrow login_failure) since the user picked \"gate on severity\" which was described as the primary fix, and said \"I will not weaken the critical/attack detection either way.\"\n\nThe user chose only ONE option: \"Gate on severity (recommended)\". So I should focus on that. But the description of that option was: \"Only ''critical'' events (auth failures, lockouts, privesc) get the fullscreen cat. ''warning'' events log to the journal only. Tighten usb_attach to ignore built-in xHCI root hubs. Real attacks still take over the screen.\"\n\nSo the chosen option includes:\n- severity gate (critical only fullscreen)\n- warning → journal only\n- tighten usb_attach to ignore built-in xHCI root hubs\n\nThat''s what I should implement. The option did NOT include #3 (sudo self-exemption) or #4 (narrow login_failure) or the selftest fix.\n\nBut wait — the stale systemd unit. Should I fix it? The user didn''t select it in this round. However, it''s genuinely broken and will crash-loop. Let me think about scope. The user asked \"lets also ensure shield kitty doesnt display against me\" and then chose the severity gate.\n\nI should implement the chosen option precisely, and flag the broken unit + the other false positives as remaining items, asking if they want them. Actually, the unit being broken means the service will crash-loop on restart — that''s a real problem but it also means it WON''T display (it''ll just fail). Fixing the unit would make it start again. Hmm, but the user wants it to not display against them. If I fix the unit AND apply the severity gate, then: real critical attacks still display, USB warnings don''t. That''s coherent.\n\nLet me be careful and not overstep. The user selected the severity gate option. Let me implement exactly that, and clearly report the other findings (sudo self-exemption, login_failure breadth, unsound selftest, broken unit) as still-open, asking whether to proceed.\n\nActually — I should just do the selected fix well, and mention the rest. Let me not ask again immediately; the user has answered enough questions. Let me implement the chosen change, then report the remaining items concisely.\n\nLet me be careful about the working directory and commit. The repo is at ~/Projects/shield-kitty. It has a remote? Let me check — earlier `git remote -v | head -2` printed nothing for shield-kitty, meaning NO remote. And no remote configured. So I can commit locally but can''t push.\n\nLet me now write the changes.\n\nDesign for severity gate:\n\nIn `shield-kitty-watch.py`:\n- `fire_alert(events)` should only be called for critical. Or better: split into `fire_alert` (critical, fullscreen) and `log_event` (warning, journal only).\n- In `main()`: `if events and (time.time() - last_fire) > DEBOUNCE: fire_alert(events)`. Change to only fire if any event is critical.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n```python\ndef critical_events(events):\n    return [e for e in events if e[\"severity\"] == \"critical\"]\n\ndef log_only(events):\n    for e in events:\n        log(f\"        [warn] {e[''label'']} x{e[''count'']}  (not displayed)\")\n```\n\nIn main:\n```python\nevents = classify(lines)\ncrit = [e for e in events if e[\"severity\"] == \"critical\"]\nif events and (time.time() - last_fire) > DEBOUNCE:\n    if crit:\n        fire_alert(crit)\n        last_fire = time.time()\n    else:\n        # warnings: journal only, never take over the display\n        for e in events:\n            log(...)\n```\n\nHmm, but should warnings still respect debounce? They just log. Let me keep it simple and log them each cycle.\n\nFor usb_attach tightening: the problem is `usb .*(?:new high-speed USB device|Product:)` matches built-in xHCI root hubs. Let me make it match only genuine device attachment, excluding root hubs.\n\nReal kernel lines for a new USB device:\n```\nusb 3-7: new high-speed USB device number 3 using xhci_hcd\nusb 3-7: Product: USB2.0 HD UVC WebCam\nusb 3-7: Manufacturer: ...\n```\nRoot hub lines:\n```\nusb usb1: Product: xHCI Host Controller\nusb usb2: Product: xHCI Host Controller\n```\nThe distinguishing feature: root hubs use `usb usbN:` (bus named \"usbN\") while real devices use `usb <bus>-<port>:` e.g. `usb 3-7:`. Also built-in controllers report \"xHCI Host Controller\", \"EHCI Host Controller\", \"UHCI Host Controller\", \"OHCI Host Controller\", \"root hub\".\n\nSo a better regex: match `usb \\d+-[\\d.]+:` (device on a real bus) and require a Product/manufacturer line, OR require \"new high-speed/full/low-speed USB device number\" AND exclude root hub.\n\nActually, the most robust: keep `new (full|high|low)-speed USB device number` which only appears for actual device attach on a non-root hub? No — root hub also gets \"new high-speed USB device number\" in some kernels: `usb usb1: new high-speed USB device number 2 using xhci_hcd`. Hmm.\n\nLet me check the actual journal to design the regex against real data. Let me look at all usb lines from boot.\n\nLet me do that first — empirical regex design is better than guessing. Let me examine the journal usb lines.\n\nActually, I should be efficient. Let me look at the raw usb lines to design correctly.\n\nLet me run a command to see all usb-related lines from the last boot.\n\nAlso, since severity gating means USB warnings never display, tightening usb_attach is somewhat belt-and-braces (it reduces journal noise). But the user explicitly chose the option whose description says \"Tighten usb_attach to ignore built-in xHCI root hubs.\" So I''ll do it.\n\nLet me get the data first.\n\nLet me be mindful of the token/time budget. Let me gather usb lines.","time":{"start":1790450866552,"end":1790450878423}},"time":1790450878423}');
