INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df30eaa7001YUD8DslsRny5pq','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',5362,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df30d50e001XiBVTe6A6QrkR4","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df30c45c001IH0mTJD1LqGhMt","type":"text","text":"Built, tested, and committed. Here''s what you have.\n\n## Evidence chain — verified against three attack shapes\n\nEvery detection is appended to an HMAC-chained log. I tested it by actually forging evidence:\n\n| Attack | Result |\n|---|---|\n| **Edit** a past event to look benign | **caught** — HMAC mismatch |\n| **Delete** a record to hide it | **caught** — seq gap + prev-hash mismatch |\n| **Truncate** the chain to erase history | **caught** — anchor mismatch |\n\nThat third one initially **passed verification** — a real failure I found in my own design, and precisely the attack an attacker would use. `seal` fixes it by anchoring the chain head outside the log. The hourly timer now re-seals automatically.\n\nFull pipeline proven live: attack logged → watcher detected → evidence chained → seal → verify green.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\n~/Projects/shield-kitty-forensics/evidence.py verify           # integrity check\n~/Projects/shield-kitty-forensics/evidence.py report --since 24\n~/Projects/shield-kitty-forensics/evidence.py bundle --id 1     # signed evidence bundle\n~/Projects/shield-kitty-forensics/evidence.py list\n```\n\nEach bundle contains the record, surrounding journal lines, system state at capture time, the full chain, and a signed manifest of SHA-256 hashes.\n\n## What I did not build, and why\n\n**Automatic reporting to authorities.** I built the evidence and integrity layer; transmission is a decision for you, not a log line.\n\nThe concrete reason: I injected two fake attacks into your journal about 20 minutes ago to test the watcher. A tool that auto-dispatched on detection would have dispatched fabricated evidence to a police agency. That judgment — is this real, is it worth involving law enforcement, is this actually an attack versus a misconfigured service — can''t be automated safely. The report is formatted so you or a lawyer can act on it; sending it stays your call.\n\n## Two other things you should know\n\n**Your repos are being renamed by something outside this session.** Three commits by `Living_Sin` renamed `nyan-gfsf-alert` → `shield-kitty`, including \"on-screen wordmark.\" It broke the running service''s path — the process survived only because Python had already loaded the file; a restart would have failed. I repointed it. Worth checking that''s you.\n\n**Two bugs of mine, both caught and fixed:** the `chmod 0444` I set on the anchor silently broke hourly re-sealing (`PermissionError`), and the `ProtectSystem=strict` hardening broke the service with `226/NAMESPACE`.\n\n**Current state:** watcher running, seal timer hourly, health scan daily 07:00, firewall live on `wlo1` with only `ssh` allowed.","time":{"start":1790450914574,"end":1790450920103}},"time":1790450920103}');
