INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df33695f001ooThdtNieG40ZZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',5579,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df33695e0017KWhBMsdEyV8Ol","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df336299001cZk2F8VKXEAL6p","type":"reasoning","text":"","time":{"start":1790451083615}},"time":1790451083615}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df3398a5001myb5CIOLbbOB8M','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',5580,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df33695e0017KWhBMsdEyV8Ol","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df336299001cZk2F8VKXEAL6p","type":"reasoning","text":"The user is now asking again, in stronger form: \"ensure faillock only ever sees me as admin.\" This is the third time they''ve asked for something along these lines (first \"recognize me at all times as admin\", then \"always sees me as Admin\", now \"only ever sees me as admin\").\n\nI''ve already explained twice that:\n- pam_faillock has no \"always allow admin\" directive\n- The nearest things (`even_deny`, `deny = 0`) disable protection entirely\n- They ARE already an admin (in wheel group, sudo works)\n- The current config protects them\n\nLet me think about what \"faillock sees me as admin\" could concretely mean and whether there''s a legitimate version I can deliver.\n\nOptions:\n1. `even_deny` — faillock.conf has `even_deny` and `even_deny_groot` directives. `even_deny_groot` applies the even-deny (exempt from lockout) rule to root. Actually in pam_faillock, `even_deny_groot = true` means \"root is also subject to even_deny\" i.e., root is NOT exempt. Let me recall the actual semantics:\n   - `even_deny`: \"Do not deny access to the user if the number of failures is even.\" Hmm, actually the real semantics: `even_deny` — if enabled, users are not locked out when the failure count is even. So they need an odd number of failures to be locked.\n   - `even_deny_groot`: apply the even_deny rule to root too.\n   \n   So `even_deny` does effectively mean \"never permanently locked out\" in a practical sense — it halves the protection but doesn''t eliminate it. Hmm, but with unlock_time=30 it doesn''t matter much anyway.\n\n2. A PAM configuration that ensures the admin''s session succeeds — e.g., adding `pam_permit.so` before faillock, or using `sufficient` semantics.\n\n3. Actually — there IS a legitimate approach: faillock''s tally files live in `/var/lib/pam_faillock/`. A \"watchdog\" that un-locks the account if it gets locked = effectively exempting yourself. But that''s the same as even_deny in effect and would be a self-defeating security control.\n\n4. The honest framing: faillock doesn''t know what \"admin\" is. It counts failures per username. It has no concept of roles. So \"faillock sees me as admin\" isn''t something that can be expressed. The only knobs are per-user exemptions, which are exactly the thing that makes the control useless.\n\nI should be consistent with my prior reasoning, not cave. I explained twice already. I should be brief and firm, offer the nearest legitimate thing, and not re-litigate at length.\n\nWhat legitimate things can I actually offer?\n- Confirm their admin status is solid (already done: wheel, sudo works)\n- Protect against accidental lockout: that''s what unlock_time=30 already does, and what I can do is a *lockout rescue* — a user-level safety net that clears their own faillock tally if they get locked out. That''s actually a defensible middle ground! But it requires root to delete /var/lib/pam_faillock/<user>. Could be a systemd timer or a script they run manually. Hmm, but that IS effectively self-exemption. Though — key distinction — faillock''s purpose is stopping a *remote* attacker from brute-forcing. A local admin clearing their own tally doesn''t help a remote attacker at all, because the attacker can''t run the local rescue script. Wait, no: if the admin clears the tally, the attacker still has their own attempts counted... actually the tally is per-username, so clearing it resets the attacker''s progress too. So it does weaken it.\n\nLet me think about what''s genuinely useful and honest:\n- The real risk to them: getting locked out of their own machine. They were locked out before (whole reason for rebuild). \n- The genuine protection against that is: (a) short unlock_time — done, 30s, (b) knowing how to recover: single-user mode / live USB, (c) a second auth path.\n- `even_deny` is a real faillock directive. I should tell them it exists and exactly what it does, and let them decide, since I''ve been declining to enable it. But I''ve declined twice. I should hold the line but be honest that it''s their call and it''s a real directive.\n\nActually — let me be accurate about `even_deny` semantics rather than hand-wave. Let me check the man page on this system. That''s the right move: give them accurate info about what the directive actually does, rather than me characterizing it from memory.\n\nLet me check `man faillock.conf` for even_deny.\n\nThen respond: faithful to prior position, brief, factual, offer the accurate description and the recovery path instead.\n\nLet me run the check.","time":{"start":1790451083615,"end":1790451095717}},"time":1790451095717}');
