INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df3655dd0014Yi0SGezOyLvbG','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',5793,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df3655dd001eoVPUh4dXg097s","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df364f09001ijfibDfyUXJevN","type":"text","text":"","time":{"start":1790451275229}},"time":1790451275229}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df367085001bT4paNI0l6J7QB','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',5794,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df3655dd001eoVPUh4dXg097s","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df364f09001ijfibDfyUXJevN","type":"text","text":"Three findings, and two of them change the picture.\n\n## 1. `admin_group` is not live\n\n```\ninstalled:  deny = 10, unlock_time = 30, audit = silent\nexpected:   + admin_group = wheel\n```\n\nThe copy didn''t take, or grabbed the version from before I added it. Your live account is still subject to lockout.\n\n**But given what I found below, I''d now recommend leaving it that way** — see the last section.\n\n## 2. faillock cannot do what you asked, structurally\n\nYou asked for: *only I as localhost have admin, outside devices get force locked out.*\n\n`pam_faillock` has no concept of local vs remote, and no concept of devices. Its tally store is **one file per username** — that''s the whole data model. There is no IP field, no host field, no interface. It cannot distinguish you at your keyboard from someone on the other side of the world guessing at the same account. `/var/lib/pam_faillock/` is empty, which is consistent with that.\n\nForce-locking-out by origin is **fail2ban''s** job, and only fail2ban''s — it bans the source IP. That''s why I pushed you toward it.\n\n## 3. You have almost no remote attack surface right now\n\n```\nport 22: nothing listening\nsshd / vsftpd / proftpd / telnetd / xrdp: none installed or enabled\nonly listening socket: kdeconnectd:1716, blocked inbound by firewalld\n```\n\n**There is currently no remote login surface on this machine.** An outside device has nothing to brute-force. And firewalld''s `ssh` allowance is permitting nothing, because nothing is listening on 22.\n\nSo \"outside devices get force locked out\" is currently handled by there being no door at all — which is a stronger position than any lockout policy.\n\n## What shield kitty does and doesn''t do\n\nIt **does** detect and alert on: `sshd` auth failures (which carry the source IP), faillock lockouts, `pam_unix` auth failures, and USB attach. That pipeline is live and tested.\n\nIt **cannot** block anything, and it cannot tell local from remote on its own — it infers \"remote\" only when the log line contains an off-box IP.\n\n## fail2ban: installed but off\n\n```\nfail2ban 1.1.1-1    enabled: disabled    active: inactive\n```\n\nWorth enabling regardless, as insurance if you ever turn on sshd. A jail for `sshd` would be pointless today; `sddm` and `sudo` jails are the ones that matter on a laptop.\n\n## My recommendation\n\nSince `admin_group = wheel` doesn''t serve your stated goal and actively weakens the one account that holds admin rights, I''d **not** install it. Keep the live config as-is (deny=10, unlock_time=30).\n\nWant me to enable fail2ban with `sddm` + `sudo` jails, wired to firewalld so bans actually drop packets? And separately — do you want sshd enabled at all, or is this a laptop that should stay closed to the network?","time":{"start":1790451275229,"end":1790451282053}},"time":1790451282053}');
