INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df3816b0001ug58sciKJD2x9G','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',5996,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df380317001tpZi6Zrx1X7fMv","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df37fb43001efqtHjwUu16k0C","type":"text","text":"I''ve built the config, but I need to be straight with you: **this is not something faillock can do**, and the tool that can do it is not free of a serious catch.\n\n## Why faillock can''t\n\nStructurally, not as a workaround:\n\n- `man faillock.conf` has **zero** matches for ip/host/remote/network. There is no directive that takes a source address.\n- Its data model is one tally file per **username**. No IP dimension exists.\n- There is no permanent state. Every lockout expires when `unlock_time` elapses.\n\nSo it cannot lock out \"a system.\" It can only lock an *account*, temporarily. \"Any system attempting to bruteforce\" requires keying on the source, which means fail2ban.\n\n## What I built\n\n`~/Projects/zen-hardening/shield-kitty-fail2ban.local` — four jails, all `bantime = -1` (permanent), dropping packets via `firewallcmd-rich-rules`:\n\n| Jail | Covers | maxretry |\n|---|---|---|\n| `sddm` | graphical login | 5 |\n| `sudo` | privilege escalation | 4 |\n| `sshd` | remote login (inert — sshd disabled) | 4 |\n| `recidive` | repeat offenders, permanent | 3 |\n\n**Validated:** every filter and action referenced actually exists. My first draft used `filter = sddm` and `filter = sudo` — those packages **don''t exist in Arch**, so fail2ban would have failed to start. Rewrote both to `pam-generic`, which matches the same PAM failure lines.\n\nInstall with:\n```bash\nsudo cp ~/Projects/zen-hardening/shield-kitty-fail2ban.local /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/\nsudo systemctl enable --now fail2ban\nsudo fail2ban-client status\n```\n\n## The catch you need to decide on\n\n**Permanent bans don''t expire, and fail2ban cannot tell you apart from an attacker.** If you fat-finger your SDDM password 5 times, your own IP gets banned forever — no remote login surface exists, so recovery means a console.\n\nI set `ignoreip` to cover loopback and RFC1918, and verified you''re currently on `10.245.106.176` → covered by `10.0.0.0/8`. **But you''re on wifi.** If that network ever hands you a public address, you''re not covered, and five wrong keystrokes at the login screen is a permanent lockout.\n\nSo the honest tradeoff: this gives you what you asked for and introduces a self-lockout path that didn''t exist before. If you want that risk reduced, the fix is a non-permanent `bantime` on `sddm` only, keeping `sudo`/`recidive` permanent — remote attackers can''t reach your login screen, so a short ban there costs you nothing.\n\nAlso still unresolved from before: `admin_group = wheel` is **not** in your live faillock config.","time":{"start":1790451385111,"end":1790451390128}},"time":1790451390128}');
