INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df3c2def00133edi5A0Jx0QAK','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',6316,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df3c1f22001noMeSSkWU54vMa","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df3c167b0013s7eIuzPackQTb","type":"text","text":"Third bug, and it''s mine again. Your system is **journald-only**:\n\n```\n/var/log/auth.log     → does not exist\nsyslog-ng / rsyslog   → not installed, not running\n```\n\n**The cause:** I set `backend = auto`. Per fail2ban''s own shipped docs, `auto` tries only **pyinotify, then polling** — it does *not* include systemd. So my `journalctl -f ...` logpath lines were never treated as journal sources. With no syslog file and no systemd backend, fail2ban found nothing to read.\n\n**The fix** — `backend = systemd` with `journalmatch`, and no `logpath` (it''s invalid for that backend):\n\n| Jail | journalmatch | Verified |\n|---|---|---|\n| `sddm` | `_SYSTEMD_UNIT=sddm.service + _TRANSPORT=auth` | 88 lines |\n| `sudo` | `_COMM=sudo + _TRANSPORT=auth` | matches |\n| `sshd` | `_SYSTEMD_UNIT=sshd.service + _TRANSPORT=auth` | inert (sshd off) |\n| `recidive` | `backend = polling`, `logpath = /var/log/fail2ban.log` | disabled until first run |\n\nI confirmed each selector returns real data from your journal rather than assuming it.\n\nRun this:\n\n```bash\nsudo cp ~/Projects/zen-hardening/shield-kitty-fail2ban.local \\\n        /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/shield-kitty.conf\nsudo rm -f /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/shield-kitty-fail2ban.local\nsudo fail2ban-client -t\n```\n\nThree bugs in a row from that one file: wrong extension, a jail that can''t bootstrap, and the wrong log backend. All caught by `fail2ban-client -t` before anything started, which is the only reason this stayed cheap.\n\n`recidive` stays disabled until after the first successful start — it needs `/var/log/fail2ban.log` to exist, which requires fail2ban to have run once.","time":{"start":1790451654434,"end":1790451658222}},"time":1790451658223}');
