INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df4b5a03001WiBcQKaHuPAX3w','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',7112,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df4b3d94001WXW9iotkC365tC","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df4b37770019TIAxJYabSQRfV","type":"reasoning","text":"So the current state is already fairly locked down. The main actions:\n\n1. **Remove `ssh` from the public zone** — pure win, it''s a hole for a non-running daemon\n2. **Keep `dhcpv6-client`** — needed for IPv6\n3. **Keep ICMP unblocked** — critical for PMTU/IPv6\n4. **Add explicit deny-by-default insurance**: firewalld already default-denies unlisted, so no change needed, but I could make it explicit\n5. **Optionally create a `trusted` zone for home wifi** so kdeconnectd works at home but not on public wifi\n6. **Verify DHCPv4 still works** (firewalld allows it implicitly)\n\nThe one genuinely open question: does the user want KDE Connect to work on their *home* network? That''s a real tradeoff:\n- Allow it always → simpler, but on coffee-shop wifi any nearby device could reach your KDE Connect\n- Block always → safest, but loses the feature\n- Split: `trusted` zone on home SSID, strict on public SSID → best of both, but requires knowing the home SSID\n\nLet me ask about that one, since it''s the only real judgment call. And I should note that the current zone name is \"USL - Public\" which suggests it might already be a specific profile.\n\nAlso worth noting: the user said \"save needed wifi communications\" — so DHCP + DNS + ICMP. All of that is already fine.\n\nLet me also think about whether there''s anything else that could accept inbound:\n- fail2ban''s firewallcmd-rich-rules — currently no rich rules, and no bans yet. Fine.\n- D-Bus? It''s unix socket, not network. Fine.\n- CUPS? Not listening.\n- Anything else: only 1716, 5353, 9050.\n\nSo the actual hardening work is small: remove ssh. Everything else is already correct.\n\nLet me be honest that the system is already mostly locked down, and the real win is removing the ssh hole. I shouldn''t overstate how much I''m doing.\n\nLet me also consider: should I add a firewalld policy that makes this more robust for future zones? E.g., a policy object that enforces this across all wifi connections. That would be the durable \"rebuild-proof\" answer the user wants. firewalld policies are a good fit — they can set the default zone behavior for all interfaces matching.\n\nActually, a cleaner durable approach: set firewalld''s **default zone** to public (already is), and remove ssh from the *permanent* config (not just runtime) so it survives reboot. And ensure the default zone config doesn''t re-add ssh.\n\nLet me check permanent vs runtime config.\n\nLet me present the plan and ask the one question. Let me be concise since I''ve been verbose.\n\nLet me check permanent config to give precise commands.","time":{"start":1790452645268,"end":1790452652546}},"time":1790452652547}');
