INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df4db8cc001PoCZJO9o9hlgci','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1190,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df4d8f3c0012cbCBKP3BiFQEM","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df4d78cd001LgHbpSTSXwNZtS","type":"reasoning","text":"The user says \"continue when ready\". I have a set of outstanding items. Let me review my todo list state and figure out what to continue with.\n\nOutstanding items:\n1. Stale systemd unit: `~/.config/systemd/user/shield-kitty-watch.service` still points at the deleted `~/Projects/nyan-gfsf-alert/` path → crash-loop on restart. This is a real breakage I found. Fixing it doesn''t need sudo (systemctl --user).\n2. `void-bin` needs the `electron34` build — long-running, and needs the DEPS pre-seed.\n3. Theme reapply skipping KDE font.\n4. firewalld — blocked on sudo.\n5. Durability gap — 3 repos without remotes; I pushed hermes-blood. shield-kitty now has new commits (3845292) and no remote either.\n\nAlso the display gate the user chose earlier (\"Gate on severity\") — that''s now moot because the other agent replaced fullscreen with notifications entirely, which is a superset of the fix. So I shouldn''t redo it.\n\nWait — but there''s one thing: the alert script now defaults to notify-send. But the *watcher* still calls `fire_alert` unconditionally. That''s fine now because fire_alert → alert script → notification. So severity gating is no longer needed. Good, genuinely moot.\n\nBut I should verify the service actually works after fixing the unit — the running process is still the zombie from the old inode.\n\nLet me pick the highest-value work that doesn''t need sudo and isn''t blocked:\n\nA. Fix the stale systemd unit (no sudo needed) — clear breakage.\nB. Push shield-kitty to a remote (needs repo creation on GitHub — needs `gh` or manual). I could check if I can push... shield-kitty has no remote. I could create the repo via SSH? No, GitHub doesn''t allow repo creation over SSH. Needs gh or web UI. Blocked-ish. But I could at least journal it.\nC. Start the electron34 DEPS pre-seed — this is the long pole for void-bin. I can pre-seed the 164 DEPS repos shallowly, which is scriptable and doesn''t need sudo. Then the build.\nD. Theme reapply skipping KDE font.\n\n\"Continue when ready\" — ambiguous but I should just pick up the most valuable blocking work and proceed. The user has been repeatedly asking me to continue. Let me be decisive and work through the list, prioritizing things that are unblocked.\n\nLet me start with the stale systemd unit since it''s a concrete breakage I found, then move to the DEPS pre-seed which is the long pole.\n\nActually, let me think about what''s most valuable. The user''s original goal was void-bin. That''s the long pole and it''s multi-hour. I should start that work in the background while doing other things? But the DEPS pre-seed needs network and time.\n\nLet me be strategic:\n1. Fix stale systemd unit (quick, 2 min).\n2. Write the DEPS pre-seed script and run it in background (long).\n3. While that runs, do the theme reapply skipping KDE font.\n4. Journal everything.\n\nLet me start. First the systemd unit.\n\nLet me check the current state of the service and fix it.\n\nActually, important: the running process (PID 186750) is running the OLD deleted inode of the old script. If I restart the service now with the fixed unit, it''ll run the new code. That''s good. But will restarting cause an alert storm? The new code uses a cursor, and on start it does `cursor = read_cursor() or latest_cursor()` — so it starts from the current cursor, no replay. Good, safe to restart.\n\nBut wait — with the new agent''s changes, is there a \"seal timer\" and \"evidence chain\" mentioned in c9f7bc9? \"Add tamper-evident evidence chain, seal timer, and fix service paths\". So the service paths may already be fixed in the repo. Let me check the repo''s systemd unit.\n\nLet me look.","time":{"start":1790452797244,"end":1790452807884}},"time":1790452807884}');
