INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df4f1554001R09Dd10IMFIArO','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',7396,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df4f04ae001GhN3g10PVy4m5t","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df4efceb001kaz81FycKIvTAN","type":"reasoning","text":"A sed error — my sed used `|` as delimiter but the replacement contains `/` characters (`enable=\"no\"/>`). The `|` delimiter should be fine with `/` in the content... wait, the issue is the `s|...|...|` with `|` delimiter and the content has no `|`. Hmm, \"unknown option to `s''\" at char 17.\n\nLet me look: `sed -i ''s|<service name=\"mdns\" disable=\"yes\"/>|<service name=\"mdns\" enable=\"no\"/>|''`\n\nChar 17 would be around `name=\"mdns\"`. Hmm. Actually the problem might be that the shell interpreted something. Or the `/` in `/>`...\n\nWait, with `|` as the delimiter, `/` is literal and fine. Let me count char 17: `s|<service name=` — s(1) |(2) <(3) s(4)e(5)r(6)v(7)i(8)c(9)e(10) (11)n(12)a(13)m(14)e(15) =(16) \"(17). So the delimiter got confused — that means the delimiter was NOT `|`.\n\nAh, I bet the issue is that my heredoc/quoting mangled it. Or the outer command had escaping issues.\n\nLet me just check whether the sed actually worked — the output showed the \"AFTER\" grep as still `disable=\"yes\"`. Yes! The AFTER grep shows `<service name=\"mdns\" disable=\"yes\"/>` — so the sed FAILED and the file is unchanged.\n\nLet me use the edit tool instead, which is more reliable. And I should be honest: the firewall is down, I need to move fast and not waste time on shell quoting.\n\nLet me use the edit tool.","time":{"start":1790452892846,"end":1790452897108}},"time":1790452897108}');
