INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df562207001ue2g8hRiGqsBJ7','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',7848,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df56053f001zYlmv5F33HYfr9","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df55de21001gyThqmgXkYEnTF","type":"reasoning","text":"The user says they didn''t run `--add-icmp-block`. So where did those errors come from at 12:57?\n\nLet me investigate. The errors were:\n```\nSep 26 12:57:16 zero firewalld[186159]: ERROR: INVALID_ICMPTYPE: v6-neighbour-solicit\nSep 26 12:57:18 zero firewalld[186159]: ERROR: INVALID_ICMPTYPE: v6-neighbour-advert\nSep 26 12:57:20 zero firewalld[186159]: ERROR: INVALID_ICMPTYPE: v6-redirect\n```\n\nNote the 2-second gaps between them (12:57:16, :18, :20). That''s a pattern of sequential automated calls, not a human typing.\n\nAnd the hostname at that time was already `zero` (journal shows \"zero\" as host at 12:57). So set-anon-identity.sh had already run.\n\nWhat could call these? Let me think:\n- `firewalld` itself when reloading?\n- fail2ban? No.\n- A script I or Hermes wrote? Let me check. The `set-anon-identity.sh` I wrote does NOT touch icmp.\n- Did I write anything that does icmp-block? No.\n- Could it be NetworkManager? Or some Garuda config?\n- Actually — could it be a *previous* attempt by me? No, I only ran read-only queries.\n\nWait — the timestamps 12:57 with hostname `zero`. The user ran set-anon-identity.sh around 12:57. My script does: hostnamectl, sed /etc/hosts, nmcli mac-address-mode, nmcli dhcp-send-hostname. No icmp.\n\nHmm, but what about `firewall-cmd --reload` being triggered? No.\n\nLet me actually investigate rather than speculate. Let me search:\n1. Any script in Projects that does icmp-block\n2. firewalld''s own permanent config in /etc (maybe something got written there)\n3. Check if there''s an icmp-block in the current config\n\nActually — important possibility: when firewalld was in FAILED state and something called `firewall-cmd --reload`, firewalld tries to re-apply the built-in zone which includes icmp-block entries, and it logs INVALID_ICMPTYPE for the v6-* ones because those aren''t valid block types in this version.\n\nActually, the built-in public.xml or another built-in zone might have `<icmp-block name=\"...\"/>` entries. Hmm.\n\nLet me look for what generates those. Let me check if any zone file has icmp-block entries with those names.\n\nActually, a strong candidate: **NetworkManager''s firewalld integration**, or **firewalld''s own reload path** when a zone references icmp blocks.\n\nLet me just investigate concretely:\n1. Check /etc/firewalld for any permanent config with icmp-block\n2. Check built-in zones for those icmp names\n3. Check the current icmp-block state\n\nLet me do that.\n\nAlso — I should check whether those errors indicate a real problem. If firewalld is failing to apply icmp blocks, that could be a separate latent issue.\n\nLet me investigate.","time":{"start":1790453351744,"end":1790453359110}},"time":1790453359111}');
