INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df5885a9001RDU7ArhwylSdVJ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',7922,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df587b26001G4LrtSxTxBavnH","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df5871cc001so3iM6oq8rRO4d","type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting. `firewall-offline-cmd --check-config` says \"Default zone ''workstation'' is not valid. Using ''public''\".\n\nThat means the default zone is set to `workstation` in the config, but the zone file doesn''t exist. So either:\n1. The `cp` didn''t happen (workstation.xml not in /etc/firewalld/zones/)\n2. Or the default zone was already set to workstation (from the earlier failed attempt at 13:07:18) but the file isn''t there\n\nRecall earlier: at 13:07:18 the journal showed \"Default zone ''workstation'' is not valid. Using ''public''.\" — so the default zone WAS set to workstation at some point, persisted in /etc/firewalld/firewalld.conf.\n\nSo the config says default zone = workstation, but the zone file is missing.\n\nThe fix: make sure workstation.xml exists, THEN check-config.\n\nLet me check whether the cp happened. Let me look at /etc/firewalld/zones/ and the firewalld.conf default zone.\n\nThis error is actually not fatal by itself — it''s telling us the default zone reference is dangling. But we want the zone file present.\n\nLet me verify the state.","time":{"start":1790453512998,"end":1790453515689}},"time":1790453515689}');
