INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df58b2f0001eBI5XiJYejmsAq','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',7941,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df589275001v0vlqPKIneGl0o","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df588afd001gE2tIMdhRdg3dX","type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed:\n- `/etc/firewalld/zones/` is EMPTY — the `cp` did not happen\n- The source file EXISTS at ~/Projects/zen-hardening/workstation.xml (1760 bytes, readable)\n- firewalld.conf is not readable without sudo (Permission denied) — so I can''t check DefaultZone\n\nSo the `cp` command didn''t run. The user may be skipping lines or the paste is failing.\n\nWhy would `sudo cp` fail silently? Let me think:\n- If they pasted the block into fish and fish mangled it\n- The multi-line paste may have issues\n\nPossible cause: when pasting multiple lines into fish, each line runs. But if a line has a typo or the paste got truncated...\n\nAnother possibility: the path. `~/Projects/zen-hardening/workstation.xml` — with sudo, `~` expands to the ROOT user''s home, not livings! \n\n**That''s it.** `sudo cp ~/Projects/...` — under `sudo`, `~` expands based on `sudo`''s environment. By default `sudo` does NOT expand `~` to the invoking user''s home. Actually, `~` is expanded by the SHELL before sudo runs, in most cases. So `sudo cp ~/Projects/...` would expand `~` to `/home/livings` because the shell expands it first.\n\nHmm, but that depends. In bash, `sudo cp ~/foo` — the shell expands `~` to the current user''s home before passing to sudo. So it WOULD be /home/livings. So that should work.\n\nUnless... the user''s shell has different behavior, or `sudo` was configured with `always_set_home` or the `-H` flag.\n\nLet me reconsider. Actually the earlier successful command was:\n`sudo cp ~/Projects/zen-hardening/firewalld-workstation.conf /etc/firewalld/zones/public.xml` — and that DID work (it created public.xml). So `~` expansion works fine.\n\nSo why didn''t the workstation.xml cp work? \n\nMaybe the user simply didn''t run it — they may have run only the check-config line. The error message shows they ran `--check-config` alone first, then got the error. So they might be running lines out of order or skipping.\n\nActually in the previous turn they ran `sudo firewall-offline-cmd --check-config` (the full line) and got the dangling-default-zone error. That means the `cp` from the prior block didn''t run, OR it ran and failed.\n\nWait — in the block I gave two turns ago, the first line was the `cp`. The user then reported running `--check-config` alone. So they probably ran only some lines.\n\nLet me just be very explicit and simple: run ONE command, verify, then the next. And use absolute paths to remove any `~` ambiguity.\n\nLet me also handle the dangling default zone: even after installing the file, it''s fine. But let me make the sequence robust:\n\n1. Install file with absolute path\n2. Verify it''s there\n3. check-config\n4. restart\n5. set default\n\nLet me give it step by step, one at a time, to avoid paste issues.\n\nLet me use absolute paths to be safe.\n\nLet me keep it focused: give step 1 only, and ask for output. That avoids another multi-line mishap.\n\nActually, let me give steps 1-2 (install + verify) since verifying the install is cheap and it''s the thing that failed.\n\nLet me do that.","time":{"start":1790453518965,"end":1790453527279}},"time":1790453527280}');
