INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df6960ba002VieKSF87jFXiVy','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',8705,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df69475a001I4iZUjmPyo1aIO","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df693cc3001j5kxqIZqFW1JS5","type":"reasoning","text":"Two important findings:\n\n1. **MAC randomization was NOT applied.** The property is `802-11-wireless.mac-address-randomization` in this NetworkManager version, NOT `802-11-wireless.mac-address-mode`. So my script''s `nmcli -g 802-11-wireless.mac-address-mode` failed silently (I had `2>/dev/null` and `|| true`-ish handling), and the randomization never got set. The current MAC is still `f4:a4:75:e8:00:b2` — the real hardware MAC.\n\nActually wait — in my script I used:\n```bash\nif nmcli -g 802-11-wireless.mac-address-mode con show \"$uuid\" 2>/dev/null | grep -qx random; then\n```\nThe property doesn''t exist → nmcli errors → output is empty → grep doesn''t match → so it goes to the else branch → `nmcli con mod \"$uuid\" 802-11-wireless.mac-address-mode random`. But that property doesn''t exist either, so `nmcli con mod` would FAIL. And my script didn''t check that... let me look. I had:\n```bash\nnmcli con mod \"$uuid\" 802-11-wireless.mac-address-mode random\nCHANGED=1\necho \"  [ok] ''${con_id}'': mac-address-mode -> random\"\n```\nNo error check! So it printed \"[ok]\" even if nmcli errored. Wait, but `set -euo pipefail` is on, so a failing command would abort the script. Hmm — `nmcli con mod` returning non-zero with `set -e` would exit the script. Unless it succeeded.\n\nActually — the correct property in NetworkManager is `802-11-wireless.mac-address-randomization` (values: 0=permanent/default, 1=random, 2=stable). Older NM used `mac-address-mode`. This NM version doesn''t have `mac-address-mode`.\n\nSo my script would have hit `set -e` and died at that line, meaning the DHCP hostname step (step 3) never ran either! Let me verify: step 2 is the MAC step. If `nmcli con mod` failed, `set -e` aborts before step 3.\n\nBut the hostname part (step 1) already completed. So the script partially ran: hostname done, MAC failed, DHCP suppression never ran.\n\nLet me verify the DHCP send-hostname state.\n\nAlso `nmcli` printed errors which means my `2>/dev/null` didn''t suppress them in the `-g` call... Actually the errors I see are from MY diagnostic command just now, not from the script. The script had `2>/dev/null` on the `-g` call but not on `nmcli con mod`. So the script would have shown the error.\n\nAnyway — the facts:\n- MAC randomization: NOT set. MAC is still the real one.\n- The property name is wrong for this NM version.\n- The script likely aborted at step 2, so DHCP hostname suppression (step 3) never ran.\n\nLet me fix the script to use the correct property and make it robust, and apply it.\n\n2. **Hostname fully verified as `zero`** everywhere, no /etc leakage.\n\nLet me fix the script properly. This is a real bug I need to own.\n\nLet me check the correct property and the current values.","time":{"start":1790454613850,"end":1790454620346}},"time":1790454620346}');
