INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df6b806e001JM5Jt22GjWNpo0','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2048,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df6b7954001djc0NPbaTVQwgf","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df6b63ee001a6COeyoIvQq23q","type":"tool","callID":"call_function_z14jxu7ohymd_1","tool":"question","state":{"status":"running","input":{"questions":[{"header":"Given the audit","options":[{"description":"Notifications are already done (Shield Kitty, enabled, notify-send). Leave BDE alone. I write the audit findings to buildlog so the next session knows why.","label":"Keep notifications only, skip BDE (recommended)"},{"description":"Install ONLY the read-only integrity patrol (bde-patrol.ts -> /var/log/bde/). No PAM changes, no hardlock, no bde-auth. I show you the exact files before copying.","label":"Install the patrol only, no PAM"},{"description":"Fix the socket path to your uid, make the PAM stack fail-open when the daemon is down, and target Garuda not BlackArch — then re-audit before anything touches /etc/pam.d.","label":"Fix the code first, decide after"},{"description":"Just document the lockout defect in the repo and stop.","label":"Document only, change nothing"}],"question":"The hardlock would deny login/sudo/sshd on this machine. How do you want to proceed?","multiple":false}]},"time":{"start":1790454759533}}},"time":1790454759533}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df6b807000196wBZWl0hPb77Y','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2049,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df6b69be001994v7biANxdHqU","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df6b63ee001a6COeyoIvQq23q","type":"text","text":"Audit complete. **Do not install this.** The code confirms a hard lockout, and it''s worse than the handoff said.\n\n**The critical defect** — `hardlock/install-hardlock.sh:16-24` appends to PAM for `login sudo gdm-password sshd su`:\n```bash\necho \"auth [success=1 default=ignore] /usr/local/bin/bde-auth\" | sudo tee -a \"/etc/pam.d/$service\"\necho \"auth required pam_deny.so\"                              | sudo tee -a \"/etc/pam.d/$service\"\n```\nThat `pam_deny.so` is unconditional. The logic is `success=1 default=ignore` — meaning if `bde-auth` succeeds, skip the next line; **if the biometric daemon isn''t running, the socket times out, or the verify fails, it falls through to `pam_deny.so` and login fails.**\n\n**And the daemon won''t be running.** `pam/modules/bde-auth` talks to a hardcoded socket:\n```python\nSOCKET_PATH = os.environ.get(\"BDE_SOCK\", \"/run/user/1000/bde-daemon.sock\")\n```\nuid 1000 is user `sin` on the old machine. You''re `livings`. The audit doc also references `/home/sin/workspace/...`. So on this machine the socket path is wrong, the daemon is absent, and every one of those five PAM services resolves to `pam_deny.so`. **That includes `sudo` and `sshd`.**\n\n**Plus it''s BlackArch-specific** — `install-hardlock.sh:1` says *\"for BlackArch Linux\"*, and it gates on `/usr/share/blackarch` existing. Wrong distro.\n\n**The patrol itself is benign.** `bde-patrol.ts` only hashes bootloader/kernel/config and writes `/var/log/bde/`. It''s `read-write` per the manifest, and there''s a 15-min `/etc/cron.d/bde-patrol` — noisy, but not dangerous.\n\n**What I did not find:** any `snapper rollback` in the BDE repos. The handoff''s 5-minute `sudo snapper rollback -c root` claim doesn''t match this code, so that behavior is still unexplained elsewhere.\n\nMy recommendation: the notification layer you asked about **is already installed and running** — Shield Kitty, enabled, using `notify-send` with no fullscreen. That covers alerts with zero root risk.","time":{"start":1790454753726,"end":1790454759536}},"time":1790454759536}');
