INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df6bd5bd0018sRo2rN8dKUTOV','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',8932,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df6bc559001628Ths5bTI8IVW","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df6bbf220017mxPGfKnRSeKg1","type":"reasoning","text":"The bytes are pure ASCII: `note \"would enable recidive (/var/log/fail2ban.log exists)\"`. That''s valid bash.\n\nSo why does bash complain? The error must be caused by an EARLIER unterminated construct that makes bash mis-parse from this point. `bash -n` reports the error where it detects it, but the root cause is earlier — likely an unterminated quote or an unclosed `$(...)` earlier in the file.\n\nLet me look for unbalanced quotes across the whole file. My earlier python check flagged lines with odd quote counts, but it counted `\"` per line which breaks for multi-line strings.\n\nLet me find the real issue: look for an unbalanced `\"` overall, or a `$(` without closing.\n\nLet me count total double quotes and look for `$(` / backticks.\n\nActually — a likely culprit: the `run()` function definition or the `[[ ... ]]`. Or the `$((FAILURES+1))`.\n\nLet me check: `FAILURES=$((FAILURES+1))` — fine.\n\nHmm, let me look for an apostrophe in a comment that opens a quote. E.g. `# firewalld''s default` inside a comment is fine (comments aren''t parsed).\n\nBut an apostrophe inside a DOUBLE-quoted string is fine too.\n\nWhat about a single-quoted string containing an apostrophe? Let me search for that.\n\nLet me systematically find the first place where bash''s parse diverges. Better approach: bisect by feeding progressively larger prefixes to bash -n.\n\nLet me do that.","time":{"start":1790454777177,"end":1790454781373}},"time":1790454781373}');
