INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df6efdf6002vUbMYEeJ0a5bhB','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',9144,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df6ee0f7001NXCsqu1nDbbdMr","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df6ed897001pGTX8pSTk3J5iF","type":"reasoning","text":"Empty output. Let me try a different approach — query NM D-Bus introspection or just try the known values.\n\nNM''s documented values for `802-11-wireless.mac-address-randomization` (since NM 1.42, changed from boolean to enum):\n- `default` (or `no`/`false`/`0`) — permanent\n- `yes` (or `true`/`1`) — random\n- `stable` — stable per-connection\n- `persistent` — persistent random\n\nActually per NetworkManager docs: the property is a tri-state/flags. In NM 1.42+, `802-11-wireless.mac-address-randomization` accepts `default`, `no`, `yes`. Hmm.\n\nActually, from nm-settings-nmcli(5):\n```\n802-11-wireless.mac-address-randomization: [default|no|yes]\n```\nHmm, but newer NM has flags: `default`, `no`, `yes`, `random`, `stable`, `persistent`.\n\nLet me just try setting \"yes\" and see if it''s accepted, and also check what nmcli reports.\n\nActually the value `never` reported now is odd. Let me try setting it and observe.\n\nLet me test candidate values on a safe basis — but modifying the connection is fine, we want to change it anyway.\n\nLet me try `yes` first.\n\nNote: my earlier `nmcli -g` calls used `con show \"$uuid\"` which worked. And my script''s detection `nmcli -g 802-11-wireless.mac-address-randomization con show <uuid>` returned successfully → so property exists. And setting \"1\" apparently... the value shows \"never\" now, which might be what nmcli maps \"no\"/false to. Hmm, or my script set it to \"1\" and NM rejected/normalized.\n\nActually wait — my script''s earlier run (before the fix) used `mac-address-mode` which failed. After the fix, the bootstrap ran `set-anon-identity.sh` which set the property to \"1\". If NM accepted \"1\" as boolean true, it''d report \"yes\". It reports \"never\". \n\nHmm, \"never\" — that IS a possible NM value. Let me look: NM 1.58 `NMSettingWirelessSecurity`... no. For `802-11-wireless.mac-address-randomization`, NMTernary/NMSettingWireless... \n\nActually there''s `NM_MAC_ADDRESS_RANDOMIZATION_FLAG`:\n- NM_MAC_ADDR_RAND_DEFAULT = 0\n- NM_MAC_ADDR_RAND_NO = 1\n- NM_MAC_ADDR_RAND_YES = 2\n\nAnd nmcli renders these as `default`, `no`, `yes`.\n\nBut we see `never`. Hmm — that''s `NM_MAC_ADDR_RAND_NO`? Maybe nmcli renders flag NO as \"never\" in this version. That would mean the value is explicitly \"no\" = permanent MAC.\n\nSo my script set it to \"1\" which NM interpreted as... flag value 1 = NO = permanent! That''s exactly the bug: \"1\" is the *numeric enum for NO*, not for YES. So setting `1` actively DISABLED randomization.\n\nThat''s a great catch and explains `never`.\n\nSo the correct value is `yes` (or `2` numerically, or `random`).\n\nLet me set it to `yes` and verify nmcli reports `yes`, then reconnect and check MAC.\n\nLet me be careful and verify.","time":{"start":1790454980855,"end":1790454988278}},"time":1790454988278}');
