INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df70e28b001PuKFZDBc5d7SNr','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2393,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df70e28a001DJssD6I54mpS70","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df70d947001nrw9CfzCnRBvQi","type":"step-start"},"time":1790455112331}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df70e28d001faammXe3pnK31d','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2394,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df70e28d001zem3VpeNSRN9hH","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df70d947001nrw9CfzCnRBvQi","type":"reasoning","text":"","time":{"start":1790455112333}},"time":1790455112333}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df71236f001zJD9zoELbg7R0L','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2395,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df70e28d001zem3VpeNSRN9hH","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df70d947001nrw9CfzCnRBvQi","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have the authoritative semantics. Critical findings:\n\n1. `unlock_time=0` means **never** — access will not be re-enabled without `faillock(8)` reset. BUT the man page explicitly warns: \"it is usually undesirable to permanently lock out users as they can become easily a target of denial of service attack.\"\n\n2. **CRITICAL**: The default tally directory `/var/run/faillock` is cleared on boot (tmpfs). So `unlock_time=0` is NOT actually permanent across reboots unless `dir=` is set to persistent storage. This is a key subtlety — the user wants \"permanently lock out\" and the default config would silently fail that (reboot clears it). To truly get permanent lock, need `dir=/var/lib/faillock` (persistent).\n\n3. `admin_group=name` — members are handled **the same as root**. Importantly: \"the options even_deny_root and root_unlock_time will apply to them\". Wait, let me parse this carefully. The doc says: \"If a group name is specified with this option, members of the group will be handled by this module the same as the root account (the options even_deny_root and root_unlock_time will apply to them.\"\n\nHmm, this is ambiguous and CRITICAL to get right. The common understanding is that admin_group members are EXEMPT from lockout (same as root by default, root is not locked unless even_deny_root). But the parenthetical says \"the options even_deny_root and root_unlock_time will apply to them\" — that suggests if you set even_deny_root, admins ARE locked too, and root_unlock_time applies to them.\n\nSo the semantics: admin_group members are treated like root. Root is not locked by default (denial-of-service prevention). If you set `even_deny_root`, then root AND admin_group members get locked too.\n\nThis is DANGEROUS for the user''s goal. The user wants \"only enroll and recognize me as admin\" = livings should be the admin exempt from lockout. That''s exactly admin_group. BUT if they also want to permanently lock everyone else, and they set even_deny_root, then livings gets locked too. So they must NOT set even_deny_root.\n\nBut wait — there''s a tension. The user says \"if any other users do exist lock them out permanently and flag them as guests to be banned.\" There are no other users. So permanent lock is a no-op in practice. But configuring unlock_time=0 with a persistent dir means: if someone DID create another user, they''d be locked permanently. And livings (in admin_group) would be exempt. That works — IF livings is actually in the group AND even_deny_root is NOT set.\n\nCritical safety issue: the current repo config has `admin_group = shield-admin` but that group DOES NOT EXIST and livings is NOT in it. If I set unlock_time=0 + persistent dir with that broken admin_group, then:\n- admin_group references a nonexistent group → no members → livings gets NO exemption\n- livings would be permanently locked after 10 failed attempts with NO way back except faillock(8) reset requiring root\n- AND the tally dir persists across reboots, so a reboot won''t clear it\n- Recovery requires: boot to console, root, run faillock --user livings --unlock\n- This is exactly the lockout scenario the user already experienced once (Great Vibes)\n\nSo the ORDERING is essential: create shield-admin group + add livings FIRST, verify membership, and set dir= persistent. Never set unlock_time=0 with a non-functional admin exemption.\n\nAlso: audit vs silent. Current live config has `audit = silent`. Note that in faillock.conf format, `audit` and `silent` are BARE FLAGS (no value). The live config has `audit = silent` which is malformed! Let me check: the man page says \"name = value format\" and `audit` / `silent` / `no_log_info` / `local_users_only` / `nodelay` are documented as bare flags. So `audit = silent` is being parsed as key `audit` with value `silent`. Depending on the parser, the value might be ignored (so audit is enabled) or it might be an unknown value. Actually, in the real implementation, faillock.conf parsing does: for each line, split on ''='', if there''s an ''='', the value is checked against known valueless options... Let me actually test this empirically rather than guess.\n\nActually the real pam code (pam_faillock.c) does:\n```\nif (ctrl & FAILLOCK_SILENT) ...\n```\nand parsing: it reads the line, splits key=value. For options like \"audit\", \"silent\", \"nodelay\", it sets the flag if key matches, ignoring value? Let me check by looking at how it''s parsed. Safer: test empirically with a test config.\n\nLet me verify empirically what `audit = silent` actually does. I can test with pam_faillock using conf= in a test. Actually testing requires actually invoking a PAM service. Hmm, I could use `faillock` command or test with a simple pamtester if available. Let me check if pamtester exists.\n\nActually, a simpler and more valuable check: verify the config file parses the way I think, using the real pam module via a test service and pamtester. Let me check for pamtester.\n\nLet me also reconsider the whole request scope. The user asked:\n1. \"ensure pam faillock ... only enroll and recognize me as admin\" — admin_group = shield-admin, create group, add livings. LEGITIMATE and safe.\n2. \"no other users on any other machines\" — can''t reach other machines; and on this machine there are none.\n3. \"if any other users do exist lock them out permanently\" — unlock_time=0 + persistent dir. This is the risky part, and the man page itself warns against permanent lockout (DoS vector). And it''s a no-op since no other users exist.\n4. \"flag them as guests to be banned\" — device blacklist / guest flagging. The shield-kitty device blacklist is NOT installed (needs sudo).\n\nI should be honest that:\n- There are no other users, so #2/#3 are no-ops. I should NOT build elaborate permanent-lock machinery for accounts that don''t exist. That''s over-engineering with real lockout risk.\n- I can do #1 safely.\n- #4 maps to the device blacklist which is deliberately not installed and I can''t install without sudo.\n\nThe genuinely dangerous thing here would be: setting unlock_time=0 + persistent dir on a machine with a broken admin_group, which creates a self-lockout with no recovery. I must flag this and not do it blindly.\n\nLet me verify the `audit = silent` parsing question and check for pamtester, and also check faillock tally dir state. Then present findings + ask the one decision.\n\nActually, I''ve been told to continue with next steps. Let me do the safe, verifiable work:\n1. Fix the malformed `audit = silent` in the repo config (bare flags).\n2. Prepare the correct faillock.conf in the repo with admin_group, and the safe ordering documented.\n3. NOT install (no sudo), NOT set unlock_time=0 with broken exemption.\n\nBut I should ask about the permanent-lock decision since it''s genuinely a judgment call with a real footgun, and the user asked for something that conflicts with the man page''s explicit warning.\n\nLet me first check pamtester availability and the tally dir.\n\nLet me be efficient — run the remaining checks in parallel.","time":{"start":1790455112333,"end":1790455128943}},"time":1790455128943}');
