INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df715c28001k3BDYbSy9W0Nab','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2418,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df715c28001IOUJMHrvq2KBW5","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df712d1b001KVEjI1TEZ482fC","type":"reasoning","text":"","time":{"start":1790455143464}},"time":1790455143464}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df719c24001JwMor7PP0HggMe','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2419,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df715c28001IOUJMHrvq2KBW5","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df712d1b001KVEjI1TEZ482fC","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a very clear picture. Let me note key findings:\n\n1. **The repo''s `faillock.conf` is well-designed and already correct** — the comments show the reasoning is sound: `admin_group = shield-admin`, NOT `wheel`, and explicitly NOT `even_deny_root` so admin is never locked.\n\n2. **The live config has `audit = silent`** which is the same malformed line. The man page says `audit` and `silent` are BARE FLAGS. So `audit = silent` is parsed as key `audit` with a junk value. In the real pam_faillock implementation, let me think about what happens. Looking at pam_faillock''s config parsing code (modutil / pam_faillock.c `read_config_file`): it does:\n\n```c\np = strchr(buf, ''='');\nif (p) { *p++ = ''\\0''; p += strspn(p, \" \\t\"); }\n// then strcmp(opt, \"audit\") etc\n```\n\nActually in the real implementation, for valueless options it checks the key only. So `audit = silent` would match \"audit\" and set the audit flag — and the value \"silent\" is simply ignored. So effectively `audit = silent` behaves like `audit` (bare), meaning audit logging IS enabled, and silent is NOT set.\n\nThat''s actually the opposite of intent if the author wanted \"silent\". The author wrote `audit = silent` presumably meaning \"audit silently\" or intending both. But it''s malformed. Let me be careful: I should verify rather than assert. I can test the parsing by feeding configs and observing behavior. Without pamtester, I could use `faillock` command? No, faillock(8) doesn''t take conf=.\n\nActually I can test empirically: use a PAM service invocation. I could create a test PAM service in /etc/pam.d (needs root — no sudo). Hmm.\n\nAlternative: I can test the parse logic by reasoning about the source, but I should be honest about uncertainty. Actually — there''s a simpler approach. I can test with a local PAM service... needs root.\n\nLet me instead check the source of pam_faillock if available, or just flag it. Actually, I can verify empirically in a different way: use `su` as the user (not root) to trigger PAM auth. `su livings` from the livings session would invoke PAM. But faillock only locks after failures; and the tally file exists (livings, 0 bytes).\n\nActually a cleaner empirical test: I can create a user-readable test. Hmm, PAM config files in /etc/pam.d need root.\n\nLet me just be accurate: state the man page defines them as bare flags, note that `audit = silent` is malformed/undefined and therefore should be written as two separate bare flags (or omitted), and flag that I could not empirically test parse behavior without root because it requires writing to /etc/pam.d. That''s honest.\n\nActually wait — I CAN test. `pam_faillock` parsing happens in the module. But there''s another approach: strace? No.\n\nLet me think about whether I can create /etc/pam.d test file. No sudo. But maybe I''m able to write to /etc/pam.d if I have write perms? Unlikely.\n\nLet me check if I can test the faillock module''s config parsing with a small C test? Overkill.\n\nBetter: I''ll check the actual pam source behavior by looking at whether Arch''s pam source is around, or just be honest and flag it as a bug to fix (write it as separate bare flags).\n\n3. **Critical safety finding for the user''s request**: `/var/run/faillock` is tmpfs, and the live tally file `livings` exists (0 bytes, owned livings:root, mode 0660). \n\n4. **`admin_group = shield-admin` in the repo refers to a group that DOES NOT EXIST**, and livings is NOT in it. So if the repo config were installed as-is:\n   - `admin_group = shield-admin` → group doesn''t exist → pam_faillock would... hmm, if the group doesn''t exist, `getgrnam` returns NULL. What does pam_faillock do? In the implementation, it looks up the group and gets the member list. If the group doesn''t exist, it may error out or treat as no members. This is important: if it errors, the module could fail. If it treats as no members, then NOBODY is exempt — including livings.\n   \n   This is exactly the \"ordering constraint\" I flagged. The repo config is DANGEROUS to install right now because:\n   - The group doesn''t exist yet\n   - livings is not a member\n   - If installed before the group is created, and if `unlock_time=0` were set, livings could be permanently locked with no exemption.\n\n   Currently `unlock_time = 30` (not 0), so the current live config is not permanently dangerous. But the repo''s comment says unlock_time=30 too. So the repo config as-is has unlock_time=30, which is safe-ish. But the request asks for PERMANENT lockout of other users.\n\n5. **The user''s request: \"lock them out permanently and flag them as guests to be banned\"**\n   - There are NO other users. So this is moot on this machine.\n   - \"other machines\" — I cannot reach other machines. I should say so.\n   - \"permanently\" → `unlock_time = 0` per man page means never re-enabled without faillock(8) reset. But the man page EXPLICITLY WARNS: \"it is usually undesirable to permanently lock out users as they can become easily a target of denial of service attack.\"\n   - Also, with default `dir=/var/run/faillock` on tmpfs, tallies vanish on reboot, so \"permanent\" wouldn''t even survive a reboot. To make it truly permanent you need `dir=` on persistent storage. But then reboots don''t clear it. \n   - AND: with `unlock_time=0`, if `admin_group` is misconfigured, livings (the sole admin) is locked out PERMANENTLY with no recovery except a manual faillock --user livings --unlock as root. Since livings is the only sudoer, that''s a hard self-lockout. This is precisely the risk.\n\n6. **\"flag them as guests to be banned\"** — this maps to the device blacklist / shield-kitty side. Earlier established: device blacklist NOT installed (no /etc/udev/rules.d/*shield*, no /var/lib). This is the \"ban\" mechanism. It needs root.\n\nSo my honest assessment:\n- The admin-recognition part (part 1) is already DESIGNED in the repo but NOT DEPLOYED and, crucially, the group doesn''t exist. So right now NOTHING recognizes livings as admin-exempt. That''s the actionable gap.\n- The \"permanently lock others / ban guests\" part has no targets on this machine, cannot be done for other machines from here, and implementing permanent lockout carries a real self-lockout footgun that I should not silently apply.\n\nLet me verify a couple more things:\n- Can I actually confirm the repo''s enroll-admin.sh exists?\n- Check what other pam stacks would be affected.\n\nLet me also double check: is there an `enroll-admin.sh` in the repo as the comment references?\n\nLet me check the repo for the enrollment script and any guest/banning mechanism.\n\nI should be efficient. Let me run a couple of checks then report and ask the key question.\n\nActually, I''ve done a lot of investigation. Let me do one more round to check for enroll-admin.sh and guest-ban mechanism, then report.\n\nThe key decision point: whether to set unlock_time=0 (permanent). I should NOT do that without explicit confirmation given the footgun, and the man page warning. And I can''t apply anything without sudo anyway.\n\nLet me check the repo for the enrollment script.","time":{"start":1790455143464,"end":1790455159843}},"time":1790455159843}');
