INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df738c25001aRfmG6n57rD6nl','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',9253,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df7379bb001yaGXMHcA3Rc9WN","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df737488001KElmX2TG6vDaXw","type":"reasoning","text":"The user says \"shred it since ur using it for security\" — shred the token.\n\nBut wait: I need to think about this carefully. The token is used for GitHub operations. If I shred it, future pushes will fail unless SSH is used. Earlier established: SSH auth works as intricos777-dot (pushing succeeded). The token is a read-only API credential at `/home/livings/.config/gh/token.clean`.\n\nBefore shredding, I should:\n1. Verify SSH push actually works WITHOUT the token (it did — all pushes used git@github.com SSH remote, not HTTPS). So the token is not needed for pushes.\n2. Check if anything else uses the token — e.g., `gh` CLI config, git credential helper.\n3. Shred it.\n\nLet me check whether the token is referenced anywhere (gh auth, git credentials, etc.) so I don''t break tooling. Then shred with proper overwrite.\n\nImportant: `shred` on modern filesystems/SSD may not be reliable. Better approaches:\n- Overwrite with random data multiple times, then delete.\n- On SSD with wear leveling, overwriting may not fully destroy. Best is `shred` + note the caveat, or use `srm` (not installed). Also consider that the file may be on a COW/overlay fs.\n\nLet me:\n1. Check for other references to the token / gh auth state.\n2. Confirm pushes use SSH (verify remote URL).\n3. Shred.\n4. Verify unreadable.\n5. Consider revoking the token on GitHub side — actually that''s the real risk. If the token was ever exposed or is stored in plaintext, shredding the local copy doesn''t invalidate it. I should recommend revoking it on GitHub. I can''t do that without the token/API. So I should tell the user to revoke it.\n\nActually, that''s an important point: shredding the local file is good hygiene but the real fix is revoking the token. If it''s a read-only token that might be a GitHub fine-grained PAT, revoking is straightforward in GitHub settings.\n\nLet me do the work.\n\nLet me first inspect the environment.","time":{"start":1790455282107,"end":1790455286821}},"time":1790455286821}');
