INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df763634002ENas6o7mCBTbcX','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2473,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df760d7c001eXy1jX41oasMpt","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df760837001ExOt0swW7iK8AE","type":"reasoning","text":"The user chose: Deploy admin exemption, skip permanent lock. I write and verify the commands but never touch /etc myself.\n\nSo my job:\n1. Fix the malformed `audit = silent` line in the repo''s faillock.conf\n2. Write a deployment script/commands that the user runs with sudo, in the correct order\n3. Verify the commands are correct (dry-run what I can, check syntax)\n4. Never touch /etc myself\n\nLet me fix the repo faillock.conf first: `audit = silent` is malformed. The man page says `audit` and `silent` are bare flags. So either:\n- `audit` alone (log the user name into syslog if user not found)\n- `silent` alone (don''t print informative messages to the user)\n\nThe intent of the original author was probably both: audit to the log, but don''t tell the user. Actually, having `silent` set means users don''t see \"account locked\" messages. The man page notes that using preauth WITHOUT silent leaks user existence info. So `silent` is a security plus (avoids user enumeration).\n\nSo the correct form is two bare flags:\n```\naudit\nsilent\n```\n\nLet me fix that in the repo file.\n\nAlso I should consider: the repo file has `unlock_time = 30` and the live has `unlock_time = 30`. Wait — earlier I said live was 30 and repo was... let me recheck. The repo file says \"30 seconds, set at user request, down from 1800s.\" And live says `unlock_time = 30`. So they match on unlock_time. The difference is the live lacks `admin_group`. And the live has the malformed `audit = silent`.\n\nWait, earlier in my summary I said \"live config has already drifted from your hardened repo — unlock_time = 30 live vs the repo''s admin_group = shield-admin\". That was a bit muddled. Actually both have unlock_time = 30. The drift is: live is MISSING admin_group. Let me be accurate in the writeup.\n\nSo the deploy plan:\n1. Create the system group `shield-admin`\n2. Add `livings` to it\n3. Create the enroll.lock file (so nobody else can be enrolled)\n4. Install the corrected faillock.conf\n5. Verify: getent group shield-admin shows livings; faillock.conf has admin_group; run faillock status\n\nAll need sudo. I''ll write a script that the user runs, with verification built in, and I can test the non-root parts.\n\nImportant: the group membership needs a new login session / reboot to take effect for PAM. That''s fine.\n\nAlso important: ordering — create group and enroll BEFORE installing the config that references admin_group. If config references a nonexistent group, what happens? Let me think about pam_faillock''s behavior when admin_group names a nonexistent group. In the source, it does getgrnam() and if it fails... Let me consider: pam_faillock''s `parse_conf` / the admin_group handling. If the group doesn''t exist, getgrnam returns NULL, and the code likely treats it as no members — meaning nobody is exempt. That''s the safe-ish failure (no exemption) but means the user could get locked. So order matters: group first, then config.\n\nActually, more important: if admin_group is set but the group doesn''t exist, would pam_faillock fail to load the config entirely and thus not enforce at all? Either way, group-first is correct.\n\nLet me also double check: should I use `groupadd --system`? The enroll-admin.sh uses `groupadd --system \"$GROUP\"`. System groups are typically not in the 1000+ range and don''t get listed by default in `ls -l` listings... but a system group CAN still be used for file ownership. For admin_group purposes it works fine. But there''s a subtlety: with `--system`, GID is allocated from system range. That''s fine.\n\nActually, one consideration: should I use --system or a regular group? The enroll-admin.sh `ensure_group` uses `--system`. For consistency I''ll match that. It doesn''t matter functionally for faillock.\n\nLet me write the deployment as a script in the repo so it''s reproducible and auditable, then have the user run it. That matches the repo''s style (install scripts with dry-run, backups).\n\nLet me first fix the faillock.conf audit line, then write the deploy script.\n\nLet me look at unban-self.sh too since it''s relevant to recovery — if the user DOES get locked out, how do they recover? That''s worth checking because we''re deploying faillock. Let me read it.\n\nLet me do these in parallel: fix faillock.conf audit line, and read unban-self.sh.\n\nActually let me read unban-self.sh first to understand recovery, and fix the config.","time":{"start":1790455451004,"end":1790455461428}},"time":1790455461428}');
