INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df7a5234001YWSoL2PHHDvaRT','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2754,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df7a52340014RBY9B25nFTkSt","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df7a43f3001M72X0q0yGRVP1x","type":"reasoning","text":"","time":{"start":1790455730740}},"time":1790455730740}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df7a6e73002H3VHDpKTDfACWZ','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',2755,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df7a52340014RBY9B25nFTkSt","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df7a43f3001M72X0q0yGRVP1x","type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants: a 1-day lockout for anything \"spoofing my system\" or \"trying to access my system from another system thru any connection device.\"\n\nThis is essentially: a 24-hour ban for:\n1. System spoofing (impersonating the system — e.g., rogue Hostname/IP/MAC announcement, DHCP spoofing, ARP spoofing, mDNS/Avahi hostname collision, clock spoofing, TPM/firmware impersonation)\n2. Remote access attempts from another system via any connection device (network-based intrusion attempts — SSH, RDP, VNC, etc.)\n\nThe good news: this is a legitimate, standard security control. This is essentially:\n- fail2ban with bantime = 86400 (1 day) for network intrusion jails (sshd, sudo, etc.)\n- Network-level spoofing detection (arpwatch-like, or systemd-level)\n\nThe earlier finding: `unban-self.sh` mentions \"The fail2ban policy uses bantime = -1 (permanent) on sddm, sudo and sshd.\" So there IS a fail2ban policy in the repo with PERMANENT bans. The user now wants 1 day instead. That''s a downgrade from permanent to 1-day — which is actually safer for the user (less self-lockout risk).\n\nImportant: changing bantime from -1 (permanent) to 86400 (1 day) is a safety improvement given the self-ban risk that `unban-self.sh` was written to mitigate.\n\nLet me investigate the fail2ban setup:\n1. Is fail2ban installed? Is it running?\n2. What''s the jail config in the repo?\n3. What about spoofing detection — is there anything? arpwatch? The device blacklist?\n\n\"spoofing my system\" — this could mean:\n- Someone spoofing the machine''s identity (hostname, MAC, IP) on the network\n- DHCP spoofing\n- ARP spoofing / MITM\n- Rogue device claiming to be the system\n\nNote the device blacklist work: \"device blacklist is NOT installed\" — there''s a udev rules mechanism in shield-kitty that unbinds HID drivers for banned devices. That IS the \"connection device\" ban mechanism (USB keyboards/mice). A banned USB device gets flagged as guest. That''s local, not network.\n\nSo \"any connection device\" likely = network interfaces (any way in) + possibly USB HID devices.\n\nLet me investigate what exists:\n1. fail2ban jails in the repo\n2. Whether fail2ban is installed/active\n3. Any spoofing detection (arpwatch, nmap, suricata, etc.)\n4. The shield-kitty device blacklist mechanism\n\nThen propose/implement a 1-day (86400s) bantime.\n\nLet me be careful: 1 day ban with fail2ban is a real, standard control. The main risk is self-ban (user gets banned for 1 day). Mitigations: ignoreip for local ranges, and the existing unban-self.sh.\n\nLet me investigate first.\n\nLet me check the repo for fail2ban config and current state.\n\nLet me run parallel checks.","time":{"start":1790455730740,"end":1790455737971}},"time":1790455737971}');
