INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df7e6368002lipBCBOJKF6ksN','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',3125,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df7e5903001uUkK06epTFL6jQ","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df7e53ed001xajQEZS7TPpt1V","type":"reasoning","text":"`loglevel = INFO` is set in `/etc/fail2ban/fail2ban.conf` — that''s what recidive needs. Good, the dependency chain holds.\n\nSo the preconditions for recidive being safely enabled:\n1. `/var/log/fail2ban.log` exists ✓ (34798 bytes, root-owned 0600)\n2. `loglevel = INFO` ✓ (so Ban events are logged and recidive can see them)\n3. `backend = polling` for recidive with `logpath` ✓ (already in config)\n\nSo flipping the repo to `enabled = true` is safe for THIS machine. But I must preserve the fresh-install warning, because a fresh machine without the log would abort all jails.\n\nNow let me update the repo config:\n- Change `enabled = false` → `enabled = true` for recidive\n- Update the comment block: the bootstrap is DONE on this machine, so record that; but keep the warning for fresh deployments, clearly marked as a first-deploy-only step.\n\nThe cleanest way to handle the tension: set `enabled = true` (matching live, as the user confirmed) and restructure the comment to say \"bootstrap completed <date>; on a FRESH machine do step 1 first or fail2ban will abort all jails.\"\n\nLet me write that carefully. The user wants the repo to reflect what they did.\n\nLet me read the current comment block precisely to edit it.\n\nLines 91-118 in the repo. Let me read them.","time":{"start":1790455994627,"end":1790455997288}},"time":1790455997288}');
