INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df7fa3bf002806lC4UKskor6r','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',9528,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df7f8f6c001tbsT1Q52H6iuMG","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df7f8958001oLP4euGvB43Ni2","type":"reasoning","text":"Multiple agents are still actively working:\n- 4 hermes processes, 8+ opencode processes\n- `buildlog` committed 53 seconds ago — active right now\n- `zen-hardening` modified 56 seconds ago, dirty=9 — and note: \"fail2ban: recidive enabled=true in repo, match...\" — someone just changed the source to recidive=true! That contradicts the documented bootstrap design. I need to watch that.\n- `bde-security-biometrics` 19 min ago, `lia-cli` 18 min ago\n\nSo agents ARE still running. The user said \"once hermes and the other instances of opencode are finished\" — so I should wait.\n\nBut waiting passively wastes time. I can do the read-only parts of the audit that don''t conflict with them, and importantly NOT modify anything they''re working on.\n\nActually the user asked me to run the audit AFTER they''re finished. I should tell the user they''re still running, and meanwhile do read-only diagnostics which are safe and useful.\n\nLet me be careful: read-only audit is safe. Let me do a comprehensive read-only pass now, then re-check for agent completion, and only do fixes at the end.\n\nImportant observation: `zen-hardening` now has `recidive enabled=true in repo` — that''s a problem for the bootstrap''s first-run design. But wait, maybe the agent decided to match live. I need to review that change. Actually the commit message says \"fail2ban: recidive enabled=true in repo, match[ing live]\". That means the source now has recidive=true, which breaks the fresh-install bootstrap because /var/log/fail2ban.log won''t exist on a fresh install. My bootstrap.sh handles this by enabling recidive only if the log exists — but if the source ships true, then a fresh install would ship true and fail.\n\nHmm, actually my bootstrap only *enables* if the log exists; it never *disables* if the log doesn''t exist. So if the source now has `enabled = true`, a fresh install would copy it as true and fail to start. That''s a regression I need to catch and fix.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of thing the audit should find. Good.\n\nLet me do a read-only audit now and track todos.\n\nLet me set up a todo list.\n\nThen run read-only diagnostics:\n1. Agent status\n2. System audit (read-only)\n3. Boot readiness (read-only)\n4. Wait for agents\n5. Fix issues\n6. Final verification\n\nLet me create the todo list and start the read-only work.\n\nActually, I should be efficient with tool calls. Let me do the todo list, then a comprehensive read-only audit.\n\nLet me be careful to not run anything destructive.\n\nLet me start.","time":{"start":1790456074092,"end":1790456079295}},"time":1790456079295}');
