INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df8085d3002SNonUd8dIdi92f','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',9697,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df806b9b001NlIZZ0KlXOxvsL","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df806197001RlNFTvxUZBUuxg","type":"reasoning","text":"Findings:\n- The 3 coredumps were `alert.py --demo` — deliberate demo tests, not the watcher. So they''re expected test crashes (someone ran --demo and it aborted). Not a live problem. Actually SIGABRT from a demo — Hermes was testing the alert path. Benign but worth noting they''re test artifacts.\n- `is_banned` grep returned nothing — the function may have been renamed or the file changed. The running process is `opencode` session, not the watcher? Wait: `pgrep -f ''shield-kitty-watch.py'' | head -1` returned 188697 which is the opencode process (because its command line contains the handoff text mentioning shield-kitty-watch.py). So my pgrep matched the wrong process. The real watcher is 287282.\n\nSo I need to be careful with pgrep. The actual watcher PID is 287282 per the earlier pgrep -af.\n\n- Storage: 4% used, 457G free. Healthy. But note `Data,single: Size:21.01GiB, Used:15.98GiB (76.07%)` — Data profile is single and 76% full. That''s a warning: on `single` profile, when it hits 100% the whole FS goes read-only. Currently 76%. Worth flagging — metadata is fine but data is at 76%. Actually \"Size:21.01GiB\" for data means the data chunk group allocation. 76% used. This is the btrfs reported allocation. Should flag as a medium risk: if it fills, writes fail. But df says 4% used overall. The discrepancy is because btrfs \"Data, single\" size is the allocated chunk. Hmm, 15.98GiB used of 21.01GiB. With 457G free on the device, btrfs just needs a new chunk. Not urgent but worth noting.\n\n- Memory: 7.4Gi total, 5.5Gi used, only 825Mi free, 1.9Gi available. Swap 2.9Gi used of zram. That''s high memory pressure! 5.5/7.4 = 74% used with 8 concurrent opencode/hermes agents. That''s expected given the agents. After they finish it should drop. But worth noting — 2.9GB zram swap usage indicates real pressure.\n\n- efivarfs 70% used — fine.\n\nLet me now check:\n- package integrity / pending updates (important: pending kernel update = need reboot; but also partial upgrades break boot)\n- pacman DB consistency, especially if an update was interrupted\n- The dracut config\n- systemd-analyze\n- user@1000 errors (bch-faucet .env) — is that going to spam/fail at boot? It''s a user service, will retry. Not boot blocking.\n- Check for any interrupted package update (`/var/lib/pacman/db.lck`)\n- Check pacman version mismatch (partial upgrade is a classic Arch boot-breaker)\n\nThat''s critical for \"will it boot\": a partial upgrade (pacman -Sy then -u without full -Syu) can leave a system that won''t boot.\n\nLet me check that carefully.\n\nLet me also verify no db.lck.\n\nLet me run these.","time":{"start":1790456130459,"end":1790456137171}},"time":1790456137171}');
