INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df812f06002srDweodJ9OC2Wm','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',9802,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df81184d001aZAWfI7A7vO0Jl","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df8110b8001ekEm41XpjtW94M","type":"reasoning","text":"Agents are STILL active. `zen-hardening` committed 67 seconds ago. 17 agent processes.\n\nSo I must not touch zen-hardening yet. Let me report the audit findings so far and wait.\n\nThe user asked me to run the audit once agents finish. I''ve done the read-only portion which is safe. Let me present findings and note that fixes are pending until agents are done.\n\nLet me use the question tool to ask whether to wait or proceed? Actually the user said \"once hermes and the other instances of opencode are finished\" — so I should wait. But the opencode processes might be persistent (some are \"gateway\" daemons that run forever). The hermes gateway is a daemon — it may never \"finish\".\n\nImportant distinction: \n- `/opt/hermes-agent/venv/bin/python -m hermes_cli.main gateway` — this is a persistent gateway daemon, not a task. It will run forever.\n- `hermes_cli.main` (pid 179173) — could be a worker.\n- `opencode run --session ... HANDOFF FROM HERMES` — these are agent tasks with handoff prompts. They may be idle/session processes that persist.\n- `opencode` bare processes (44753, 176729) — likely the interactive TUI instances (the user''s own sessions).\n\nSo \"finished\" is ambiguous. The gateway is infrastructure, not a task. The real signal is repo activity — which is still happening (67s ago).\n\nI should ask the user how to interpret \"finished\", because:\n1. Some of these are daemons that never exit.\n2. Repo writes are still occurring, so editing now risks conflicts.\n\nThis is a genuine decision point. Let me ask.\n\nLet me present the audit findings first (valuable), then ask about the wait.\n\nActually, let me be efficient: present findings, then ask the question. The question tool is appropriate here.\n\nLet me summarize findings clearly.\n\nKey findings:\n1. Shield Kitty: running as bare process, no systemd unit → will NOT survive reboot. FIX NEEDED.\n2. zen-hardening recidive regression (agent just did it) → fresh install would break. Need to review.\n3. bch-faucet .env missing, but unit not installed → not a boot issue.\n4. Coredumps = alert.py --demo test runs → benign.\n5. kdeconnectd/mDNS bound but firewall blocks → by design.\n6. Boot chain clean: no pacman lock, up to date, no pacnew, no queued jobs, 49.5s boot, graphical 2.8s.\n7. Storage healthy: 4% used, 457G free. Data profile 76% of its chunk (minor).\n8. Memory pressure: 5.5/7.4Gi used, 2.9Gi zram swap — from 17 agents.\n9. dmesg restricted by our own hardening (expected).\n10. Firewall verified: target default, only dhcpv6-client + ICMP, no icmp-blocks, wlo1 bound.\n\nLet me ask about waiting.\n\nI''ll present concisely then ask.","time":{"start":1790456174669,"end":1790456180486}},"time":1790456180486}');
