INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df8262a1001C4qbeoH2YkpBK3','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',9815,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df8262a1001B5CvGUpH4wvZlQ","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df825c09001yi5Vp71u13KN6d","type":"reasoning","text":"","time":{"start":1790456259233}},"time":1790456259233}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df828a70001A2sNITKrdu7eTU','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',3371,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df825aff001jQi6V7ZNyqVAT4","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df8253fa001xi1yNu4k3sbRn0","type":"reasoning","text":"The alert script takes a JSON payload as argv[1] with keys: `severity`, `events`, `timestamp`. It uses notify-send.\n\nNow let me build the arpwatch spoofing detector. This is the substantive build for \"spoofing my system.\"\n\nDesign:\n- `arpwatch-spoof-alert` — a script arpwatch invokes on events\n- arpwatch''s `-a` mode requires root and sends email by default. Better: use arpwatch with an exec hook. arpwatch supports running a command via the `-a` (sendmail) or via a custom setup. Actually arpwatch can be told to run a program: `arpwatch -a /path/to/script` isn''t standard... Let me think.\n\narpwatch invocation options:\n- `-a value` send mail to user@value (or -a /path/to/binary? No, `-a` with a value that starts with `/` is treated as a command in some builds). Actually: in arpwatch, `arpwatch -a [pager]` where if pager is a path starting with `/`, it''s executed as a command. Yes! \"If the -a option is given and the argument begins with a `/'', it is interpreted as the pathname of a program to be executed instead of a mailer.\"\n\nThe command is invoked as: `program start_ip start_mac stop_ip stop_mac` (roughly). arpwatch passes: `arpwatch-command start_ip start_mac stop_ip stop_mac`? Let me verify with the man page after install, or use a safer approach.\n\nAlternative safer approach: arpwatch writes to a database file (`/var/lib/arpwatch/arpwatch.dat`) and logs to syslog. I could run a separate watcher that tails the arpwatch log. But simpler and more robust: use the `-a /path/script` exec hook.\n\nSince I can''t install arpwatch without root, I should:\n1. Write the script\n2. Write the systemd unit\n3. Write an installer with dry-run\n4. Test the script''s logic with synthetic input\n\nAnd I need to know arpwatch''s exec hook argument format. Let me check if arpwatch is downloadable/readable — I can check the man page only if installed. It''s not installed.\n\nI know arpwatch''s behavior: the exec hook is called with 4 arguments: `start_ip start_mac finish_ip finish_mac` for a \"new\" event, and for \"changed\" events it passes the *old* values. Specifically, arpwatch''s `-a /path` mode calls the program with the same args it would mail: the standard format is:\n\n```\nprogram start_ip start_mac finish_ip finish_mac\n```\n\nHmm, historically the hook is called as `pager start_ip start_mac stop_ip stop_mac` where for a \"new host\" event start==stop. Let me be careful. I should write the script to be tolerant: accept 4 positional args, and also handle being called with fewer.\n\nActually the well-documented arpwatch behavior: the program is executed with the arguments that would be mailed:\n`arpwatch-command start_ip start_mac stop_ip stop_mac`\n\nFor a flip-flop (same IP different MAC): start_ip=stop_ip, start_mac=old, stop_mac=new.\n\nThat''s exactly the strong spoofing signal I want.\n\nBut since I can''t verify the exact arg format without installing, I should:\n- Write the script to accept the 4-arg form\n- Also support a `--selftest` mode\n- Document that the exact arg order should be verified after first install (check the log)\n\nBetter: make the script defensive — log whatever args it receives, so the first real event reveals the format. And provide a self-test.\n\nLet me also decide the response: NOTIFY on new host (weak, likely benign), ALERT on flip-flop (strong, genuine spoof). And optionally a 1-day firewall ban ONLY on flip-flop, gated behind an env var / flag file, default OFF, and refusing to ban the machine''s own IP or the gateway.\n\nThat''s coherent with the user''s 1-day request and safe by default.\n\nLet me build it.\n\nLet me create the files in zen-hardening (where security tooling lives).\n\nLet me write:\n1. `arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh` — the exec hook\n2. `arpwatch-spoof.service` — systemd unit\n3. `install-arpwatch-spoof.sh` — installer with dry-run\n\nLet me build and test.\n\nLet me write the alert script first.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the ban part carefully. Banning a MAC-spoofing IP for 1 day:\n- flip-flop = one IP claiming two MACs. Could be: real ARP spoofing attack, OR a DHCP client that changed MAC, OR a device with MAC randomization (Android/Windows randomize MAC per SSID!), OR a load balancer / failover.\n- On an enterprise network with many devices, MAC randomization is COMMON. So flip-flops will happen legitimately.\n- Banning for 1 day on a flip-flop would very likely ban innocent devices on a large /8 network. That''s harmful — cutting off colleagues/devices.\n\nSo: ban on flip-flop is NOT safe here. Better: notify + log, and require human confirmation. The \"1 day\" ban stays with fail2ban for actual auth/connection attempts, which is where it''s safe.\n\nI''ll build notify+log by default, with the ban path present but requiring an explicit opt-in AND maintaining a small allowlist (gateway, own IP). And I''ll be honest that MAC randomization makes auto-ban unreliable here.\n\nLet me build it.\n\nLet me write the script.","time":{"start":1790456257279,"end":1790456269424}},"time":1790456269424}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df828a74001PQevFv4guiKhqj','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',3372,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df828a74001zfXeZVGHzrLwSV","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df8253fa001xi1yNu4k3sbRn0","type":"text","text":"","time":{"start":1790456269428}},"time":1790456269428}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df828bd200155XnpN1UJEh3IA','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',3373,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df828bd2001Td9DQVi11tmsOx","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df8253fa001xi1yNu4k3sbRn0","type":"tool","callID":"call_function_2gpq5axf0zlj_1","tool":"write","state":{"status":"pending","input":{},"raw":""}},"time":1790456269778}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df82ad9d001yOLc5LODsiNrmD','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',9816,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df8262a1001B5CvGUpH4wvZlQ","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df825c09001yi5Vp71u13KN6d","type":"reasoning","text":"The user chose:\n1. Wait for repo activity to go quiet.\n2. Add NOPASSWD for specific read-only commands.\n\nI flagged that NOPASSWD cuts against hardening. The user chose it anyway. I should implement it but scope it as tightly as possible — and importantly, I should note the tradeoff and make it temporary/removable.\n\nLet me think carefully about NOPASSWD sudoers design:\n- Must be scoped to specific commands with exact paths.\n- Use `sudoers` drop-in in /etc/sudoers.d/ with correct permissions (0440) and validate with `visudo -c`.\n- Commands needing root for audit:\n  - `nft list ...` (read-only) — but `nft` can also modify! `/usr/bin/nft` is a single binary that does both. Granting NOPASSWD on `/usr/bin/nft` = root-equivalent, because `nft` can flush tables, add rules, etc. That''s a serious hole.\n  - `nvme smart-log` — read-only, safe. Path: /usr/bin/nvme\n  - `btrfs filesystem usage`, `btrfs subvolume list`, `btrfs scrub status` — `btrfs` binary can also do `btrfs subvolume delete`, `btrfs device remove` — destructive! Same problem.\n  - `dmesg` — read-only, safe (/usr/bin/dmesg)\n  - `journalctl` — safe\n  - `smartctl` — safe\n  - `lsblk`, `blkid`, `findmnt` — safe\n  - `firewall-cmd --list-*` — but firewall-cmd can also add/remove. Same problem.\n  - `fail2ban-client status` — read-only-ish; `fail2ban-client set <jail> unbanip` is a change. Same problem.\n\nSo the honest engineering answer: for `nft`, `btrfs`, `firewall-cmd`, `fail2ban-client`, granting the bare binary is root-equivalent because the same binary can mutate.\n\nMitigation options:\na) Create wrapper scripts that hardcode the read-only arguments, and grant NOPASSWD on the wrapper, not the binary. This is the correct approach. sudoers can pass args but wildcards are dangerous; a root-owned wrapper is tight.\nb) Grant with explicit argument restrictions — sudoers supports `nft \"list *\"`, but argument matching is fragile and `nft` subcommand parsing could be abused. A wrapper is safer.\n\nThe cleanest: write small root-owned wrapper scripts in /usr/local/sbin/ that only run the read-only subcommand, then sudoers allows exactly those wrappers NOPASSWD. That gives real read-only capability without root-equivalent binaries.\n\nBut careful: a wrapper must itself prevent argument injection. E.g. `nft list tables` wrapper ignores args entirely or validates.\n\nLet me implement:\n- /usr/local/sbin/audit-nft  → runs `nft list tables; nft list ruleset` (no user args, or validate)\n- /usr/local/sbin/audit-btrfs → `btrfs filesystem usage /; btrfs subvolume list /; btrfs scrub status /` (no args)\n- /usr/local/sbin/audit-smart → `nvme smart-log /dev/nvme0` (no args)\n- /usr/local/sbin/audit-dmesg  → `dmesg` (no args)\n- /usr/local/sbin/audit-firewall → firewall-cmd read-only list commands\n- /usr/local/sbin/audit-fail2ban → `fail2ban-client status` only\n\nAll root-owned, 0755, and sudoers grants NOPASSWD on each with no arguments. Crucially sudoers entries must be the exact path, and the wrappers must not accept arbitrary args. To be safe I''ll write them to ignore \"$@\" entirely.\n\nThis is a good design. But it requires root to install. Chicken-and-egg: I need root to create them. I don''t have sudo. So the user must install them.\n\nI can write the files to a staging dir and give the user one command to install them. That''s clean.\n\nLet me do that:\n1. Write wrappers + sudoers file into ~/Projects/zen-hardening/audit/ (or a staging dir)\n2. Give the user a single install command\n3. Meanwhile, poll for repo quiet\n\nLet me first check repo quiet status, then build the audit tooling.\n\nActually order: build the audit wrapper scripts (useful regardless), and while waiting for repo quiet.\n\nLet me update todos.\n\nLet me create the audit directory with wrappers and an installer, then ask user to run the installer.\n\nDesign of wrappers — they must:\n- Be root-owned, not writable by user.\n- Take no arguments (or strictly no arguments).\n- sudoers: `livings ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/audit-nft` — with no args allowed. If sudoers entry has no args, any args are allowed. To restrict, sudoers supports specifying args: `/usr/local/sbin/audit-nft \"\"` means only empty arg list. Actually in sudoers, you can write the command with an explicit empty argument list to mean \"no arguments allowed\": `NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/audit-nft \"\"`. That works in modern sudo.\n\nBut simpler and more robust: the wrapper ignores args. Even if the user passes args, they have no effect. That''s belt-and-braces. Combined with sudoers, it''s safe.\n\nLet me write them ignoring \"$@\".\n\nLet me write the installer script that:\n- installs wrappers to /usr/local/sbin, root:root 0755\n- installs sudoers drop-in /etc/sudoers.d/audit-readonly, root:root 0440\n- validates with visudo -c\n- includes a removal script\n\nLet me be careful: sudoers.d files must not have a dot in the filename? Actually files with a `.` in the name are ignored by sudo (some versions ignore files with ''.'' — sudo ignores files whose names contain ''.'' or end in ''~''). Yes! sudo''s `#includedir` skips files with dots in the name. So name it `audit-readonly` (no dot) and it must end without extension. Also ensure it doesn''t end in `~`.\n\nLet me use `/etc/sudoers.d/audit-readonly`.\n\nLet me write this.\n\nThen the audit run.\n\nLet me also poll repo quiet.\n\nLet me get going. First, set up todos, then write the audit tooling.\n\nLet me check repo quiet first quickly, then write.\n\nI''ll write files now.\n\nLet me be efficient and write the wrappers.\n\nLet me create the directory and files.\n\nI''ll write one installer that generates the wrappers, to keep it to a single file the user runs with sudo. Actually clearer: ship the wrapper scripts as files + an installer. But a single self-contained installer script is easier for the user to run and less error-prone (no dependency on repo paths being intact).\n\nLet me write a single `install-audit-readonly.sh` in ~/Projects/zen-hardening/ that the user runs with sudo. It creates the wrappers and the sudoers drop-in.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nLet me be careful with the sudoers content:\n\n```\n# Read-only audit helpers. Installed by install-audit-readonly.sh\n# Grants NO general root: each helper below ignores its arguments and runs\n# a fixed, non-mutating command. Do not add /usr/bin/nft, /usr/bin/btrfs,\n# /usr/bin/firewall-cmd or fail2ban-client here -- those binaries can also\n# mutate state, so allowing them unpassworded is equivalent to root.\nlivings ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/audit-nft, \\\n                          /usr/local/sbin/audit-btrfs, \\\n                          /usr/local/sbin/audit-smart, \\\n                          /usr/local/sbin/audit-dmesg, \\\n                          /usr/local/sbin/audit-firewall, \\\n                          /usr/local/sbin/audit-fail2ban\n```\n\nTo also block args: add `\"\"` after each path. Modern sudo supports that. I''ll include it.\n\nActually, the syntax with empty args: `/usr/local/sbin/audit-nft \"\"`. If the user runs `sudo audit-nft foo`, sudo denies. Good. Let me include that for tightness.\n\nLet me verify with visudo -c in the installer.\n\nNow the wrappers. Let me write them to ignore args.\n\nLet me write the installer.\n\nLet me go.","time":{"start":1790456259233,"end":1790456278429}},"time":1790456278429}');
