INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df857170001WaytCcotpsAZN6','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',3851,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df8571700013kvhIyCT8l1fuz","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df856b26001HPHgduK2D1Ls8U","type":"step-start"},"time":1790456459632}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df857174001g1x51inf0dCCul','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',3852,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df857173001UgeZRSmwLeBsn2","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df856b26001HPHgduK2D1Ls8U","type":"reasoning","text":"","time":{"start":1790456459635}},"time":1790456459636}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df859fd5001Q9hacMAEt1eQIB','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',3853,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df857173001UgeZRSmwLeBsn2","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df856b26001HPHgduK2D1Ls8U","type":"reasoning","text":"The alert path resolved correctly (no \"alert script missing\"). But there''s a \"Permission denied\" writing to /var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log because in this test I''m running as `livings` (env -i doesn''t change uid), and the log is owned by root:livings 0640 — wait, I set it to livings:livings in the installer but the file doesn''t exist yet, and the default /var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log... Actually the error says permission denied, meaning the file exists and is root-owned, or it doesn''t exist and /var/log isn''t writable by livings.\n\nActually in the real deployment, the installer creates it as livings:livings 0640, so the hook (running as livings) can write. In this test the file doesn''t exist yet (installer hasn''t run), so livings can''t create it in /var/log. That''s expected and not a real bug — the installer creates it.\n\nBut \"alert delivery failed\" — why? The notify call failed. Let me check why. The log line 85 is the `log \"alert delivery failed\"` and it also couldn''t write. But the actual reason for delivery failure is captured in the appended stderr (2>>\"$LOG\") which ALSO failed to write. So I can''t see the real error.\n\nThe delivery failure is likely because... hmm. With env -i, DISPLAY and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS are not set! So notify-send can''t reach the session. That''s why it failed.\n\nThat''s an important finding: the hook needs the session environment (DISPLAY, WAYLAND_DISPLAY, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS) to deliver notifications. Under systemd with Environment=HOME only, DISPLAY won''t be set → notify-send fails!\n\nThis is a real bug I need to fix. The unit must pass the graphical session environment to the hook, OR the hook must import it from the user''s session.\n\nOptions:\n1. Add the env vars to the unit: Environment=DISPLAY=:0 Environment=WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0 Environment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000 — but these are session-specific and can change.\n2. Better: make the unit a systemd *user* service, or have the hook pull the environment from the user session.\n3. Best practical: have the hook import the environment from the user''s systemd user manager / or use `systemd-run --user`? Complicated.\n4. Simplest robust: the unit can use `EnvironmentFile` generated at install time by importing the current session env. But session env changes (e.g., after reboot, DISPLAY may differ).\n\nActually the cleanest approach for a system service needing user notifications: have the hook resolve the session env at runtime by reading the user''s session environment. On Arch with systemd, `/run/user/1000/systemd/env` or we can use `systemctl --user show-environment`.\n\nPractical approach: in the hook, if DISPLAY/DBUS are unset, try to import from the user session:\n```bash\nimport_session_env() {\n  local uid; uid=\"$(id -u)\"\n  # systemd user manager environment\n  if [ -r \"/run/user/$uid/systemd/env\" ]; then\n    while IFS= read -r l; do case \"$l\" in DISPLAY=*|WAYLAND_DISPLAY=*|XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=*|DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=*) export \"$l\";; esac; done < \"/run/user/$uid/systemd/env\"\n  fi\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but note: `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000` — the hook runs as livings (uid 1000) after arpwatch''s -u, so /run/user/1000 is correct.\n\nActually — wait. Is this over-engineering? Let me reconsider.\n\nAlternative, much simpler and more reliable: make this a **systemd user service** instead of a system service. Then it inherits the user session environment automatically (DISPLAY, WAYLAND_DISPLAY, DBUS, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR). But a user service can''t open the raw AF_PACKET socket without CAP_NET_RAW... actually it can''t bind to a raw socket as non-root. So no.\n\nSo system service + explicit env import is the way. Or: keep system service for arpwatch (raw socket), but have the hook hand off the notification to a user-level notifier.\n\nSimplest correct approach: hook imports the session environment from the user''s systemd user manager at runtime. Let me implement that robustly with a few sources:\n1. `/run/user/$UID/systemd/env` (systemd user manager env)\n2. fallback: `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$UID` + `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus` — this alone is often enough for notify-send on Wayland/KDE! Because notify-send talks over D-Bus, and if DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS points to the user bus, and DISPLAY/WAYLAND_DISPLAY is set for the notification server... Actually for KDE, the notification daemon listens on the session bus, so DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS + WAYLAND_DISPLAY may be enough. But notify-send also may need DISPLAY for some backends.\n\nLet me implement: import from /run/user/$UID/systemd/env if readable, else construct DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS and set XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, and detect DISPLAY from the running session.\n\nLet me be pragmatic and test what actually works. Let me check what''s in /run/user/1000/systemd/env.\n\nLet me investigate first.\n\nThis is a genuine, important bug. Let me verify and fix.\n\nAlso the log permission issue in the test was just because the installer hadn''t created the file. But I should make the hook resilient: if it can''t write the log, it should still deliver the alert (log failure shouldn''t block notification). Currently `log()` writes to LOG and falls back to stderr — that''s fine. But `notify()` appends stderr to \"$LOG\" which fails silently. Let me make the error destination fall back to stderr when the log isn''t writable.\n\nLet me fix both:\n1. Session env import in the hook (critical)\n2. Log-write resilience (minor but good)\n\nLet me check the env sources first.","time":{"start":1790456459635,"end":1790456471509}},"time":1790456471509}');
