INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df90b0c3001RwKyseVj0NOY2G','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10009,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df9096460010TY5T90HCbiw96","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df9081dc001Vqrg2AWgzdSAY8","type":"reasoning","text":"sudo 1.9.17p2. That version DOES support `\"\"` empty args? Let me think. sudo''s sudoers manual: \"If the command name is preceded by a `!` ... \" and arguments are supported. Empty string arguments — sudo 1.9.14+ supports quoting. Actually sudo has supported `\"\"` in sudoers for a while.\n\nHmm, but the more likely issue: my printf-generated line. Let me look at it:\n\n```\nprintf ''%s ALL=(root) NOPASSWD:'' \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\"\nfor h in ...; do printf '' /usr/local/sbin/%s \"\",'' \"$h\"; done\nprintf ''\\n''\n```\n\nThis produces:\n```\nlivings ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/audit-nft \"\", /usr/local/sbin/audit-btrfs \"\", ...\n```\n\nThat should be valid. Hmm.\n\nWait — `$SUDO_USER` is set by sudo. The user ran `sudo bash install-audit-readonly.sh`, so SUDO_USER=livings. Good.\n\nBut the actual error the user saw — I need it. Let me think about what else could fail.\n\nActually! Big one: `visudo -cf \"$SUDOERS_FILE\"` — the `-f` flag with sudo 1.9.17 requires the file to be readable AND... hmm, `visudo -c -f file` should work.\n\nBut note: visudo -c on a drop-in validates syntax. If the file is fine, it prints \"parsed OK\".\n\nThe user said \"ran into some errors but parsed okay i guess?\" — \"parsed okay\" suggests visudo DID say parsed OK. So maybe visudo passed but the entry still didn''t work.\n\nPossible cause: the `\"\"` argument restriction means `sudo -n /usr/local/sbin/audit-nft` (with NO arguments) does NOT match `\"\"`!\n\nIn sudoers, an empty argument list `\"\"` means the command must be run with no arguments. Hmm, or does `\"\"` mean \"one empty argument\"? That would mean the command never matches, because you can''t pass an empty argument without quoting. That''s exactly the bug!\n\nIf `\"\"` is interpreted as \"exactly one argument, which is the empty string\", then running with zero args fails to match → password required. That matches the observed behavior exactly.\n\nSo the fix: drop the `\"\"` entirely, OR use the correct syntax for \"no arguments\". In sudoers, to require no arguments you write the command with no args: `/usr/local/sbin/audit-nft`. And sudo 1.9.16+ has a way to require no arguments... Actually there''s `\"\"` documented as: \"an empty string argument list\" — hmm.\n\nGiven the observed behavior, the pragmatic fix is: remove `\"\"` and rely on the wrapper ignoring arguments. The wrapper is the real control anyway.\n\nLet me make the installer:\n1. Write sudoers without `\"\"`.\n2. Validate with visudo.\n3. Then TEST that `sudo -n <helper>` actually works, and if not, report clearly.\n\nAdding a functional test is the key improvement — validate behavior, not just syntax. That''s exactly the lesson from this whole session.\n\nLet me rewrite that portion.\n\nLet me also add a verification step that runs each helper with sudo -n and confirms.\n\nLet me implement.\n\nAlso the `\"\"` explanation is worth reporting to the user as the cause.\n\nLet me edit the installer.","time":{"start":1790457189958,"end":1790457196738}},"time":1790457196739}');
