INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df9437e20010BxAOknHc0wZG3','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',4486,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df942b7c001lL4Va1bO1LR1Hc","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df941d1e001rTcjtmjVNXRTuT","type":"reasoning","text":"Still failing with the same usage message. So the flags are STILL wrong. The unit installed is the NEW one (`-F -i wlo1 -f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat -s -q -N -z`) and the ExecStart in the status output confirms it. But arpwatch still prints usage and exits 1.\n\nSo which flag is invalid? Let me test the flags directly. I need root for the raw socket, but the usage error happens during argument parsing — BEFORE any socket access. So I can test as non-root and see whether it complains about args or about permissions.\n\nLet me test each flag combination as non-root and see what it says.\n\nLet me run arpwatch with the flags and observe.\n\nNote: arpwatch prints \"Version 3.9\" then usage. That happens on getopt error. Let me find which flag.\n\nPossible issues:\n- `-q` might not be valid in combination? No, it''s listed.\n- The flags listed: [-CdFNpqsvzZ] — so -C -d -F -N -p -q -s -v -z -Z are valid single letters.\n- -D arpdir, -f datafile, -i interface, -P pidfile, -w, -W, -n, -x, -r\n\nAll of -F -i -f -s -q -N -z appear valid. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the issue is that `-i` requires the interface to exist AND... no, that wouldn''t print usage.\n\nLet me actually run it and see. As non-root it should get past parsing and fail on the socket. Let me test.\n\nLet me test incrementally.","time":{"start":1790457424764,"end":1790457427938}},"time":1790457427938}');
