INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df945515001ENcMe5eym93Wo6','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',4508,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0df9449180015FCdGa1e9prefq","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0df943e2f001vHmADjOc89wxQU","type":"reasoning","text":"Found it: **`-q` is the bad flag.** The man page lists `-q` in the synopsis `[-CdFNpqsvzZ]`, but the OPTIONS section says:\n\n\"The -q flag suppresses reports being logged or printed to stderr.\"\n\nYet the binary rejects `-q`. Interesting — the man page and binary disagree. The synopsis includes it but getopt rejects it. Possibly the synopsis is a typo in the man page, or `-q` requires something.\n\nLet me check: maybe the man page''s synopsis `[-CdFNpqsvzZ]` should be read as individual flags, and `-q` was documented in OPTIONS but not actually implemented in the getopt string.\n\nSo the fix: remove `-q`.\n\nLet me verify the combination without -q parses OK.\n\nLet me test.\n\nAlso note: without -q, arpwatch will log reports to syslog. That''s fine (we do our own detection); it''s just extra syslog noise. Alternatively `-d` sends reports to stderr instead of syslog and inhibits forking — but -d also inhibits backgrounding which we don''t want with -F... Actually -F prevents forking, -d inhibits forking AND sends to stderr. Using -d with -F: -F stays foreground, -d sends reports to stderr (journald). That could be useful. But simplest: drop -q.\n\nLet me confirm `-F -i wlo1 -f ... -s -N -z` parses, then check whether it gets further (needs root for socket).\n\nLet me verify and fix.","time":{"start":1790457432345,"end":1790457435413}},"time":1790457435413}');
