INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df94bb56001POG4EuX0zUFfDe','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10067,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df94ad1a001Zea1piaNisBV0r","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df949a22001UMZ2jYzAd1d4SY","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I can see the real errors:\n\n1. `syntax error, 242:` — column 242. My line has a **trailing comma**! `... audit-journal-err,` — the trailing comma before newline is a syntax error in sudoers. That''s the bug.\n\n2. Separately, there are pre-existing bad permissions:\n   - `/etc/sudoers.d/editor: bad permissions, should be mode 0440`\n   - `/etc/sudoers.d/insults: bad permissions`\n   - `/etc/sudoers.d/opencode: bad permissions`\n   - `/etc/sudoers.d/pwfeedback: bad permissions`\n   \n   These are from the Arch sudo package (editor, insults, pwfeedback are standard sudo defaults) and `opencode` — someone (an agent) created `/etc/sudoers.d/opencode` with wrong permissions. That''s a real security finding: a world-writable or overly-permissive sudoers file.\n\nLet me fix:\n- Remove the trailing comma in my generator.\n- Flag the pre-existing permission issues, especially `/etc/sudoers.d/opencode`.\n\nThe `opencode` sudoers file is interesting — an agent may have created a NOPASSWD entry. I should check its contents and permissions. If it''s mode 0644 that''s still readable but sudo warns; the danger is if it''s 0666 (world-writable) — that''s a privilege escalation vector.\n\nLet me fix the comma and ask about the opencode file. I can check permissions of /etc/sudoers.d files? The dir is 0750 root-only so I can''t list it. But I can try to stat specific known files.\n\nLet me try `stat /etc/sudoers.d/opencode`.\n\nLet me fix the script first.\n\nFix: build the line with a separator that doesn''t leave a trailing comma. Use a loop with a leading separator, or join with \", \".\n\nLet me fix.","time":{"start":1790457457946,"end":1790457461590}},"time":1790457461590}');
