INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0df96977e001zLsw04Zej1qKvZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10208,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0df96904a001YMf49lu9BHdIUz","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0df96891e001u7jganQTckoRJn","type":"reasoning","text":"My test harness had the argument order wrong: `set_rec false \"$t\"` but the function signature is `set_rec() { ... \"$1\" ... }` where `$1` is the want value and the file is `$1` in the awk call — wait, I wrote `awk ... \"$1\" > \"$1.new\"` which uses `$1` as the file, but `$1` is \"false\".\n\nThe function should be `set_rec() { local want=\"$1\" file=\"$2\"; awk -v want=\"$want\" ... \"$file\" > \"$file.new\" && mv ...; }`.\n\nThat''s a bug in my TEST harness, not necessarily in bootstrap.sh. Let me check bootstrap.sh: I used `awk -v want=\"$RECIDIVE_WANT\" ''...'' \"$CONF\" > \"$CONF.new\"` — that''s correct there (explicit variables).\n\nSo the test harness was wrong. Let me fix the test and re-run to actually validate the logic.\n\nLet me redo the test correctly.","time":{"start":1790457581643,"end":1790457583486}},"time":1790457583486}');
