INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa05bea001oJQfPbUJVKbfzU','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',4836,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa03a55001Y1tH4qDoetqybK","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfa03236001JWBSnV1fW2nszo","type":"reasoning","text":"Key findings:\n1. `/var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat` is MISSING! The service is running and \"listening on wlo1\" but the datafile doesn''t exist. That''s a problem — or maybe arpwatch only creates it when it first records something. Actually arpwatch(8) said an empty arp.dat must be created before the first run. The installer creates it. But it''s missing.\n\nWait — `install -m 0644 -o root -g root /dev/null /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat` should have created it. Unless the installer step 3 ran BEFORE... no. Hmm, but the user ran the installer. Let me check: maybe the dir is 0750 root:root and the file... \"missing\" means it doesn''t exist.\n\nHmm, actually maybe arpwatch deleted it? Or maybe the installer failed at that step. Or maybe arpwatch recreated/removes it.\n\nActually — important: the service IS running and listening. arpwatch with `-f datafile` writes entries as it sees them. If the file is missing, it creates it on first write. So it may just not have written anything yet (no ARP traffic observed), or it writes to cwd.\n\nThe warning `chdir(/usr/arpwatch): No such file or directory (using current working directory)` suggests arpwatch''s arpdir is invalid. For the \"datafile\" arpwatch uses -f. But the arpdir (-D) is where it looks for the datafile by default. Since we pass -f explicitly, it should use that path.\n\nI can''t read the dir to verify (0750 root). I need the user to run a command with sudo.\n\n2. The scan service has NO User= directive — it runs as root. That was the flagged bug: it would derive /run/user/0 for the notification bus, so notifications would fail. I noted this as unresolved and it''s still unresolved. I should fix it now.\n\n3. `/var/lib/arpwatch` is 0750 root:root — so if I set the scan service to User=livings, it couldn''t read the datafile. I''d need to adjust ownership/permissions: e.g., 0755 dir and 0644 datafile, or root:arpwatch group with livings added.\n\nLet me fix the scan service properly:\n- Run as livings (so notifications work with /run/user/1000/bus)\n- Make the datafile readable by livings\n\nOwnership decision: arpwatch runs as root and writes arp.dat. The scanner runs as livings and only needs read. So:\n- /var/lib/arpwatch: root:root 0755 (traversable)\n- /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat: root:root 0644 (readable)\n\nThat''s reasonable and safe — the datafile only contains MAC/IP pairs on the local network segment.\n\nAlso fix the chdir warning: set WorkingDirectory=/var/lib/arpwatch in the collector unit so the fallback cwd is sane, or create /usr/arpwatch. Cleaner: add `WorkingDirectory=/var/lib/arpwatch` and optionally `-D /var/lib/arpwatch`. Actually -D is the arpdir; the datafile is looked up in arpdir when -f has no directory component. Our -f has an absolute path, so -D is unnecessary. The chdir warning is harmless but noisy; adding WorkingDirectory makes the fallback harmless too. Let me do that.\n\nLet me also double check: does the scan service need to write state? Yes, it writes a state file. Where? Let me check the scanner''s state path default. If it''s in /var/lib/arpwatch owned by root, running as livings would fail to write. Let me check the scanner''s defaults.\n\nLet me look at the scanner script''s state/paths.\n\nLet me check.\n\nI need to be careful and check the actual defaults.\n\nLet me read the relevant part of the scanner.\n\nSo next steps:\n1. Check scanner''s state path and datafile defaults\n2. Fix scan service to run as livings\n3. Fix permissions in installer\n4. Add WorkingDirectory to collector\n5. Ask user for one sudo command to verify the datafile is populating\n\nLet me check the scanner defaults first.\n\nAlso: I should answer the user''s question concisely — I already did at the start. Now let me do the work.\n\nLet me look at the scanner.","time":{"start":1790458214997,"end":1790458223594}},"time":1790458223594}');
