INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa07bea001N8456tbOeG2wQZ','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',4853,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa06768001mh80Yw91rWk8T0","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfa0615d001lUQIZnAdvtCM54","type":"reasoning","text":"So the scanner needs:\n- READ on /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat\n- WRITE on /var/lib/arpwatch/spoof-state\n- WRITE on /var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log (it''s livings-owned per earlier, so fine if running as livings)\n- Session bus at /run/user/1000\n\nBest fix: run the scan service as `livings`, and make /var/lib/arpwatch a shared location:\n- dir: root:root 0755 (traversable so livings can enter)\n- arp.dat: root:root 0644 (readable)\n- spoof-state: must be writable by livings → make the dir writable, or own the state file by livings.\n\nCleanest: dir root:root 0755, and pre-create spoof-state owned livings:livings 0644. The installer can do that.\n\nActually simpler and safer: give the state file to livings and keep the dir 0755. The scanner overwrites via `printf > \"$STATE\"` which needs write on the file, not the dir. So pre-creating it owned by livings works.\n\nLet me implement:\n1. scan service: User=livings, Group=livings, plus XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/DBUS env\n2. collector: WorkingDirectory=/var/lib/arpwatch (silences/benigns the chdir fallback)\n3. installer:\n   - dir 0755 root:root\n   - arp.dat 0644 root:root (only create if missing — must not clobber collected data!)\n   - spoof-state 0644 livings:livings\n   \nIMPORTANT: the installer must NOT truncate an existing arp.dat — that would destroy collected history and also could conflict while the service runs. My current code does `install -m 0644 /dev/null /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat` which TRUNCATES it every install! That''s a real bug: rerunning the installer wipes all collected data, and worse, the service may be writing to it.\n\nBut the file is missing, so it got created... and then? Hmm, if the installer created it and the service is running, it should exist. Unless the installer''s step 3 ran and created it, then the user re-ran the installer (second run) which truncated it — still exists. It''s \"missing\" though.\n\nWait, maybe arpwatch itself deletes/recreates the datafile on startup? Some arpwatch implementations truncate the datafile at startup when not using -a? Actually classic arpwatch opens the datafile in append mode.\n\nHmm, but the file is genuinely missing. Let me have the user check with sudo. That''s the honest approach — I can''t read that dir.\n\nLet me make the installer NOT clobber (only create if absent), and give the user one sudo command to inspect.\n\nLet me write the changes.","time":{"start":1790458226536,"end":1790458231786}},"time":1790458231786}');
