INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa14007001WN4008240ASRxY','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10387,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa14007001R3aI0HFMQG9As5","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfa12dee0012LFgwb7Fk4W3aH","type":"reasoning","text":"","time":{"start":1790458281991}},"time":1790458281991}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa14b86001op9yfCvLV47yGe','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10388,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa14007001R3aI0HFMQG9As5","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfa12dee0012LFgwb7Fk4W3aH","type":"reasoning","text":"Now clear:\n\n1. **Units are in `~/.config/systemd/user/`, not `/etc/systemd/user`.** So they were installed by an agent (not my script), OR my script... no, my script installs to /etc/systemd/user. Since /etc/systemd/user is empty of these and ~/.config/systemd/user has them, an agent installed them as user units in the home dir. Either way: units ARE installed, active, enabled, linger=yes. Good — the reboot-persistence goal is met. My script apparently wasn''t the one that did it (or the user didn''t run it).\n\n2. **Only ONE real watcher process** (PID 287282). The other 3 \"matches\" are opencode agent processes whose command-line text contains the string \"shield-kitty-watch.py\" from the Hermes handoff prompt. So there''s no double-alert problem. My pgrep was over-counting. Good.\n\n   But note: PID 287282''s PPID is 1185 — not systemd user manager. So this is still the manually started process? Or is 1185 the systemd --user manager? Let me check. Actually if the unit is active, systemd would have started its own. Let me verify PID 287282 is the systemd-started one by checking its cgroup.\n\n3. Audit helpers NOT installed.\n\n4. Sudoers fix — unknown, need to check.\n\nSo the real state: the Shield Kitty goal appears MET (units active, enabled, linger). But I should verify the running process belongs to systemd, not a stray.\n\nLet me check the cgroup of 287282 and the sudoers state.\n\nLet me run those checks.","time":{"start":1790458281991,"end":1790458284934}},"time":1790458284934}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa14b8a001yxm27OypY12w1Q','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10389,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa14b8a0018K76bI1kjFPVjM","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfa12dee0012LFgwb7Fk4W3aH","type":"text","text":"","time":{"start":1790458284938}},"time":1790458284938}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa14c57001XoDm5Vj8WutKxv','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',5014,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa13b88001zofG6EsjKaLB5u","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfa13338001k51mhSsBnX8dLB","type":"text","text":"Good news and bad news in that output.\n\n**Good:** the service is genuinely healthy now — `listening on wlo1`, and the timer is cycling (last 1m2s ago, next in 57s).\n\n**Bad:** `Active: active (running)` was hiding two real defects, which I''ve now fixed (`fafed7f`).\n\n1. **The scan service had no `User=`, so it ran as root.** It resolves `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` from its own uid → `/run/user/0`, a bus that doesn''t exist. Notifications would have failed *silently* while the scan reported healthy — the worst failure mode, because the log claims success. Now `User=livings` with the bus variables set explicitly.\n\n2. **The installer was truncating your datafile on every run.** It did `install /dev/null arp.dat` unconditionally, which wipes the file. That''s also why `arp.dat` looked missing to me. It was destroying collected addresses while the live collector was appending to them. Now it''s only created when absent.\n\nThat second one also revealed a third: `/var/lib/arpwatch` was `0750 root:root`, which locks `livings` out of the directory entirely no matter what the datafile''s own mode says. Now `0755`, with `spoof-state` created owned by `livings` so the scanner can rewrite its dedup state.\n\nReinstall:\n\n```\nsudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n```\n\nThen this — I need this one, since `/var/lib/arpwatch` is root-only and I can''t read it:\n\n```\nsudo ls -la /var/lib/arpwatch/ && sudo wc -l /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat\n```\n\nI want to confirm it''s actually *gaining entries* over time. A collector that runs but writes nothing looks identical to a healthy one in `systemctl status`, which is exactly what just fooled me.\n\nTo answer your question directly: don''t paste everything. Journal and `/var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log` are both readable by me, and the one thing I genuinely can''t see is that root-only directory.","time":{"start":1790458280840,"end":1790458285143}},"time":1790458285143}');
