INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa2d396001bKUU5yiRgO5mtb','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10413,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa2d396001j2eT3qQObXPN9h","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfa2c87f001T6N1o3qByJMgWt","type":"tool","callID":"call_function_0ih0cuvbsruq_1","tool":"bash","state":{"status":"pending","input":{},"raw":""}},"time":1790458385302}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa2d5ed001brmj653U6FtMSg','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',5065,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa2af89001mp1b8OFVnLb2ZS","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfa2a87d00195TOyxCw7D3FYD","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I see the real bug clearly!\n\n`arp.dat-` — 101 bytes, created at 14:28. That''s a BACKUP FILE. arpwatch creates `arp.dat-` as a backup of the previous datafile before rewriting!\n\nWait, 101 bytes with content. Let me think: arpwatch 3.9 writes the datafile by first renaming/copying to `datafile-`? Or it writes `arp.dat-` as a temp then renames?\n\nActually, 101 bytes — that could be the actual collected data that got moved to `arp.dat-`!\n\nHmm. So arpwatch wrote 101 bytes of data to `arp.dat-` and `arp.dat` is 0 bytes.\n\nThis suggests arpwatch:\n1. Reads arp.dat (empty)\n2. Writes new content to a temp/backup file `arp.dat-`\n3. Should rename arp.dat- → arp.dat\n\nBut the rename didn''t happen, OR the process was restarted mid-write (Restart=on-failure, but it''s not failing).\n\nWait — mtime 14:28 for both. The service started 14:28:51.\n\nSo arpwatch wrote 101 bytes to arp.dat- at startup. That''s likely the initial dump.\n\nCritical: `ethercodes.dat` is 872549 bytes from Nov 27 2025 — that''s arpwatch''s standard MAC vendor database (part of the package). So arpwatch''s arpdir is /var/lib/arpwatch, NOT /usr/arpwatch! But the chdir warning said /usr/arpwatch...\n\nHmm, the chdir warning said `chdir(/usr/arpwatch): No such file or directory (using current working directory)`. And now WorkingDirectory=/var/lib/arpwatch is set. And ethercodes.dat is in /var/lib/arpwatch — that''s the package''s data dir.\n\nSo the real arpdir is /var/lib/arpwatch. The `-D arpdir` should be /var/lib/arpwatch.\n\nNow, why is arp.dat 0 and arp.dat- 101?\n\nPossibility: arpwatch 3.9''s datafile handling — it may use `datafile-` as a lock/temp. Or 101 bytes = one ARP entry line (~50 chars each, so ~2 lines).\n\nLet me think about what to do: I need to see the content of arp.dat- to understand. The user can cat it.\n\nActually, maybe the flow is: arpwatch writes new entries to the file only when it exits or periodically, and `arp.dat-` is a backup made at startup (of the 0-byte original... but 101 bytes?).\n\nHmm, 101 bytes at 14:28 — same minute as service start. If arp.dat was 0 bytes at start, a backup would be 0 bytes.\n\nSo arp.dat- got 101 bytes of NEW data. Meaning arpwatch wrote to `arp.dat-` not `arp.dat`. Why?\n\nOH. I bet arpwatch 3.9 appends a `-` suffix... no.\n\nAlternative: maybe the `-f` argument parsing: `-f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat` and arpwatch internally uses `arp.dat-` as the \"current\" file and `arp.dat` as... no.\n\nLet me just ask the user to cat arp.dat- so we can see. That''s the empirical approach.\n\nActually — I recall: arpwatch''s database file format. Some versions of arpwatch (the Thomas Habets/arpwatch 3.x) use a single file. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe this is arpwatch from \"extra\" which is actually a different codebase. Let me look at the package details and the man page section on the datafile to understand `-` behavior. I can read the man page (unprivileged) and the package files.\n\nLet me investigate the man page and the arpwatch binary behavior for the `-` file.\n\nThat''s the right move: read the man page section about the data file.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nAlso: the datafile format my scanner expects is \"MAC IP timestamp [hostname]\" — arpwatch''s standard format. Let me confirm from the man page.\n\nLet me check `man arpwatch` for the database file description.\n\nLet me run it.","time":{"start":1790458376073,"end":1790458385900}},"time":1790458385901}');
