INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa555b2002Hfpngl0cEWg9WH','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10558,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa54215001lAajgj8zfMaAhX","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfa53b1c001ajDFuKLRZQUtvq","type":"text","text":"`02f1bf3` pushed. Restore your sudo first, then re-run:\n\n```bash\n# 1. RESTORE the wheel sudo rule I removed\nsudo cp /root/sudoers-quarantine-20260926-143446/10-installer /etc/sudoers.d/10-installer\nsudo chmod 0440 /etc/sudoers.d/10-installer\nsudo chown root:root /etc/sudoers.d/10-installer\nsudo visudo -c\nsudo -l | tail -3\n\n# 2. now install the audit helpers (per-file validation fix)\nsudo bash /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-audit-readonly.sh\n```\n\nStep 1 uses the `sudo` you still have via the `audit-readonly` NOPASSWD entry — that entry is currently the only active sudo grant, which is why the script reported \"sudo still works? yes\". Restore `10-installer` before anything else.\n\nYou do **not** need to re-run `fix-sudoers-permissions.sh`; `editor`, `insults`, `pwfeedback` and `opencode` are all correctly at `0440` now.\n\n## What I got wrong\n\nI broke your sudo and reported success while doing it. My script quarantined `%wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL` — the stock Arch rule granting wheel full sudo with a password prompt — and my own summary line then claimed sudo was fine because the NOPASSWD audit entry answered. Two compounding errors: an over-broad classifier, and a check that reported the wrong thing.\n\nThe classifier''s premise was wrong from the start. I treated \"grants root access\" as the threat, when the actual threat is \"grants root *without a password*\". Password-protected sudo is the baseline you''re trying to preserve, not a vulnerability.\n\nThat''s four config bugs this session with the same shape — accepted silently, reported success, wrong or no effect: `mac-address-mode`, `mac-address-randomization 1`, sudoers `\"\"`, sudoers trailing comma. The pattern is consistent enough that I''d treat \"it printed OK\" as evidence of nothing in this project until an independent check confirms the actual runtime value.\n\n## Verified working\n\nShield Kitty now genuinely survives reboot: `active`/`enabled`, cgroup `.../shield-kitty-watch.service`, parent `systemd`, `linger=yes`. Boot path sound — no pacman lock, 0 updates, 0 failed units, 0 queued jobs, ESP `fsck`-clean, initramfs newer than kernel.","time":{"start":1790458544661,"end":1790458549682}},"time":1790458549682}');
