INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa64d24001K1H1M2iO2N5tKi','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10615,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa63c8c0014hEN1ihtFEJ0Np","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfa636bc001G0v9kUwWSrYdZF","type":"text","text":"A KDE password dialog should appear. Enter your normal login password. Then paste the output.\n\nAfter it prints `RESTORED_OK`, confirm with:\n\n```bash\nsudo -l | tail -3\n```\n\n## What went wrong, plainly\n\nMy `fix-sudoers-permissions.sh` moved `/etc/sudoers.d/10-installer` — the stock Arch rule `%wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL` — into quarantine. That was the *only* thing granting your account sudo. Then `install-audit-readonly.sh` failed on its own trailing-comma bug and deleted the NOPASSWD entry that was accidentally covering for it, so you ended up with no grant at all.\n\nTwo of my scripts, run back to back, each removed one of the two ways you had to run the other. That''s a compounding failure I should have anticipated: a script that touches sudoers must never leave the invoking user without sudo, and mine did.\n\n## What I''m doing about it\n\nThe corrected classifier already stops treating `ALL=(ALL) ALL` as dangerous — that fix is in `02f1bf3`. But the deeper flaw is that the script *removes files at all* while the invoking user may depend on them. It needs a hard safety property: **never quarantine a file that grants the calling user sudo, and never finish in a state where `sudo -n true` fails for the invoker.**\n\nI''ll add that check before that script is used again. Right now I''d rather you not re-run it — the three remaining files (`editor`, `insults`, `pwfeedback`, `opencode`) are already correctly at `0440`, so it has no work left to do anyway.\n\nNothing here affects boot. Your firewall, fail2ban, identity and Shield Kitty units are all intact, and the reboot-persistence fix is still verified in place.","time":{"start":1790458608780,"end":1790458613028}},"time":1790458613028}');
