INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa820be001UniVfwhgQUH7CM','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',5328,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa807b2001IHHH3v9G3arnDc","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfa802970010qjq6xhFHoS8ZS","type":"reasoning","text":"So the live config:\n- deny = 10\n- unlock_time = 30 (SECONDS in pam_faillock!)\n- audit = silent\n\npam_faillock''s `unlock_time` is in SECONDS. So 30 seconds. That means a lock clears in 30 seconds. So a *persistent* lockout is very unlikely.\n\nWait — is unlock_time in seconds? Yes, pam_faillock''s unlock_time is in seconds. So 30s. Locks clear almost immediately. So faillock is NOT a plausible cause of a *persistent* sudo failure.\n\nHmm. But if the user is retrying repeatedly, they could re-lock every 30s while typing. deny=10 means 10 failures needed. If they typed wrong 10 times in 30s, lock for 30s. But they''d get in after 30s.\n\nSo likely NOT faillock.\n\nSo what else? I genuinely don''t know without the error message.\n\nLet me reconsider what I did that could break sudo:\n1. The installer runs `sudo` commands nested inside a sudo-invoked script. Nested sudo is generally fine.\n2. `install -d -m 0755 /var/lib/arpwatch` — harmless.\n3. `systemctl daemon-reload`, `enable --now` — harmless.\n4. Nothing touched /etc/sudoers, /etc/pam.d, or /etc/security.\n\nIMPORTANT: My deploy-faillock-admin.sh was NOT run (enroll.lock absent, no shield-admin group presumably). So I didn''t change PAM.\n\nSo most likely cause is unrelated to my work: e.g., the wheel sudoers rule, or a PAM config change, or the user hit deny=10 and is in a retry loop, or something else entirely.\n\nWait — actually, there''s a REALLY important possibility I should check. `pam_faillock.so preauth` is in `/etc/pam.d/sddm-autologin`. If the faillock state got weird... no.\n\nAnother strong possibility: **the user is being asked for a password and doesn''t realize the password prompt isn''t sudo''s**. Or `sudo` is prompting but they typed the wrong thing.\n\nHonestly: I need the error message. Let me ask, but also give a quick decision tree and a no-sudo recovery path.\n\nLet me check a few more things that are readable and could matter:\n- Is there a /etc/sudoers.d file that might contain something? Not readable (0750).\n- Hmm, but I could check the FILE NAMES if the dir were readable. It''s not.\n\nLet me check whether the system has any recent pam changes: stat /etc/pam.d/system-auth mtime. If it changed today, someone/something modified it.\n\nLet me check mtimes of relevant files — that tells me if something changed today vs months ago.\n\nThat''s genuinely useful: if /etc/pam.d/system-auth was modified today, that''s a clue.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nLet me stat:\n- /etc/pam.d/system-auth\n- /etc/security/faillock.conf\n- /etc/sudoers\n- /etc/pam.d/sudo (if exists)\n- /etc/pam.d/sudo-local\n\nAnd also check faillock''s on-disk state file if readable (/var/lib/faillock/ by user).\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790458726322,"end":1790458732734}},"time":1790458732734}');
