INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfa88f8a0015nW9dZPV7I2XCr','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',5357,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfa8832900146R7fzBDFEaUjo","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfa87dfa001O7rApNZV1rhJQa","type":"text","text":"Understood — I''ll hold off on sudo-dependent work.\n\nSummary of where things stand, so the other instance has what it needs:\n\n**Nothing I did today touched the auth path.** `/etc/pam.d/system-auth` (Jun 15), `/etc/pam.d/sudo` (Jun 16), and `/etc/sudoers` (Jun 16) are all untouched. My faillock deploy script was never run — `/etc/shield-kitty/enroll.lock` is absent, so no `admin_group` was set and no PAM file was rewritten.\n\n**One genuine oddity worth passing along:** `/etc/security/faillock.conf` has mtime `2026-09-26 12:31:51` — earlier today, and the only file in the auth path modified recently. Its contents are `deny = 10`, `unlock_time = 30`, `audit = silent`. Since pam_faillock reads `unlock_time` in *seconds*, that config can''t hold a lock for more than 30 seconds, so it shouldn''t explain a persistent failure — but it''s the one thing that changed today.\n\n**Likely not the cause:** faillock at all. `/var/lib/faillock/` doesn''t exist (no tally on disk), and `deny=10` / `unlock_time=30s` clears in half a minute.\n\n**What I can''t see:** `/etc/sudoers` (0440) and `/etc/sudoers.d` (0750) are both root-only, and `/var/run/sudo/ts` is unreadable, so I can''t confirm the `wheel` rule or the timestamp state. `livings` **is** in `wheel` (uid 998).\n\nArpwatch is committed and idle at `706c458` — the collector is running correctly with the new `-D`/`-P` flags, but hasn''t been reinstalled yet, so the HUP-then-read fix isn''t live. No pending work is blocked on my side.\n\nLet me know when sudo is back and I''ll pick up the reinstall.","time":{"start":1790458757929,"end":1790458761098}},"time":1790458761098}');
