INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfac0df30016jL24IyPWN5fbq','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10769,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfabfcca001QoyWT1hsXmi7Bd","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfabf514001lnUOuDIfoAf7Ux","type":"reasoning","text":"The recovery worked. All files parsed OK, audit helpers work.\n\nOne oddity: the permissions show `-r--r-----` which is 0440. Good. But the script reported \"440 -> 0440\" — meaning the original was `440` (i.e., 0440 without leading zero, stat -c%a gives \"440\"). And it chmod''d to 0440 → same thing. So they were already fine; the \"440 -> 0440\" is just a leading-zero cosmetic difference. Fine.\n\nSo sudo should work now. Let me verify independently rather than trust.\n\nLet me verify:\n1. sudo -l works\n2. audit-nft works\n3. Full security state re-verified\n4. Then the root-only diagnostics I never got to run (SMART, btrfs scrub, dmesg, nft rules)\n\nYes, they should run the test commands — but actually I can verify sudo myself now if the NOPASSWD helpers work. Let me just verify directly.\n\nLet me run the verification, including the long-deferred root-only diagnostics.\n\nLet me do that — this is the payoff.\n\nLet me run:\n- sudo -n audit-nft (firewall nft rules)\n- sudo -n audit-btrfs (scrub, subvolumes, device stats)\n- sudo -n audit-smart (nvme health)\n- sudo -n audit-dmesg (kernel errors)\n- sudo -n audit-fail2ban (jails)\n- sudo -n audit-firewall\n\nAnd confirm sudo itself works for normal use — but `sudo -n true` isn''t whitelisted, so it needs a password. I can''t test that without the user. But `sudo -l` with password... the user should run it. However, since audit-readonly is NOPASSWD, I can at least confirm the wheel rule is present (already shown parsed OK).\n\nLet me run the diagnostics now.\n\nLet me batch them.\n\nPriority: complete the audit that was interrupted.\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790458985674,"end":1790458990067}},"time":1790458990067}');
