INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfaccdf5001uN3fc8Onj7AULT','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',10912,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfacbe5b0018Vt8LI9s9XiLyp","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfacb666001vMD1HYMt56IMRe","type":"reasoning","text":"arpwatch-spoof.service:\n- active, enabled\n- Was in a restart loop (restart counter at 27!) because of a bad argument — it printed usage and exited 1 at 14:28:45\n- Then at 14:28:51 it started successfully and is \"listening on wlo1\"\n- The remaining \"error\" is a harmless chdir warning: `/usr/arpwatch: No such file or directory` — arpwatch wants a data dir\n\nSo it WAS crash-looping (27 restarts) due to a bad command-line argument, and another agent appears to have fixed it at 14:28:51 (it''s now listening). The current state is working.\n\nThe `/usr/arpwatch` missing directory is a real (minor) issue: arpwatch''s default database directory doesn''t exist, so it falls back to CWD. That means:\n- The ARP database file gets written to the CWD (which is / for a systemd service)\n- Change detection across restarts may not work properly\n- It could fail to write if CWD isn''t writable\n\nThat''s a genuine finding worth reporting: create /usr/arpwatch (root-owned, 0755) or point arpwatch at a proper data dir.\n\nLet me check what the service does and whether it''s writing anywhere problematic.\n\nLet me check the unit and whether a database file is being created in /.\n\nThis matters: writing files to / as a security daemon is untidy and could fail.\n\nLet me look at the unit file and check for stray files.\n\nLet me investigate, then report. This is another agent''s work, so I should report rather than fix without care — but it''s a real bug.\n\nLet me check.","time":{"start":1790459035227,"end":1790459039221}},"time":1790459039221}');
