INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfb33394001CiTD6X04eOSfZL','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',11293,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfb32cff001Dl1WcSEe7pSnSM","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfb328d6001U9JkGwHiM99W18","type":"reasoning","text":"Line 167 rewrites `/run/user/1000` to `/run/user/$TARGET_UID`. So it does handle the uid. But the hardcoded `echo 1000` fallback on line 55 is a bad default — if `id -u` fails, it silently uses 1000, which could be another user''s uid. Let me fix that to fail loudly instead.\n\nAlso line 51-53 comment mentions the hardcode, and line 167 rewrites it. That''s a bit fragile (sed-based path rewriting) but functional.\n\nLet me fix line 54-55 to be strict:\n```\nTARGET_USER=\"$SUDO_USER_NAME\"\nTARGET_UID=\"$(id -u \"$TARGET_USER\" 2>/dev/null)\"\n[ -n \"$TARGET_UID\" ] || { echo \"ERROR: cannot resolve uid for ''$TARGET_USER''\" >&2; exit 1; }\n```\n\nThat removes the `stat -c %U` fallback (unnecessary now) and the dangerous `|| echo 1000`.\n\nLet me edit.","time":{"start":1790459456767,"end":1790459458452}},"time":1790459458452}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfb333950016GSjLNT3ng8Shm','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',11294,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfb33395001FFLctEFTDQbIyh","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfb328d6001U9JkGwHiM99W18","type":"text","text":"","time":{"start":1790459458453}},"time":1790459458453}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfb333e9001bNR8vFg0450xuJ','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',5910,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfb32665001ZrW5fmnIqbl2zd","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfb3209a001Dp1gqNnEMMgxnv","type":"text","text":"Good catch before you ran it. `enable --now` doesn''t restart an already-running unit, so the old collector would have kept its old flags — no `-P`, no pidfile, and the scanner''s HUP would have silently failed, putting us right back to reading 15-minute-stale data. Now it''s `enable` + `restart`, and the installer verifies the pidfile appeared and warns loudly if not.\n\nRun it:\n\n```\nsudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n```\n\nYou should see this new line:\n\n```\ncollector pid NNNNN -- scan unit can force a flush\n```\n\nThen verify the scan path — this is the part that was silently broken twice, so trigger one scan directly rather than waiting for the timer:\n\n```\nsudo systemctl start arpwatch-spoof-scan.service && sudo systemctl status arpwatch-spoof-scan.service --no-pager\n```\n\n```\nsudo cat /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat\n```\n\nThat last one matters: it was 0 bytes with 101 bytes sitting in `arp.dat-`. The HUP should now flush it, so you should see real entries for the first time.\n\nCommitted `ebe2c80`. Two things still open from earlier, neither blocking:\n\n- The **faillock admin exemption** is prepared but never deployed (`819c363`) — you chose to deploy it, it just needs a sudo run.\n- **fail2ban `ignoreip`** still covers all of `10.0.0.0/8`, so same-network addresses are exempt. I won''t narrow that without your real broadcast domain.\n\nI also still owe you an answer on my own question: should successful alert delivery write a positive log line? Right now a delivered alert and a dropped one both look like silence, which is the root of several bugs today.","time":{"start":1790459455077,"end":1790459458537}},"time":1790459458537}');
