INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfb4e261001BRbX6XuKjfwYvo','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',11629,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfb4c5fe001d2z9mOx8sHFaOe","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfb4c079001aZsSx9l5R7h53S","type":"reasoning","text":"Work is safe locally. One unpushed commit, 4 files, clean working tree.\n\nNow I need to stop and ask the user, because:\n1. The push requires their GitHub access\n2. I genuinely can''t proceed on this without them\n\nBut wait — the user said \"Continue if you have next steps, or stop and ask for clarification if you are unsure how to proceed.\"\n\nThis is a case where I should stop and ask. The push blocker needs their input.\n\nBut let me also consider: is there anything else productive I can do that doesn''t need the push? \n\nThe live system is fine. The scripts are fixed and committed locally. The reboot is theirs.\n\nActually, one thing: I should also flag that the audit-readonly helper and other live state is unaffected by the push failure. The push is only about the backup/canonical repo. So the system is ready to reboot regardless.\n\nAlso worth noting: the fact that the SSH key stopped working mid-session is itself a security signal worth mentioning — someone/something removed it. Combined with the token shredding earlier, it''s worth the user checking their GitHub account security log.\n\nThat''s actually valuable context: if a token was revoked AND an SSH key removed, that pattern suggests either the user doing hygiene or a compromise. Let me flag it.\n\nLet me ask the user with the question tool about how to proceed on the push.\n\nActually, let me present the situation clearly and ask. Let me use the question tool since there''s a genuine decision.\n\nOptions for the push:\n1. User re-adds the SSH key to GitHub (they''d need to do it via browser — but no browser installed, and the token is shredded)\n2. User creates a new SSH key and adds it\n3. Skip pushing; the local repo is the record\n4. Investigate the GitHub account first (check security log)\n\nGiven no browser is installed, option 1/2 requires a browser. That''s a chicken-and-egg.\n\nHmm, this is actually a real problem: no browser + no working git credentials = can''t do anything on GitHub.\n\nLet me lay this out and ask.\n\nLet me write the summary and ask the question.\n\nI should be honest and concise. Let me present:\n1. The push blocker\n2. That the system is ready to reboot regardless\n3. The security question (why did the key stop working?)\n4. Ask how to proceed\n\nLet me use the question tool.\n\nActually, let me first double check one thing — is it possible the repo is fine but the user lost access to that specific repo? \"Permission denied (publickey)\" is key-level, not repo-level. So it''s the key.\n\nLet me ask the user.\n\nI''ll present the question about how to handle GitHub access.\n\nLet me keep it tight.","time":{"start":1790459561470,"end":1790459568737}},"time":1790459568737}');
