s when the push\n+  failed. The pipeline exit status is `sed`''s, so git''s failure is discarded.\n+  This is the worst instance on the list, because it does not merely mis-set a\n+  value, it asserts a falsehood. Use `${PIPESTATUS[0]}`, or\n+  `git-verified.sh`, which propagates the real status.\n \n So: verify behaviour, not configuration. If a script here prints `[ok]`, treat\n that as evidence of nothing until an independent check confirms the runtime\n value.\n","filediff":{"file":"/tmp/opencode/hss/README.md","patch":"Index: /tmp/opencode/hss/README.md\n===================================================================\n--- /tmp/opencode/hss/README.md\n+++ /tmp/opencode/hss/README.md\n@@ -28,8 +28,13 @@\n   argument\", not \"no arguments\". A rule using it parses cleanly and can never\n   match a real invocation.\n - Whole-tree `visudo -c` exits non-zero for unrelated pre-existing warnings, so\n   gating on it can delete a perfectly valid drop-in.\n+- `git push ... | sed && echo \"[ok] pushed\"` reports success when the push\n+  failed. The pipeline exit status is `sed`''s, so git''s failure is discarded.\n+  This is the worst instance on the list, because it does not merely mis-set a\n+  value, it asserts a falsehood. Use `${PIPESTATUS[0]}`, or\n+  `git-verified.sh`, which propagates the real status.\n \n So: verify behaviour, not configuration. If a script here prints `[ok]`, treat\n that as evidence of nothing until an independent check confirms the runtime\n value.\n","additions":5,"deletions":0},"diagnostics":{}},"time":{"start":1790459738828}}},"time":1790459738833}');
