INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfb79e64001oUcPXOczmb2F04','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',11782,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfb78b9b001Sf7kntu3BZct31","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfb786b7001zyh0Gh4ykkX50K","type":"text","text":"Done with the follow-ups. FireDragon is open on both token pages.\n\n## Where things stand\n\n**Open FireDragon and finish the token revocation.** Two tabs:\n- `/settings/personal-access-tokens` — fine-grained\n- `/settings/tokens` — classic\n\nDelete it in whichever has an entry. While you''re in **Settings → Security log**, check for sign-ins you don''t recognise — you destroying the SSH key explains the push rejection, but the token revocation should be on the record too.\n\n**Then clear the audit surface:**\n\n```bash\nsudo bash /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-audit-readonly.sh --remove\n```\n\nVerified safe — it removes only `/etc/sudoers.d/audit-readonly` and the seven `/usr/local/sbin/audit-*` wrappers. It never references `10-installer` or the `%wheel` rule.\n\n**Pipestatus is now fixed in code, not just intention.** `git-verified.sh` runs git without a pipe and propagates the real exit status. Tested: `0` on success, `128` on push failure, `1` on a bad subcommand. A failure can no longer be chained into a success. The README''s verification list now records this as the worst instance of the pattern, because it''s the only one that asserts something untrue rather than mis-setting a value.\n\n## Corrections I owe you\n\nTwo, both mine:\n\n1. **\"You have no browser installed\" was wrong.** FireDragon was installed the whole time at `/usr/bin/firedragon`. My scan tested `firefox` and never `firedragon`, and I''d have had you install a redundant browser. It also means my earlier suggestion to install Firefox for the token task was unnecessary — the tool was already there.\n\n2. **`firefox` does appear installed** despite `command -v` finding nothing. I noted this in passing and moved on rather than resolving it. Probably a `firedragon` dependency or a PATH quirk, but I haven''t confirmed which, so treat it as unexplained rather than settled.\n\n## Reboot status\n\nUnaffected by any of this — the system itself is fully configured and 27/27 checks pass. Two commits are safely local in `/tmp/opencode/hss` (`d72d480`, `5a7bf5d`) and unpushable until you make a new key. **Local git is not a backup**, so if this machine fails before then, those fixes exist only on that disk. Generating a fresh key and adding it via the FireDragon tab you already have open takes about a minute whenever you want it.","time":{"start":1790459743131,"end":1790459747939}},"time":1790459747940}');
