INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfba1333001Xdy5rbN3sPUHoq','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6161,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfb9e508001Xn6PW8n1TgE6Ms","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfb9de64001q5SsyKfOJ8Nom4","type":"reasoning","text":"The user says: this is a local-only build, just for them, nobody else. If anyone is trying to view their machine, ban them.\n\nSo the /8 concern about colleagues is moot — there are no colleagues. It''s a single-user machine. So narrowing ignoreip is now SAFE and desirable.\n\nTheir intent: anyone trying to view/access the machine should be banned. So:\n1. Narrow ignoreip to only this machine + loopback (and maybe the gateway? No — the gateway is the router; banning the gateway would cut all network access. Actually banning the gateway IP in firewalld would break all outbound traffic. So keep the gateway ignored? Hmm — fail2ban only bans IPs that fail auth. The gateway would never fail auth. But if it somehow did, banning it would kill networking. Safer to keep gateway + this host ignored.)\n\nLet me think about what to ignore:\n- 127.0.0.1/8, ::1 — loopback, must ignore\n- The machine''s own IP 10.197.250.246/32 — must ignore (otherwise a local process could self-ban; actually fail2ban already ignores its own... no it doesn''t automatically. sshd from localhost would be banned. Important to ignore own IP.)\n- fe80::/10, 169.254.0.0/16 — link-local, harmless to keep\n- The default gateway 10.128.128.128/32 — keep ignored so a spurious ban can''t kill the network. Hmm, but the user wants anyone accessing banned. The gateway is their own router, not an attacker. Keep it.\n- 192.168.0.0/16, 172.16.0.0/12 — these are private ranges; if nothing uses them, no need to ignore. But leaving them ignored is harmless and protects against a future network on those ranges. Given \"ban anyone\", should I remove them? If their network is 10/8 only, then 192.168/16 and 172.16/12 can''t be attackers. Keeping them ignored is a small gap but irrelevant since no one can reach those from the internet (RFC1918). Actually an attacker can''t source from 192.168/16 over the internet either (not routable). So keeping them is harmless. But for strictness per the user''s intent, I could remove them. The risk: none, because RFC1918 sources can''t appear from outside. But the user''s own machine might be reached via... no.\n\nDecision: narrow to loopback + own IP + gateway + link-local. Remove 10/8, and remove 192.168/16 + 172.16/12 for consistency with \"ban anyone\" — actually, careful: removing them is also safe because if the network later uses 192.168.x, you''d want to ban those too. So removing is MORE aligned with the user''s stated intent.\n\nHmm, but there''s a subtlety: if some Docker/VM bridge or a VPN interface uses 192.168.x, and something in it fails auth, it''d be banned. That''s arguably correct behavior.\n\nLet me keep it simple and aligned with intent: ignore only loopback, own IP/32, default gateway/32, and link-local (fe80::/10, 169.254.0.0/16). Remove 10/8, 192.168/16, 172.16/12.\n\nAlso important: the jails. sshd is enabled but sshd is INACTIVE — so that jail never fires. recidive is enabled and works. So with the /8 removed, what actually generates bans? Only recidive (repeat offenders across jails) — but recidive only bans IPs that were banned by other jails. If sshd is the only real jail and it''s inert, nothing will ever be banned!\n\nSo to actually \"ban anyone trying to view the machine\", we need an active jail. Options:\n- The shield-kitty jail? The config has [sshd] and [recidive] only. Earlier summary mentioned \"only default, sshd, recidive\".\n- Without sshd running, no auth failures are logged → no bans.\n\nThis is the crucial insight: narrowing ignoreip alone changes nothing because there''s no active jail generating events. The user wants intrusion attempts banned. The real gap is: **no jail is watching anything meaningful**, and **no host firewall blocking unsolicited inbound**.\n\nSo the honest, high-value action:\n1. Narrow ignoreip (as asked) — but note it''s inert until a jail fires.\n2. Actually block unsolicited inbound traffic (the real \"view my machine\" threat) — firewalld is active; ensure the workstation zone blocks incoming by default. Let me check what''s allowed.\n3. Consider enabling a jail for the services that DO log auth (sudo is in system-auth, journald) — a sudo jail would catch local+remote sudo attempts. But sudo is only reachable locally... Actually if someone gets a shell, sudo attempts would be logged. A sudo jail on journald would work and would fire.\n\nGiven the user''s threat model (\"anyone trying to view my machine\"), the most valuable thing is:\n- Verify firewalld blocks unsolicited inbound (the actual \"viewing\" vector)\n- Narrow ignoreip so any real offender gets banned\n- Make sure a jail exists that can actually fire\n\nLet me check the firewall state first — that''s the real answer to \"view my machine\".\n\nLet me check firewalld zones/ports, and what''s listening.\n\nLet me gather that, then propose concrete changes.\n\nLet me be careful not to break their network (e.g., don''t block DHCP, don''t block the gateway).\n\nLet me check.\n\nAlso I should reconsider: \"if anyone is trying to view my machine ban them now\" — they may mean enable blocking now. Let me check current listening services and firewall.\n\nLet me gather evidence.\n\nLet me run the checks.","time":{"start":1790459897096,"end":1790459908915}},"time":1790459908915}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfba13350013vHvY2S74vPGnS','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6162,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfba1335001hnxh521NdrXn3h","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfb9de64001q5SsyKfOJ8Nom4","type":"text","text":"","time":{"start":1790459908917}},"time":1790459908917}');
