INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfbb767400249wCSVBJarEV3g','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',11804,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfbb4349001vX8hIwDdLoxfUh","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfbb3a97001rTq8Z22Q6VW6wT","type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants a full system check for potential boot errors, including all snapshot images. Let me do a comprehensive job.\n\nKey areas:\n1. Current boot state — failed units, journal errors, all units\n2. Boot chain: bootloader, fstab, initramfs, kernel, microcode\n3. All btrfs snapshots — can each one boot? Check fstab consistency in each snapshot, kernel/initramfs presence, etc.\n4. Pending jobs, systemd-analyze\n5. udev rules, blacklist issues\n6. mkinitcpio/dracut config validity\n7. Boot partition, ESP contents\n8. Any unit that could fail on next boot\n\nThe snapshots angle is interesting: btrfs snapshots of the root subvolume. If a user boots into an old snapshot, would it work? Things that break: kernel/initramfs mismatch (snapshots share /boot, so fine), fstab referencing UUIDs that changed, systemd unit changes, missing modules.\n\nLet me be thorough and actually check things. Let me start.\n\nImportant: I need root for some checks. The audit wrappers are NOPASSWD but only 7 specific ones. I can''t use sudo generally without a password. Let me check what''s readable as the user.\n\nActually — the user asked to remove the audit surface, but may not have done it yet. Let me check if the wrappers still exist. I can use them for btrfs/dmesg/journal/nft/firewall/fail2ban/smart.\n\nFor things not covered by wrappers, I''ll need to note that I can''t check them without root, and ask the user to run a command. But let me first see how far I get.\n\nLet me plan the checks:\n\n**A. Current boot health**\n- systemctl --failed\n- systemctl list-jobs\n- systemd-analyze blame / critical-chain\n- journalctl -b -p err..alert\n- systemctl list-units --state=failed\n\n**B. Boot chain integrity**\n- /etc/fstab validity, UUID resolution\n- ESP: /boot/efi contents, EFI entries, fallback entries\n- kernel + initramfs present, versions match installed modules\n- dracut config\n- microcode\n- bootloader config (systemd-boot? grub?)\n\n**C. Snapshot analysis** — the big one\n- List all snapshots with dates\n- For each: does it have consistent fstab? Are there UUID references that no longer exist?\n- Do they all have the same kernel? (they share /boot so yes)\n- Check for anything in old snapshots that would break boot\n\n**D. Predictive: what will fail next boot**\n- Units with Restart= that might loop\n- Units referencing missing binaries\n- Mount units for missing fstab entries\n- Generators that might fail\n\nLet me start executing. I should be efficient and batch things.\n\nLet me first check the general state and what tools I have.\n\nLet me be careful about the \"check all snapshot images\" — that''s a lot. Let me see how many there are (14 earlier).\n\nLet me go.\n\nI''ll do this in stages and report findings. Let me start with boot chain + current state.\n\nLet me use the audit wrappers where possible since they''re NOPASSWD.\n\nLet me check if they still exist first.\n\nLet me run a broad first pass.\n\nActually, one important consideration: for snapshot boot analysis, I need to read files inside each snapshot. Snapshots are under / probably at /.snapshots or similar. Let me find them. As user, can I read them? btrfs snapshots of root subvol are likely mode 755 root-owned, readable. Let me check.\n\nLet me start.\n\nLet me do a comprehensive first batch.\n\nI should be mindful of not making this too long in output, but thorough. Let me structure it well.\n\nLet me begin.\n\nPlan of tool calls:\n1. Check audit wrappers still present + overall state\n2. Boot chain: fstab, ESP, kernel/initramfs, bootloader\n3. Journal errors full\n4. Snapshots: list, then per-snapshot checks\n\nLet me go.\n\nLet me first do a broad state check.\n\nNote: I must remember the user''s earlier instruction context — they want to check for boot errors. Let me be thorough and honest, and flag anything I can''t check without root.\n\nLet me execute.\n\nOne more: \"all snapshot images\" — could also mean Timeshift snapshots or snapper. Let me check for those too. Earlier I found 14 btrfs snapshots at top level 256. Let me confirm what they are.\n\nLet me go.\n\nLet me write the commands.\n\nI''ll batch independent checks in parallel.\n\nFirst batch:\n- A: current boot state (failed units, jobs, analyze)\n- B: boot chain (fstab, ESP, kernel/initramfs, bootloader detection)\n\nLet me run.\n\nActually let me combine into fewer calls but keep output readable.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nCareful with `systemd-analyze` needing root for full output but works as user mostly.\n\nLet me go.\n\nLet me run two parallel bash calls.\n\nCall 1: current boot health\nCall 2: boot chain integrity\n\nThen snapshot analysis.\n\nLet me write them.\n\nFor journal, I need the wrapper (root) since dmesg/journal is restricted. Let me check wrapper presence first in call 1.\n\nLet me go.\n\nLet me write call 1.\n\nI''ll check:\n- wrappers present\n- failed units\n- jobs\n- systemd-analyze\n- coredumps\n- user@1000 errors (163 earlier — worth understanding)\n\nCall 2:\n- fstab + UUID resolution\n- ESP mount + contents + EFI entries\n- kernel/initramfs/modules versions\n- bootloader type\n- dracut config\n\nLet me write.\n\nLet me be careful that output isn''t enormous. Use head/tail wisely. The instructions say prefer Read tool over head/tail but for command output within bash, using sed/head is normal. The tool description says avoid using cat/head/tail for file reading — but for filtering command output it''s acceptable. I''ll use sed -n ranges and grep -c etc. Actually the instruction says avoid grep too, prefer Grep tool. But for command output processing within a single bash invocation, it''s necessary. I''ll keep it reasonable.\n\nLet me execute.\n\nLet me go with the two parallel calls.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check: is the user asking me to also verify that each snapshot CAN boot? That''s the deep question. Snapshots of / with shared /boot can boot if you add a GRUB menuentry or use the snapshot''s kernel. But since /boot is shared, all snapshots see the same kernel — so a snapshot from before a kernel update will still boot the new kernel, which may lack drivers for old userspace. Usually fine.\n\nThe real risks per snapshot:\n1. fstab with UUIDs that no longer resolve (e.g., if a disk was reformatted)\n2. Snapshot predates the LUKS/btrfs layout change\n3. Snapshot has a broken /etc/fstab from the sudoers incident era? No, fstab wasn''t touched.\n4. Old snapshots may have the old hostname (not boot-breaking)\n5. Snapshots taken while a chroot/partial state existed\n\nLet me check fstab in each snapshot and validate UUIDs.\n\nLet me go.\n\nLet me start executing.","time":{"start":1790459986761,"end":1790459999860}},"time":1790459999860}');
