INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfbcf268002H7cNIil133YmZn','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12015,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfbcd1a0001yOpHg2WGG4hvCo","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfbcbafe001Y4SPqXeVK8mtjZ","type":"reasoning","text":"My P1 check is garbage — the `/0` and `/a]` are parsing artifacts from systemctl show output (e.g., `{ path=... ; argv[]=... }`), my grep -oE ''/[^ ;{}]+'' picked up fragments. So those 33 \"missing\" are false positives. Except possibly `garuda-pacman-lock.service /var/lib/pacman/db.lck` which is legitimately a file that doesn''t exist (it''s a lock file, created at runtime — that''s correct behavior, not a bug).\n\nI need to redo P1 properly. Let me extract ExecStart paths correctly.\n\nBetter approach: use `systemctl show -p ExecStart` and parse `path=` field, or use `systemctl cat` and parse ExecStart lines. Or simplest: check the binary via `systemctl show --value -p ExecStart` with proper parsing of `{ path=/usr/bin/foo ; argv[]=... }`.\n\nLet me redo it properly.\n\nP2: fstab is complete — all btrfs subvol mounts present, ESP present, tmpfs /tmp. No crypttab (LUKS handled via kernel cmdline, which is normal for a single LUKS root). Good.\n\nP3: snapper-cleanup.timer active, last run 2min ago succeeded, no errors. snapper-timeline.timer inactive — meaning no automatic timeline snapshots. Notable: snapshots are being created by something (Garuda?) but the timeline timer is off, so snapper isn''t auto-snapshotting. Not a boot risk.\n\nWait — but snapshots ARE being created (11 today, last at 14:47). And timeshift is not installed. So what creates them? Maybe Garuda-assistant, or a manual `snapper create`. Since snapper-timeline.timer is inactive, they''re manual. Fine.\n\nImportant: snapper-cleanup.timer is active. If snapper''s config has limits, cleanup could DELETE snapshots. Also, a known issue: snapper-cleanup can fail if the btrfs volume is read-only or if /.snapshots isn''t a proper snapper subvolume config. It ran fine 2 min ago.\n\nNow the critical remaining item: GRUB integration. Do the snapshots actually appear in the GRUB menu? grub-btrfs.cfg is present. But is it wired into grub.cfg? And does it correctly find /.snapshots?\n\nLet me check that, plus redo P1 properly.\n\nAlso I should check: is /.snapshots listed in snapper config with the right SUBVOLUME and FSTYPE? A misconfigured snapper could cause cleanup to fail on next boot.\n\nAnd check for a real boot risk: `grub-btrfs` needs the btrfs module and correct GRUB_DEVICE. Let me check grub.cfg includes grub-btrfs.cfg.\n\nLet me also verify the initramfs actually contains what''s needed (btrfs, luks, nvme) — a missing module in initramfs = boot failure after kernel update. Let me check with lsinitcpio (needs root for the file, but /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img is 0644 readable).\n\nThat''s a genuinely valuable predictive check. Let me do it.\n\nLet me run:\n1. Redo P1 correctly\n2. GRUB snapshot integration\n3. initramfs contents (lsinitcpio) — critical for boot\n4. snapper config validity\n\nLet me go.\n\nlsinitcpio /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img | grep -E ''btrfs|luks|crypt|nvme'' — check the storage modules are present.\n\nLet me run.\n\nThis is the most important remaining check: if the initramfs lacks btrfs/crypt modules, the next reboot fails. Let me verify.\n\nLet me do it.","time":{"start":1790460088736,"end":1790460097128}},"time":1790460097128}');
