INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfbd4905001iITVxN2GpNEqKd','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12064,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfbd2b37001XjlQM14Gd03Sr4","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfbd24d1001G6XeeCXvuAR7GC","type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: dracut, not mkinitcpio. So `lsinitcpio` is the wrong tool — that''s why everything showed MISSING. False alarm, as I suspected.\n\nAnd `garuda-pacman-lock.service` has `ExecStart=rm -f /var/lib/pacman/db.lck` — a bare `rm`, relying on PATH. My parser reported \"rm\" as missing because it''s not an absolute path. Bare command names in ExecStart are technically an error in systemd (it requires absolute paths since v240), BUT systemd actually tolerates it in some cases by searching PATH... Actually, systemd requires the executable to be an absolute path in ExecStart; a bare name is rejected. Hmm, but the unit is enabled and presumably works.\n\nWait — systemd docs: \"the first item must be either an absolute path to an executable or a simple file name without any slashes... If it''s a simple file name, it''s searched in a fixed set of directories.\" Actually systemd does support simple names searched in $PATH-like default dirs (/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin). Let me not overstate. systemd will resolve `rm` via a default search path. It works.\n\nActually, systemd''s documented behavior: \"the command line accepts either an absolute path to the executable or a simple file name without any slashes. ... it''s recommended to use absolute paths.\" So bare names work.\n\nSo that''s not a bug either. My P1''s single \"missing\" was `rm` because I only tested `-e \"rm\"` relative to cwd.\n\nSo P1 is actually clean: 0 real missing targets.\n\nNow the file permission issue: `file -b /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img` → \"regular file, no read permission\". So I can''t read the initramfs as livings. Need root.\n\nSo to verify initramfs contents I need root. Let me use the dracut tool if available and ask user, OR note it.\n\nActually — I have the audit wrappers. None inspect initramfs. So I need the user to run it, OR I can note that the initramfs was verified fresh earlier (newer than kernel) and dracut config is what matters.\n\nLet me check /etc/dracut.conf contents (readable?) for correctness — that''s the config that determines whether the initramfs can boot.\n\nLet me check dracut.conf and dracut.conf.d.\n\nKey: `hostonly`, `add_drivers`, and whether btrfs/crypt are included. If hostonly=\"yes\" (default), the initramfs only includes what''s on the current machine — which is fine.\n\nLet me read the dracut config. And check the kernel cmdline from GRUB to confirm the root= parameter matches (that''s what the initramfs uses to find root).\n\nActually a REALLY important predictive check I CAN do without root: read GRUB''s grub.cfg to see the kernel command line, and confirm `root=UUID=...` or `rd.luks.uuid=...` matches the actual disk. If GRUB''s cmdline references a stale UUID, boot fails.\n\nLet me check /boot/grub/grub.cfg — is it readable? It''s in @ subvolume root, 0644 probably. Let me try.\n\nThat''s the highest-value remaining check. Let me do it.\n\nLet me read grub.cfg kernel lines.\n\nLet me also read dracut.conf.\n\nLet me run.\n\nAlso check mkinitcpio/dracut cmdline: root=UUID, and whether `cryptdevice` or `rd.luks.uuid` is present for LUKS.\n\nLet me go.\n\nNote: /boot/grub/grub.cfg is root-owned but likely world-readable (0644). Let me try.\n\nLet me run these checks.\n\nAlso I should check the ESP grubx64.efi existence — needs root. Flag it.\n\nLet me do the GRUB cmdline check now.","time":{"start":1790460111671,"end":1790460119301}},"time":1790460119301}');
