INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfbd8951001k9Dqmpd3AP6Uqj','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12088,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfbd61e6001lpOuXSda8UJ2OC","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfbd5c0a001b9DGCkCn4opmMc","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have the authoritative cmdline:\n```\nroot=UUID=51bd7935-dbd0-4976-8bb3-5804a5e8e4e8 rw rootflags=subvol=@ quiet rd.luks.uuid=8c39203b-6764-4e5c-b1a9-6dedb64c05c1 loglevel=3\n```\n\nBoth UUIDs match reality:\n- root=UUID=51bd7935... = lsblk UUID of nvme0n1p2 ✓\n- rd.luks.uuid=8c39203b... = the LUKS mapper name 8c39203b-6764-4e5c-b1a9-6dedb64c05c1 ✓\n\nSo the running cmdline is correct. But this is the cmdline the CURRENT boot used, generated by GRUB. The question is whether the grub.cfg on disk would generate the same. grub.cfg isn''t readable as user.\n\nImportant: `BOOT_IMAGE=/@/boot/vmlinuz-linux-zen` — this is the snapshot-relative path. For the top-level snapshot, GRUB uses `/@/boot/...`. For snapshots it''d be `/.snapshots/37/snapshot/boot/...`. That confirms grub-btrfs is working and the top-level entry is `@`.\n\ngrub-btrfs.cfg has 22 subvol references — snapshots are discoverable.\n\nBut: `grep -c ''grub-btrfs'' /boot/grub/grub.cfg` returned nothing (the line didn''t print) because grub.cfg isn''t readable. So I couldn''t verify that grub.cfg actually sources grub-btrfs.cfg. That''s a gap. However, the fact that the system booted with BOOT_IMAGE=/@/boot/... and grub-btrfs.cfg exists with 22 subvol refs strongly suggests it''s wired. But not proven.\n\nLet me note that as needing root.\n\nNow, a KEY finding I should focus on: **the dracut config has `install_items+=\" /etc/crypttab /crypto_keyfile.bin \"`** in `/etc/dracut.conf.d/calamares-luks.conf`.\n\nThat''s a leftover from the Garuda installer (calamares). It adds `/etc/crypttab` and `/crypto_keyfile.bin` to the initramfs. But:\n1. `/etc/crypttab` DOESN''T EXIST (we checked: \"crypttab entries: no crypttab\")\n2. `/crypto_keyfile.bin` — does it exist? This is the LUKS keyfile used to auto-unlock at boot!\n\nThis is a REAL risk: if the initramfs references a keyfile that doesn''t exist, and dracut''s crypt module tries to use it, boot could hang waiting for a passphrase... but actually the system boots fine using rd.luks.uuid which prompts or uses the keyfile.\n\nCritical question: does `/crypto_keyfile.bin` exist? If it doesn''t, and the system relies on it for auto-unlock, then unattended boot would FAIL — but manual boot works because you type the passphrase. That would be a serious issue for automatic restart after power loss.\n\nBut the system IS booting... does it auto-unlock? If the user types a passphrase at boot, no keyfile needed.\n\nLet me check whether /crypto_keyfile.bin exists. That''s readable? It''s at / root, probably 0600 root. Let me check existence via stat (may be permission denied).\n\nThis is a genuinely important boot-risk finding. Let me investigate.\n\nLet me check:\n1. /etc/crypttab exists? (no)\n2. /crypto_keyfile.bin exists?\n3. Is there a systemd cryptsetup unit for the root device? From the mounts: `/run/credentials/systemd-cryptsetup@luks\\x5cx2d8c39203b...service` — that indicates systemd-cryptsetup was involved! So there IS a systemd-cryptsetup service for the LUKS device, which uses /etc/crypttab or a keyfile.\n\nWait, the credentials path suggests cryptsetup used credentials (a keyfile or password passed via credential). If /etc/crypttab doesn''t exist, where does it get the key? \n\nHmm, `systemd-cryptsetup@luks-8c39203b...` with credentials — that could be from a keyfile specified in a unit, or it could be the standard credential mechanism.\n\nLet me investigate what unlocks the root LUKS device. This determines whether the machine auto-boots unattended or needs a typed passphrase. That''s a legitimate \"potential boot error\" concern — if it needs a passphrase, that''s fine; but if a keyfile is expected and missing, boot would FAIL.\n\nSince the system is currently running, the unlock succeeded. But was it automatic or manual? I can check the journal for cryptsetup messages.\n\nLet me look at the journal for the cryptsetup unit and check the keyfile existence.\n\nThis is the most important remaining item. Let me dig.\n\nLet me run.\n\nLet me check:\n- ls -l /crypto_keyfile.bin (permission may deny)\n- journalctl -b -u systemd-cryptsetup@... \n- dracut --print-cmdline (shows what dracut baked)\n- The cryptsetup unit''s properties\n\nLet me do it.\n\nNote audit-journal-err wrapper gives root journal access. And audit-dmesg gives dmesg. Let me use those for root-only journal entries.\n\nLet me check.","time":{"start":1790460125670,"end":1790460135761}},"time":1790460135761}');
