INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfbdae69001Vs02sXHyzofYjf','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12107,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfbd9940001AaMLZojq1luM65","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfbd9247001FrCtdoEkGgl3gR","type":"reasoning","text":"Good news: `/etc/crypttab` DOES exist (my earlier check said \"no crypttab\" — that was because `grep -vcE` returned 0 and my `|| echo` didn''t trigger, and I printed \"no crypttab\" incorrectly). Let me correct: earlier I ran `grep -vcE ''^\\s*#|^\\s*$'' /etc/crypttab 2>/dev/null || echo ''no crypttab''` — grep -c returns 0 count with exit status 1, so `||` fired and printed ''no crypttab''. But the file EXISTS; it just has 0 non-comment lines?? That can''t be, since systemd-cryptsetup ran and the keyfile is used.\n\nWait, /crypto_keyfile.bin exists (2048 bytes, 0600 root). And /etc/crypttab exists. Let me read crypttab.\n\nHmm, if crypttab has entries, grep -vc would be >0. Unless the file has no trailing newline or different formatting.\n\nLet me actually read /etc/crypttab.\n\nThe key finding: the LUKS device was unlocked automatically (finished in ~4s, no passphrase prompt visible), and the cipher is aes-xts-plain64 with 512-bit key. systemd-cryptsetup used credentials (the /run/credentials path we saw).\n\nSo: unattended unlock works via keyfile. Good — no boot risk there.\n\nBut this IS a security finding worth noting: a 2048-byte keyfile at / on the LUKS volume, referenced by the initramfs. That means anyone who can read /crypto_keyfile.bin (root) can unlock the disk. That''s standard for auto-unlock setups. Fine.\n\nLet me read /etc/crypttab to confirm the keyfile configuration.\n\nLet me also correct my earlier wrong statement about crypttab not existing.\n\nLet me read it.\n\nAlso, I should verify: does the initramfs actually contain the keyfile? dracut''s `install_items` added it. Can''t verify without root.\n\nLet me read crypttab.\n\nThen wrap up with:\n- ESP EFI file verification (needs root — must ask user)\n- grub.cfg readable check (needs root)\n- initramfs content check (needs root)\n\nLet me read crypttab and then compile the report.\n\nLet me also do a final check on the snapshots: check the sudoers state in pre/post-incident snapshots, since that''s relevant (a snapshot from during the incident would restore broken sudoers). Snapshot 34/35 at 14:11 predate the incident (14:34), 36/37 at 14:47 postdate. So no snapshot captured the broken state. Let me verify that claim by checking 10-installer presence in each snapshot''s /etc/sudoers.d.\n\nThat''s a genuinely useful check: would any snapshot restore a broken sudoers?\n\nLet me do both.\n\nLet me run.\n\nNote: /etc/sudoers.d in snapshots is root-owned 0440 — may not be readable as user. Let me check readability. 0440 root:root → not readable by livings. So I can''t check without root. But I can check file existence via directory listing if the dir is 0755.\n\nLet me try listing.\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790460139840,"end":1790460145256}},"time":1790460145257}');
