INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfbdeb35001YTlB6RO9bnnm0a','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12134,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfbdc06c0014jXZXQqQV8Oyz5","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfbdb998001LN4KBI446P7G68","type":"reasoning","text":"The `[dir empty/unreadable]` for ALL snapshots, and 10-installer MISSING in all — but I know 10-installer EXISTS on the live system (restored). So the snapshot sudoers.d dirs are unreadable to me (root-owned, 0750 or 0700), meaning `ls` returns nothing and `[ -e ]` returns false due to permission denial on the parent dir traversal.\n\nSo this check is INCONCLUSIVE — I cannot read snapshot /etc/sudoers.d without root. I must not claim the snapshots lack 10-installer.\n\nLet me be honest: this check couldn''t be performed. `/etc/sudoers.d` on the live system was mode 0750 root:root (we saw KDE preview errors about it). Snapshots preserve permissions, so they''re also 0750 root — unreadable by livings.\n\nI need root for this. Let me flag it.\n\nAlso crypttab is unreadable as user (output empty) — so I can''t confirm the keyfile config, though the runtime evidence (4s unlock, credentials path) proves it works.\n\nSo remaining items genuinely requiring root:\n1. ESP contents — does \\EFI\\Garuda\\grubx64.efi exist? (CRITICAL — if missing, firmware falls through to Boot0005)\n2. /boot/grub/grub.cfg — does it source grub-btrfs.cfg? (determines if snapshots are bootable)\n3. initramfs contents — btrfs/crypt/nvme modules (dracut, need lsinitrd)\n4. Snapshot /etc/sudoers.d — any snapshot with broken sudoers?\n5. /etc/crypttab content\n6. grubenv contents\n\nLet me write a single root script the user can run that answers all of these, and give it to them. That''s the useful deliverable.\n\nActually — I should reconsider. Can I get root another way? The user said they''d do a manual reboot. I have 7 NOPASSWD wrappers, all read-only audit tools, none of which do this.\n\nI could ask the user to run one command. Let me write a compact, safe, read-only script and hand it over. That''s the right move — it''s read-only, and it''s the honest way to close the remaining gaps.\n\nLet me write /tmp/opencode/boot-check-root.sh that does all 6 checks read-only, and give them the command.\n\nLet me make sure it''s genuinely read-only and clearly so.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nThen present the full report.\n\nLet me be careful the script is read-only — only reads, no writes. Use ls/find/cat/lsinitrd/visudo -c (read-only), btrfs subvolume show (read-only).\n\nLet me write it.\n\nLet me also compute a summary of what I found.\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nI''ll make it print clear PASS/FAIL/UNKNOWN per item.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nThen summarize findings:\n\nCLEAN:\n- 0 failed units, 0 queued jobs\n- fstab: all UUIDs resolve, all 7 subvol mounts present\n- kernel/initramfs consistent, initramfs newer than kernel, only 1 kernel (no orphans)\n- LUKS auto-unlock works (4s, keyfile, aes-xts-xts-512)\n- cmdline UUIDs match actual devices\n- Boot0002 Garuda NVRAM entry PARTUUID matches nvme0n1p1\n- all 11 snapshots: fstab present, all UUIDs resolve, kernel+initramfs+modules present\n- snapper-cleanup.timer active, last run clean, no errors\n- 0 enabled units with missing ExecStart targets\n- btrfs: no errors, all counters 0\n- no hardware faults, no OOM\n\nFINDINGS (not boot-breaking):\n1. Stale NVRAM entries: kali, BlackArch_Linux (VenHw QEMU path) — leftovers from VM use. BootOrder includes them but after Garuda. Harmless but clutter; if Garuda entry ever fails, firmware would try PXE, then fallback, then these.\n2. 14.9s firmware time — long. Consistent with firmware probing entries. Worth noting.\n3. /home (@home) has NO snapshots — rollback asymmetry. Not boot-breaking but a real consistency gap.\n4. dracut config references /etc/crypttab and /crypto_keyfile.bin — both exist, fine. But the keyfile is 2048 bytes of auto-unlock secret; anyone with root can unlock. Standard.\n5. snapper-timeline.timer inactive — no automatic snapshots.\n6. 4 coredumps (3 alert.py --demo tests + 1 Xorg at startup).\n\nNEEDS ROOT TO CONFIRM (the real remaining risk):\n1. **ESP: does \\EFI\\Garuda\\grubx64.efi exist?** ← THE critical unknown. NVRAM points there; BootCurrent was 0002 so it worked at last boot, but I can''t confirm the file is present now.\n2. grub.cfg sources grub-btrfs.cfg? (snapshot bootability)\n3. initramfs has btrfs/dm_crypt/nvme via lsinitrd\n4. any snapshot with broken sudoers\n5. crypttab content\n\nLet me write the script and present.\n\nLet me be honest that the #1 remaining risk is the ESP, because if the EFI file were deleted the firmware would fall through to Boot0005 (\\EFI\\BOOT\\BOOTX64.EFI) which I also can''t verify, and then to PXE — which could mean no boot at all.\n\nThat''s a genuine, specific boot risk worth flagging clearly.\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nLet me make it good.\n\nI''ll write it to /tmp/opencode/boot-check-root.sh.\n\nLet me include the checks.\n\nThen final report.\n\nLet me write.","time":{"start":1790460149868,"end":1790460160821}},"time":1790460160821}');
