INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfbfcccc001XbNQmu4kxfEnKo','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6411,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfbf85a5001BSCdYPnbwmMn60","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfbf76de001ptfWEliQjGxFqj","type":"reasoning","text":"\nThe user wants to block any and all known remote-viewing/remote-access applications from being used remotely on their system. This is a legitimate security request on their own machine.\n\nRemote viewing/remote access tools to consider:\n- TeamViewer\n- AnyDesk\n- VNC (TigerVNC, RealVNC, TightVNC, x11vnc)\n- RDP (xrdp, freerdp)\n- NoMachine\n- Chrome Remote Desktop\n- RustDesk\n- Splashtop\n- LogMeIn\n- GoToMyPC\n- Parsec (remote desktop, gaming)\n- Sunlogin/RayLink (向日葵)\n- Any others\n- XRDP\n- SSH with X11 forwarding / port forwarding (but sshd is off)\n- Telnet/rsh/rlogin (legacy)\n- KDE Connect (already present, blocked by firewall)\n\nApproach: \n1. Check what''s installed on the system\n2. Block whatever exists via firewall rules (firewalld rich rules dropping traffic to/from those processes, or blocking their ports)\n3. Consider also blacklisting their binaries/ports\n\nBest mechanism on this system: firewalld is active with firewallcmd-rich-rules (and fail2ban''s banaction uses it). I can add rich rules to the workstation zone that drop traffic to/from those applications.\n\nBut how to identify \"remote viewing applications\" on the machine? Let me:\n- Check installed packages for known remote-access tools\n- Check listening ports\n- Check for their binaries\n\nLet me first inventory what''s actually present. Then block.\n\nLet me check:\n- pacman -Q for teamviewer, anydesk, vnc, xrdp, nomachine, rustdesk, splashtop, logmein, anydesk, parsec, sunlogin, tightvnc, tigervnc, realvnc, x11vnc, krfb, remmina, freerdp\n- Listening ports\n- Any processes\n\nLet me gather.\n\nThen I''ll write a script that blocks them. The cleanest approach: firewalld rich rules that drop packets to/from the process? firewalld doesn''t easily do per-process. But it can block by port.\n\nBetter approach: create a firewalld rich rule dropping traffic on the ports those apps use, plus check for installed packages.\n\nActually, the most robust approach for \"block any and all known remote viewing apps from being used remotely\":\n1. Block their known ports at the firewall (inbound)\n2. Check if any are installed and if so, note them\n3. Since nothing is listening remotely (firewall drops unsolicited inbound), the main risk is an app being installed and exposing a port\n\nLet me inventory first.\n\nLet me check installed packages and binaries.\n\nLet me run the inventory.\n\nKey tools and their default ports:\n- TeamViewer: 5938 (tcp), 80/443\n- AnyDesk: 7070 (tcp), 80/443\n- VNC: 5900+ (tcp), 5900+50*n\n- xrdp: 3389\n- NoMachine: 4000 (tcp)\n- RustDesk: 21115-21119\n- Splashtop: various\n- Chrome Remote Desktop: 443\n- Parsec: 8000-8010\n- Sunlogin (向日葵): various\n- Krfb: 5900\n- x11vnc: 5900\n- tigervnc: 5900\n- RealVNC: 5900\n\nLet me inventory what''s installed.\n\nLet me check pacman -Qq for these.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nAlso check `pacman -Q | grep -i` etc.\n\nLet me run a comprehensive inventory.\n\nLet me also check for listening ports that shouldn''t be there (already did: 5353, 1716 - both firewalled).\n\nLet me inventory packages and binaries.\n\nI''ll write the inventory command.\n\nLet me be thorough but efficient.\n\nLet me check:\n1. Installed packages matching remote-access names\n2. Binaries in /usr/bin, /usr/sbin\n3. Listening ports (already have: 5353, 1716)\n4. KDE Connect (1716) - should be blocked\n\nLet me run.\n","time":{"start":1790460265893,"end":1790460284108}},"time":1790460284108}');
