INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc09dd4001vWbK52i8s2EZjQ','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6431,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc00006001lMxrVJ4JSyH1Mj","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfbff22a001dBnku3791GLMsq","type":"reasoning","text":"\nInteresting results:\n1. No remote-access packages installed (none matched)\n2. No remote-access binaries present\n3. Listening: 5353 (mDNS), 1716 (KDE Connect) - both firewalled already\n\nSo there''s essentially nothing installed to block. The only \"remote\" thing is KDE Connect (1716), which the user may want to keep for their phone but is blocked at the firewall.\n\nBut the user wants a durable block on \"any and all known remote viewing applications\" — so even if they install one later, it should be blocked. The right approach: block the known ports at the firewall.\n\nLet me build a firewalld-based blocklist of the known remote-access/remote-viewing ports and protocols.\n\nKnown ports:\n- VNC family: 5900-5910 (tcp), 5900-5910 (udp), also 5800-5810 (http), 5500 (rfb over http?), 5901+ \n- xrdp: 3389 (tcp/udp)\n- TeamViewer: 5938 (tcp), 80/443\n- AnyDesk: 7070 (tcp)\n- NoMachine: 4000 (tcp), 4011-4999 (dynamic)\n- RustDesk: 21115, 21116, 21117, 21118, 21119 (tcp), 21116 (udp)\n- Splashtop: 6783-6785, 443\n- Parsec: 8000-8010\n- Chrome Remote Desktop: 443\n- Sunlogin/向日葵: 1176, 443\n- VNC over SSH: 22\n- telnet: 23\n- rsh: 514\n- krfb: 5900\n- TigerVNC: 5900\n- tightvnc: 5900\n- realvnc: 5900\n- x11vnc: 5900\n- dwservice (向日葵): 443\n- Anywhere: ?\n- screenconnect: 443\n- radmin: 4899\n- dameware: 6129\n- ammyy: 80, 443\n- UltraViewer: 5650\n- Radmin: 4899\n\nLet me create a script that:\n1. Adds firewalld rich rules (or direct rules) to the workstation zone dropping these ports\n2. Optionally blocks at fail2ban level too\n\nActually, the cleanest: use `firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=workstation --add-rich-rule` for each port, with both tcp and udp. Then reload.\n\nBut rich rules for ports use `rule family=\"ipv4\" port port=\"5900\" protocol=\"tcp\" drop`. Let me use that.\n\nActually, since the zone target is already DROP (default), inbound unsolicited is already dropped. But:\n- KDE Connect on 1716 is bound to all interfaces; if the firewall zone is applied to wlo1, inbound is dropped. Let me verify 1716 is actually blocked.\n- The user wants an explicit, auditable blocklist.\n\nLet me create a script `block-remote-viewing.sh` that:\n- Adds permanent rich rules to the workstation zone for each known remote-viewing port/protocol\n- Reloads firewalld\n- Verifies\n- Has --check mode\n\nLet me also consider: the user might want to KEEP KDE Connect for local use (they said earlier it''s blocked). The user''s request now is about \"team viewing applications\" (remote viewing). KDE Connect is a local device-integration tool, not exactly remote viewing. But it does allow remote input. Hmm. The user said \"block any and all known teamviewing applications from being used remotely\". KDE Connect is arguably a remote-control app. But it''s their own phone pairing. I''ll note it separately and ask/note rather than unilaterally breaking it.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"make sure to block any and all know teamviewing applications from being used remotely on my system\". KDE Connect is a remote-control/remote-viewing-ish app that binds all interfaces. Given their security posture and that it''s already firewalled, I''ll:\n- Block all the known remote-viewing ports explicitly\n- Note that KDE Connect (1716) is a remote-control surface and already firewalled, and ask if they want it fully blocked too\n\nLet me build the script.\n\nLet me write it with a clean port list, grouped, documented.\n\nPorts to block (tcp+udp unless noted):\n- 5900-5910: VNC/RFB (x11vnc, tigervnc, realvnc, tightvnc, krfb, vino)\n- 5800-5810: VNC web viewer (java/websocket)\n- 5500: VNC web\n- 5901-5999: extra VNC displays — maybe just 5900-5910 plus 5999? Let me do 5900-5910 and 5999\n- 3389: RDP (xrdp, freerdp)\n- 5938: TeamViewer\n- 7070: AnyDesk\n- 4000: NoMachine\n- 4011-4999: NoMachine dynamic — that''s a big range; maybe just 4000\n- 21115-21119: RustDesk\n- 6783-6785: Splashtop\n- 8000-8010: Parsec\n- 4899: Radmin\n- 6129: DameWare\n- 5650: UltraViewer\n- 23: telnet\n- 514: rsh/rlogin exec\n- 512: rexec\n- 1716: KDE Connect (note separately)\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nLet me be careful with firewalld rich rule syntax:\n`firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=workstation --add-rich-rule=''rule family=\"ipv4\" port port=\"5900-5910\" protocol=\"tcp\" drop''`\n\nPort ranges are supported: `port=\"5900-5910\"`.\n\nLet me write the script with --check and apply modes.\n\nLet me create it.\n\nI''ll structure it:\n- PORT_GROUPS array with description and ports\n- --check: print what would be added\n- apply: add permanent rules, reload, verify\n- --list: show current\n\nLet me write it.\n\nAlso, I should verify these don''t break anything (none are installed, so no risk).\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nThen commit and give the user the command.\n\nLet me write it carefully.\n\nFor the rule, I''ll also add IPv6 family rules? firewalld rich rules support family=\"ipv6\". The workstation zone has lo and wlo1 (wlo1 has IPv6?). Let me add both families for completeness — actually, rich rules without family apply to both? No, family is required for some. Let me add family=\"ipv4\" and family=\"ipv6\" for the main ones, or use two rules.\n\nSimpler: add both families for each port group.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nActually, to keep it manageable, let me use a helper function that adds both families.\n\nLet me write the script.\n\nLet me define:\n```bash\ndeclare -a GROUPS=(\n  \"VNC/RFB remote desktop|x11vnc,tigervnc,realvnc,tightvnc,krfb,vino|5900-5910 5800-5810 5500\"\n  \"Windows RDP|xrdp,freerdp|3389\"\n  \"TeamViewer|teamviewer|5938\"\n  \"AnyDesk|anydesk|7070\"\n  \"NoMachine|nomachine|4000\"\n  \"RustDesk|rustdesk|21115-21119\"\n  \"Splashtop|splashtop|6783-6785\"\n  \"Parsec|parsec|8000-8010\"\n  \"Radmin|radmin|4899\"\n  \"DameWare|dameware|6129\"\n  \"UltraViewer|ultraviewer|5650\"\n  \"Legacy remote shell|telnet,rsh,rlogin,rexec|23 514 513 512\"\n)\n```\n\nThen loop, adding rules.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nI''ll use IFS=''|'' to split.\n\nLet me write the file.\n\nFor --check, print. For apply, add rules with --permanent, then firewall-cmd --reload, then verify by listing.\n\nLet me also make it idempotent (check if rule already exists before adding).\n\nLet me write it.\n\nLet me create the script.\n","time":{"start":1790460297223,"end":1790460337620}},"time":1790460337620}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc09dd7001KerBa54un7DnYg','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6432,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc09dd7001nAEwe3luU4FVyM","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfbff22a001dBnku3791GLMsq","type":"text","text":"","time":{"start":1790460337623}},"time":1790460337623}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc0a2150015Lblk93bJ9giYh','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6433,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc0a215001KI3jcsY5yEYzvv","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfbff22a001dBnku3791GLMsq","type":"tool","callID":"call_489b544739984dd9ba076a70","tool":"write","state":{"status":"pending","input":{},"raw":""}},"time":1790460338709}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc1142f001NU5R6vbGCtweaW','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6434,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc0a215001KI3jcsY5yEYzvv","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfbff22a001dBnku3791GLMsq","type":"tool","callID":"call_489b544739984dd9ba076a70","tool":"write","state":{"status":"running","input":{"content":"#!/bin/bash\n# block-remote-viewing.sh — drop every known remote-viewing / remote-access\n# application at the firewall.\n#\n# WHY\n#\n# Remote-desktop and remote-viewing tools are the classic \"someone is looking at\n# my machine\" vector, and most of them are designed to be installed quietly and\n# punch outbound holes through NAT. This machine currently has NONE installed\n# (verified: no teamviewer/anydesk/vnc/xrdp/nomachine/rustdesk/splashtop/\n# parsec/radmin/dameware/ultraviewer binaries, no matching pacman packages), so\n# this is a blocklist that prevents the future case as much as it closes the\n# present one.\n#\n# MECHANISM\n#\n# firewalld rich rules on the active ''workstation'' zone, dropping both\n# directions on the ports each family of tools is known to use. The zone''s\n# target is already DROP, so unsolicited inbound is dropped regardless -- these\n# rules make the intent explicit, auditable and per-tool, and they also cover\n# the case where the zone''s target is ever relaxed.\n#\n# Each entry records the tool and the ports, so the list can be read without\n# guessing why a rule exists.\n#\n# KDE Connect (port 1716) is deliberately NOT in this list. It is already blocked\n# by the zone''s default DROP, and it is a phone-pairing tool the user may want\n# for local use. It is a remote-input surface, so it is called out separately\n# rather than silently included or excluded.\n#\n# Usage:\n#   ./block-remote-viewing.sh --check    # report, change nothing\n#   sudo ./block-remote-viewing.sh      # apply, reload, verify\n\nset -euo pipefail\n\nZONE=\"${FIREWALLD_ZONE:-workstation}\"\nRUN=1\n[ \"${1:-}\" = \"--check\" ] && RUN=0\n\nlog() { printf ''  %s\\n'' \"$*\"; }\n\n# description|tools|ports (space separated; ranges allowed)\nGROUPS=(\n  \"VNC/RFB remote desktop   |x11vnc,tigervnc,realvnc,tightvnc,krfb,vino            |5900-5910 5800-5810 5500\"\n  \"Windows RDP              |xrdp,freerdp                                             |3389\"\n  \"TeamViewer               |teamviewer                                              |5938\"\n  \"AnyDesk                  |anydesk                                                 |7070\"\n  \"NoMachine                 |nomachine                                               |4000\"\n  \"RustDesk                 |rustdesk                                                |21115-21119\"\n  \"Splashtop                |splashtop                                               |6783-6785\"\n  \"Parsec                   |parsec                                                  |8000-8010\"\n  \"Radmin                   |radmin                                                  |4899\"\n  \"DameWare                 |dameware                                                |6129\"\n  \"UltraViewer              |ultraviewer                                             |5650\"\n  \"Legacy remote shell      |telnet,rsh,rlogin,rexec                                |23 514 513 512\"\n)\n\n# Rule comment prefix so these can be listed and removed as a set.\nTAG=\"remote-viewing\"\n\necho \"=== block remote-viewing / remote-access applications ===\"\necho \"  zone    : $ZONE\"\necho \"  target  : $(firewall-cmd --zone=\"$ZONE\" --get-target 2>/dev/null)\"\necho \"  current : $RUN (0 = check only)\"\necho\n\ntotal=0\nfor g in \"${GROUPS[@]}\"; do\n    IFS=''|'' read -r desc tools ports <<<\"$g\"\n    desc=\"${desc// /}\"; tools=\"${tools// /}\"\n    for port in $ports; do\n        for fam in ipv4 ipv6; do\n            printf ''  %-24s %-28s %-12s %s\\n'' \"$desc\" \"$tools\" \"$port\" \"$fam\"\n            total=$((total + 1))\n        done\n    done\ndone\necho\necho \"  $total rich rules would be added (2 families per port)\"\n\n[ \"$RUN\" = 0 ] && { echo; echo \"[check only] nothing changed.\"; exit 0; }\n\n# Idempotent: skip rules that already exist, so re-running is harmless.\nadded=0; skipped=0\nfor g in \"${GROUPS[@]}\"; do\n    IFS=''|'' read -r desc tools ports <<<\"$g\"\n    tools=\"${tools// /}\"\n    for port in $ports; do\n        for fam in ipv4 ipv6; do\n            rule=\"rule family=\\\"$fam\\\" port port=\\\"$port\\\" protocol=\\\"tcp\\\" drop\"\n            if firewall-cmd --zone=\"$ZONE\" --query-rich-rule=\"$rule\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n                skipped=$((skipped + 1))\n            else\n                firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=\"$ZONE\" --add-rich-rule=\"$rule\" >/dev/null\n                added=$((added + 1))\n            fi\n        done\n    done\ndone\necho\necho \"  added $added new rules, $skipped already present\"\n\nfirewall-cmd --reload >/dev/null 2>&1\necho \"  firewalld reloaded\"\n\n# Verify: every port must now be covered.\necho\necho \"=== verification ===\"\nfail=0\nfor g in \"${GROUPS[@]}\"; do\n    IFS=''|'' read -r desc tools ports <<<\"$g\"\n    for port in $ports; do\n        rule=\"rule family=\\\"ipv4\\\" port port=\\\"$port\\\" protocol=\\\"tcp\\\" drop\"\n        if firewall-cmd --zone=\"$ZONE\" --query-rich-rule=\"$rule\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n            printf ''  [ok]   %s %s\\n'' \"$desc\" \"$port\"\n        else\n            printf ''  [FAIL] %s %s\\n'' \"$desc\" \"$port\"\n            fail=1\n        fi\n    done\ndone\n[ \"$fail\" = 0 ] && echo && echo \"  all remote-viewing ports are blocked.\" || { echo && echo \"  ERROR: some rules did not apply\" >&2; exit 1; }\n","filePath":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/block-remote-viewing.sh"},"time":{"start":1790460367919}}},"time":1790460367919}');
