INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc158b9002ODv3tYGsD7TG9B','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12177,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc123a2001k3px8vQCq3cU5s","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfc11ad10010PsY2m4goxQpSp","type":"reasoning","text":"Critical: the initramfs check says btrfs, dm_crypt, dm_mod, luks2 are ABSENT but nvme is present. And the system boots fine.\n\nThis is exactly the same class of false negative as before — my grep pattern `/$m[^/]*\\.ko` on `lsinitrd` output. Let me think about why.\n\n`lsinitrd` output format for dracut: it lists files with full paths. Module names appear as e.g. `usr/lib/modules/7.2.7-zen1-1-zen/kernel/fs/btrfs/btrfs.ko.xz`. My grep was `/$m[^/]*\\.ko` which requires the module to be directly after a `/` with no `/` in the name. For btrfs: path is `.../kernel/fs/btrfs/btrfs.ko.xz` — so `/btrfs.ko` would match `/btrfs.ko.xz`? `/$m[^/]*\\.ko` = `/btrfs` then `[^/]*` then `.ko` → `/btrfs.ko` matches since [^/]* matches empty then `.ko`. Hmm that should match.\n\nBut wait — the modules might be BUILT INTO the kernel, not modules! dracut with hostonly might use a builtin btrfs. Actually no, btrfs is a module.\n\nOr the modules are named differently: `dm_crypt.ko` might be at `.../kernel/drivers/md/dm-crypt.ko` — note the HYPHEN not underscore! Kernel module files use hyphens: `dm-crypt.ko`, `dm-mod.ko`. But module NAMES use underscores: `dm_crypt`, `dm_mod`.\n\nSo my grep for `dm_crypt` wouldn''t match `dm-crypt.ko.xz`. Same for dm_mod → dm-mod.ko.\n\nBut `btrfs` → `btrfs.ko` should match. And `luks2` → the luks2 userspace tool is `/usr/sbin/cryptsetup`, not a kernel module. `luks2` isn''t a .ko at all! That''s my error — luks2 is userspace (cryptsetup binary), not a kernel module. Though there is a `dm_crypt` kernel module for LUKS.\n\nSo btrfs should have matched. Why didn''t it?\n\nPossibility: btrfs is BUILT INTO the kernel (CONFIG_BTRFS_FS=y rather than =m). If built-in, there''s no .ko in the initramfs and it''s in the kernel image itself. That would explain nvme matching (a module) and btrfs not.\n\nOR: dracut in hostonly mode with `--no-hostonly` ... Actually for Garuda/Arch with dracut, btrfs may well be built-in.\n\nLet me verify properly. I should check:\n1. `lsinitrd` actual output for btrfs/dm-crypt\n2. Whether the kernel has btrfs built-in (`grep CONFIG_BTRFS_FS /proc/config.gz` or /boot/config-*)\n\nThis is a real finding requiring verification — NOT another false alarm I assume away. I got burned 6 times last turn by assuming. Let me actually verify this properly before concluding.\n\nThe evidence that the system boots: it IS running, and the current boot used the same initramfs (BOOT_IMAGE=/@/boot/vmlinuz-linux-zen, dracut-cmdline ran). The root filesystem IS btrfs on LUKS and it mounted. So functionally, the initramfs works — proven by the fact that this system booted.\n\nBut \"it booted this time\" vs \"it will always boot\" — the initramfs is the same file, so if it worked, it works. The only scenario where it breaks is a future kernel/dracut regeneration.\n\nSo the honest answer: the ABSENT results are almost certainly my grep being wrong (hyphens vs underscores, and luks2 not being a module), and the definitive proof is that this system booted from this exact initramfs 5 hours ago.\n\nBut I must verify rather than assert. Let me run the corrected check.\n\nLet me check:\n- `lsinitrd /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img | grep -iE ''btrfs|dm-crypt|dm_crypt|dm-mod|crypt''` properly\n- kernel config for built-in btrfs\n\nI have dracut now. `lsinitrd` needs root? /boot/initramfs is root-only readable (we saw \"no read permission\" for `file`). So I need the user to run it, OR... let me check if I can read it now. Earlier `file -b` said \"no read permission\". So no.\n\nHmm. But I can check the KERNEL config which is more accessible? /boot/config-* also root. /proc/config.gz needs root or CONFIG_IKCONFIG.\n\nLet me check /proc/config.gz readability.\n\nActually — a smarter approach that needs no root: the system HAS booted successfully from this initramfs. The `lsinitrd` grep is the only thing failing. Let me ask the user to run a corrected grep, OR check if there''s a readable copy.\n\nLet me first check what I CAN read, then give a precise corrected command.\n\nLet me check /proc/config.gz and /boot/config-*.\n\nAlso: the other important findings from the output:\n\n1. **ESP is fine** — both grubx64.efi (Garuda) and bootx64.efi (fallback) present, 606208 bytes each. Critical risk resolved. Only 2 files on ESP, 299M free. Note: directory is `EFI/boot` (lowercase) — UEFI spec says `/EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI` case-insensitive on FAT, fine.\n\n2. **grub-btrfs IS wired** — all 11 snapshots listed as `subvol=\"@/.snapshots/N/snapshot\"`. All snapshots bootable from the menu. Note grub-btrfs.cfg mtime 10:38 but snapshots up to 37 were created at 14:47 — yet grub-btrfs.cfg lists 28-37! That means grub-btrfs.cfg was regenerated after 14:47, OR it''s generated at boot/menu time. Actually mtime of grub-btrfs.cfg wasn''t shown, only grub.cfg (10:38). grub-btrfs.cfg is regenerated on each boot by the grub-btrfs service. So it currently lists all 11. Good.\n\n3. **No snapshot has broken sudoers** — all 11 have `10-installer mode=440 %wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL`. The broken window (14:34–14:47) was captured by NO snapshot. Snap 34/35 at 14:11 predate, 36/37 at 14:47 postdate. Excellent — no snapshot can restore the lockout.\n\n4. **Live sudoers all parse OK.**\n\n5. **crypttab is correct** — maps luks-8c39203b to /crypto_keyfile.bin, and both crypttab AND crypto_keyfile.bin are IN the initramfs (lsinitrd showed them). So unattended unlock is properly configured. Confirmed.\n\nSo the ONLY unresolved item is the initramfs module check, which I believe is my grep''s fault.\n\nLet me verify properly and give a corrected check.\n\nLet me look for readable config.\n\nLet me run.\n\nI''ll check:\n- /proc/config.gz\n- /boot/config-*\n- module files on the live filesystem (readable? /usr/lib/modules is readable) — to confirm naming with hyphens\n\nThat last one is clever: I can confirm the naming convention from the LIVE /usr/lib/modules, which IS readable. If dm-crypt.ko is the filename, my underscore grep was definitely wrong.\n\nLet me do that — it needs no root and proves the point.\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790460371874,"end":1790460385465}},"time":1790460385465}');
