INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc1f322001kWmJKE1uyME5TY','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6477,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc1ada2001vRZ9w3IgJ6D1nj","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfc18514001kUbSdvBZLtZuBa","type":"reasoning","text":"\nFound it. The array `${GROUPS[0]}` is `[1000]` — not the string! So `GROUPS` is being treated as an integer array, not a string array.\n\nWhy? Because `GROUPS=( \"...\" \"...\" )` — if bash auto-indexes... Actually, the issue is likely that the array is being declared with `GROUPS=(...)` but somewhere it''s assigned as an integer.\n\nWait, `${GROUPS[0]}` returns `1000`. That''s bizarre. Unless... the array is being redefined somewhere, or `GROUPS` is being interpreted differently.\n\nOH. I bet the issue is that `GROUPS` is a special/automatic variable? No.\n\nActually — `desc=1000`, `desc=983`, `desc=989` — those look like PIDs or... 1000, 983, 989. These are increasing numbers. That looks like the array is storing... process substitution? No.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. The values 1000, 983, 989 — could these be from the herestring? No.\n\nActually — I think the problem is that the GROUPS array declaration is failing and `GROUPS` ends up being an indexed array of something else. But where would 1000, 983, 989 come from?\n\nWait — could it be that the `GROUPS=(...)` lines with `|` and the multi-word strings are being parsed as something else? Let me look: the declaration spans multiple lines with `\\`? No, I used actual newlines in the array.\n\nLet me reconsider. `printf ''  [%s]\\n'' \"${GROUPS[0]}\"` gave `[1000]`. \n\nOH WAIT. I think I see. The variable might be getting clobbered because `GROUPS` — no.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the possibility that bash is treating the parenthesized list with `|` ... no, they''re in quotes.\n\nHmm, but `desc=1000` in the read — that means `$g` was `1000` (or started with 1000). And GROUPS[0]=1000.\n\nWhere would 1000 come from? The string \"VNC/RFB remote desktop   |x11vnc,...\" — no 1000.\n\nUnless... the array indices. Let me check: maybe `GROUPS` was already set to something in the environment? No.\n\nActually — could this be that the file has some issue where the array literal isn''t being parsed? Let me look at the actual bytes.\n\nWait, actually — I wonder if the issue is that these numbers are the PID of the subshell or something from `<<<`.\n\nLet me just check `declare -p GROUPS` to see what it actually is.\n\nLet me run that.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider: `desc=1000`, `desc=983`, `desc=989`. And `${GROUPS[0]}` = `1000`. These are 3 different values. 1000, 983, 989.\n\nHmm, what if the array is being populated by word-splitting of something? \n\nActually — I bet the real issue is that the herestring `<<<\"$g\"` combined with `IFS=''|''`... no, test 1 worked fine.\n\nLet me just inspect with declare -p.\n\nLet me run `bash -c ''source ...''` or add a debug. Let me check declare -p GROUPS.\n\nLet me do that.","time":{"start":1790460407202,"end":1790460424994}},"time":1790460424994}');
