INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc25ade0011QClbHZbzbOj55','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6494,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc20f42001BAbbzMnYSSgQ6r","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfc1fe72001gtgHnuzGLuH1e8","type":"reasoning","text":"\nThe GROUPS array is `[0]=\"1000\" [1]=\"983\" [2]=\"989\" [3]=\"992\" [4]=\"998\" [5]=\"967\"`. These are 6 elements, all numbers.\n\nThe array has 6 elements (one per GROUPS entry I have... wait, I have 12 GROUPS entries). Hmm, 6 elements with numbers 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967.\n\nWait — these look like PIDs. And there are 6 of them.\n\nOH. I think I understand now. The `GROUPS=(...)` with the multi-line strings containing `|` — bash might be parsing the `|` as a pipe operator OUTSIDE of the quotes? No, they''re in double quotes.\n\nActually wait — the numbers 1000, 983, 989... these are suspiciously like the output of `echo $$` or subshell PIDs.\n\nHmm, but actually — could this be that the strings contain `$` or backticks that are being evaluated? Let me look at my strings: \"VNC/RFB remote desktop   |x11vnc,tigervnc,realvnc            |5900-5910 5800-5810 5500\" — no $ or backticks.\n\nBut wait, the count is 6, and I have 12 entries. So it''s not even one per entry.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The values 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967 — if I look at them, they could be PIDs of processes. But why would they end up in the array?\n\nHold on — maybe the issue is that `GROUPS` is a readonly/special variable? No.\n\nActually — I wonder if the problem is that the script content got mangled when I wrote it. Let me check the actual bytes of the file. Maybe the multi-line array with the `|` characters got corrupted.\n\nLet me look at the raw file around the GROUPS declaration.\n\nActually, the most likely explanation: the `write` tool wrote the file, but maybe there''s an issue with how the multi-line array was stored. Let me just check the actual file content with cat -A or sed.\n\nLet me read the file section.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — the values are 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967. Six values. My GROUPS array has 12 entries. Hmm.\n\nWait, could these be from `firewall-cmd` or something? No, this is a standalone bash -c.\n\nLet me look at the file directly to see what''s actually there.\n\nLet me cat the relevant lines with line numbers.\n\nLet me use read to see the exact content.\n\nActually — I bet the issue is that when I wrote the file, the array entries with `|` and multiple spaces were written, but maybe bash is interpreting something. Let me just look at the file.\n\nLet me read the file.","time":{"start":1790460432194,"end":1790460451550}},"time":1790460451550}');
