INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc2e29c001bhbbs6ChckXmNR','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12265,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc2cd5d001Y66F5VBA4Ts3Qt","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfc2c73b001tI25Va3m87Movz","type":"text","text":"**The machine did not reboot.** The commands passed, but they can''t have tested what you thought they tested.\n\nThe evidence is unambiguous:\n\n- **Boot time is still `2026-09-26 10:48:01`**, uptime 4h 19m. It''s now 15:07.\n- **`systemd-analyze` is byte-identical** to the pre-reboot reading: `14.961 + 21.243 + 2.759 + 7.550 + 3.038 = 49.552s`. Boot timings don''t repeat to the millisecond across boots.\n- **Zero shutdown/reboot markers** in the journal.\n- `last -r` is empty.\n\nSo the two commands you ran checked the *current* session, which was already healthy — which is why they reported no failures. `systemctl --user is-active` answers \"is it running now,\" not \"does it start on a cold boot.\"\n\nAnd the unit timestamps confirm it: the boot was at 10:48:01, but `evidence-seal.timer` activated at **12:28:04** and `shield-kitty-watch.service` at **13:54:52**. Both started *mid-session*, hours after boot, not at boot. `Linger=yes` is set and the watcher is correctly in `user@1000.service/app.slice/`, so the configuration is right — but it has still never been exercised by an actual boot.\n\n**The one property this entire session was built to establish remains unproven.** I reported it as \"ready to reboot\" repeatedly, and it still is — but ready is not verified.\n\nTwo commands, in this order:\n\n```bash\n# 1. actually reboot\nsudo systemctl reboot\n\n# 2. AFTER you log back in\nsystemctl --user is-active shield-kitty-watch.service evidence-seal.timer\nuptime -s\n```\n\n`uptime -s` must show a time *after* 15:07. If it shows 10:48 again, the reboot didn''t happen. Then check the activation timestamps — they should land within seconds of the new boot time, not hours later:\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --user show shield-kitty-watch.service -p ActiveEnterTimestamp --value\n```\n\nThat''s the actual pass condition: activated within ~30s of boot, before login. If it''s hours later, linger isn''t doing its job and I''ll fix it.\n\nTwo other things while I''m here: the audit wrappers are still **7/7 present**, so `install-audit-readonly.sh --remove` hasn''t been run yet. And if you did intend to revoke the GitHub token, that FireDragon tab is still open — worth doing before the reboot, since a reboot closes it.","time":{"start":1790460480862,"end":1790460486300}},"time":1790460486300}');
