INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc3223d001Eso8iL4HEdV7ss','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6543,'session.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","info":{"id":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","slug":"shiny-harbor","projectID":"global","directory":"/home/livings","path":"home/livings","summary":{"additions":0,"deletions":0,"files":0},"cost":0,"tokens":{"input":877507,"output":135602,"reasoning":110552,"cache":{"read":32343309,"write":0}},"title":"New session - 2026-09-26T19:03:50.589Z","agent":"build","model":{"id":"longcat-2.5-preview-free","providerID":"opencode","variant":"high"},"version":"1.18.32","time":{"created":1790449430589,"updated":1790460502589}}}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc33b4b00137tC5kiY4rgqig','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6544,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc30e09001E6De5Lj7Cq3fpc","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfc2f4a0001gUC96iVPHZaHyv","type":"reasoning","text":"\nNow I see it. With `MYGROUPS` it works perfectly (2 elements). With `GROUPS` it produces 6 numeric elements.\n\nSo `GROUPS` is a SPECIAL/readonly variable! In bash, `GROUPS` is a readonly array that contains the group IDs of the current user. That''s why:\n- `declare -a GROUPS=([0]=\"1000\" [1]=\"983\" ...)` — these are the user''s group IDs\n- The user is in groups: livings(1000), realtime(983), video(989), lp(992), input(998), wheel(967)\n\nThat matches the earlier output: `groups: livings realtime video lp input wheel` and `id` showed uid=1000(livings) gid=1000(livings) groups=1000(livings),983(realtime),989(video),992(lp),998(input),967(wheel)\n\nSo GROUPS[0]=1000 (livings), GROUPS[1]=983 (realtime), etc. Exactly 6 groups!\n\nThat''s the bug. `GROUPS` is a bash built-in readonly array holding group IDs. I can''t assign to it.\n\nThe fix: rename the variable to something else, e.g., `BLOCKGROUPS` or `RULE_GROUPS`.\n\nLet me rename to `BLOCK_GROUPS`.\n\nThis is a great catch — and exactly the kind of thing the audit is for. Let me rename and re-test.\n\nLet me edit the script to use a different variable name.\n\nLet me use `BLOCK_GROUPS`.\n\nLet me do a global replace of `GROUPS` → `BLOCK_GROUPS` in that file.\n\nCareful: the file has `GROUPS=(`, `\"${GROUPS[@]}\"`, `${#GROUPS[@]}` etc. Let me use sed or edit.\n\nLet me use sed to replace all occurrences.\n\nActually, let me be careful — there might be other things. Let me just sed ''s/GROUPS/BLOCK_GROUPS/g'' on the file. But that would also affect comments mentioning GROUPS? Let me check — the comments say \"GROUPS\" nowhere else I think. Let me just do the replacement and verify.\n\nLet me run sed.\n\nActually, let me use the edit tool with replaceAll for the specific patterns. There are a few: `GROUPS=(`, `\"${GROUPS[@]}\"` (appears twice), `${#GROUPS[@]}`.\n\nLet me just sed it.\n\nLet me do: sed -i ''s/\\bGROUPS\\b/BLOCK_GROUPS/g'' — but \\b in sed needs -E or GNU sed supports \\b. GNU sed supports \\b. Let me use it.\n\nLet me run.\n\nThen re-test.\n\nThis is a nice find to report to the user.\n","time":{"start":1790460497417,"end":1790460509002}},"time":1790460509003}');
