INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc502b100170aqkFUHw1VA1m','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6563,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc502b0001RkU9ABLQuZapwB","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfc502af001aKD5DxfZhOkBlA","type":"text","text":"baasically i want all devices trying to connect to my p.c. to be blacklisted if they try to access my pc automatically without my password"},"time":1790460625585}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc502b300158cUmgMAN68Z2S','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6564,'session.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","info":{"id":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","slug":"shiny-harbor","projectID":"global","directory":"/home/livings","path":"home/livings","summary":{"additions":0,"deletions":0,"files":0},"cost":0,"tokens":{"input":878425,"output":135817,"reasoning":111188,"cache":{"read":32481549,"write":0}},"title":"New session - 2026-09-26T19:03:50.589Z","agent":"build","model":{"id":"longcat-2.5-preview-free","providerID":"opencode","variant":"high"},"version":"1.18.32","time":{"created":1790449430589,"updated":1790460625587}}}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc5190c00360x90RXFpB1ITm','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6565,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc369a7001Wz2UdwlHRYk86D","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfc34c2a0015RLOv3xy265YdR","type":"reasoning","text":"\nThe user wants \"setup a tool to auto blacklist all remote connections to my device\". So an automatic mechanism that detects remote connection attempts and blacklists the source IP.\n\nWe already have fail2ban + firewalld (banaction = firewallcmd-rich-rules). So the \"tool\" should be a jail that watches for incoming connection attempts and auto-bans.\n\nBut wait — the machine has NO exposed services. sshd is inactive. So there are no remote connections to detect.\n\nHowever, the user wants a tool. Let me think about what would actually be valuable and honest:\n\n1. There are no remote connections today (nothing listening, firewall drops unsolicited inbound).\n2. \"All remote connections\" — they want any remote connection attempt auto-blacklisted.\n\nThe honest approach: build a fail2ban jail that watches for unsolicited inbound connection attempts at the firewall level (firewall-cmd / firewalld logged drops), or watches the journal for connection attempts to any port.\n\nActually, a really good mechanism: fail2ban can watch firewalld''s logs for DROPs and ban those sources. Or use `fail2ban` with a custom filter on the kernel log / journal for connection attempts.\n\nBut there''s a subtlety: the firewall already drops them, so they''re not \"connections\" — they''re rejected packets. Banning them is still useful (deters scanners).\n\nAnother approach: a netfilter-based blocklist. But firewalld is the manager here.\n\nLet me think about the best design:\n- Create a fail2ban jail that watches for inbound TCP SYN attempts to non-listening ports (scanning) or to any port.\n- Source: journalctl / kernel log for \"DROP\" entries, or `firewall-cmd` rich rules with log.\n\nActually, a clean approach: use fail2ban''s `systemd` backend watching the journal for `firewalld` DROP messages that contain the source IP, then ban.\n\nBut does firewalld log drops? By default, firewalld does NOT log every dropped packet (that would be a log flood). We can add a rich rule that logs and drops, or enable firewalld''s logged drops.\n\nAlternatively, use the kernel''s log for dropped packets.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what''s actually practical and honest here.\n\nGiven the machine has no exposed services, the realistic threat is port scanning / connection attempts from the LAN (or anywhere). The user wants those auto-blacklisted.\n\nDesign:\n1. Add firewalld rich rules that LOG (and drop) inbound connection attempts to the workstation zone.\n2. fail2ban jail watches the journal for those log lines, extracts the source IP, and bans it via firewallcmd-rich-rules (already configured).\n3. bantime 86400 (consistent with existing policy).\n\nBut wait — fail2ban''s banaction is firewallcmd-rich-rules which adds a rich rule to block the IP. That''s the ban mechanism.\n\nLet me check: does firewalld log to journald? firewalld''s default log-denied setting. `firewall-cmd --get-log-denied` — options: all, unicast, off, broadcast, multicast. Default is \"off\" I think. If we set log-denied=unicast, firewalld logs unicast dropped packets to syslog/journald.\n\nThen fail2ban can filter on those.\n\nLet me plan:\n- Set `firewall-cmd --set-log-denied=unicast` (permanent)\n- fail2ban jail `[remotescan]` or `[autoblock]` with a systemd backend watching journald for firewalld DROP messages, filter extracts SRC=IP, ban via firewallcmd-rich-rules.\n- maxretry small (1-2), findtime short, bantime 86400.\n\nBut there''s a risk: banning the gateway or legitimate LAN devices. The user said single-user local-only, ban everyone. But we must be careful not to ban the gateway (that would kill networking). The ignoreip already covers the gateway and own IP.\n\nAlso risk: banning broadcast/multicast sources (SRC=255.255.255.255) — the filter must only match unicast source IPs, and log-denied=unicast handles that.\n\nAnother risk: fail2ban banning IPs that are just doing normal LAN chatter (ARP, mDNS). But those aren''t TCP connection attempts. We''d filter for TCP/UDP connection attempts specifically.\n\nActually, a cleaner and more targeted approach: watch for inbound TCP connection attempts (SYN) to any port. The kernel log would show `IN=wlo1 OUT= MAC=... SRC=<ip> DST=10.197.250.246 PROTO=TCP SPT=<port> DPT=<port> ...` with `DROP` or `REJECT`.\n\nLet me design the fail2ban filter regex for firewalld DROP lines.\n\nfirewalld log-denied format (via journald): something like:\n`firewalld[PID]: DROP: IN=wlo1 OUT= MAC=... SRC=10.0.0.5 DST=10.197.250.246 LEN=60 TOS=0x00 PREC=0x00 TTL=64 ID=... DF PROTO=TCP SPT=54321 DPT=22 WINDOW=...`\n\nHmm, actually firewalld''s log output format. Let me check what it looks like. It logs to syslog with prefix. The exact format depends on firewalld version.\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me be pragmatic and build it, then TEST it by generating a fake connection attempt and verifying a ban.\n\nActually, testing requires generating traffic from another IP, which I can''t easily do. But I can test the fail2ban filter with `fail2ban-regex` against a sample log line. That''s the standard way.\n\nLet me build:\n1. `fail2ban-filter-remote-attempt.conf` (or inline in the jail) — filter regex.\n2. Jail config `[remoteattempt]` enabled=true, backend=systemd (journald), filter, maxretry, findtime, bantime=86400, banaction=firewallcmd-rich-rules, zone=workstation.\n3. Enable firewalld log-denied=unicast.\n4. A deploy/install script that sets this up idempotently, backs up, validates, restarts.\n5. Test with fail2ban-regex.\n\nLet me also consider: the user said \"auto blacklist all remote connections\". So the tool should be automated — fail2ban IS the tool. I''m essentially wiring fail2ban to firewalld''s drop logs.\n\nLet me name it something clear: `install-autoblock-remote.sh` or a service. Given the zen-hardening pattern, a deploy script + fail2ban jail config.\n\nLet me design the files:\n- `fail2ban-autoblock-remote.conf` (jail.d drop-in) — but filters need to go in filter.d. fail2ban jail.d drop-ins can define `[remoteattempt]` with `filter = <name>` and the filter goes in `/etc/fail2ban/filter.d/remoteattempt.conf`.\n\nSo two files:\n- `/etc/fail2ban/filter.d/autoblock-remote.conf`\n- `/etc/fail2ban/jail.d/autoblock-remote.conf`\n\nPlus set log-denied.\n\nLet me build the deploy script that installs these and enables log-denied.\n\nLet me first check firewalld''s log-denied current setting and the exact log format by enabling it and dropping something.\n\nLet me test: enable log-denied=unicast, then send a SYN to a closed port from... I only have one IP. I can use a different source IP? No, I can''t spoof easily. But I can connect from the localhost to a closed port — that would be SRC=127.0.0.1, which is ignored. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, I can check the firewalld source/docs for the log format. Or enable logging and see what a drop produces by connecting to a closed high port from the same host (SRC=127.0.0.1).\n\nActually, I can test the format by connecting to a closed port on the external interface from the same machine via its own IP (10.197.250.246) — but firewalld''s zone is on wlo1, and connecting to own IP might not traverse. Let me just enable log-denied and try a connection to a closed port, then read the journal.\n\nLet me do that to get the real log format. That''s the honest way.\n\nPlan:\n1. Enable log-denied=unicast (temporarily or permanently).\n2. Attempt a connection to a closed port (e.g., nc to 10.197.250.246:59999) — but that''s same-IP. Better: use the loopback? No.\n3. Actually, to get a non-ignored source, I''d need another machine. I don''t have one.\n\nHmm. But I can still get the log format from a drop. Let me enable logging and drop something. Even SRC=127.0.0.1 would produce a log line (if the zone applies). But lo is in the workstation zone too.\n\nActually, connecting to a closed port on the external IP from the same machine: the packet goes out wlo1 and comes back? No, it goes out via lo for own IP. Actually connecting to your own external IP — the kernel routes it via lo I think, or via wlo1. It''s ambiguous.\n\nLet me just enable log-denied and generate ANY drop, then inspect the format. I can drop by connecting to a closed port on 127.0.0.1 (if lo is in the zone and log-denied applies). Let me try.\n\nActually, simpler: I''ll look at firewalld''s documented log format and build the filter, then validate with `fail2ban-regex` using a representative line. But I''d rather get the REAL format.\n\nLet me enable log-denied and generate traffic. Let me try connecting to a closed port.\n\nLet me do it step by step.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach for honesty. The user wants \"auto blacklist all remote connections\". The most robust, simple, and honest implementation:\n\nA fail2ban jail watching the journal for inbound connection attempts (SYN packets) that get dropped by the firewall, auto-banning the source for 24h.\n\nLet me get the real log format first.\n\nLet me enable log-denied and generate a drop.\n\nLet me run:\n- firewall-cmd --set-log-denied=unicast (runtime)\n- nc -z -w1 127.0.0.1 59999 (closed port) — but lo in zone\n- check journal for firewalld DROP\n\nHmm, if lo is in the workstation zone, connecting to 127.0.0.1:59999 (closed) would be dropped by the zone and logged.\n\nLet me try that.\n\nActually, let me just do it and see.\n\nLet me be efficient and gather the log format.\n\nLet me run the command.\n\nWait, I should be careful: enabling log-denied could flood the log. unicast logging of every dropped packet. On a quiet machine it''s fine, but if something starts scanning, it floods. That''s actually the point (we want to see them), and fail2ban will ban them.\n\nBut log flood risk: if a scanner hits us with thousands of packets/sec, we''d log each. That could be a DoS vector on the disk. I should set a rate limit. firewalld''s log-denied doesn''t have a rate limit option directly. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: use a firewall rich rule with `log limit` to rate-limit. firewalld rich rules support `rule ... log prefix=... level=... limit value=\"x/m\"`. That rate-limits logging.\n\nSo the design:\n- Add a rich rule to the workstation zone: for inbound traffic to wlo1 that would be dropped, log+drop with a rate limit. But the zone target is already DROP, so we need the log rule BEFORE the drop. Rich rules with `log` + `drop`... actually a rich rule can `log` and `drop`? In firewalld, `rule family=ipv4 source address=... log prefix=... drop`. But to log ALL inbound, we''d add a rule that logs+drop.\n\nHmm, but the zone target DROP handles the drop; we just need a LOG rich rule that matches the traffic we care about (inbound TCP connection attempts). But rich rules are evaluated before the zone target? Actually rich rules in a zone are processed, and a `log` rule with `drop` action would log and drop.\n\nWait, but if we add a log+drop rule for all inbound, it would drop everything including legitimate. We want to log only NEW connection attempts (SYN) to non-listening ports, i.e., actual scan/attempt traffic.\n\nThis is getting deep. Let me simplify:\n\nApproach: fail2ban watching the journal for firewalld DROP entries with rate-limited logging.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe the cleanest \"auto blacklist\" tool is simpler than I think:\n\nUse firewalld''s own rich rules with a `rule ... log` for inbound TCP SYN, and fail2ban reading those logs.\n\nOR — even simpler and very robust: use the kernel''s `nf_log` / iptables LOG target via a rich rule with a limit, and fail2ban filters on the log prefix.\n\nLet me go with:\n1. firewalld rich rule: `rule family=\"ipv4\" source address=\"0.0.0.0/0\" ... ` hmm.\n\nLet me think about the rule to catch inbound connection attempts:\n- We want packets coming IN on wlo1 (not from us, not the gateway) that are TCP SYN to any port.\n- rich rule: `rule family=\"ipv4\" destination address=\"10.197.250.246\" protocol=\"tcp\" tcp flags... ` — firewalld rich rules support `tcp flags`? Not sure.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me step back and use the most pragmatic approach that I can actually verify:\n\n**Use firewalld log-denied=unicast + fail2ban filtering on the journal.**\n\nTo avoid log flood, I''ll add a rate limit via a rich rule, OR accept that on a quiet machine it''s fine and note the risk.\n\nActually, firewalld''s log-denied has no rate limit. But I can reduce risk by noting it.\n\nHmm, but a scanner could flood. Let me add a rich rule with limit instead of using log-denied.\n\nLet me design a rich rule that logs inbound new TCP connections with a rate limit:\n\n```\nrule family=\"ipv4\" destination address=\"10.197.250.246\" protocol=\"tcp\" \\\n     tcp flags=\"SYN\" \\\n     log prefix=\"AUTOBLOCK: \" level=\"warning\" limit value=\"10/m\" drop\n```\n\nHmm, but firewalld rich rule syntax for TCP flags... firewalld doesn''t have a `tcp flags` match in rich rules directly. It has `protocol=\"tcp\"` and `port`. No flags match.\n\nWithout flags match, I can''t distinguish SYN from ACK. But if I match inbound TCP to our IP and log+drop, I''d log ALL inbound TCP including established connections (our own outbound''s return traffic). That''s too noisy.\n\nAlternative: match TCP to our IP on ports that are NOT listening. But we don''t know all closed ports.\n\nHmm. Actually, since the zone target is DROP, ALL inbound TCP to our IP is already dropped (except allowed). So the only inbound TCP that arrives is unsolicited connection attempts. So logging ALL inbound TCP to our IP is exactly what we want!\n\nBecause: legitimate inbound connections don''t exist (nothing listens, firewall drops). So ANY inbound TCP to 10.197.250.246 from a non-ignored source IS a remote connection attempt.\n\nSo the rule: log+drop inbound TCP to our IP. Since the zone already drops it, we just need to LOG it. A rich rule with `log` and `drop`:\n\n```\nrule family=\"ipv4\" destination address=\"10.197.250.246\" protocol=\"tcp\" log prefix=\"REMOTE-CONN: \" level=\"info\" limit value=\"5/m\" drop\n```\n\nWait, but rich rule `log` action — firewalld rich rules support `log` as an action with prefix/level, and also `drop`. Can a rule have both log and drop? Yes: `rule family=\"ipv4\" ... log prefix=\"...\" drop`.\n\nAnd `limit value=\"5/m\"` rate-limits the log.\n\nBut there''s a catch: return traffic. When we initiate an outbound TCP connection (e.g., web browsing), the server''s packets come IN to our IP on an established connection. The kernel would route those via conntrack as ESTABLISHED, and firewalld''s zone... hmm. The zone target DROP applies to packets NOT matching rules. For established connections, firewalld''s conntrack handling (via the `ctstate` or the zone''s handling of related/established) typically allows them.\n\nActually, firewalld zones by default DROP all incoming unless there''s a rule or the connection is established/related (conntrack). firewalld does allow ESTABLISHED/RELATED traffic by default via the `ctstate` handling. So return traffic for our outbound connections is allowed by conntrack, and our rich rule (which matches inbound TCP to our IP) might ALSO match return traffic and log+drop it — breaking outbound connectivity!\n\nThat''s a real risk. I need to exclude ESTABLISHED/RELATED.\n\nfirewalld rich rules don''t have ctstate match directly... Actually, firewalld rich rules DO support `ctstate`? Let me recall. firewalld rich rule elements: family, source, destination, protocol, port, icmp-block, icmp-type, log, audit, accept, reject, drop, mark, limit, service name. I don''t think ctstate is supported in rich rules.\n\nHmm. So matching inbound TCP would catch return traffic too.\n\nAlternative: use `fail2ban` with the kernel''s connection tracking? No.\n\nAlternative approach: Instead of firewall logging, use a dedicated `iptables`/`nftables` LOG rule with `--ctstate NEW` to log only NEW inbound connections. But firewalld manages iptables/nftables; adding manual rules is fragile (firewalld may overwrite).\n\nHmm. Actually, firewalld does support `--direct` rules which are not overwritten by firewalld. `firewall-cmd --direct --add-rule ...`. Direct rules are added to firewalld''s direct chain and persist. But they''re still managed alongside firewalld.\n\nAlternatively, use firewalld rich rules with `limit` and accept that return traffic might be logged — but not dropped (use log-only, let the zone drop). A log-only rich rule would log return traffic too (noise) but not break anything. Then fail2ban would ban based on those logs — including our own outbound servers'' IPs! That''s bad (we''d ban google.com).\n\nSo I need to filter out established/return traffic. \n\nOption: fail2ban filter could exclude packets that are part of established connections. But the log line wouldn''t easily tell.\n\nBetter option: Use `fail2ban` watching a source that only logs NEW inbound connections. \n\nHmm, what about using the kernel''s `nf_conntrack` and `iptables` LOG with `--ctstate NEW --syn`? Via firewalld direct rules:\n\n```\nfirewall-cmd --permanent --direct --add-rule ipv4 filter INPUT 0 -i wlo1 -p tcp --syn -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -j LOG --log-prefix \"REMOTE-CONN: \"\n```\n\nBut firewalld''s direct rules go into the `direct` chain which firewalld manages. This could work but is fragile.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe I''m overcomplicating.\n\nLet me reconsider the threat model: the machine has NO exposed services. \"All remote connections\" would be:\n- Port scans (SYN to closed ports)\n- Connection attempts to services (none running)\n\nThe user wants these auto-blacklisted.\n\nSimplest robust mechanism: **fail2ban watching the kernel journal for `iptables`/`nftables` LOG entries of NEW inbound TCP connections.** But we need those logs to exist.\n\nGiven firewalld manages the firewall, the cleanest is to use firewalld''s logging. firewalld rich rules CAN do `log` + `drop`, and there IS a way to match only new connections... \n\nActually! firewalld rich rules support the `limit` element, and there''s also... hmm.\n\nWait, let me reconsider: firewalld rich rules DO support `ctstate`? Let me check the firewalld documentation. Actually, I recall firewalld rich rules do NOT support ctstate. But they support `protocol` and `port`.\n\nHmm, but there''s another angle: firewalld''s `log-denied` logs packets that are DENIED by the zone. The zone''s conntrack handling allows ESTABLISHED/RELATED, so `log-denied` would only log packets that are actually denied — i.e., NEW inbound connections that aren''t allowed! Return traffic for established connections is allowed by conntrack, so it''s NOT denied, so NOT logged.\n\nYES.","time":{"start":1790460520871,"end":1790460631308}},"time":1790460631308}');
