INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc58e30001QcgQxAVuKU34jJ','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6577,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc58e2d001SSZDgxhgR9bEZi","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfc58e24001aZYQCbUcG2cy8Q","type":"text","text":"baasically i want all devices trying to connect to my p.c. to be blacklisted if they try to access my pc automatically without my password"},"time":1790460661296}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc58e32001EklFGmiBIZCXcP','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6578,'session.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","info":{"id":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","slug":"shiny-harbor","projectID":"global","directory":"/home/livings","path":"home/livings","summary":{"additions":0,"deletions":0,"files":0},"cost":0,"tokens":{"input":878425,"output":135817,"reasoning":111188,"cache":{"read":32481549,"write":0}},"title":"New session - 2026-09-26T19:03:50.589Z","agent":"build","model":{"id":"mimo-v2.6-flash-free","providerID":"opencode","variant":"default"},"version":"1.18.32","time":{"created":1790449430589,"updated":1790460661298}}}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc59424004a9NT8T7JIpH2vX','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',6579,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc52b3e001j9sQbbnUZw43vN","sessionID":"ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk","messageID":"msg_0dfc51cab001GzMbE1UuxW6gil","type":"reasoning","text":"\nThe user wants a tool that auto-blacklists all remote connections to their device — any device trying to access their PC automatically without a password should be blacklisted.\n\nLet me think about what this means concretely:\n- \"all devices trying to connect to my p.c.\" — inbound connection attempts\n- \"if they try to access my p.c. automatically without my password\" - Attempts to authenticate/access without valid credentials\n\nSo they want: any inbound connection attempt that isn''t authorized gets the source IP blacklisted (banned) automatically.\n\nCurrent state:\n- firewalld workstation zone, target DROP — unsolicited inbound already dropped\n- No listening services except KDE Connect (1716) and mDNS (5353), both firewalled\n- fail2ban with sshd (inactive) and recidive jails\n- No SSH, no exposed auth services\n\nThe key insight: with no exposed services, there''s nothing to \"try to access without a password.\" The threat model is: if a service IS exposed (or gets exposed), failed auth attempts should auto-blacklist the source.\n\nWhat I can build:\n1. A fail2ban jail that watches for connection attempts to sensitive ports and bans the source\n2. Since there''s no auth service, the realistic approach is: block/ban any NEW inbound connection attempt from unknown sources\n\nBut \"blacklist all remote connections\" is aggressive. Let me think about what''s actually useful and safe:\n\nOption A: fail2ban jail on the firewall log / kernel log for dropped packets to sensitive ports → ban source IPs\n- firewalld logs drops? Not by default. Could enable logging.\n- Actually, fail2ban can watch the systemd journal for firewall drops.\n\nOption B: A \"port knocking\"/auto-ban approach: any SYN to a closed port from an unknown IP → ban\n- This is essentially what fail2ban + firewall logging does\n\nOption C: Since the user wants \"all devices trying to connect\" blacklisted, the cleanest interpretation: any inbound connection attempt (to any port) from an IP that isn''t already trusted gets banned.\n\nBut that would ban legitimate things (e.g., the gateway, DNS, NTP, DHCP). Need to be careful.\n\nLet me think about the most robust, safe design:\n\n**Auto-blacklist tool design:**\n1. Watch the systemd journal / kernel log for inbound connection attempts (SYN packets) to ports that aren''t explicitly allowed\n2. For each attempt from an untrusted IP, add a fail2ban ban (24h) via firewallcmd-rich-rules\n3. Whitelist: own IP, gateway, loopback, link-local, and any explicitly trusted IPs\n\nThe mechanism: fail2ban can use the `systemd` backend and a custom filter. But writing a fail2ban filter for raw SYN packets is tricky.\n\nAlternative: use `iptables`/`nftables` logging + fail2ban. Or use firewalld''s logging.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach on this system:\n- firewalld is the firewall\n- fail2ban''s banaction is firewallcmd-rich-rules\n- I can create a fail2ban jail with a custom filter that matches firewall drop logs\n\nBut firewalld doesn''t log drops by default. I''d need to enable it.\n\nHmm, let me think about a simpler, more direct approach that''s robust:\n\n**A dedicated \"auto-blacklist\" watcher script** that:\n1. Tails the kernel log (dmesg/journal) for SYN packets to non-whitelisted ports\n2. Extracts source IPs\n3. Calls fail2ban-client to ban them (or directly adds firewall rich rules)\n\nActually, the most reliable signal on a modern Linux system is the systemd journal with `journalctl -f -k` or the netfilter log.\n\nLet me reconsider. The user said \"all devices trying to connect to my p.c. ... automatically without my password.\" \n\nThe phrase \"without my password\" suggests authentication attempts. But there are no auth services exposed. So the practical interpretation is: any unauthorized inbound connection attempt.\n\nGiven the constraints, I think the best tool is:\n\n**A fail2ban-based auto-blacklist that watches for connection attempts to sensitive/closed ports and bans the source for 24h.**\n\nImplementation:\n1. Enable firewalld logging of dropped packets (or use a logging rule)\n2. Create a fail2ban jail with a filter matching those logs\n3. Ban action = firewallcmd-rich-rules (already configured)\n\nOR, more self-contained:\n\n**A systemd service that tails the journal for new connection attempts and bans sources.**\n\nLet me think about which is more robust and testable.\n\nActually, I realize the cleanest and most testable approach given the environment:\n\nUse `fail2ban` with the `systemd` backend watching for kernel messages about dropped/rejected packets. But generating those requires firewall logging.\n\nLet me check: does firewalld log drops? By default, firewalld doesn''t log every drop (that would be a lot). But I can add a rich rule that LOGs then DROPs for specific ports.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me step back and think about what''s genuinely useful and safe.\n\nThe user''s machine:\n- No exposed auth services\n- Firewall drops unsolicited inbound\n- They want auto-blacklisting of connection attempts\n\nThe most valuable, safe tool: **a fail2ban jail that bans any IP attempting to connect to a set of sensitive ports** (the remote-viewing ports I just blocked, plus SSH, RDP, VNC, etc.), triggered by firewall log entries.\n\nTo get firewall logs, I can add a firewalld rich rule that logs dropped","time":{"start":1790460635967,"end":1790460662820}},"time":1790460662820}');
