INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc908dc001gZwFI6h1dmNgSm','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12296,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc908db001R60wkEFFenTYt3","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfc9033e001sMMvjOHCAO9WOX","type":"text","text":"","time":{"start":1790460889307}},"time":1790460889307}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfc91ad8001jK7CiDaBV0djyh','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12297,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfc908db001R60wkEFFenTYt3","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfc9033e001sMMvjOHCAO9WOX","type":"text","text":"`torbrowser-launcher` **is** installed — you got it in already. So anonymity is genuinely covered; you didn''t need my earlier `pacman -S` suggestion after all. Two corrections to my own guidance there: I told you no browser was installed (FireDragon was), and I recommended installing Firefox (unnecessary).\n\n## Remaining: three items, and only one matters\n\n**1. The reboot — the only thing left that actually proves anything.**\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl reboot\n# then, after logging back in:\nuptime -s\nsystemctl --user is-active shield-kitty-watch.service evidence-seal.timer\n```\n\n`uptime -s` must be later than 15:07. The pass condition is that the units activate within ~30s of the new boot, not hours later — that''s the thing no amount of static checking can confirm.\n\n**2. Audit NOPASSWD surface — still 7/7 live.** You''ve been using these wrappers all session (including the last few checks), so leaving them is a decision, not an oversight. But they''re root-adjacent and temporary by design:\n\n```bash\nsudo bash /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-audit-readonly.sh --remove\n```\n\nVerified earlier it touches only `/etc/sudoers.d/audit-readonly` and the seven `/usr/local/sbin/audit-*` files — never the `%wheel` rule.\n\n**3. Two unpushed commits.** `d72d480` (the pkexec identity-resolution fixes) and `5a7bf5d` (git-verified.sh) sit in `/tmp/opencode/hss`. **Local git is not a backup** — if this disk fails before you restore access, those fixes are gone. That needs a new SSH key, which needs the FireDragon tab you just closed.\n\nWorth doing 2 and 3 before the reboot, since the reboot costs you the browser session.\n\nOne thing I''d flag as genuinely useful rather than tidy: I have now produced **seven false findings** in this audit, all from asserting verdicts my checks hadn''t earned. The scripts and the reboot test are sound. My unreviewed output is the weak link, and I''d rather you discount it than trust it.","time":{"start":1790460889307,"end":1790460893912}},"time":1790460893912}');
