INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0dfcaffe90013HdoGbQROtLFFG','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',12349,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","part":{"id":"prt_0dfcaf4dd001JTVnD9jAk6825R","sessionID":"ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g","messageID":"msg_0dfcaeda1001Bz0xvux2VT4XOA","type":"text","text":"Saved to `~/Desktop/POST-REBOOT-CHECKLIST.txt` — 210 lines, and I verified every binary it references actually exists on this system, so nothing will fail on a typo at 3am.\n\n**Structure:** 8 steps, each with explicit PASS conditions rather than just commands, plus known-benign errors listed so you don''t chase them. Ends with a copy/paste quick version.\n\n**The three things I''d flag:**\n\n1. **Step 2 comes before step 3 deliberately.** `uptime -s` must show later than 15:07. That''s the check whose absence let the last \"passing\" run mean nothing.\n\n2. **The real pass condition is the timestamp, not `is-active`.** `systemctl --user is-active` returning `active` proves nothing about cold boot — that''s the exact trap from earlier. The unit''s `ActiveEnterTimestamp` must land within ~30s of boot, before login. If it''s hours later, linger is broken.\n\n3. **I included the `firewall-cmd --state` caveat.** It reports `running` while enforcing nothing, so the file pairs it with the nft reject check that actually proves default-deny.\n\nI also put the two non-reboot items in step 8 — the audit-surface removal, and a note that `d72d480` and `5a7bf5d` exist only on this disk. Restoring push access needs a browser, so that''s easier to do now than after a reboot closes your session.\n\nThen:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl reboot\n```\n\nPaste the quick version at the bottom of the file once you''re back in. Bring me the output and I''ll read it properly — including anything that looks off, which is the part I''ve been getting wrong.","time":{"start":1790461015261,"end":1790461018089}},"time":1790461018089}');
