INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0e9930d230018S3SVKdtfDIqu7','ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2',325,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2","part":{"id":"prt_0e992f0b40015AeEG9MXPr577N","sessionID":"ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2","messageID":"msg_0e992dc77001wzEfo5eKCbrpkt","type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: hermes state.db contains messages where \"lia-cli is corrupted source — truncated imports and docstring text used as code. This never worked.\" So the \"older corrupted versions\" refers to the older lia-cli source found corrupted. Let me find which lia-cli version was corrupted — perhaps in the git history or a different copy. Let''s search hermes state.db messages for context around that, and look for the corrupted lia source files.\n\nLet me query more context from hermes messages about lia corrupted, and find where those corrupted files live (maybe in `_inventory` clones from GitHub — the full-history clones of all 120 repos; maybe an earlier version of lia-cli in git history had truncated source).\n\nPlan for the actual deliverable:\n1. Export data: hermes memories, opencode sessions (4 months), hermes state.db sessions/messages, terminal sessions, fish history, lia data (both checkouts + inventory git history + voynich memories copies + other lia bridges), curator backups, pastes, logs.\n2. Verify \"older corrupted versions\" of lia-cli: examine git history in _inventory clone for corrupted source versions, extract them for the record.\n3. Create a private repo, push via gh (which works). User said \"thru hermes\" — hermes CLI has sync commands; let me check `hermes sync --help` to see if it pushes to GitHub (gitbus.py - \"GitHub is the backchannel\").\n\nLet me check hermes sync capabilities and the date ranges of sessions.","time":{"start":1790625116340,"end":1790625123619}},"time":1790625123619}');
