INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0e99365a00013gYLTvglhKhl5s','ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2',353,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2","part":{"id":"prt_0e993285d001RKJDXN5JL4VVRh","sessionID":"ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2","messageID":"msg_0e9931d4c001WW9mSioF47wUuU","type":"reasoning","text":"Important findings:\n\n1. `hermes sync` works — it pulls/pushes via GitHub (https://github.com/intricos777-dot/hermes-cli). So \"push thru hermes\" → hermes sync pushes its git repo. \"push: no changes to sync\" means the hermes-cli repo itself has a sync mechanism. But we need to push the *data* to a private repo. Options: create a new private repo with gh, add data, push; and/or put data into the hermes-cli repo and run `hermes sync`. Actually hermes sync seems scoped to the hermes-cli inventory clone. Let me read gitbus.py/sources.py to understand.\n\n2. opencode.db has only 3 sessions (from 2026-09-26) — this system was rebuilt Sep 26. So \"past sessions\" going back 4 months aren''t in this opencode.db. Where are older opencode sessions? Maybe in `~/.local/share/opencode/repos` or older backups (blood-build-backup?) or in GitHub inventory clones (some repos may hold session exports). Also hermes state.db sessions started_at show 1970 (bogus) — 11 sessions. Messages4117.\n\n3. The4-month session history under other UIDs — since the machine was rebuilt Sep 26, older data may live in:\n   - `~/.local/share/opencode/repos`\n   - `~/.hermes` older (created Sep 26)\n   - GitHub repos: e.g., `later-work`, `sin-archive-2026`, `buildlog`, curator backups\n   - fish history (may include old machine history)\n   - konsole logs?\n   - `~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc.tui-migration.bak`\n   - Old opencode data might be in `blood-build-backup` or `~/.cache`\n\nLet me check opencode/repos, and search for older session storage (e.g., `~/.local/share/opencode/` history, `project` table in opencode.db which might list old projects with directories from previous installs).\n\nAlso the user''s earlier hermes request: \"check the past 4 months work of sessions under my UID Sin 1000\" and \"look at all past sessions from opencode and your terminals under the UID''s Living_sin Living_Sin Sin and livingsin\". So the data may be scattered in the inventory audit clones? Or in GitHub (old repos with session logs)? Or on disk with old ownership names in file contents.\n\nLet me check the opencode.db project table and account table for old paths/usernames, and check hermes state.db messages for session discovery results (the hermes agent may have already found data — messages mention \"session_id\": \"20260926_154356_2b91b6\", \"source\": \"cli\" — that''s a search/discover mode result, implying hermes has a session index somewhere. That looks like `hermes services`/`watch` search. Look at gitbus.py/sources.py? Actually \"discover\" mode query results with session_id suggests a session search index in state.db or in `~/.hermes/state/`.\n\nLet me look at ~/.hermes/state/ directory.","time":{"start":1790625130589,"end":1790625146271}},"time":1790625146272}');
