INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0e9972a45001vyYezABtHKc0YI','ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2',472,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2","part":{"id":"prt_0e996b68f001405rjqSKGqzJND","sessionID":"ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2","messageID":"msg_0e996a410001KDnHLIvcrZp6eD","type":"reasoning","text":"Found the corrupted file: `/home/livings/Projects/_inventory/lia-cli/kg_schema.py` contains the truncated `from owlready2 import` code — that''s the \"older corrupted version\". The working checkout `/home/livings/Projects/lia-cli` doesn''t have kg_schema.py at all? Actually earlier listing of working repo showed no kg_schema.py (inventory has it, working doesn''t). Wait the working listing didn''t show kg_schema.py or `keygen key.txt` or main.py or __init__.py. So inventory clone (upstream GitHub) has the corrupted files that the working clone... hmm, both cloned from same GitHub. Working repo was cloned 13:21, inventory 13:11. Working doesn''t have kg_schema.py, main.py, __init__.py, keygen key.txt? Let me re-check: working listing shows no `__init__.py`, no `main.py`, no `kg_schema.py`, no `keygen key.txt`. But reflog says clone at 13:21:34 from same URL... then maybe they deleted those files after clone? git status of working repo was empty (no output from status --short... actually output shows nothing between === markers? Let me recheck: the second command output showed \"===INV===\" with empty fsck, then reflog, then `git status --short | head` output nothing, then remotes. Actually the working repo `git status --short` printed nothing → clean tree. But kg_schema.py missing from listing? Let me verify.\n\nWait, actually the listing I did of `/home/livings/Projects/lia-cli` shows: CREDITS.md, data, .env.example, .git, .gitignore, lia-image-server.js, lia_legal_bridge.py, lib, node_modules, package.json, package-lock.json, __pycache__, src, systemd, tasks, test-bridge-contract.sh, test-import.js, test-import.ts, tsconfig.json. No `__init__.py`, `kg_schema.py`, `keygen key.txt`, `main.py`. But git status clean and inventory (older commit 609f4bc) has them. So they must have been removed in a later commit (working has commits up to 1e727cc including \"feat: add lia-inbox collector\"). A removal commit probably deleted them. Or... status clean means working tree == HEAD. So HEAD (1e727cc) doesn''t have them → they were deleted in commits between 609f4bc and 1e727cc? The log between: f0f6235, 41e3b0b, 1e727cc — those are inbox-related. Hmm, \"A\" status only showed adds. Let me check with git log --diff-filter=D. Not critical.\n\nSo: \"older corrupted versions\" = `_inventory/lia-cli/kg_schema.py` (truncated owlready2 code) + `keygen key.txt` (old ssh keygen log for user sin@old-host) + `main.py`/`__init__.py` minimal stubs, plus `lia-vault.json` (Sep 3) and the `lia-memory.vault.json` (Jul 17). Also the whole git history of lia-cli (pre-inbox commits). And `lia-groq-bridge` etc.\n\nAlso there''s `_verify.json` syntax failures for lia-cli, and `_secrets.json` audit.\n\nNow for the main deliverable — assembling the export. Let me define the scope:\n\n**User data sources (4 months back = since 2026-05-28), usernames Living_Sin/Living_sin/sin/living_sin, UID 1000:**\nSince system was rebuilt 2026-09-26, most on-disk data starts Sep 26. Older data sources:\n1. Git history in inventory clones (120 repos, full history) — but that''s 1.1G, too big to push wholesale. Instead: history of key repos? The user said \"all of my user data\" — ambiguous. Reasonable: session/memories/logs data, not all 120 repos (those are already on GitHub).\n2. `~/.hermes/` — memories, state.db (sessions+messages), .hermes_history, logs, pastes, terminal-sessions, curator backups, kanban.db, shared-state.db, cron executions.db, config (redact), SOUL.md.\n3. `~/.local/share/opencode/` — opencode.db + log.\n4. Lia.CLI — both checkouts'' data/memory, git history diff, voynich memories copies, other lia bridges'' vault data.\n5. Shell/terminal: fish_history, konsole, recently-used.xbel, bash history (empty?), Desktop notes.\n6. `sin-archive-2026` repo (name suggests sin archive) — check its content.\n7. `buildlog` repo (logs/buildlog.md mentioned living_sin).\n8. `hermes-pending-memories.json`.\n9. Old paths referenced: /home/sin (not present on this system).\n\nLet me check sin-archive-2026 and buildlog content, plus find any old home backups (e.g., timeshift, /mnt, /media). Also check fish_history date coverage.\n\nSize considerations: state.db 15MB + WAL 2.5MB, opencode.db 36MB+4MB WAL — as raw DB copies fine (git handles ~50MB, GitHub limit 100MB/file). Total repo maybe 60-80MB. OK.\n\nSecrets to exclude: ~/.hermes/auth.json (10KB tokens), ~/.hermes/vault/vault.key, ~/.hermes/shared/nous_auth.json, ~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json, keygen key.txt (contains fingerprint only — actually no private key material, just output log; it reveals old host/usernames — user data, fine to include), _secrets.json (audit of secrets — contains secret values? probably findings with values; risky), fish history may contain inline secrets (passwords typed), gh hosts.yml (not a token), .ssh keys (exclude), hermes config.yaml (has no secrets? model provider config — fine).\n\nLet me check _secrets.json content briefly and fish history for secrets. Also check Desktop.\n\nPlan the export directory: `/home/livings/Projects/user-data-archive-2026-09-28/` with subdirs, git init, gh repo create --private, push. User said \"thru hermes\" — I''ll also run `hermes sync` at the end? hermes sync syncs the hermes-cli repo (cfg repo_dir). That''s separate. Better interpretation: they want the push performed using hermes as the channel. The hermes CLI''s gitbus is generic-ish: cfg has repo_dir and remote. I could configure hermes config to point repo_dir at the export dir... risky — changes hermes behavior. Alternative: use hermes `send`/`journal`? No.\n\nHonest approach: create the repo, push via git (gh auth), and then record/report through hermes (e.g., `hermes send` a message to the owner/journal, and `hermes sync` to push hermes'' own repo state which includes... hmm the export dir isn''t in hermes repo).\n\nWait — maybe better: the hermes-cli repo (cfg[\"repo_dir\"]) — where is it? config.load_config reads config.yaml which didn''t show repo_dir. Let me read config.py to see defaults. If repo_dir is e.g. ~/Projects/_inventory/hermes-cli, then I could place the export inside that repo and `hermes sync` would commit and push it — that IS \"pushing thru hermes\". Let''s check config.py.\n\nAlso `hermes sync` earlier pulled from https://github.com/intricos777-dot/hermes-cli and pushed \"no changes\" — so repo_dir works with GitHub HTTPS. \n\nLet me read hermes config.py.","time":{"start":1790625363599,"end":1790625393220}},"time":1790625393221}');
