INSERT INTO event VALUES('evt_0e998b37d001O2A77ZpMIGbE62','ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2',522,'message.part.updated.1','{"sessionID":"ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2","part":{"id":"prt_0e997d155001MwHAOz6Hb9JjRZ","sessionID":"ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2","messageID":"msg_0e997c1f4001flTrKTA4O2dTB0","type":"reasoning","text":"- `~/github-intricos777/hermes-cli` EXISTS (created Sep 28 12:52 — today, when I ran `hermes sync`? No, it existed at 12:52 which is now-ish; the dir was created by git init during my `hermes sync` run? The sync output said \"pull: From https://... [new branch] main -> origin/main\" so it fetched. It has just `hermes-cli` subdir with 18 bytes dir... Let me look inside).\n\nActually `ls -la` of `~/github-intricos777` shows only `hermes-cli` dir dated today 12:52. Probably created by my sync command (git init + fetch). Interesting — so REPO_DIR was created fresh. It probably only has .git (fetch got origin/main). \"push: no changes to sync\" because dirty() false (clean, no files checked out? git status in a repo with no checkout but fetched origin/main — status would show... hmm \"no changes\" means status --porcelain empty. With only .git and no files, status is empty. OK.)\n\nWait — but config.yaml overlay: `config_path()` = REPO_DIR/config.yaml. So hermes.CLI config = DEFAULT_CONFIG since no config.yaml exists. Fine.\n\n- kanban tasks: empty (no rows).\n- No \"rani\" found in md files.\n- No external mounts. No old /home/sin.\n\nSo \"garuda rani systems\" has no concrete referent on disk — likely the user''s informal name for their Garuda machines (perhaps old host sin-... + new host zero). I''ll note in the report that only host `zero` (this machine) and the old host `sin-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc` (from sin-archive manifest) are identifiable, and no other systems are reachable from here.\n\nNow, data collection plan. Export root: `/home/livings/Projects/user-data-export/` (or better name: `sin-user-data-2026`). Contents:\n\n```\nREADME.md (manifest: sources, date range, withheld files, hostname notes)\nIDENTITY/ (passwd entry, hostnames, known_hosts, manifest of usernames/uids found)\nhermes/\n  memories/ (MEMORY.md USER.md)\n  hermes_history\n  logs/ (agent.log errors.log gateway*.log — maybe exclude big? include, they''re user data)\n  pastes/\n  terminal-sessions/\n  curator_backups/ (blobs — 10MB)\n  kanban.db, shared-state.db, cron/executions.db\n  state.db (+wal/shm) — sessions & messages (live db; better: dump to .sql for safety? A live copy risks inconsistency with WAL. Safer: sqlite3 .dump to SQL file + also copy raw. I''ll do .dump for both dbs, plus copy raw.)\n  config.yaml (hermes-agent config — no secrets)\n  SOUL.md\n  runtime/active_sessions.json\nopencode/\n  opencode.db dump (.sql) + log/\n  auth.json WITHHELD\n  config: ~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc + tui-migration.bak + tui.json (user config)\nlia/\n  working-checkout data/ (memory vault, legal reports) + git bundle of history?\n  inventory-checkout (older, includes corrupted kg_schema.py, keygen key.txt, main.py, __init__.py) — git bundle from GitHub would be redundant (already on GitHub); but the *working* checkout has unpushed commits? Working repo has 4 commits after clone — f0f6235, 41e3b0b, 1e727cc are local (were they pushed? remote is github; `git status` clean but push status unknown). Let me check if local is ahead of origin. If unpushed, bundle it.\n  voynich copies: lia-vault.json, hermes-pending-memories.json\n  lia-groq-bridge, lia-sync.py etc. — already in inventory GitHub clones; maybe skip (they''re on GitHub already) — but note in manifest.\n  corrupted source report: kg_schema.py copy + _verify.json syntax failures excerpt\nshell-terminal/\n  fish_history, konsole profiles+BlackRed, recently-used.xbel, bash files Desktop/NEXT-REBOOT-READ-ME.txt\nidentity/ (username/uid evidence: /etc/passwd line, old host names from manifests, sin-archive MANIFEST)\ngit-history-summaries/ (4-month commit log across inventory repos: `git log --since=2026-05-28` for each repo → one TSV/MD) — this is the \"4 months of work\" record without duplicating 1.1G.\naudit/ (_verify.json, _secrets.json?? contains findings — check for actual secret values. Suspicious-filename findings likely fine. But \"likely_live: true\" entries might include file paths & values. Risky. I''ll include _verify.json and _clone_results.json; _secrets.json maybe WITHHOLD if it contains values — need to check.)\n```\n\nAlso `buildlog/logs/buildlog.md` (matched living_sin earlier) — a log repo, include a copy? It''s on GitHub already. Include the file since it matched the username search? It''s already in private GitHub. Manifest note.\n\nRegarding \"push thru hermes\": options — (a) set hermes.CLI config repo_dir to the export dir and run `hermes sync` (gitbus pull/commit/push with hermes identity). That literally pushes thru hermes. The config at REPO_DIR/config.yaml — but REPO_DIR is fixed as the config location (config_path uses REPO_DIR constant, not cfg repo_dir... wait load_config reads cfg_path = REPO_DIR/config.yaml and overlays into cfg which includes repo_dir — so I can write `repo_dir: /path/to/export` INTO ~/github-intricos777/hermes-cli/config.yaml, and then `hermes sync` will pull/push THAT dir with remote from cfg (default remote = hermes-cli.git — wrong remote for the export). I can also set `remote: https://github.com/intricos777-dot/<export-repo>.git` in that config.yaml. Then `hermes sync \"message\"` does: pull (fetch/rebase), dirty check, commit, push — exactly \"push the data to a private repo thru hermes\".\n\nBut careful: writing config.yaml into REPO_DIR affects hermes CLI for everything (paths, entities...) — since I''d only add repo_dir+remote keys (merge with defaults), other commands (ledger, journal) would then operate on the export repo''s state? cmd_ledger uses cfg repo_dir state. Hmm — `_load_state(cfg)` probably reads REPO_DIR/state. Changing repo_dir redirects state. Could disrupt the running hermes gateway? The gateway (hermes-agent, the Nous product) is separate from hermes.CLI. hermes.CLI seems to be a separate tool (Projects/_inventory/hermes-cli). Risk: changing hermes.CLI config persists. Alternative cleaner: use env var `HERMES_REPO_DIR` — no, config_path also uses REPO_DIR (env override changes both config location and default repo_dir — messy).\n\nCleaner: run hermes sync with a temporary config? The CLI doesn''t support --config. I could run with env HERMES_REPO_DIR pointing to a temp dir that has config.yaml specifying repo_dir=export, remote=export-repo... but then remote identity etc. Actually that works: create `/tmp/opencode/hermes-cfg/config.yaml` with repo_dir + remote + branch; run `HERMES_REPO_DIR=/tmp/opencode/hermes-cfg python3 .../hermes sync \"message\"`. config_path = $HERMES_REPO_DIR/config.yaml → loads our overlay; repo_dir=export dir; remote=export repo. gitbus.pull/init, commit, push with default identity Hermes.CLI <intricos777@gmail.com>. \n\nBut wait: will the export repo already be committed by me? I''ll git init + commit locally first, then `hermes sync` handles push (dirty → commit+push; if I leave changes uncommitted, hermes commits them — even more \"thru hermes\"). Simplest: create repo, add remote, stage files, then let hermes do commit+push. If dirty → commit_and_push pushes. But commit_and_push needs remote in cfg (yes) and origin set in repo (pull() adds it). Order in gitbus.sync: pull first (adds remote, fetch), then commit_and_push. Good: I can leave everything staged/unstaged and hermes commits & pushes.\n\nRepo creation: `gh repo create <name> --private` — works with token (repo scope). Then push via HTTPS remote — does git push to https use gh credential helper? `gh auth setup-git` configures helper. Need to verify `git config --global -l` has credential. helper. If not, run `gh auth setup-git` (modifies global git config — acceptable? It''s the standard gh setup; user asked to push, and they logged into gh themselves. I''ll check first.)\n\nAlternatively remote URL `https://x-access-token:$(gh auth token)@github.com/...` — embeds token in remote (bad, persists in .git/config). Prefer credential helper.\n\nAlso: should the repo be created under intricos777-dot — yes, gh authenticated as that.\n\nSize guard: GitHub file limit 100MB, repo practical size — curator blobs 10MB, state.db ~18MB, opencode dump maybe 20-40MB (1328 messages? no — opencode.db has how many messages? earlier count 1328 messages in opencode; hermes messages 4117). Fine.\n\nAlso include git-history summary across 120 repos for the 4-month window — that''s a nice \"4 months of work\" index. Generate with a loop writing to one file. That could take a while for 120 repos but OK (local disk). Maybe limit: `git log --since=2026-05-28 --pretty=... ` per repo → append to file. Let''s do it with a timeout.\n\nAlso the user''s original ask mentions \"all of my usernames and UID ... under Living_Sin Living_sin sin and living_sin\" — include an identity map: grep for those usernames across /etc/passwd (only livings), old host references, and files owned/named accordingly. Also `getent passwd sin` etc.\n\nSecrets decisions (state plainly at end):\nWITHHOLD: ~/.hermes/auth.json, ~/.hermes/vault/* (vault.key + enc), ~/.hermes/shared/nous_auth.json, ~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json, ~/.ssh/*, gh hosts (no token anyway), _secrets.json (check first), keygen key.txt — contains no private key (just ssh-keygen transcript incl. fingerprint & comment sin@host) → include as user data. fish history — scan for secrets before including (user typed sudo commands; possible tokens). Check.\n\nAlso `~/.hermes/logs/process-results` (could be big) — check size. agent.log 1.2MB fine.\n\nAlso opencode db contains API keys? Possibly in config/message content. It''s user data; private repo; user asked. I''ll include dumps but note that live credentials files were withheld.\n\nLet me now:\n1. Check sizes: curator blobs, process-results, logs, opencode.db dump size.\n2. Check git ahead status of lia-cli working vs origin (unpushed commits → bundle).\n3. Check fish history & recently-used for obvious secrets.\n4. Check _secrets.json for actual values.\n5. Verify gh credential helper.","time":{"start":1790625435989,"end":1790625493884}},"time":1790625493885}');
