INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dee540f3001HGOVczj2NOV4uk','msg_0dee53a84001e9qZySajGRWVxV','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790445961459,1790445966389,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user just pasted a GitHub fine-grained personal access token directly into the chat, exactly as I warned against. I need to handle this carefully.\n\nKey facts:\n1. This is a real-looking fine-grained PAT (prefix `github_pat_`).\n2. It''s now exposed in the session transcript. I told them not to do this.\n3. Security best practice (from my system prompt): \"Never introduce code that exposes or logs secrets and keys. Never commit secrets or keys to the repository.\"\n\nWhat should I do?\n- I should NOT use this token. Using it would be actively harmful — it''s compromised now.\n- I should tell them to revoke it immediately. Revoking is the ONLY correct remediation; a leaked token can''t be \"un-leaked.\"\n- I should not repeat the token value in my response.\n\nAlso, I still don''t know what the \"hermes blood build\" is. That''s the actual blocker.\n\nLet me be direct but not preachy. This is a genuine security issue where being direct is the helpful thing.\n\nShould I offer to revoke it for them? I can''t — I''d need the token to do it, and that''s circular. Actually, revoking requires clicking in the browser. I could point them at the right page.\n\nLet me think about whether there''s any legitimate use. No. The token is burned. Any use of it from here would be using a compromised credential.\n\nI should be concise. Let me state:\n1. Revoke it now — link to settings.\n2. Why: it''s in the transcript now, permanently, so treat as compromised.\n3. I still need the repo URL.\n\nI should NOT echo the token back. Let me keep it short and actionable.\n\nThe system prompt says if I cannot/will not help, don''t be preachy, offer alternatives, 1-2 sentences. But this is a real security remediation that requires action, so a bit more than 2 sentences is warranted — but I''ll keep it tight.\n\nLet me not write the token to any file either. Good.\n\nI''ll give them the revoke URL: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens — well-known settings page.\n\nLet me be careful and concise.","time":{"start":1790445961459,"end":1790445966388}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dee55436001X8xz117b01agpg','msg_0dee53a84001e9qZySajGRWVxV','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790445966390,1790445968598,'{"type":"text","text":"**Revoke that token right now.** It''s compromised — it''s in this session''s transcript, which means it''s in the model''s context, your shell history/logs, and anywhere this session gets stored. Treat any secret that lands in a chat as burned.\n\nRevoke it here: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens (click it, then Delete). Don''t wait — you can mint a replacement in a minute.\n\nI''m not going to use it. A token that''s been exposed can''t be safely reused, and quietly writing it into git config or a shell command would just spread it to more places.\n\n**What I actually need from you: the repo URL.** That''s the only thing blocking the rebuild. If it''s public:\n\n```bash\ngit clone <url>   # no credentials required\n```\n\nIf it''s private, better options that never involve pasting a secret into chat:\n- **SSH** — if you have a key in `~/.ssh`, `git clone git@github.com:owner/repo.git` just works.\n- **Credential helper** — `git config --global credential.helper store`, then paste the token directly into git''s own prompt.\n\nEither way, I still don''t know what \"hermes blood build\" is. Give me the URL and I''ll take it from there.","time":{"start":1790445966390,"end":1790445968597}}');
