INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0deee7abe0019KUTIa2i8J3I1K','msg_0deee767c001AzyrRs5Tv5B812','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446566078,1790446566079,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0deee7ac0001hBKa6dS8ixaHk9','msg_0deee767c001AzyrRs5Tv5B812','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446566080,1790446574057,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a complete and accurate picture. Let me lay out the corrections and findings.\n\n**Corrections I owe the user:**\n\n1. **There are no previous sessions.** Not 3 weeks, not 2 days. The opencode DB has exactly ONE session — this one, created today 18:02 UTC. So \"check previous sessions from 3 weeks ago / 2 days ago\" cannot be done. The machine''s opencode install is from today 11:00.\n\n2. **I was wrong last turn to say \"There is no Hermes.\"** I checked `which hermes` and `~/.hermes/` and concluded no Hermes existed. That conclusion was too broad. Hermes is real — it''s `hermes-agent` from NousResearch, and there IS a substantial \"fleet\" subsystem in it. I found the source tree. I should own that error clearly.\n\n**What''s actually true about \"the fleet tool we made\":**\n- The fleet code is in the **upstream hermes-agent 2026.9.24 source tree**, not something authored in a prior session by us. The AUR git clone is clean (`## master...origin/master`, only untracked file is the downloaded tarball). So **no local modifications** — nobody \"we\" made changes to fleet code. The `hermes-fleet-fix@localhost` file is part of upstream''s fixture data (test fixtures), not a real contributor.\n- So \"the fleet tool we made\" doesn''t match the evidence. It''s an upstream feature.\n\n**What IS broken:**\n- `hermes-agent` is **not installed** (`pacman -Q` → not found). The AUR build was started today (10:53–11:04) and abandoned/incomplete.\n- The `pkg/` staging dir is `d--x--x--x` — execute-only, unreadable. That''s abnormal; normally it''d be drwxr-xr-x. Suggests the build died mid-package() or something odd happened with permissions.\n- The tarball is present (75MB), src/ is extracted, so `makepkg` got past extract and into build/package before stopping.\n\n**About \"rebuild everything that was broken\":**\nThe rebuild would be `paru -S hermes-agent` (or `makepkg -si`). But:\n- It needs sudo (AUR builds install to /opt, /usr/bin)\n- It''s a heavy build: npm ci with dev deps for 3 workspaces, electron, a full uv venv sync with `all,messaging` extras. Long.\n- I should ask before kicking off a sudo-requiring, long, network-heavy build. That''s a significant action.\n\nAlso worth noting: PKGBUILD depends on `python311` which IS in the paru cache (they were building that too), `nodejs`, `uv`, etc. So the machine was mid-setup installing a whole toolchain.\n\nLet me also flag: the user said \"rebuild everything that was broken\" — I''ve already rebuilt/fixed the hermes-blood theme. The hermes-agent build is the other half.\n\nLet me check whether the required deps are present, to say whether the build would even succeed. That''s genuinely useful pre-flight. And check the pkg/ dir situation and any paru log.\n\nLet me do a quick dependency check so my recommendation is grounded. Then report and ask before the sudo build.\n\nLet me check deps.","time":{"start":1790446566080,"end":1790446574056}}');
