INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0deeffaf00019em0s2PO64nPih','msg_0deefef59001B6Lzlq3vAyhUTQ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446664432,1790446664433,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0deeffaf4001x2go7Wbkak2PzT','msg_0deefef59001B6Lzlq3vAyhUTQ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446664436,1790446671048,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants to set up passwordless sudo for me. Let me think about this carefully.\n\nThis is a real security tradeoff. Setting up NOPASSWD sudo means anything running as this user can become root without a password. That''s a significant reduction in security. It''s a common practice for single-user dev machines though, and it''s the user''s own machine.\n\nBut here''s the thing — the build just failed for a completely different reason, and passwordless sudo would NOT have fixed it. That''s the key insight I should lead with. The user is offering to solve the wrong problem.\n\nThe actual failure:\n```\nnpm error notsup Required: {\"node\":\"^22.22.0 || ^24.11.0 || >=26.0.0\",\"npm\":\"<11.10.0 || >=11.17.0\"}\nnpm error notsup Actual:   {\"node\":\"v20.20.2\",\"npm\":\"12.1.0\"}\n```\n\nSo hermes-agent requires Node 22 or 24, but the system has Node v20.20.2. This is a hard dependency mismatch in the PKGBUILD — the AUR PKGBUILD declares `depends=(''nodejs''...)` which pulled in Node 20, but the package''s package.json engines field requires Node ^22.22.0 || ^24.11.0 || >=26.\n\nSo this is a genuine AUR packaging bug. The fix is to install Node 22+ (or 24), not to grant sudo.\n\nLet me be honest and helpful here:\n1. Passwordless sudo would NOT fix this build. The failure is a Node version mismatch, entirely unprivileged.\n2. I should offer the real fix options.\n3. Regarding passwordless sudo itself — I should note the tradeoff briefly but not be preachy. The user is the owner and it''s a legitimate dev practice. But I should point out it''s unnecessary for the current problem.\n\nActually, let me think about whether passwordless sudo is even needed. The build itself needs no sudo (makedepends satisfied). Only the final `pacman -U` needs it. So the user can just run one command with their password. No NOPASSWD needed.\n\nLet me verify the Node situation to give precise options.\n\nLet me check what node versions are available and what the actual requirement is.\n\nLet me investigate the node situation and whether the npm engine check can be worked around.\n\nKey question: is there a newer node available in the repos? Let me check.\n\nLet me run the checks.\n\nAlso — important: the `npm ci` with `--ignore-scripts` still enforces engines. There''s a way to bypass: `--engine-strict=false` is default actually... npm''s default for engine-strict is false, BUT if the package.json has \"engines\" and npm... hmm, actually the error says \"npm error notsup\" which is EBADENGINE. This happens when engine-strict is true, OR when it''s a workspace root. Actually npm sets engine-strict behavior; by default npm warns but doesn''t fail. But here it failed.\n\nWait, the PKGBUILD does `npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-fund --no-audit --progress=false --include=dev`. The failure is EBADENGINE. Possibly there''s an .npmrc with engine-strict=true in the tarball. Let me check.\n\nLet me look into this properly and give real options.\n\nLet me check node versions available and the .npmrc.","time":{"start":1790446664436,"end":1790446671046}}');
