\n  src/.npmrc: # minimatch 10.2.6 includes fixes for vulns. remove this when 10.2.6 is > 2wks  old\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=minimatch\n  src/.npmrc: \n  src/.npmrc: \n  src/.npmrc: # brace-expansion 5.0.9 includes fixes for vulns. remove this when 5.0.9 is > 2wks  old\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=brace-expansion\n  src/.npmrc: \n  src/.npmrc: # js-yaml 4.3.1 includes fixes for GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj. remove when > 2wks old (rel 2026-07-31)\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=js-yaml\n  src/.npmrc: \n  src/.npmrc: # nanoid 3.3.17 includes fixes for GHSA-2v37-7h3g-55p8. remove when > 2wks old (rel 2026-08-03)\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=nanoid\n  src/.npmrc: \n  src/.npmrc: # mermaid 11.16.1 includes fixes for 5 GHSAs. remove when > 2wks old (rel 2026-08-04)\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=mermaid\n  src/.npmrc: \n  src/.npmrc: # dompurify 3.4.13 includes fixes for GHSA-55q2-fjhq-7xh7. remove when > 2wks old (rel 2026-08-03)\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=dompurify\n  src/.npmrc: \n  src/.npmrc: # vite 8.2.0 is the first release depending on rolldown >= 1.2.1, which fixes\n  src/.npmrc: # a rolldown panic that breaks `npm run build` in apps/desktop\n  src/.npmrc: # (rolldown/rolldown#10337 — a regression in 1.1.5, the version vite 8.1.5\n  src/.npmrc: # pins as ~1.1.5). @oxc-project/types is here because rolldown 1.2.1 pins it\n  src/.npmrc: # as `=0.142.0` — an exact pin, so no older release satisfies it and the age\n  src/.npmrc: # gate would fail the whole install with ETARGET.\n  src/.npmrc: # remove these once vite 8.2.0 is > 2wks old.\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=vite\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=rolldown\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=@rolldown/*\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=@oxc-project/types\n  src/.npmrc: \n  src/.npmrc: # ink needs\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=lightningcss\n  src/.npmrc: min-release-age-exclude[]=postcss\n    \"engines\": {\n      \"node\": \"^22.22.0 || ^24.11.0 || >=26.0.0\",\n      \"npm\": \"<11.10.0 || >=11.17.0\"\n    },\n    \"allowScripts\": {\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== the actual failure ===\"\ngrep -E \"notsup Required|notsup Actual|EBADENGINE\" /tmp/opencode/hermes-build.log | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== current node/npm ===\"\nnode --version 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  node /''; npm --version 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  npm  /''\necho\necho \"=== newer node in repos? ===\"\npacman -Ss \"^nodejs\" 2>&1 | head -6\necho\necho \"=== is engine-strict forced on? ===\"\ncat ~/.npmrc 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  ~ \\/npmrc: /'' || echo \"  no ~/.npmrc\"\ncat ~/.cache/paru/clone/hermes-agent/src/hermes-agent-2026.9.24/.npmrc 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  src\\/.npmrc: /'' || echo \"  no .npmrc in source\"\ngrep -E ''\"engines\"'' -A4 ~/.cache/paru/clone/hermes-agent/src/hermes-agent-2026.9.24/package.json 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''","time":{"start":1790446673049,"end":1790446673352}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def01dcc001mfNzzcT3JQze47','msg_0deefef59001B6Lzlq3vAyhUTQ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446673356,1790446673356,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":47955,"input":357,"output":318,"reasoning":711,"cache":{"write":0,"read":46569}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def025e8001KhlPpWW4P58qQF','msg_0def01dd8001P073EPsx9B4x9V','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446675432,1790446675433,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def025ea0010CvF4vCpnArPjD','msg_0def01dd8001P073EPsx9B4x9V','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446675435,1790446682053,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have the complete diagnosis. Let me summarize:\n\n**The real problem:** hermes-agent''s `package.json` requires `node: ^22.22.0 || ^24.11.0 || >=26.0.0`, and its own `.npmrc` sets `engine-strict=true` (deliberately, for security). The system has **Node v20.20.2**. So `npm ci` hard-fails with EBADENGINE.\n\nThis is an **AUR packaging bug**: the PKGBUILD declares `depends=(''nodejs''...)` which resolved to Node 20, but upstream requires Node 22+. The Arch `extra/nodejs` is at **26.10.0** now, which satisfies `>=26.0.0`.\n\n**Key insight: passwordless sudo does NOT fix this.** The build failed at `build()` step, entirely unprivileged. Sudo is only needed at the final install.\n\n**The real fix:** upgrade Node. `extra/nodejs 26.10.0-1` is available and satisfies `>=26.0.0`. But upgrading nodejs system-wide requires sudo, and might affect other things.\n\nWait — the system has node v20.20.2 but extra/nodejs is 26.10.0. That''s odd. Maybe the installed nodejs is pinned/held, or it''s from a different source. Let me check what version is installed.\n\nActually `pacman -Ss \"^nodejs\"` showed extra/nodejs 26.10.0-1 as available. But `node --version` says v20.20.2. So the installed one is older — maybe held with IgnorePkg, or a versioned downgrade. Let me check.\n\nLet me check pacman -Q nodejs and IgnorePkg.\n\nThat''s important for the fix path.\n\nNow, about passwordless sudo: I should tell the user it''s unnecessary for the build (build needs no root), but IS needed for `pacman -S nodejs` (upgrading node) and the final package install. So passwordless sudo would actually help *this* path — because upgrading node needs root.\n\nBut I should be honest: I don''t need blanket passwordless sudo. Options:\n1. User runs the two sudo commands themselves (node upgrade, package install).\n2. User grants NOPASSWD just for specific commands (`pacman -S`), which is much safer than blanket NOPASSWD ALL.\n\nOption 2 is a good middle ground and I should recommend it — scoped NOPASSWD for pacman only, not blanket.\n\nAlso important: upgrading system nodejs could break other tools that depend on node 20. That''s a real risk worth flagging. Alternative: build node 22/24 locally into the build dir without touching system node. But that''s more complex — though actually it''s the *safer* approach since it avoids breaking the system.\n\nHmm, let me think about the cleanest fix:\n\nOption A: Upgrade system nodejs to 26 via pacman. Simple, but affects the whole system (opencode-bin is a node app? opencode-bin is a binary, probably fine). Risk: other node apps break.\n\nOption B: Install node 22/24 into a local prefix and prepend to PATH just for the makepkg build. Doesn''t touch the system. More work but safer. Could use `nvm`, or download official node tarball to /tmp or ~/.local.\n\nOption B is cleaner and avoids sudo entirely for the build. And it doesn''t require the user to upgrade system node and risk breaking things.\n\nLet me check nodejs installed version and IgnorePkg first, then recommend.\n\nLet me gather that.\n\nGiven my response should be concise, let me get the facts and give a clear recommendation.\n\nLet me check.","time":{"start":1790446675435,"end":1790446682051}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def03fc70017jJeKXXzIGQ4qL','msg_0def01dd8001P073EPsx9B4x9V','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446682055,1790446684440,'{"type":"text","text":"Passwordless sudo is the wrong lever here — that failure is **unprivileged**. It died in `build()`, before packaging. Real diagnosis:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Required | `node: ^22.22.0 \\|\\| ^24.11.0 \\|\\| >=26.0.0` |\n| Installed | `node v20.20.2` |\n| Enforced by | hermes-agent''s own `.npmrc` → `engine-strict=true` |\n\nThat''s an **AUR packaging bug**: `PKGBUILD:17` declares `depends=(''nodejs'')`, but upstream demands Node ≥22 and the repo now carries **26.10.0**. The maintainer''s dep is too loose, so it resolves to a Node that upstream refuses. Sudo can''t fix that; only a Node upgrade can.","time":{"start":1790446682055,"end":1790446684439}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def044c7001cfsiZg9cUrhvlM','msg_0def01dd8001P073EPsx9B4x9V','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446683335,1790446684660,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_ad7mzi2yr3yd_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== installed vs available nodejs ===\"\npacman -Q nodejs 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  installed: /''\npacman -Si nodejs 2>&1 | grep -E \"^Version\" | sed ''s/^/  available: /''\necho\necho \"=== is it held? ===\"\ngrep -E \"IgnorePkg|IgnoreGroup\" /etc/pacman.conf 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  no IgnorePkg set\"\necho\necho \"=== what else depends on nodejs (upgrade blast radius)? ===\"\npacman -Qi nodejs 2>&1 | grep -E \"Required By\" | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== npm 12.1.0 also unsupported by node 20? ===\"\ngrep -c \"does not support Node.js v20\" /tmp/opencode/hermes-build.log"},"output":"=== installed vs available nodejs ===\n  installed: nodejs-lts-iron 20.20.2-1\n  available: Version         : 26.10.0-1\n\n=== is it held? ===\n  # Pacman won''t upgrade packages listed in IgnorePkg and members of IgnoreGroup\n  #IgnorePkg   =\n  #IgnoreGroup =\n\n=== what else depends on nodejs (upgrade blast radius)? ===\n  Required By     : node-gyp  nodejs-nopt  npm  semver  yarn\n\n=== npm 12.1.0 also unsupported by node 20? ===\n1\n","metadata":{"output":"=== installed vs available nodejs ===\n  installed: nodejs-lts-iron 20.20.2-1\n  available: Version         : 26.10.0-1\n\n=== is it held? ===\n  # Pacman won''t upgrade packages listed in IgnorePkg and members of IgnoreGroup\n  #IgnorePkg   =\n  #IgnoreGroup =\n\n=== what else depends on nodejs (upgrade blast radius)? ===\n  Required By     : node-gyp  nodejs-nopt  npm  semver  yarn\n\n=== npm 12.1.0 also unsupported by node 20? ===\n1\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== installed vs available nodejs ===\"\npacman -Q nodejs 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  installed: /''\npacman -Si nodejs 2>&1 | grep -E \"^Version\" | sed ''s/^/  available: /''\necho\necho \"=== is it held? ===\"\ngrep -E \"IgnorePkg|IgnoreGroup\" /etc/pacman.conf 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  no IgnorePkg set\"\necho\necho \"=== what else depends on nodejs (upgrade blast radius)? ===\"\npacman -Qi nodejs 2>&1 | grep -E \"Required By\" | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== npm 12.1.0 also unsupported by node 20? ===\"\ngrep -c \"does not support Node.js v20\" /tmp/opencode/hermes-build.log","time":{"start":1790446684424,"end":1790446684660}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def049f6001FfO979RAz3g09F','msg_0def01dd8001P073EPsx9B4x9V','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446684662,1790446684662,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":50741,"input":1618,"output":395,"reasoning":775,"cache":{"write":0,"read":47953}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def05023001h0ZdpgM2wfdy41','msg_0def049fe001Lxaf7Le1S0lk0M','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446686243,1790446686244,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def05025001IVaeM3VleHE6S7','msg_0def049fe001Lxaf7Le1S0lk0M','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446686245,1790446699534,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have the full picture:\n\n**The system doesn''t even have `nodejs` — it has `nodejs-lts-iron` (20.20.2)!** That''s the \"LTS Iron\" variant = Node 20. The standard `nodejs` package is 26.10.0.\n\nSo they installed `nodejs-lts-iron` deliberately (or by default) which gives Node 20, and hermes-agent needs Node ≥22.\n\n**Required By nodejs-lts-iron:** node-gyp, nodejs-nopt, npm, semver, yarn. So replacing it could affect those. But those are all node packages; installing `nodejs` 26 would conflict with `nodejs-lts-iron` (they provide the same files, `nodejs` and `nodejs-lts-iron` conflict).\n\n**Options:**\n\n**Option A: Switch nodejs-lts-iron → nodejs (26.10.0).**\n- `sudo pacman -R nodejs-lts-iron && sudo pacman -S nodejs`\n- This removes the LTS iron, installs current 26. Satisfies `>=26.0.0`.\n- Risk: node-gyp/npm/semver/yarn get pulled/reinstalled. Minor risk.\n- Needs sudo.\n\n**Option B: Build with a local Node, don''t touch the system.**\n- Download official node 22 or 24 tarball to ~/.local/node-22, prepend to PATH during makepkg.\n- Zero system impact, no sudo needed for the build.\n- Then only the final `pacman -U` needs sudo.\n- This is the safest and I can do it entirely myself without root.\n\nOption B is clearly better for not breaking their system. And it means passwordless sudo is only needed for one command at the end (or the user runs it).\n\nI should recommend Option B, do it myself, and be clear about the one sudo command they need to run at the end.\n\nOn passwordless sudo: I should tell them I don''t need it if we go Option B — they just run one command at the end. If they want me to do everything, scoped NOPASSWD for `pacman` is a reasonable middle ground, but blanket NOPASSWD ALL is unnecessary and I''d advise against it.\n\nLet me be concise. The user offered passwordless sudo; I should redirect them to the actual fix and explain they may not need it at all.\n\nLet me offer the recommendation and ask which path, or just proceed with Option B since it''s non-destructive and clearly better. Option B is non-destructive (just extracts a tarball to ~/.local) so I can proceed without asking — it''s in the spirit of \"reinstall hermes.\"\n\nLet me do Option B: fetch node 22 LTS (or 24) and rebuild with it.\n\nWhich version? Required: ^22.22.0 || ^24.11.0 || >=26.0.0. Node 22.22.0+ or 24.11.0+. Let me use Node 24 LTS (or 22). Let me check what''s available. I''ll go with 24.x LTS as it''s the current LTS. Actually 22 is also LTS (\"Jod\"). Either works. Let me pick 24 since it''s newer LTS and satisfies ^24.11.0.\n\nActually, careful: npm version matters too — required `npm: \"<11.10.0 || >=11.17.0\"`. Node 24 ships npm 11.x. Node 26 ships npm 12. The tarball''s own npm version matters. If I install node 24 with its bundled npm, need npm >= 11.17.0. Node 24.11+ bundles npm 11.6+... hmm, might be < 11.17. That could fail.\n\nLet me think: npm 12.1.0 is currently installed (system, from nodejs-lts-iron? no — npm 12.1.0 warned it doesn''t support node 20). Interesting, system npm is 12.1.0.\n\nRequirement: npm \"<11.10.0 || >=11.17.0\". So npm 11.17+ or 12.1.0 both satisfy. The current npm 12.1.0 satisfies the npm constraint fine. The failure was purely node version.\n\nSo if I use a local Node 24 and the system npm 12.1.0... npm runs on node. If I put node 24 first in PATH, npm 12.1.0 would run on node 24. npm 12.1.0 supports node ^22.22.2 || ^24.15.0 || >=26. Node 24.15+ would be needed for npm 12.1.0 to be happy.\n\nThis is getting fiddly. Cleanest: download Node 24.15+ (satisfies both node ^24.11.0 and npm 12''s ^24.15.0), use its bundled npm. Or download node 26 (matches repo''s extra/nodejs 26.10.0).\n\nSimplest and most consistent with the repo: use **Node 26.10.0** — same as what pacman offers. I can extract the official tarball. But downloading from nodejs.org is a network fetch; I need the right URL. I shouldn''t guess URLs... but nodejs.org/dist/v26.10.0/node-v26.10.0-linux-x64.tar.xz is a standard, predictable pattern. Hmm, my instructions say don''t guess URLs unless confident it helps with programming. This is a well-known official distribution pattern. But to be safe, I could instead extract node from the pacman cache if available, or just tell the user to run the pacman command.\n\nActually — the simplest, most reliable, least-guessy path: **have the user run one sudo command** to switch to nodejs 26, then I rebuild. That avoids me fabricating URLs and avoids tarball gymnastics. And it''s their machine; a package manager swap is the \"correct\" Arch way.\n\nBut that requires them to act. Alternatively I use the local-tarball approach with a URL I''m confident about.\n\nLet me reconsider — cleanest recommendation that respects \"don''t guess URLs\" and keeps the system intact:\n\nRecommend: user runs `sudo pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron && sudo pacman -S nodejs` (gets Node 26.10.0, satisfies everything), then I rerun the build (no sudo needed). One command from them, I do the rest.\n\nAnd address the passwordless sudo offer: not needed for that (it''s one command), and if they want me to handle even that, scoped NOPASSWD for pacman only.\n\nLet me present this clearly and concisely. I''ll ask them to run the node swap, then I continue.\n\nLet me not over-engineer. Let me give the recommendation.\n\nI should be concise now — this has been a long thread.","time":{"start":1790446686245,"end":1790446699533}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def084110012i0zeAmQhbbbIx','msg_0def049fe001Lxaf7Le1S0lk0M','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446699537,1790446704243,'{"type":"text","text":"**Passwordless sudo isn''t the fix, and you probably don''t need it.** Here''s the actual situation:\n\nYour system doesn''t even have `nodejs` — it has **`nodejs-lts-iron` (20.20.2)**, the LTS-Iron variant. The repo''s current `nodejs` is **26.10.0**, which satisfies hermes''s `>=26.0.0`.\n\nSo the AUR PKGBUILD declares `depends=(''nodejs'')`, but what''s actually installed is `nodejs-lts-iron` — a different package. Upstream then refuses to build. The maintainer''s dep spec is too loose to catch this.\n\n**Recommended fix — one command from you, then I do the rest:**\n\n```bash\nsudo pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron && sudo pacman -S nodejs\n```\n\nThat gets Node 26.10.0, which satisfies both constraints (node `>=26.0.0`, npm bundled with it). Blast radius is small — only `node-gyp`, `nodejs-nopt`, `npm`, `semver`, `yarn` depend on it, and pacman will handle the transition. Nothing is held by `IgnorePkg`.\n\nThen I re-run the build, which needs **no sudo at all**, and hand you one final `sudo pacman -U` for the package.\n\n**On the sudo offer** — I''d push back on blanket `NOPASSWD ALL`. If you want me to handle even the node swap, scope it to just pacman, which is far less exposed:\n\n```\n# /etc/sudoers.d/opencode  (visudo this, don''t echo it)\nlivings ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pacman -S *, /usr/bin/pacman -Rns *\n```\n\nYour call:\n\n1. Run the pacman swap yourself, then tell me to continue — **my recommendation**, no sudo config changes at all\n2. Add the scoped sudoers rule, then I run the swap and the build\n3. Not blanket NOPASSWD — it grants root to everything running as you, including every script and dependency this build executes\n\nAlso still open: **\"supercode\"** — you said it''s a Linux alternative to opencode, but it isn''t in the AUR, the repos, or on disk. I need a repo URL or package name to install it. I won''t guess a URL and clone something arbitrary.","time":{"start":1790446699537,"end":1790446704241}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def09675001la1DPPW7xXoWyp','msg_0def049fe001Lxaf7Le1S0lk0M','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446704246,1790446704247,'{"reason":"stop","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":52837,"input":1037,"output":507,"reasoning":1448,"cache":{"write":0,"read":49845}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def10dc8002zHlIoePsFTUpzm','msg_0def10dc8001PBw96uqz86OuMx','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446734794,1790446734795,'{"type":"text","text":"sudo pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron && sudo pacman -S nodejs\n[sudo] password for livings:\nchecking dependencies...\nerror: failed to prepare transaction (could not satisfy dependencies)\n:: removing nodejs-lts-iron breaks dependency ''nodejs'' required by node-gyp\n:: removing nodejs-lts-iron breaks dependency ''nodejs'' required by nodejs-nopt\n:: removing nodejs-lts-iron breaks dependency ''nodejs>=20.17.0'' required by npm\n:: removing nodejs-lts-iron breaks dependency ''nodejs'' required by semver\n:: removing nodejs-lts-iron breaks dependency ''nodejs'' required by yarn "}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def117a1001FXM8HtL8jH8Vjk','msg_0def10dd4001pzyL17TATrdil7','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446737313,1790446737315,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def117a4001ux9By1tRy1CqT4','msg_0def10dd4001pzyL17TATrdil7','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446737316,1790446753113,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user ran the command and got a dependency error. This is a classic Arch chicken-and-egg: you can''t remove nodejs-lts-iron before installing nodejs, because things depend on `nodejs` (the provide name).\n\nThe fix: install `nodejs` FIRST, then remove `nodejs-lts-iron`. Or use `pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron` with the install in the same transaction so pacman resolves it — actually pacman can handle this in one transaction if you do `pacman -S nodejs` and `pacman -R nodejs-lts-iron` together, but they conflict (both provide /usr/bin/node etc.), so it''s a file conflict — pacman will refuse unless you use `--overwrite`.\n\nThe clean solution on Arch:\n1. `sudo pacman -S nodejs` — this will conflict with nodejs-lts-iron (file conflicts). Need `--overwrite=''*''` or remove lts-iron in the same transaction.\n\nStandard approach:\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S nodejs --overwrite=''*''\n```\nThis installs nodejs 26 over the lts-iron files. Then:\n```bash\nsudo pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron\n```\nBut after overwriting, nodejs-lts-iron is still \"installed\" per the db, just its files are replaced. Removing it after would remove... hmm, it would remove the files it thinks it owns, which now belong to nodejs. That could delete nodejs files. Risky.\n\nBetter approach: do it in one transaction so pacman knows:\n```bash\nsudo pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron -S nodejs\n```\nHmm, pacman doesn''t allow -R and -S in one command in older versions... Actually `pacman -Rns pkg -S otherpkg` isn''t valid syntax. But you CAN do:\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S nodejs --overwrite ''*'' \n```\nthen pacman sees nodejs-lts-iron still installed. Then to remove it without deleting nodejs''s files, use `--overwrite` on the removal too? Removal doesn''t take --overwrite.\n\nCleanest and safest: **just install nodejs with --overwrite and leave nodejs-lts-iron installed-but-neutralized**? That''s messy, leaves a phantom package.\n\nActually, the truly clean way: pacman CAN handle remove+install in one transaction using two `-U`/or using `pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron` with `--assume-installed`...\n\nLet me think about what actually works reliably on Arch:\n\nOption 1: `sudo pacman -Syu nodejs --overwrite=''*''`\n- Installs nodejs 26, overwriting the conflicting files from lts-iron.\n- nodejs-lts-iron remains in the db as installed but its files are clobbered.\n- Then `sudo pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron` would want to remove /usr/bin/node, /usr/bin/npm etc. → would break nodejs.\n\nHmm. Actually when you install nodejs over lts-iron with --overwrite, the file entries in the db for lts-iron still list those paths. Removing lts-iron deletes them. Bad.\n\nOption 2: Remove the dependents first, then swap:\n```bash\nsudo pacman -Rns npm yarn nodejs-nopt semver node-gyp   # hmm, node-gyp is needed by things\nsudo pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron\nsudo pacman -S nodejs npm\n```\nThat''s disruptive.\n\nOption 3: **The clean, standard Arch way** — nodejs and nodejs-lts-iron conflict; you do:\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S nodejs --overwrite=''*''\n```\nand that''s it. Many people then leave lts-iron. But actually, in practice, does pacman remove the conflicting package automatically? No.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Actually the real standard approach used widely:\n\n```bash\nsudo pacman -Rns $(pacman -Qqtd)  # no\n```\n\nLet me think about `pacman -Syu nodejs --overwrite ''*''` more carefully. Actually I recall that in practice, people DO run:\n`sudo pacman -S nodejs-lts` to switch between variants and it errors, so they use `--overwrite`.\n\nThe safest path that leaves a consistent db: **install nodejs into a separate prefix and don''t touch the system package at all.** i.e., Option B from my earlier message — local Node in ~/.local. This avoids all of this mess entirely, and I''ve been recommending minimal system disruption.\n\nGiven the dependency tangle, the local-node approach is now clearly better. And I can do it without sudo.\n\nBut I need a download URL. nodejs.org/dist/v26.10.0/node-v26.10.0-linux-x64.tar.xz — I''m fairly confident of this pattern. It''s the official Node distribution site. This is a legitimate programming need. I''ll use it but verify the download succeeds and check the file.\n\nAlternatively — I could avoid the URL guessing entirely by using `pacman -Sw` to download the nodejs package to the cache without installing, then extract it manually into a local prefix with `bsdtar`. That''s clever: `pacman -Sw nodejs --cachedir /tmp/opencode/pkgs` downloads but doesn''t install, no sudo needed? Actually `pacman -Sw` may need sudo for the DB lock... it needs the db lock which is root-owned, so it''d prompt. Hmm. `pacman -Sw` typically requires root.\n\nAlternative without sudo and without URL guessing: use `curl` on the Arch mirror? Also a URL.\n\nOK. Let me be pragmatic and honest: I''ll offer to fetch the official Node tarball from nodejs.org (standard, well-known URL pattern for a programming need) — that''s legitimate per my instructions (\"URLs for helping the user with programming\"). And it requires zero sudo and zero system changes.\n\nBut first, let me give them the immediate correct fix for the error they hit, since they asked implicitly by showing the error. The error is just an ordering problem.\n\nActually, there IS a clean single-transaction solution:\n```bash\nsudo pacman -Rns nodejs-lts-iron --nodeps\n```\nno, that leaves broken deps.\n\nHmm, actually the cleanest: pacman supports removing and installing in one transaction via:\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S nodejs --overwrite ''/usr/*'' --overwrite ''/etc/*''\n```\nUgh.\n\nLet me just check reality: I can test what conflicts exist with `pacman -S nodejs --print-format` or use `pacman -Sii nodejs`. Let me actually investigate rather than speculate. I can run pacman queries without sudo.\n\nLet me check what files nodejs conflicts on vs lts-iron, and whether there''s a cleaner path. Let me also check if npm/yarn etc. would be satisfiable.\n\nLet me gather facts: pacman -Sii nodejs to see conflicts/replaces, and check which packages provide what.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nActually, the most robust and least invasive recommendation, given they''re clearly fighting pacman dependency order: **use a local Node install**. No sudo, no system breakage, reversible with one rm. I''ll do it.\n\nLet me verify the URL works by fetching the SHASUMS or just downloading. Let me try.\n\nLet me first quickly confirm the nodejs-lts-iron package situation, then go local-node.\n\nLet me be efficient: run diagnostics and attempt the local node download.\n\nLet me check conflicts first.","time":{"start":1790446737316,"end":1790446753111}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def1555c0012a4Qjlnats3gvh','msg_0def10dd4001pzyL17TATrdil7','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446753116,1790446754278,'{"type":"text","text":"Classic Arch ordering problem — you can''t remove the provider before its dependents are satisfied. Let me check the actual conflict surface:","time":{"start":1790446753116,"end":1790446754277}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0def155c3001lVDsTVKVSSsDww','msg_0def10dd4001pzyL17TATrdil7','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790446753219,1790446755578,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_yfmt03nrg62e_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== nodejs (26) vs nodejs-lts-iron: conflicts/replaces ===\"\npacman -Sii nodejs 2>&1 | grep -E \"^(Conflicts|Provides|Replaces)\" | sed ''s/^/  nodejs: /''\necho\npacman -Sii nodejs-lts-iron 2>&1 | grep -E \"^(Conflicts|Provides|Replaces)\" | sed ''s/^/  lts-iron: /''\necho\necho \"=== do the dependents have updates available that accept nodejs? ===\"\nfor p in npm yarn nodejs-nopt semver node-gyp; do\n  printf ''  %-14s installed=%-18s available=%s\\n'' \"$p\" \"$(pacman -Q $p 2>/dev/null | awk ''{print $2}'')\" \"$(pacman -Si $p 2>/dev/null | awk ''/^Version/{print $3}'')\"\ndone"},"output":"=== nodejs (26) vs nodejs-lts-iron: conflicts/replaces ===\n  nodejs: Provides        : None\n  nodejs: Conflicts With  : None\n  nodejs: Replaces        : None\n\n  lts-iron: Provides        : nodejs=20.20.2\n  lts-iron: Conflicts With  : nodejs\n  lts-iron: Replaces        : None\n\n=== do the dependents have updates available that accept nodejs? ===\n  npm            installed=12.1.0-1           available=12.1.0-1\n  yarn           installed=1.22.22-2          available=1.22.22-2\n  nodejs-nopt    installed=10.0.1-1           available=10.0.1-1\n  semver         installed=7.8.5-1            available=7.8.5-1\n  node-gyp       installed=13.0.2-1           available=13.0.2-1\n","metadata":{"output":"=== nodejs (26) vs nodejs-lts-iron: conflicts/replaces ===\n  nodejs: Provides        : None\n  nodejs: Conflicts With  : None\n  nodejs: Replaces        : None\n\n  lts-iron: Provides        : nodejs=20.20.2\n  lts-iron: Conflicts With  : nodejs\n  lts-iron: Replaces        : None\n\n=== do the dependents have updates available that accept nodejs? ===\n  npm            installed=12.1.0-1           available=12.1.0-1\n  yarn           installed=1.22.22-2          available=1.22.22-2\n  nodejs-nopt    installed=10.0.1-1           available=10.0.1-1\n  semver         installed=7.8.5-1            available=7.8.5-1\n  node-gyp       installed=13.0.2-1           available=13.0.2-1\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== nodejs (26) vs nodejs-lts-iron: conflicts/replaces ===\"\npacman -Sii nodejs 2>&1 | grep -E \"^(Conflicts|Provides|Replaces)\" | sed ''s/^/  nodejs: /''\necho\npacman -Sii nodejs-lts-iron 2>&1 | grep -E \"^(Conflicts|Provides|Replaces)\" | sed ''s/^/  lts-iron: /''\necho\necho \"=== do the dependents have updates available that accept nodejs? ===\"\nfor p in npm yarn nodejs-nopt semver node-gyp; do\n  printf ''  %-14s installed=%-18s available=%s\\n'' \"$p\" \"$(pacman -Q $p 2>/dev/null | awk ''{print $2}'')\" \"$(pacman -Si $p 2>/dev/null | awk ''/^Version/{print $3}'')\"\ndone","time":{"start":1790446754260,"end":1790446755577}}}');
