INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0defe2970001fUz8ZM2CYScsOn','msg_0defe2149001P9NiKqeKpPVzKo','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790447593840,1790447601935,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants me to:\n1. Check all builds and tools \"made from the past 3 months\" in \"your sessions and hermes sessions\"\n2. Bug check, error check, audit them all\n3. Reinstall them, excluding Windows tools/frameworks, save for ones needed on Steam\n\nKey problems:\n1. **\"past 3 months\" of sessions** — I already established there is exactly ONE opencode session (today). There is no 3-month session history. I verified this in the DB. I must not pretend otherwise.\n2. **\"hermes sessions\"** — hermes-agent was never installed, so it has no sessions. It''s not installed (the package is built but not installed). So no hermes sessions exist. I should verify this again post-install... well, it isn''t installed yet.\n3. The build actually SUCCEEDED — package produced. I should report that first.\n\nSo the honest answer: there is no session history to audit. The 3-month premise is false.\n\nWhat CAN I do that''s genuinely useful and in the spirit of the request? I can audit the **actual artifacts on this machine** — the AUR packages that were built/attempted today, the installed AUR packages, and the repos. That''s real, verifiable work that matches their actual intent (\"audit my tools and reinstall the good ones\").\n\nThe machine was set up today (Sep 26). So \"past 3 months\" of work is actually \"today.\" Let me look at what''s real:\n- Installed AUR packages: accounts-qml-module, opencode-bin, python311 (earlier check, now also nodejs)\n- paru clone cache: electron34, hermes-agent, opencode-bin, python311, void-bin\n- Repos on GitHub: 28 public repos, many C++ game-engine-ish projects (tf2-elysium, half-life-elysium, etc.), skyrim mods, voynich corpus, etc.\n- \"void-bin\" in paru cache — Void Linux related?\n- \"electron34\" — a Windows-ish/electron framework? electron is cross-platform.\n\nThe user''s filter: \"excluding any windows tools or frameworks save for the ones needed on steam.\" This suggests they think some of their tools are Windows-only. On Arch, Windows tools wouldn''t be installed. Hmm. But some repos might be Windows-targeted game mods/tools.\n\nLet me be honest and do real work:\n1. Report the build succeeded (important — they need to install it)\n2. State clearly: no session history exists; can''t audit \"3 months of sessions\"\n3. Offer/do the real equivalent: audit what''s actually on the machine + the GitHub repos\n4. Ask about \"needed on Steam\" since I can''t determine Steam requirements without checking if Steam is installed and what Proton games need\n\nLet me first gather facts: is steam installed? What are the paru clone dirs? What''s installed? Let me check.\n\nLet me report the successful build and gather the real inventory.\n\nLet me do this efficiently.\n\nLet me check:\n- Steam installed? proton? wine?\n- paru clone contents (what was being built)\n- installed AUR packages\n- the 28 repos with their languages (to spot \"tools\")\n\nLet me gather.\n\nActually, I need to be careful about the reinstall-everything ask — reinstalling 28 repos'' worth of software blindly would be reckless. I should audit first and present findings, not mass-reinstall. Especially \"reinstall them all\" without knowing what each is.\n\nLet me gather facts and be honest.\n\nLet me report build success first + inventory.","time":{"start":1790447593840,"end":1790447601933}}');
