INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0defe7cba001365pb5ZBtzMxOV','msg_0defe73d30010svkx2EeUTeeaQ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790447615163,1790447625863,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have the truth. Let me be precise:\n\n- `~/.hermes/skins/blood.yaml` — 11:14, that''s MY theme install (I did that at 11:12-11:14).\n- **Everything else was created at 11:22:00** — all within the same second (11:22:00.36).\n- 11:22 is when the hermes build was running (the build started ~11:19-11:20 and finished 11:24).\n\nSo what created these at 11:22? The **build** — specifically the `check()` step: `PYTHONPATH=\"$PWD\" venv/bin/python -c ''import hermes_cli.main''`. Importing `hermes_cli.main` would trigger the package''s `__init__`/module-level code that creates `~/.hermes/` scaffolding (sessions, cron, memories, hooks, SOUL.md, logs).\n\nSo `~/.hermes` was created as a side effect of the makepkg `check()` step importing the module. It is NOT evidence of prior sessions.\n\nCritical facts:\n- `sessions/` is **empty** (0 bytes, dir size 0, no files inside)\n- `logs/agent.log` = 0 bytes\n- `logs/errors.log` = 0 bytes\n- `memories/` empty\n- `cron/` empty\n\nSo there are genuinely ZERO hermes sessions. My earlier conclusion (\"no session history\") was CORRECT, but my earlier *evidence* was wrong — I said \"~/.hermes contains exactly one file, blood.yaml\" and inferred hermes didn''t exist. Actually the dir tree didn''t exist yet at that point (11:12), and it was created at 11:22 by the build.\n\nWait — timeline check. My earlier `find ~/.hermes -type f` was run before the build (build started later). At that time only skins/blood.yaml existed (11:14, my install). So my observation was accurate for that moment. The tree appeared at 11:22 due to the build''s check() step.\n\nSo: I was NOT wrong that there are no sessions. But I WAS wrong to conclude \"Hermes doesn''t exist\" — and I did later correct that (turn where I found hermes-agent in paru cache). Good.\n\nNow: `SOUL.md` — 667 bytes, created by the import. Let me read it, it''s interesting/relevant.\n\nNow, the main answer to the user''s request:\n\n**\"past 3 months\" / \"in your sessions and hermes sessions\"**:\n- opencode: 1 session, today. No 3-month history.\n- hermes: `sessions/` is EMPTY, `agent.log` 0 bytes, `errors.log` 0 bytes. Zero sessions. And the whole tree was created today at 11:22 by the build''s check() step.\n- Machine: earliest config file is 2026-02-02 (starship.toml), so the machine is ~7 months old. But that doesn''t mean 3 months of *build sessions*.\n\nSo I cannot audit \"3 months of sessions\" — they don''t exist. What exists is:\n- 28 public GitHub repos (their actual tool/build output)\n- AUR packages built today\n- Installed AUR packages\n\n**Steam**: NOT installed at all. No steam, no proton, no wine. So \"save for the ones needed on Steam\" is moot — there''s no Steam, and no Windows/Proton runtime. I can''t determine what''s \"needed on Steam\" without Steam installed and without knowing their library.\n\nSo the request has multiple false premises. I should be direct about that, and offer the real equivalent: audit the 28 GitHub repos (the actual artifacts of their work) + the AUR packages.\n\nAlso \"reinstall them all excluding windows tools\" — I should not mass-reinstall 28 repos blindly. That needs per-item decisions and would be reckless. I should present the inventory and get direction.\n\nLet me read SOUL.md quickly since it''s a real artifact, and get the repo inventory with language/platform info to make the audit concrete.\n\nLet me do that and then give a clear, honest report with a concrete path forward.\n\nLet me be efficient — gather the repo inventory and SOUL.md.\n\nLet me check the repos for what they are (Windows vs Linux relevant). Many are C++ game projects (TF2, Half-Life, Skyrim, Zelda). Those are source repos, not installable tools. \"Reinstall\" doesn''t apply to them.\n\nLet me get a quick classification.\n\nLet me fetch repo details (topics/description) to classify. Let me use the API for descriptions.\n\nLet me do it.","time":{"start":1790447615163,"end":1790447625862}}');
