INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df00785b001iam4O49zw99664','msg_0df006cf70010Kvd7oSMv0j219','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790447745115,1790447745117,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df00785e0012gRFTZWTJWBE2g','msg_0df006cf70010Kvd7oSMv0j219','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790447745118,1790447749079,'{"type":"text","text":"Opened. This time the token is genuinely the right tool — unlike the first attempt, it solves something SSH can''t (listing private repos). So make it, with these settings:\n\n- **Resource owner:** `intricos777-dot`\n- **Repository access:** All repositories\n- **Permissions:** `Contents: Read-only` + `Metadata: Read-only` (metadata is required, and read-only is all the audit needs)\n- **Expiration:** 7 days, not \"no expiration\"\n\nThen hand it over **without putting it in this chat.** Write it to a file only you can read, and tell me the path:\n\n```bash\nread -rs -p \"token: \" T && printf ''%s'' \"$T\" > ~/.config/gh/token && chmod 600 ~/.config/gh/token && echo saved\n```\n\nThat keeps it out of the transcript, out of shell history, and out of any `ps` output. Then I''ll read it from that path, use it to enumerate all 120 repos, and clear it when we''re done.\n\nTwo things to confirm while you''re in there:\n\n1. **Did you revoke the first token** from earlier in this session? You never answered. If not, revoke it now — it''s in the transcript and should be treated as burned.\n2. Keep **read-only**. The SSH key already grants full git write access to everything, so a token doesn''t need to add write. If you later want me to push a fix to `hermes-blood`, SSH covers that.\n\nOnce the file exists, say the word and I''ll run the inventory. That unblocks the real audit — all 120 repos, correctly split public/private, which is what made my last report unreliable.","time":{"start":1790447745118,"end":1790447749078}}');
