INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df0abb19001n8lPk9dV0iuyC2','msg_0df0ab0d0001vUNzZqJPzQgKk2','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790448417561,1790448421772,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user insists that we built the Blood Build together in previous sessions. I''ve verified thoroughly that there''s only one session. But let me be careful and thorough here — maybe there''s session data somewhere I haven''t checked. Let me do one more exhaustive check rather than just repeating myself. The user is confident, so I should verify hard before either agreeing or contradicting again.\n\nLet me check: backup files, other opencode installs, snap/flatpak, the DB more carefully (archived sessions, deleted sessions, event table), prompt-history.jsonl, any tarballs/zips on Desktop/Documents/Downloads, etc.\n\nLet me be rigorous and check things I haven''t: the `event`, `session_message`, `session_share`, `todo`, `project` tables; workspace/project dirs; any backups of the DB; and filesystem-wide searches for anything blood-build related.\n\nI should do this check and report honestly. If after exhaustive search there''s nothing, I need to hold my ground but be respectful and non-repetitive about it — and importantly, consider that the user may be remembering a session on a *different machine* (the GitHub repo has commit history from before today, and there are 92 private repos — the work exists, just not in my session store here).\n\nThat''s the key insight: the Blood Build work IS real — the git history proves it (5 commits: \"Blood Build: full system theme\", \"restore: switch font...\", \"fix: ensure all components use Great Vibes font\", \"fix: remove stray ] from BlackRed.colorscheme\", \"chore: tune gtk settings and install script\"). The user isn''t wrong that they made it — they''re wrong that it came from *my* sessions. The provenance is the git history, not a session store.\n\nLet me verify exhaustively and then reframe helpfully.","time":{"start":1790448417561,"end":1790448421769}}');
