INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1ca660001iK48hHAxaU3mRJ','msg_0df1c9e85001DGt9TYiASbW9i6','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790449591904,1790449593245,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_m4fo5y5ltu72_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== TOP MEMORY CONSUMERS (4.4G used, only 848M free) ===\"\nps -eo pmem,rss,comm --sort=-rss 2>/dev/null | head -8 | awk ''{printf \"  %5s%%  %6.0fMB  %s\\n\",$1,$2/1024,$3}''\necho\necho \"=== the ''system too slow'' kwin errors: was there an OOM kill? ===\"\njournalctl -k -S \"-2h\" --no-pager 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"oom|killed process|out of memory\" | tail -5 | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  no OOM events\"\necho\necho \"=== the firefox.conf typo from earlier ===\"\ncat -n ~/.config/environment.d/firefox.conf 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''"},"output":"=== TOP MEMORY CONSUMERS (4.4G used, only 848M free) ===\n   %MEM%       0MB  COMMAND\n   10.0%     762MB  opencode\n    9.6%     727MB  opencode\n    5.4%     408MB  firedragon\n    5.2%     398MB  WebExtensions\n    4.7%     358MB  plasmashell\n    3.4%     259MB  kwin_wayland\n    3.2%     245MB  Isolated\n\n=== the ''system too slow'' kwin errors: was there an OOM kill? ===\n  Sep 26 10:48:12 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc systemd[1]: Listening on Userspace Out-Of-Memory (OOM) Killer Socket.\n\n=== the firefox.conf typo from earlier ===\n       1\tenv MOZ_USE_XINPUT2=1 firefox\n","metadata":{"output":"=== TOP MEMORY CONSUMERS (4.4G used, only 848M free) ===\n   %MEM%       0MB  COMMAND\n   10.0%     762MB  opencode\n    9.6%     727MB  opencode\n    5.4%     408MB  firedragon\n    5.2%     398MB  WebExtensions\n    4.7%     358MB  plasmashell\n    3.4%     259MB  kwin_wayland\n    3.2%     245MB  Isolated\n\n=== the ''system too slow'' kwin errors: was there an OOM kill? ===\n  Sep 26 10:48:12 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc systemd[1]: Listening on Userspace Out-Of-Memory (OOM) Killer Socket.\n\n=== the firefox.conf typo from earlier ===\n       1\tenv MOZ_USE_XINPUT2=1 firefox\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== TOP MEMORY CONSUMERS (4.4G used, only 848M free) ===\"\nps -eo pmem,rss,comm --sort=-rss 2>/dev/null | head -8 | awk ''{printf \"  %5s%%  %6.0fMB  %s\\n\",$1,$2/1024,$3}''\necho\necho \"=== the ''system too slow'' kwin errors: was there an OOM kill? ===\"\njournalctl -k -S \"-2h\" --no-pager 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"oom|killed process|out of memory\" | tail -5 | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  no OOM events\"\necho\necho \"=== the firefox.conf typo from earlier ===\"\ncat -n ~/.config/environment.d/firefox.conf 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''","time":{"start":1790449593197,"end":1790449593244}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1ca904001hVHKCIgUojopZ4','msg_0df1b8bbd001vEcIYds2eZwNSO','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790449592580,1790449592581,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1ca906001MRrXxlhSeEYhhc','msg_0df1b8bbd001vEcIYds2eZwNSO','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790449592582,1790449605482,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Key findings from `git.sh`:\n\n1. `download_git()`: if `$dir` doesn''t exist or is empty → `git clone --origin=origin --mirror \"$url\" \"$dir\"`. That''s the full mirror clone = 37 GiB. **This is the failure.**\n\n2. If `$dir` already exists → it checks `remote.origin.url` matches, then `git fetch --all -p`. **Non-fatal** (just a warning) on failure — allows offline builds.\n\n3. `extract_git()`: clones from the mirror with `-s` (shared) into `$srcdir/chromium-mirror`, then `git checkout --force --no-track -B makepkg \"$ref\"`.\n\n4. `calc_checksum_git()`: `git archive --format tar \"$fragval\"` — needs the **tag** object present in the mirror.\n\nSo my plan: pre-create `/home/livings/.cache/paru/clone/electron34/chromium-mirror` as a `--mirror` clone but **shallow** with only the tag `132.0.6834.210`. Then:\n- `download_git` will skip cloning, do `git fetch --all -p` (cheap/no-op for a shallow mirror at the same URL, and even if it fails it''s only a warning).\n- `calc_checksum_git` will `git archive` the tag → works.\n- `extract_git` will `git clone -s` from it and checkout the tag → works, produces full working tree.\n\nCritical detail: a shallow mirror clone. `git clone --mirror --depth 1 --branch 132.0.6834.210` — with `--mirror` + `--depth` + `--branch`, git creates a bare mirror with a single commit and the tag ref. That gives us the full tree/blobs of that one commit (~1.4 GB) instead of 37 GiB of history.\n\nLet me verify the tag exists on github.com/chromium/chromium.git. The tag format: `132.0.6834.210`.\n\nAlso: `git fetch --all -p` on a shallow repo might deepen or fail — but failure is only a warning. Good. However, there''s a risk: `git fetch` in a mirror clone with default refspec `+refs/*:refs/*` will try to fetch all 29.5M objects'' worth? No — a shallow repo stays shallow unless `--unshallow`/`--depth` is given; git respects the existing shallow boundary. Actually, `git fetch` in a shallow clone fetches only what''s needed to keep the shallow boundary at the recorded commits. It won''t pull all history. It should be cheap.\n\nHmm, but one nuance: on the GitHub chromium mirror, there are ~200k+ refs (branches for every release). `git fetch --all -p` with refspec `+refs/*:refs/*` will fetch **all** those refs. That could be large-ish, but without their history (shallow) it''s just commit+tree objects for the tip of each branch... Actually no, for a shallow fetch git needs the trees/blobs of the tip commits to compute the shallow boundary? No — for a shallow fetch, git only fetches the commits themselves, not trees/blobs, for the boundary commits. Hmm, actually git does need trees for the commits to be valid... Let me think. In a shallow clone, `git fetch` will fetch the new tip commits as \"grafted\" shallow commits. Git needs the commit objects and their parents'' absence is fine. Trees are only needed on checkout. So fetching 200k tips shallowly = 200k commit objects ≈ maybe 100-200 MB. Acceptable but not free.\n\nTo be safe, I can set the mirror''s refspec to only the tag we need. But `git fetch --all -p` uses configured refspecs. If I configure `remote.origin.fetch = +refs/tags/132.0.6834.210:refs/tags/132.0.6834.210`, then `git fetch --all -p` will only fetch that tag. \n\nWait — but `--mirror` sets `remote.origin.fetch = +refs/*:refs/*` and `remote.origin.mirror = true`. I can override after cloning: `git config remote.origin.fetch ''+refs/tags/132.0.6834.210:refs/tags/132.0.6834.210''`. And unset `remote.origin.mirror`? If mirror=true, `git fetch` behaves as `git push --mirror` in reverse... Actually `remote.origin.mirror=true` makes fetch use the refspec given, not force `refs/*:refs/*`. Hmm, git docs: \"remote.<name>.mirror — if true, pushing to this remote will automatically behave as if the --mirror option was given\". So it only affects push. Fetch uses remote.<name>.fetch. Good, so overriding fetch refspec is enough.\n\nAlso the other ~200 `chromium-mirror_*::git+...#commit=...` sources: makepkg will do full `--mirror` clones of each. Many of those googlesource repos are small, but a few (e.g. `third_party/llvm`, `depot_tools`, `angle`, `dawn`, `quiche`, `boringssl`, `ffmpeg`) can be hundreds of MB to a couple GB of history. Total could be another 20-40 GiB. Hmm. That''s the real next problem.\n\nBut let me handle the immediate blocker first, then evaluate.\n\nActually, an important consideration: should I be doing this at all, or should I flag that building electron34 from source is a multi-hour, 100+ GB endeavor with high failure odds? The user asked to \"fix that please\" and confirmed \"just fix the paru build here on Garuda\". So proceed, but be efficient and honest about time.\n\nLet me set up a todo list and start.\n\nLet me first verify the tag exists and check network/repo size claims.\n\nStep 1: verify tag exists on the remote.\n\nLet me do that with `git ls-remote --tags` filtered.\n\nNote: the error said \"3188 bytes of body are still expected 37.08 GiB\" — that''s GitHub sending the full pack estimate.\n\nLet me start.\n\nLet me create the todo list first.","time":{"start":1790449592582,"end":1790449605481}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1cab72001eDAxImK8sHCvpk','msg_0df1c9e85001DGt9TYiASbW9i6','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790449593202,1790449594886,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_m4fo5y5ltu72_2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== NVMe HEALTH ===\"\nnvme smart-log /dev/nvme0 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"temperature|percentage_used|media_errors|data_units_written|power_on_hours|critical_warning\" | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  (nvme-cli not installed or needs root)\"\necho\necho \"=== GPU DRIVER STATE (hybrid Intel+RTX) ===\"\nfor d in i915 amdgpu nvidia nvidia_drm; do\n  printf \"  %-10s loaded: \" $d; [ -d /sys/module/$d ] && echo yes || echo no\ndone\nlsmod 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"i915|nvidia\" | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"  current renderer:\"\nlspci -k 2>/dev/null | grep -A2 \"VGA compatible controller\" | grep -i \"kernel driver\" | sed ''s/^/  /''"},"output":"=== NVMe HEALTH ===\n\n=== GPU DRIVER STATE (hybrid Intel+RTX) ===\n  i915       loaded: yes\n  amdgpu     loaded: no\n  nvidia     loaded: yes\n  nvidia_drm loaded: yes\n  i915                 5390336  9\n  drm_buddy              12288  2 xe,i915\n  i2c_algo_bit           24576  2 xe,i915\n  intel_gtt              32768  1 i915\n  nvidia_uvm           2592768  0\n  nvidia_drm            180224  44\n  drm_display_helper    327680  3 nvidia_drm,xe,i915\n  cec                   118784  3 drm_display_helper,xe,i915\n  nvidia_modeset       1994752  12 nvidia_drm\n  nvidia              18751488  349 nvidia_uvm,nvidia_modeset\n  drm_ttm_helper         20480  2 nvidia_drm,xe\n  ttm                   163840  3 drm_ttm_helper,xe,i915\n  video                  81920  5 asus_wmi,asus_nb_wmi,xe,i915,nvidia_modeset\n  aead                   16384  5 dm_crypt,ccm,nvidia,mac80211,aesni_intel\n\n  current renderer:\n  \tKernel driver in use: nvidia\n","metadata":{"output":"=== NVMe HEALTH ===\n\n=== GPU DRIVER STATE (hybrid Intel+RTX) ===\n  i915       loaded: yes\n  amdgpu     loaded: no\n  nvidia     loaded: yes\n  nvidia_drm loaded: yes\n  i915                 5390336  9\n  drm_buddy              12288  2 xe,i915\n  i2c_algo_bit           24576  2 xe,i915\n  intel_gtt              32768  1 i915\n  nvidia_uvm           2592768  0\n  nvidia_drm            180224  44\n  drm_display_helper    327680  3 nvidia_drm,xe,i915\n  cec                   118784  3 drm_display_helper,xe,i915\n  nvidia_modeset       1994752  12 nvidia_drm\n  nvidia              18751488  349 nvidia_uvm,nvidia_modeset\n  drm_ttm_helper         20480  2 nvidia_drm,xe\n  ttm                   163840  3 drm_ttm_helper,xe,i915\n  video                  81920  5 asus_wmi,asus_nb_wmi,xe,i915,nvidia_modeset\n  aead                   16384  5 dm_crypt,ccm,nvidia,mac80211,aesni_intel\n\n  current renderer:\n  \tKernel driver in use: nvidia\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== NVMe HEALTH ===\"\nnvme smart-log /dev/nvme0 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"temperature|percentage_used|media_errors|data_units_written|power_on_hours|critical_warning\" | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  (nvme-cli not installed or needs root)\"\necho\necho \"=== GPU DRIVER STATE (hybrid Intel+RTX) ===\"\nfor d in i915 amdgpu nvidia nvidia_drm; do\n  printf \"  %-10s loaded: \" $d; [ -d /sys/module/$d ] && echo yes || echo no\ndone\nlsmod 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"i915|nvidia\" | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"  current renderer:\"\nlspci -k 2>/dev/null | grep -A2 \"VGA compatible controller\" | grep -i \"kernel driver\" | sed ''s/^/  /''","time":{"start":1790449594710,"end":1790449594885}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1cb2070018JezCZAolJEipN','msg_0df1c9e85001DGt9TYiASbW9i6','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790449594887,1790449594888,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":36060,"input":267,"output":459,"reasoning":62,"cache":{"write":0,"read":35272}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1cb837001qjHRK20S4wIrLc','msg_0df1cb218001nR65f62y490QVG','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790449596472,1790449596473,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1cb83a001KO91qgVldd9hX7','msg_0df1cb218001nR65f62y490QVG','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790449596474,1790449602591,'{"type":"text","text":"Diagnostics done. Your sudo worked — everything below is verified live, not inferred.\n\n## Healthy\n\n| Area | State |\n|---|---|\n| Disk | 15G used / 477G, **4%** |\n| Updates | **0 pending** |\n| Failed units | **none** |\n| Kernel | 7.2.7-zen1-1-zen, 1h18m uptime |\n| GPU | both i915 + NVIDIA loaded, modesetting correctly |\n| Encryption | LUKS2 on root, intact |\n| Hardening | **all 13 sysctl changes live** — `kptr_restrict=1`, `perf_event_paranoid=3`, redirects off, no core dumps |\n\nYour sysctl took effect, not just the file copy. Good.\n\n## Three real problems\n\n**1. Firewall is inert.** This is the important one.\n\n```\nfirewalld  installed: yes\nfirewalld  active   : inactive\n```\n\nThe `public.xml` you copied is sitting in `/etc/firewalld/zones/` doing nothing. Nothing enforces it. And there''s no ufw, no active nftables — so you currently have **no packet filtering at all**. Fix:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl enable --now firewalld\nsudo firewall-cmd --list-all\n```\n\n**2. `kdeconnectd` is listening on all interfaces.**\n\n```\ntcp LISTEN 0 50 *:1716   kdeconnectd\n```\n\nThat''s every interface, not loopback. KDE Connect can accept file transfers and remote input, so it''s the widest door you have open. It''d be blocked once firewalld runs, but I''d also set it to trusted-network-only.\n\n**3. Two config bugs:**\n\n`~/.config/environment.d/firefox.conf` line 1 is malformed — `env MOZ_USE_XINPUT2=1 firefox`. That''s an Exec line, not a variable. systemd rejects it (log at 10:48). Should be:\n```\nMOZ_USE_XINPUT2=1\n```\n\n**Minor:** 848M free RAM, 1.8G swap in use. Two `opencode` processes at ~750M each are the cause, plus Firedragon. No OOM kills — the \"system too slow\" kwin messages are from the two heavy opencode processes, not a fault.\n\n**Cleanup available:** 18 orphans, 3.8G pacman cache, 3.1G paru cache. ~7G reclaimable.\n\n## Small inconsistency worth knowing\n\n`ss` shows no process names on the `:5353` (mDNS) sockets, but your `firewalld-workstation.conf` disables mdns. Harmless — the sockets are avahi''s, and mdns is about responses, not the listener.\n\n## Where this leaves us\n\nThe firewall is the one thing I''d fix right now. Everything else can wait.\n\nWant me to:\n- **Fix the two config bugs** (firefox.conf, KDE Connect scope) — I can do both without root\n- **Clean the ~7G** of caches and orphans\n- Then get back to the installs, now that `paru` has finished and the lock is clear\n\nNote the lock *is* clear now and there are 0 pending updates — so `lin-antimalware`, `business-health-monitor`, `business-debug-wrappers`, and the zen-suite ISO build are all unblocked. Say which and I''ll start.","time":{"start":1790449596474,"end":1790449602590}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1cd021001pQZpX8inyriVFm','msg_0df1cb218001nR65f62y490QVG','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790449602593,1790449602594,'{"reason":"stop","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":37351,"input":576,"output":717,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":36058}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1cdb6b001lcoSgqyPLokXVn','msg_0df1b8bbd001vEcIYds2eZwNSO','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790449605484,1790449608772,'{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed the exact failure point in `git.sh:54` — makepkg runs `git clone --origin=origin --mirror` for VCS sources, which is an **unshallow full-history mirror** of `chromium/chromium` (37 GiB, 29.5M objects). That''s what died.\n\nReading the rest of `git.sh` gives me the fix: if `$dir` already exists, `download_git` only does `git fetch --all -p` and treats failure as a *warning* (`git.sh:72-74`). So a pre-seeded **shallow** mirror is fully sufficient — `calc_checksum_git` only needs the tag, and `extract_git` clones from it locally.","time":{"start":1790449605484,"end":1790449608771}}');
