INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df1cf96d001txA1tj8335WdKF','msg_0df1cf3dd001Es730SIqHctd8Z','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790449613165,1790449624229,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Tag exists: `refs/tags/132.0.6834.210` → commit `5c0cb964bca15fcf41718d54f4b8d70d6b9079de`.\n\nNote: `git ls-remote --tags` output shows only one line for that tag pattern — meaning it''s a lightweight tag pointing directly at the commit (no `^{}` dereference line). Good, so `git tag -l --format=''%(tag)''` will return the same name, and the forged-tag check in `git.sh:133` passes.\n\n164 chromium-mirror_* DEPS sources + 1 main chromium source = 165 git sources. Plus the electron repo.\n\nNow let me do the shallow mirror clone. Considerations:\n- `git clone --mirror --depth 1 --branch 132.0.6834.210` — with `--mirror` and `--branch`, does it work? `--mirror` implies `--bare`. `--depth 1` with `--branch <tag>` should work. Let me try.\n\nActually there''s a known wrinkle: `--mirror` sets refspec `+refs/*:refs/*`, and combined with `--depth 1 --branch`, git does a shallow fetch of just that branch. That works.\n\nBut careful: with `--mirror`, `git clone` fetches ALL refs in the initial fetch? Let me think — `git clone --mirror` runs `git fetch` with the mirror refspec, which would try to get all refs. Combined with `--depth 1`, it becomes a shallow fetch of all refs. That could be 200k+ branch tips at depth 1 → that could be big (each tip needs its commit + trees? no, shallow = commits only... but actually for a shallow clone git does need trees/blobs for checkout of the branch).\n\nHmm, risk. Safer approach: use a plain bare clone first with explicit refspec, then set it up to look like a mirror.\n\nBest approach:\n```\ngit init --bare chromium-mirror\ncd chromium-mirror\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/chromium/chromium.git\ngit config remote.origin.fetch ''+refs/tags/132.0.6834.210:refs/tags/132.0.6834.210''\ngit fetch --depth 1 --no-tags origin tag 132.0.6834.210\n```\n\nHmm, `git fetch --depth 1 origin tag <name>` fetches just that tag shallowly. But for a mirror, makepkg''s `extract_git` does `git clone -s $dir chromium-mirror` then checks out `132.0.6834.210`. Wait — ref = fragment value = `132.0.6834.210`, and it checks out `-B makepkg 132.0.6834.210`. From a `-s` clone of the mirror, the local branch... `git clone -s` of a bare mirror clones the HEAD and all branches. If the mirror''s HEAD points to master but we only have the tag, then clone -s will create origin/HEAD etc. The checkout `git checkout --force --no-track -B makepkg 132.0.6834.210` — resolving `132.0.6834.210` as a local ref. In the mirror, the tag ref `refs/tags/132.0.6834.210` exists. When you `git clone` a repo, tags come along. So `132.0.6834.210` resolves as a tag → works.\n\nAlso `git tag -l --format=''%(tag)'' 132.0.6834.210` must equal `132.0.6834.210`. Since it''s a lightweight tag pointing at a commit, `%(tag)` gives the tag name `132.0.6834.210`. Good — matches.\n\nBut careful: in a bare repo, `git tag -l` works fine.\n\nAlso, `extract_git` checks `[[ -d \"${dir##*/}\" ]]` in $srcdir — if `src/chromium-mirror` already exists it does `git fetch` in it. There''s a stale empty `src/` dir. Fine.\n\nNow, another concern: `git clone -s` (shared) from a shallow repo. Shared clone alternates pointing to a shallow object store — that works, but the resulting clone is also considered shallow? `git clone -s` from a shallow repo: git will set up alternates and copy the shallow file. Should be OK.\n\nHmm, actually there''s a real risk: cloning a shallow repo locally. Git supports it; the local clone will also be shallow. Then `git checkout` at the tag works since we have the full tree/blobs for that commit (depth 1 → we have the complete tree).\n\nWait — do we have the blobs? A `--depth 1` clone of a repo, git fetches the full tree+blobs for the tip commit (otherwise checkout couldn''t work). Yes, depth-1 clone = complete snapshot. So checkout works.\n\nAlternatively use `--filter=blob:none` to defer blobs, but then checkout would lazily fetch them — messy with the shared alternates. Just use plain `--depth 1`.\n\nEstimated size: chromium source tree at one commit ≈ 1.5-2 GB (packed maybe ~1.2 GB). Plus we''ll need the working copy extracted (another ~3-4 GB uncompressed in src/). Disk has 462 GB free. Fine.\n\nLet me also think: do we even need `--mirror`? makepkg''s `download_git` at line 52 checks `[[ ! -d \"$dir\" ]] || dir_is_empty \"$dir\"`. Our dir exists and is non-empty → skips clone. Then line 65: `git -c safe.bareRepository=all config --get remote.origin.url` must equal `https://github.com/chromium/chromium.git` (url with `#tag=...` stripped, and `git+` stripped). So `remote.origin.url` must be exactly `https://github.com/chromium/chromium.git`. Good.\n\nThen `git fetch --all -p`. With my restricted refspec pointing at just the tag, this fetches only that tag. Cheap.\n\nNote: `url=${url%%.git}` strips trailing `.git` for comparison, and same for remote_url. So even if it had `.git` it''d be fine.\n\nLet me now execute. I''ll do it with explicit steps and verify.\n\nOne more: makepkg sets `GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/etc/makepkg.d/gitconfig`. Let me check that file — it might have settings affecting us.\n\nLet me check it and then run the clone.\n\nLet me run the clone in background? It could take a while (~1.5 GB at 10 MB/s ≈ 2.5 min). Timeout default 120s, so I''ll set a longer timeout. Let me use 600000 ms (10 min).\n\nLet me first check /etc/makepkg.d/gitconfig.","time":{"start":1790449613165,"end":1790449624228}}');
