INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df2e92480017XHCPm6BMKAEeG','msg_0df2e7fed001lzIbXI6L260Pz3','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790450766408,1790450766409,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df2e924a001agCMybiw0LOTMe','msg_0df2e7fed001lzIbXI6L260Pz3','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790450766410,1790450782067,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the full picture. Let me address the user''s actual request: \"lets also ensure shield kitty doesnt display against me\"\n\nThe detector fires an alert when journal lines match RULES. False-positive sources that would fire \"against the user\" (their own legitimate activity):\n\n1. **`usb_attach` rule** — `usb .*(?:new high-speed USB device|Product:)` at severity \"warning\". This fires on ANY USB device attach: plugging in a keyboard, mouse, USB drive, webcam, phone. That is **routine user hardware activity**, not an intrusion. This is a big false positive source. And since `most_severe` returns \"warning\" if no critical, and `fire_alert` fires on ANY events, plugging in a USB mouse pops the fullscreen nyan cat. That''s almost certainly what the user experiences as \"displaying against me.\"\n\n2. **`login_failure` rule** — `pam_unix\\([^)]*:auth\\): authentication failure`. This can fire on legitimate local auth attempts too (e.g., unlocking, keyring unlock, gdm/pam greeter). Notably, pam_faillock and pam_unix auth failures happen for *any* wrong password locally too.\n\n3. **`faillock_denial`** — fires on \"Unlocking account \\S+\" which the admin doing `faillock --unlock` would trigger. And `pam_faillock\\(authfail\\)` — that fires when the *local admin''s own* account gets locked out after 10 bad attempts, which is self-inflicted. That''s \"displaying against me\" in the truest sense.\n\n4. **`sudo_failure`** — `sudo:.*incorrect password` fires on the user''s own typo''d sudo password. Very common, and it IS the user''s own action. Note the `sudo_unattended` rule already has a `is_local_admin_actor` guard, but `sudo_failure` (incorrect password) has NO actor guard. So the user''s own sudo typo → fullscreen critical alert.\n\n5. **`sddm_failure`** — regex `(sddm|lightdm|gdm).*(failed|auth|login).*fail` — loose; could match benign lines.\n\nSo there are multiple genuine false-positive paths, and the most likely culprits for the user''s complaint are:\n- `usb_attach` (any USB device — the user plugs in peripherals)\n- `sudo_failure` (own sudo password typo)\n- `login_failure` via pam_unix auth failure on local actions\n\nThe cleanest fix that honors \"don''t display against me\" while keeping security value:\n- Add a self-exclusion for the local user''s own sudo failures (actor guard, mirroring `sudo_unattended`)\n- Make `usb_attach` not fire a fullscreen alert by default (or make it log-only / not display)\n- Add a \"log-only\" mode for warnings so only critical events get the fullscreen nyan cat\n\nDesign decision needed: should I (a) just fix the false positives, or (b) add a way to suppress display entirely? The user said \"ensure shield kitty doesnt display against me\" — they want no display *against them* (false positives), not no display at all (presumably real intrusions should still alert).\n\nSo the right fix: suppress false positives from the user''s own legitimate activity, keep genuine intrusion alerts.\n\nConcrete changes I''d make:\n1. Guard `sudo_failure` with the local-user actor check (own typo → no alert). But careful: if an attacker gets the user''s sudo password wrong from a remote shell, the actor would still be the local user... Hmm. Actually `sudo: <user> : 1 incorrect password` — actor is the user. A remote attacker who somehow has a shell as the user and typos sudo would be the same actor. But that''s a much weaker signal. The tradeoff: user''s own typos are frequent. Given \"don''t display against me,\" suppressing own sudo typos is what they want. I could downgrade it to log-only instead of fullscreen.\n\nBetter approach: introduce severity-aware display. `warning` severity → log only, no fullscreen alert. `critical` → fullscreen. And downgrade the noisy rules from \"critical\" to \"warning\":\n- `usb_attach` is already \"warning\" but still triggers a fullscreen alert because `fire_alert` fires on any events. So making warning = log-only fixes usb_attach.\n- `sudo_failure` own-actor → skip or log-only.\n\nThat gives a clean, principled fix:\n- `fire_alert` only for critical; log-only for warnings\n- own-actor guard on sudo_failure\n- keep everything else\n\nLet me verify: currently `fire_alert(events)` is called whenever `events` is non-empty, and `most_severe` returns critical if ANY is critical else warning. So a lone USB attach → severity \"warning\" → still fires fullscreen. That''s the bug. Fix: only fire display for critical.\n\nAlso need to fix the **broken installed systemd unit** (stale path) — otherwise the service can''t restart and, more importantly, the user has a running zombie. Actually, for the user''s request (\"don''t display against me\"), the stale unit matters: on next login, the service will fail to start (path missing), so it won''t display at all. But that''s not a fix, it''s breakage. I should fix the unit to the correct path so behavior is intentional.\n\nLet me ask the user before making the changes, since there''s a design choice (log-only vs suppress entirely). Actually, the user''s request is fairly clear and I''ve been asking a lot. But this is a security tool — changing detection logic has real tradeoffs, and \"display against me\" has multiple valid interpretations. Let me present the findings and a recommended change, and ask ONE focused question about the policy.\n\nHmm, the user has been asked a lot of questions. But modifying a security detector''s rules without confirming intent is worse. Let me be efficient: present the concrete false-positive sources I found, state my recommended fix precisely, and ask for a go/no-go with the one real design choice.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — I can make this decision defensibly. The user said \"ensure shield kitty doesnt display against me.\" The minimal, safe, correct interpretation: don''t fire the fullscreen alert on the user''s own benign activity; keep firing on real attacks. The cleanest implementation is the severity split (warning = log only). That''s a clear improvement and preserves security.\n\nBut there''s a real question: do they want USB attach to still be *logged* and visible somehow, or fully suppressed? Log-only is strictly better than fullscreen. I''ll go with log-only for warnings + own-actor sudo guard, and mention it.\n\nGiven the volume of questions, let me just do the well-justified fix and report, offering the alternative. Actually no — let me ask, because \"doesn''t display against me\" could mean they want it fully off, and there''s a legitimate reason to keep it on. One focused question, then act.\n\nLet me first acknowledge the handoff (required) and present findings, then ask.\n\nLet me be concise but complete. Let me combine the handoff acknowledgment + findings + one question.\n\nLet me quickly test what the current rules would fire on in the recent journal, to ground the claim. That''s valuable evidence. Let me run the selftest and also check the last 10 min for matches.\n\nLet me do that to give concrete evidence.","time":{"start":1790450766410,"end":1790450782066}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df2e94c5001P9Pm7at0Iffvfq','msg_0df2e8bf8001k7CMMUyY2coC3h','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790450767046,1790450767047,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df2e94c80010k6hZkEXVVR7gr','msg_0df2e8bf8001k7CMMUyY2coC3h','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790450767048,1790450770939,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now let me find the actual false positives empirically. Let me run the selftest and also test the rules against known-benign journal lines that occur on this machine.\n\nKey candidate false positive I can see from reading: the `sudo_unattended` rule. The `is_local_admin_actor` only fires for `sudo_unattended`. But there''s an important bug: the actor regex `sudo:\\s*(?P<actor>\\w+)\\s*:` — journald sudo lines look like:\n`sudo: livings : a password is required` — actor = \"livings\" ✓\nBut ALSO: `sudo: pam_unix(sudo:session): session opened for user root(0) by user=livings` — doesn''t match \"a password is required\".\n\nAnd critically: **the service runs under systemd with a minimal environment. `os.environ.get(\"USER\")` may be UNSET in a systemd user service**, falling back to `getpass.getuser()` which returns the real username from /etc/passwd — that works. OK so LOCAL_USER should resolve.\n\nBUT — there''s a subtle and serious issue: `getpass.getuser()` checks LOGNAME, USER, LNAME, USERNAME env vars first, then falls back to pwd. Under systemd, USER is typically set for user services. So probably fine.\n\nNow the real question: what actually fires against the user? Let me just run the selftest and also scan a wider window.\n\nLet me run:\n1. `--selftest`\n2. Scan last 2000 lines over a longer window and see what classifies\n\nLet me also test specific known-benign lines:\n- `sudo: livings : a password is required` (own sudo — should be suppressed)\n- `sudo: root : a password is required`?\n\nLet me empirically run the classifier against real journal lines. Best: run a python snippet that imports the module and classifies a large window, printing the actual matching lines. That tells me exactly what would display.\n\nLet me do that — it''s the most direct evidence.\n\nLet me run the selftest first plus a wide classification dump.\n\nNote: I must be careful — running the watch script in foreground would launch alerts. `--selftest` does NOT launch alerts (it only calls classify). Good, safe.\n\nLet me run selftest and a wide scan.\n\nLet me write a small python harness that imports the classify function and dumps matches over a big window.\n\nThis is the key diagnostic. Let me do it.\n\nNote the module has `if __name__ == \"__main__\"` guard, so importing is safe.\n\nLet me do it.","time":{"start":1790450767048,"end":1790450770938}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df2ea3fc0013pSaGol6DVNXo3','msg_0df2e8bf8001k7CMMUyY2coC3h','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790450770940,1790450772759,'{"type":"text","text":"Now let me find what''s actually firing against you, rather than guess. Importing the classifier (the `__main__` guard makes this safe — no alert window will launch):","time":{"start":1790450770940,"end":1790450772758}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df2ea63e001OJfCP8sY2hyALK','msg_0df2e8bf8001k7CMMUyY2coC3h','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790450771518,1790450772926,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_c3mqbr53itdy_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd ~/Projects/shield-kitty && python3 - <<''EOF''\nimport importlib.util, subprocess, collections\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"sk\", \"shield-kitty-watch.py\")\nm = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(m)\nprint(\"LOCAL_USER resolved to:\", repr(m.LOCAL_USER))\nprint()\n# wide window, 6000 lines\nout = subprocess.run([\"journalctl\",\"--no-pager\",\"-q\",\"-n\",\"6000\",\"-o\",\"short-iso\"],\n                     capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.splitlines()\nprint(\"scanned\", len(out), \"journal lines\\n\")\nhits = collections.defaultdict(list)\nfor line in out:\n    for name, pat, label, sev in m.RULES:\n        mm = pat.search(line)\n        if not mm: continue\n        if name == \"sudo_unattended\" and m.is_local_admin_actor(mm): continue\n        hits[f\"{sev:8} {name}\"].append(line.strip())\nfor k, v in sorted(hits.items(), key=lambda x: -len(x[1])):\n    print(f\"### {k}  -> {len(v)} hits\")\n    for s in v[:2]:\n        print(\"    \", s[:150])\n    print()\nif not hits: print(\"NO HITS in 6000 lines\")\nEOF","timeout":120000},"output":"LOCAL_USER resolved to: ''livings''\n\nscanned 6457 journal lines\n\n### warning  usb_attach  -> 8 hits\n     2026-09-26T10:48:05-07:00 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc kernel: usb usb1: Product: xHCI Host Controller\n     2026-09-26T10:48:05-07:00 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc kernel: usb usb2: Product: xHCI Host Controller\n\n### critical login_failure  -> 1 hits\n     2026-09-26T12:23:40-07:00 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc livings[186777]: sshd[99999]: Failed password for invalid user root from 198.51.100.42 p\n\n### critical faillock_denial  -> 1 hits\n     2026-09-26T12:23:40-07:00 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc livings[186778]: pam_faillock: User livings (uid=1000) tries to authenticate but locked \n\n","metadata":{"output":"LOCAL_USER resolved to: ''livings''\n\nscanned 6457 journal lines\n\n### warning  usb_attach  -> 8 hits\n     2026-09-26T10:48:05-07:00 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc kernel: usb usb1: Product: xHCI Host Controller\n     2026-09-26T10:48:05-07:00 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc kernel: usb usb2: Product: xHCI Host Controller\n\n### critical login_failure  -> 1 hits\n     2026-09-26T12:23:40-07:00 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc livings[186777]: sshd[99999]: Failed password for invalid user root from 198.51.100.42 p\n\n### critical faillock_denial  -> 1 hits\n     2026-09-26T12:23:40-07:00 living-asustufdashf15fx517zcfx517zc livings[186778]: pam_faillock: User livings (uid=1000) tries to authenticate but locked \n\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd ~/Projects/shield-kitty && python3 - <<''EOF''\nimport importlib.util, subprocess, collections\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\"sk\", \"shield-kitty-watch.py\")\nm = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec); spec.loader.exec_module(m)\nprint(\"LOCAL_USER resolved to:\", repr(m.LOCAL_USER))\nprint()\n# wide window, 6000 lines\nout = subprocess.run([\"journalctl\",\"--no-pager\",\"-q\",\"-n\",\"6000\",\"-o\",\"short-iso\"],\n                     capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.splitlines()\nprint(\"scanned\", len(out), \"journal lines\\n\")\nhits = collections.defaultdict(list)\nfor line in out:\n    for name, pat, label, sev in m.RULES:\n        mm = pat.search(line)\n        if not mm: continue\n        if name == \"sudo_unattended\" and m.is_local_admin_actor(mm): continue\n        hits[f\"{sev:8} {name}\"].append(line.strip())\nfor k, v in sorted(hits.items(), key=lambda x: -len(x[1])):\n    print(f\"### {k}  -> {len(v)} hits\")\n    for s in v[:2]:\n        print(\"    \", s[:150])\n    print()\nif not hits: print(\"NO HITS in 6000 lines\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1790450772752,"end":1790450772926}}}');
