INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df340734001FPs0rVTUj6DXM6','msg_0df340066001OJWmHmGT7iIyKy','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451124021,1790451124021,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df340736001IgVw4pULwlCP79','msg_0df340066001OJWmHmGT7iIyKy','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451124022,1790451126751,'{"type":"text","text":"Config ready. It needs root, so:\n\n```bash\nsudo cp ~/Projects/zen-hardening/faillock.conf /etc/security/faillock.conf\n```\n\n**First: I was wrong.** I told you twice that faillock had no \"always allow admin\" directive. `admin_group` does exactly that — I should have checked the man page properly instead of asserting it didn''t exist. My earlier grep for valid directives missed it because I was matching the wrong section.\n\n**What it does:** members of `wheel` are handled exactly like the root account. Since `even_deny_root` isn''t set, root is exempt from lockout — so `livings` now is too. Fail-lock cannot lock you out.\n\n**Verified:** `admin_group` is compiled into your `/usr/lib/security/pam_faillock.so`, and `wheel:x:998:livings` confirms you''re in the group.\n\n**Net effect on your account:** faillock now provides no lockout protection for you at all. `deny = 10` and `unlock_time = 30` still apply to every non-wheel user, and `audit = silent` still logs unknown-username attempts so brute-force enumeration stays visible in the journal.\n\nAgainst a remote attacker guessing *your* password specifically, the remaining controls are password strength and the firewall. I''ve made that argument twice and won''t repeat it — it''s what you asked for three times, and it''s a supported pam_faillock feature, not a hack.\n\nBackups: `faillock.conf.1800s.bak` (30-min unlock), `faillock.conf.pre-admin.bak` (no admin_group).","time":{"start":1790451124022,"end":1790451126750}}');
