INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df347142001Jh6UJ4Iq2PNsjK','msg_0df347141001fPRx5EwdfHrpP9','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451151174,1790451151175,'{"type":"text","text":"perfect thats what i want so even if someone tries to bruteforce my system they are not recognized as admin"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df34770e001LzJpBIxFb19you','msg_0df347160001pOA3Jt4Tbttewa','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451152654,1790451152656,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df3477120016gMUJPeHTm4hd8','msg_0df347160001pOA3Jt4Tbttewa','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451152658,1790451160228,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user says \"perfect thats what i want so even if someone tries to bruteforce my system they are not recognized as admin\"\n\nThis is a misunderstanding of what `admin_group` does, and I should correct it clearly because it''s a security-relevant misunderstanding.\n\nWhat `admin_group = wheel` does: it makes members of wheel (i.e., livings) be treated like root by faillock — exempt from lockout. It does NOT do anything about how an attacker is \"recognized.\" It doesn''t change what happens during authentication for the attacker.\n\nThe user''s mental model seems to be: \"fail_lock will treat an attacker as admin.\" That''s not what admin_group does. admin_group says: YOUR account is admin, so don''t lock it out. It has nothing to do with recognizing attackers.\n\nSo the user has a misunderstanding. The actual situation:\n- If an attacker brute-forces your `livings` account, `admin_group=wheel` makes it *easier* for them, because your account is never locked out. That''s the opposite of \"attackers are not recognized as admin.\"\n- What actually stops an attacker from being \"recognized as admin\" is: they need the password, and they need to be in the `wheel` group (which requires root to add them — you can''t self-escalate). Membership in `wheel` is the actual admin determination, and that''s governed by /etc/group, not faillock.\n\nSo there are two distinct concepts being conflated:\n1. **Authorization** (is this user an admin?) — determined by group membership in `/etc/group` (`wheel`), and sudoers. An attacker who guesses your password logs in as `livings`, who IS in wheel → they get your admin privileges. `admin_group` doesn''t help with that at all; it makes it easier.\n2. **Rate limiting / lockout** (faillock) — `admin_group` exempts you from this.\n\nThis is important to correct clearly. The user thinks they''ve hardened against brute force; in fact for their own account they''ve removed the lockout protection. I already flagged the tradeoff, but the user has now stated a belief that is factually wrong about the mechanism, and if they believe it, they may make worse decisions (e.g., think they can use a weak password now).\n\nI should correct this plainly and concisely. Not preachy — I''ve made the tradeoff argument already and said I wouldn''t repeat it. But this is a *different* point: it''s a factual error about what the directive does, not a re-argument of risk. I should correct it because leaving it would be misleading.\n\nKey facts to convey:\n- `admin_group = wheel` exempts YOU from lockout. It does nothing about recognizing attackers.\n- Whether someone is an admin is determined by `/etc/group` wheel membership + sudoers, not faillock.\n- If an attacker guesses your password, they log in as `livings`, who is in `wheel` → they have your admin rights. Nothing in faillock stops that.\n- `admin_group` actively makes password guessing against your account easier (no lockout).\n- What actually determines \"attacker is not admin\": the password, and that you can''t add yourself to wheel without root.\n\nI should offer the thing that DOES address their actual goal: fail2ban (rate limiting by IP, works regardless of account), which is the right tool for \"bruteforce\" protection. That''s genuinely the answer to \"even if someone tries to bruteforce my system.\" fail2ban bans the IP, not the account. That works for all accounts including exempt ones.\n\nLet me be brief and clear. And offer fail2ban.\n\nLet me verify the group/sudo facts quickly to be accurate, then respond. Actually I already know: livings is in wheel, sudo works with password. Let me just confirm sudoers %wheel rule to be precise about who gets admin.\n\nLet me check quickly.","time":{"start":1790451152658,"end":1790451160227}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df3494a5001Qg1K3uLMSixpqZ','msg_0df347160001pOA3Jt4Tbttewa','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451160229,1790451161181,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_2insnvzhbr4g_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== what actually makes someone an admin on this box? ===\"\ngrep -E \"^%wheel|^%sudo\" /etc/sudoers 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"  -> admin = membership in ''wheel'' group. Nothing to do with faillock.\"\necho\necho \"=== is fail2ban available? (the tool that actually stops bruteforce) ===\"\npacman -Q fail2ban 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  not installed\""},"output":"=== what actually makes someone an admin on this box? ===\n\n  -> admin = membership in ''wheel'' group. Nothing to do with faillock.\n\n=== is fail2ban available? (the tool that actually stops bruteforce) ===\n","metadata":{"output":"=== what actually makes someone an admin on this box? ===\n\n  -> admin = membership in ''wheel'' group. Nothing to do with faillock.\n\n=== is fail2ban available? (the tool that actually stops bruteforce) ===\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== what actually makes someone an admin on this box? ===\"\ngrep -E \"^%wheel|^%sudo\" /etc/sudoers 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"  -> admin = membership in ''wheel'' group. Nothing to do with faillock.\"\necho\necho \"=== is fail2ban available? (the tool that actually stops bruteforce) ===\"\npacman -Q fail2ban 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  not installed\"","time":{"start":1790451161132,"end":1790451161180}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df34985f001nA5MUEqnuoUq4j','msg_0df347160001pOA3Jt4Tbttewa','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451161183,1790451161185,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":107841,"input":34,"output":143,"reasoning":807,"cache":{"write":0,"read":106857}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df349f71001355akkRjV4zwZm','msg_0df349874001Ub52XuRSD1xyLe','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451162993,1790451162994,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df349f730014o9HE5IlVjpYKS','msg_0df349874001Ub52XuRSD1xyLe','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451162995,1790451164327,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_bh3a4vv78nel_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== sudoers: who can sudo? ===\"\ngrep -rE \"^[^#].*wheel\" /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/ 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  (reading /etc/sudoers.d)\"\nsudo -n -l 2>&1 | grep -iE \"may run|wheel\" | head -3 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== fail2ban availability ===\"\npacman -Si fail2ban 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"^Repository|^Version\" | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  not in repos\""},"output":"=== sudoers: who can sudo? ===\n\n=== fail2ban availability ===\n  Repository      : extra\n  Version         : 1.1.1-1\n","metadata":{"output":"=== sudoers: who can sudo? ===\n\n=== fail2ban availability ===\n  Repository      : extra\n  Version         : 1.1.1-1\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== sudoers: who can sudo? ===\"\ngrep -rE \"^[^#].*wheel\" /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/ 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  (reading /etc/sudoers.d)\"\nsudo -n -l 2>&1 | grep -iE \"may run|wheel\" | head -3 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== fail2ban availability ===\"\npacman -Si fail2ban 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"^Repository|^Version\" | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  not in repos\"","time":{"start":1790451164123,"end":1790451164326}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df34a4a8001q8NAlKkoTvq1Dh','msg_0df349874001Ub52XuRSD1xyLe','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451164328,1790451164329,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":108077,"input":66,"output":172,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":107839}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df34aabc001mRJWXmUWmFwvu4','msg_0df34a4bd001ly7kQP5MPE7V3D','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451165884,1790451165884,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df34aabd001a3M4XlMd1MQ8lF','msg_0df34a4bd001ly7kQP5MPE7V3D','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790451165886,1790451170674,'{"type":"text","text":"I need to correct the inference, because it matters for your security.\n\n**`admin_group = wheel` does the opposite of what you just described.**\n\nIt doesn''t tell faillock to distrust attackers. It tells faillock to **trust you more** — to stop locking *your* account. Its only effect is on how faillock treats members of `wheel`, which is you and nobody else. It has no bearing on how an attacker''s attempt is judged.\n\nConcretely, what happens if someone brute-forces you:\n\n```\nattacker guesses \"livings\" password\n  → logs in AS livings\n  → livings is in wheel\n  → attacker now HAS your admin rights\n  → admin_group never entered into it\n```\n\nYour admin status comes from `/etc/group`, not from faillock. Anyone who authenticates as `livings` inherits `livings`''s privileges — including sudo. Faillock counts failures; it does not classify users by trust level.\n\n**So the net effect of the config you just asked for is:** your account is now the *easiest* account on the machine to brute-force, because it never locks. An attacker gets unlimited attempts at exactly the account that holds admin rights.\n\n**What actually delivers what you want** is IP-based rate limiting, which is indifferent to which account is targeted and works regardless of faillock exemptions:\n\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S fail2ban\n```\n\nThat bans the *source IP* after repeated failures — attackers get one attempt, not thousands, and it covers every account at once including root. It''s in `extra`, version 1.1.1.\n\nI''d genuinely recommend it. It''s the correct tool for the problem you''re describing, and it doesn''t require weakening your own account''s protection.\n\n**Where that leaves us:** the `admin_group = wheel` config is written and waiting for your `sudo cp`. Nothing is installed yet — the live file still says `unlock_time = 1800` with no `admin_group`. So you can install fail2ban first and decide on the faillock change after, with no cleanup needed.\n\nWant me to install fail2ban and write a jail config for sshd, sddm, and sudo?","time":{"start":1790451165885,"end":1790451170673}}');
