INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df48a26d0012MmaK5dooAgIsg','msg_0df489d990011nj01R1XVDwdtg','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452474477,1790452478713,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Let me now explain the key correction and propose a proper architecture.\n\nKey findings:\n- Nothing installed: no tor, no torbrowser-launcher, no firefox at all\n- So \"reinstall tor\" — there was no tor. And no browser either! That''s notable — the user has no browser installed.\n- Firewall is firewalld with nftables backend (iptables v1.8.13 nf_tables)\n- nft ruleset shows 0 lines (firewalld manages its own tables)\n- DNS: 10.128.128.128 — a Tailscale-style or private DNS address. Actually 10.128.128.128 is Tailscale''s MagicDNS range. Interesting — the user has Tailscale? Let me note that. Actually that''s the Tailscale DNS address. That''s ANOTHER privacy leak: Tailscale MagicDNS.\n\nWait — this is important. If Tailscale is running with MagicDNS, that''s a network-level identity leak AND it would conflict with Tor routing. Let me check for tailscale.\n\nLet me check that, because it''s a real finding.\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Correct the misconception: don''t point Firefox at system tor for browsing. Use Tor Browser.\n2. But there IS a legitimate reason for a system tor daemon: routing non-browser traffic (package updates, git, curl) — but that''s a \"reduce tracking\" goal, not anonymity, and it breaks lots of things.\n3. \"Fastest possible anonymity AND security\" is contradictory. Be honest.\n\nLet me check Tailscale and then present a clear plan.\n\nLet me also check if there''s a browser at all — the user said \"browser interfaces\" but nothing is installed. Let me verify more broadly.\n\nLet me check.","time":{"start":1790452474477,"end":1790452478712}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df48b2fb001HNr2781pa6Kg14','msg_0df489d990011nj01R1XVDwdtg','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452478716,1790452481129,'{"type":"text","text":"Two things worth flagging before I proceed — one is a finding, the other is a correction.","time":{"start":1790452478715,"end":1790452481129}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df48b2ff001Azh5GBYk1jhn50','msg_0df489d990011nj01R1XVDwdtg','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452478719,1790452479802,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_2cua9bphzu4m_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ IS TAILSCALE RUNNING? (10.128.128.128 is Tailscale MagicDNS) ═══\"\nsystemctl is-active tailscaled 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  tailscaled: /''\npacman -Q tailscale 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  tailscale pkg: not installed\"\ntailscale status 2>/dev/null | head -8 | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  (cannot query)\"\necho\necho \"  -> if Tailscale is up, your MagicDNS + exit node can deanonymise you\"\necho \"     even through Tor, and it fights Tor routing.\""},"output":"═══ IS TAILSCALE RUNNING? (10.128.128.128 is Tailscale MagicDNS) ═══\n  tailscaled: inactive\n\n  -> if Tailscale is up, your MagicDNS + exit node can deanonymise you\n     even through Tor, and it fights Tor routing.\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ IS TAILSCALE RUNNING? (10.128.128.128 is Tailscale MagicDNS) ═══\n  tailscaled: inactive\n\n  -> if Tailscale is up, your MagicDNS + exit node can deanonymise you\n     even through Tor, and it fights Tor routing.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ IS TAILSCALE RUNNING? (10.128.128.128 is Tailscale MagicDNS) ═══\"\nsystemctl is-active tailscaled 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  tailscaled: /''\npacman -Q tailscale 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  tailscale pkg: not installed\"\ntailscale status 2>/dev/null | head -8 | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  (cannot query)\"\necho\necho \"  -> if Tailscale is up, your MagicDNS + exit node can deanonymise you\"\necho \"     even through Tor, and it fights Tor routing.\"","time":{"start":1790452479759,"end":1790452479802}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df48b712001twPAIH3WbHMP3B','msg_0df489d990011nj01R1XVDwdtg','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452479762,1790452481334,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_2cua9bphzu4m_2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ IS THERE *ANY* BROWSER INSTALLED? ═══\"\nfor p in firefox chromium chromium-browser google-chrome-stable brave vivaldi zen-browser librewolf falkon epiphany; do\n  pacman -Q \"$p\" 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''\ndone\necho \"  (empty above = no browser at all on this system)\"\necho\necho \"═══ SO ''reinstall tor'' has nothing to reinstall ═══\"\necho \"  tor: never installed. browser: never installed.\"\necho \"  This is a fresh install, not a reinstall.\""},"output":"═══ IS THERE *ANY* BROWSER INSTALLED? ═══\n  (empty above = no browser at all on this system)\n\n═══ SO ''reinstall tor'' has nothing to reinstall ═══\n  tor: never installed. browser: never installed.\n  This is a fresh install, not a reinstall.\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ IS THERE *ANY* BROWSER INSTALLED? ═══\n  (empty above = no browser at all on this system)\n\n═══ SO ''reinstall tor'' has nothing to reinstall ═══\n  tor: never installed. browser: never installed.\n  This is a fresh install, not a reinstall.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ IS THERE *ANY* BROWSER INSTALLED? ═══\"\nfor p in firefox chromium chromium-browser google-chrome-stable brave vivaldi zen-browser librewolf falkon epiphany; do\n  pacman -Q \"$p\" 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''\ndone\necho \"  (empty above = no browser at all on this system)\"\necho\necho \"═══ SO ''reinstall tor'' has nothing to reinstall ═══\"\necho \"  tor: never installed. browser: never installed.\"\necho \"  This is a fresh install, not a reinstall.\"","time":{"start":1790452481125,"end":1790452481334}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df48bd38001WpZp7t7Uo06ORP','msg_0df489d990011nj01R1XVDwdtg','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452481336,1790452481336,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":54495,"input":221,"output":352,"reasoning":360,"cache":{"write":0,"read":53562}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df48cc66001uJUX0Dc2KBC1Ra','msg_0df48bd51001GOGWIwRJw4YivB','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452485222,1790452485223,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df48cc6d001U6hp9y9JDJm3Y8','msg_0df48bd51001GOGWIwRJw4YivB','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452485229,1790452492864,'{"type":"text","text":"Tailscale is inactive — good, no exit-node conflict.\n\n## Correction first\n\n**Don''t point a normal browser at a system Tor daemon.** That gives *worse* anonymity than Tor Browser, because your real browser carries a persistent fingerprint — cookies, profile, canvas/WebGL, font list, timezone — that links every session. Tor Browser exists specifically to throw that state away each launch. The Tor Project treats browser-tor-daemon as a known weakness, not a best practice.\n\nAlso, nothing is installed — no `tor`, no `torbrowser-launcher`, and **no browser at all**. So this is a fresh install, and \"integrate into my browser\" has no existing browser to integrate with.\n\nAnd \"fastest possible anonymity *and* security\" are in tension. Tor is slow by design; speeding it up usually means weakening anonymity. I''ll optimize for anonymity.\n\n## The leak that matters most\n\nYour DNS is `10.128.128.128`. Tor Browser does **not** use your system DNS for the sites it visits, so browsing is fine — but any non-browser traffic still goes to your resolver in the clear.\n\n## Plan\n\n**Layer 1 — Tor Browser (the actual anonymity layer)**\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S torbrowser-launcher\n```\nIsolated, disposable identity. This is the correct tool for browsing.\n\n**Layer 2 — system `tor` daemon for non-browser traffic**\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S tor\nsudo systemctl enable --now tor\n```\nSOCKS on `127.0.0.1:9050`. Per-app opt-in, not blanket.\n\n**Layer 3 — hostname/MAC (from the previous task)**\n```bash\nsudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/set-anon-identity.sh\n```\n\n## What I want your call on before touching firewall rules\n\nFull transparent proxying (route *all* system traffic through Tor) is the only way to stop DNS leaks globally, but it has real costs on your setup:\n\n- Your firewalld is on the **nftables** backend, and fail2ban''s `banaction` writes firewalld rich rules. A Tor TPROXY table will fight firewalld for the same hooks, and I''ve already spent this session fixing fail2ban.\n- It breaks video calls, gaming, and some package mirrors.\n- When Tor is slow or restarting, your **entire** connectivity drops.\n- `systemd-resolved` and Tor''s DNS handling conflict; it needs careful ordering or you get boot loops.\n\nGiven you said you can rebuild from scratch, this is recoverable — but it should be a deliberate choice, not something I bolt on silently.\n\nWhich do you want?\n\n1. **Tor Browser only** — best anonymity for browsing, zero risk to the rest of the system. No firewall changes.\n2. **Tor Browser + tor daemon as SOCKS** — also covers `curl`/`git` when you opt in. No firewall changes, no risk.\n3. **Full system-wide transparent proxy** — closes DNS leaks globally, but touches firewalld/nftables and can take your networking down.\n\nI''d start with **2**. It gets you Tor without putting the connectivity you just stabilized at risk, and you can add transparent proxying later as its own deliberate change.","time":{"start":1790452485229,"end":1790452492863}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df48ea44001Km7aLLv5XSTMvw','msg_0df48bd51001GOGWIwRJw4YivB','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452492868,1790452492869,'{"reason":"stop","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":55335,"input":160,"output":682,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":54493}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a208a001ALWLKViAOpNBd6','msg_0df4a20890015raHtOc2mf5MLc','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452572301,1790452572302,'{"type":"text","text":"basically revoke incoming or remote permissions from all outside sources save needed wifi communications "}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a253c001ad7KIJ9us0pybL','msg_0df4a20a9001hhr0GrQr3xEUHW','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452573500,1790452573501,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a253e001nVHMcRhup2n86y','msg_0df4a20a9001hhr0GrQr3xEUHW','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452573502,1790452591105,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants to revoke incoming/remote permissions from all outside sources, saving only needed wifi communications.\n\nThis is a network hardening request. Let me think carefully about what they want:\n\n\"Revoke incoming or remote permissions from all outside sources, save needed wifi communications\"\n\nSo: block all inbound connections from external networks, but keep wifi working (so the machine can still get on the network, DHCP, DNS, and presumably outgoing internet).\n\nThis is a legitimate, well-defined firewall hardening task. Very defensible.\n\nCurrent state I know:\n- firewalld active, default zone `public`, wlo1 in \"USL - Public\"\n- Allowed services: ssh, dhcpv6-client\n- sshd is DISABLED and not listening\n- kdeconnectd listening on TCP/UDP *:1716, blocked by firewalld\n- fail2ban now running with banaction = firewallcmd-rich-rules\n- nftables backend (iptables v1.8.13 nf_tables)\n- nft ruleset showed 0 lines (firewalld manages its own tables)\n- Tailscale inactive\n- Tor NOT installed (from the last turn — the user hasn''t confirmed installing it)\n\nSo the task: harden the firewall so nothing inbound except what''s needed.\n\nKey decisions:\n1. What IS needed for wifi?\n   - DHCP client (UDP 67/68) — needed to get an IP\n   - DHCPv6 client — for IPv6\n   - DNS (UDP/TCP 53) to the resolver — actually this is outbound, and firewalld handles that in the forward/output sense. But if we''re blocking inbound, DNS responses come back as established/related connections which firewalld allows by default.\n   - ICMP — for PMTU (path MTU discovery). Important! Blocking ICMP breaks IPv6 and can break TLS. Should allow icmp.\n   - IPv6 NDP (neighbor discovery) — essential for IPv6 to work at all.\n\n2. What to remove:\n   - ssh service in firewalld (port 22) — sshd isn''t even running. Remove it. This is the biggest win: right now firewalld has a hole open for a service that isn''t even running.\n   - kdeconnect — currently blocked but the user may want it. It''s a LAN service. Should decide: block (safer) or allow only on trusted LAN. The user said \"revoke incoming from all outside sources\" — kdeconnect on a public network is a risk. But KDE Connect is genuinely useful and only works on LAN. Hmm. Since the wifi is a \"public\" zone (coffee shop etc.), allowing KDE Connect on public wifi is bad — anyone on the same wifi could connect. Recommend: disable kdeconnectd, or move to a \"trusted\" profile that only allows it on home network.\n\n3. Approach options:\n   - firewalld zone-level: change the zone to `block` for ingress, or set default-deny\n   - firewalld has a `block` zone that drops everything\n   - Better approach: keep `public` zone but ensure only DHCPv6 + ICMP allowed, and use a `trusted` zone for home networks\n   - Or: use rich rules to allow only what''s needed\n\nLet me think about the cleanest firewalld approach for \"block everything inbound except what''s needed for wifi\":\n\nfirewalld''s `public` zone already has a default-deny for unlisted services but the current config added ssh. Also need to consider:\n- firewalld drops unknown inbound by default (zone default is to reject/drop for unlisted)\n- So the \"hole\" is: the services explicitly allowed (ssh) + any custom rich rules\n- Plus: firewalld''s public zone allows some ICMP by default\n\nThe most robust: add a `block` policy. But that could break the session.\n\nLet me design:\n- Set default zone to something hardened\n- Remove ssh from the zone\n- Ensure dhcpv6-client allowed (needed for IPv6)\n- Allow ICMP (PMTU — critical)\n- Allow mdns? — NO, that would be an inbound hole. mDNS responses are handled as established. Actually avahi needs 5353 for mDNS. Blocking it kills .local discovery. For anonymity, blocking mDNS is BETTER (it was broadcasting your hostname). Since the user wants anonymity too, blocking mDNS aligns.\n- kdeconnect: block by default, optionally allow on a trusted zone for home\n\nLet me be careful: the user said \"save needed wifi communications\". So: keep DHCP + DNS + ICMP working. Everything else inbound = blocked.\n\nImportant nuance: blocking mDNS also helps the \"zero\" anonymity goal. And I found earlier avahi is active and broadcasting the hostname. So blocking mDNS on public wifi is a double win.\n\nLet me also consider: the user is currently connected via wifi. If I break the firewall, I could cut their session. That''s a real risk — they''re on a laptop on presumably wifi. If I lock myself out of my own network... well, they''re physically at the machine, so they can fix it. But their session might drop.\n\nLet me build this carefully with a safety net: apply changes with a scheduled rollback, or at least verify each step. Actually since they''re at the physical machine with a keyboard, a lockout is recoverable. But if they''re remote, that''s a problem. Given earlier context (Plasma Wayland, local machine, they''ve been running sudo interactively), they''re at the machine.\n\nStill, I should be careful and use a rollback timer as a safety net. That''s good practice for firewall changes.\n\nLet me plan:\n\nStep 1: Audit current inbound surface\nStep 2: Show what''s needed vs. what''s exposed\nStep 3: Build hardened config\nStep 4: Apply with auto-rollback safety net\nStep 5: Verify\n\nLet me first do a thorough audit of the current firewalld state and all listening sockets.\n\nLet me gather the full picture.\n\nI should present a plan and get confirmation before applying, given firewall changes can disconnect them. But the user has been clear they want this. Let me do the audit, prepare the exact commands, and apply with a rollback timer. Actually — let me ask for confirmation on the couple of judgment calls (kdeconnect, mDNS, ICMP) but be decisive with recommendations.\n\nLet me gather data first.\n\nNote: Tor was NOT installed — the user moved on to this request without confirming the Tor install. So tor is still not installed. I should keep that in mind (don''t assume tor is running and try to allow its ports).\n\nLet me audit.","time":{"start":1790452573502,"end":1790452591104}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a6a030019t1I5iHxqqVQYJ','msg_0df4a20a9001hhr0GrQr3xEUHW','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452591107,1790452594294,'{"type":"text","text":"Good, well-defined request. Let me audit the actual inbound surface first — I need to know what''s listening and what''s currently permitted.","time":{"start":1790452591107,"end":1790452594293}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a6b9c001RTEifhVDf3kbaW','msg_0df4a20a9001hhr0GrQr3xEUHW','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452591517,1790452592798,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_ci2d7qpj384t_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ EVERY LISTENING SOCKET (any state) ═══\"\nss -tulpnH 2>/dev/null | awk ''{print $1, $5, $7}'' | sort -u | sed ''s/^/  /'' || sudo -n ss -tulpnH 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"═══ BOUND TO 0.0.0.0 / * = REACHABLE FROM THE LAN ═══\"\nss -tulpnH 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"0\\.0\\.0\\.0|\\*:\" | awk ''{print \"  \", $1, $5}'' | sort -u"},"output":"═══ EVERY LISTENING SOCKET (any state) ═══\n  tcp 127.0.0.1:9050 \n  tcp *:1716 users:((\"kdeconnectd\",pid=1535,fd=16))\n  udp 0.0.0.0:5353 \n  udp *:1716 users:((\"kdeconnectd\",pid=1535,fd=15))\n  udp [::]:5353 \n\n═══ BOUND TO 0.0.0.0 / * = REACHABLE FROM THE LAN ═══\n   tcp 127.0.0.1:9050\n   tcp *:1716\n   udp 0.0.0.0:5353\n   udp *:1716\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ EVERY LISTENING SOCKET (any state) ═══\n  tcp 127.0.0.1:9050 \n  tcp *:1716 users:((\"kdeconnectd\",pid=1535,fd=16))\n  udp 0.0.0.0:5353 \n  udp *:1716 users:((\"kdeconnectd\",pid=1535,fd=15))\n  udp [::]:5353 \n\n═══ BOUND TO 0.0.0.0 / * = REACHABLE FROM THE LAN ═══\n   tcp 127.0.0.1:9050\n   tcp *:1716\n   udp 0.0.0.0:5353\n   udp *:1716\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ EVERY LISTENING SOCKET (any state) ═══\"\nss -tulpnH 2>/dev/null | awk ''{print $1, $5, $7}'' | sort -u | sed ''s/^/  /'' || sudo -n ss -tulpnH 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"═══ BOUND TO 0.0.0.0 / * = REACHABLE FROM THE LAN ═══\"\nss -tulpnH 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"0\\.0\\.0\\.0|\\*:\" | awk ''{print \"  \", $1, $5}'' | sort -u","time":{"start":1790452592753,"end":1790452592798}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a7075001sdBgmbzVQS6Ugy','msg_0df4a20a9001hhr0GrQr3xEUHW','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452592757,1790452601249,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_ci2d7qpj384t_2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ FIREWALLD: what is the ''USL - Public'' zone actually permitting? ═══\"\necho \"  --- services ---\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --list-services 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    /''\necho \"  --- ports ---\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    /''\necho \"  --- rich rules (incl. fail2ban''s) ---\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --list-rich-rules 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    /'' || echo \"    none\"\necho \"  --- default action / target ---\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --get-target 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    target: /''\nfirewall-cmd --get-default-zone 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    default zone: /''"},"output":"═══ FIREWALLD: what is the ''USL - Public'' zone actually permitting? ═══\n  --- services ---\n    dhcpv6-client ssh\n  --- ports ---\n  --- rich rules (incl. fail2ban''s) ---\n  --- default action / target ---\n    default zone: public\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ FIREWALLD: what is the ''USL - Public'' zone actually permitting? ═══\n  --- services ---\n    dhcpv6-client ssh\n  --- ports ---\n  --- rich rules (incl. fail2ban''s) ---\n  --- default action / target ---\n    default zone: public\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ FIREWALLD: what is the ''USL - Public'' zone actually permitting? ═══\"\necho \"  --- services ---\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --list-services 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    /''\necho \"  --- ports ---\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    /''\necho \"  --- rich rules (incl. fail2ban''s) ---\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --list-rich-rules 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    /'' || echo \"    none\"\necho \"  --- default action / target ---\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --get-target 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    target: /''\nfirewall-cmd --get-default-zone 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/    default zone: /''","time":{"start":1790452594289,"end":1790452601248}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a91a2001Tdh3Su4BphgVu5','msg_0df4a20a9001hhr0GrQr3xEUHW','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452601251,1790452601251,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":57166,"input":27,"output":429,"reasoning":1377,"cache":{"write":0,"read":55333}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a9a69001Tx7OmMKj2FzzwN','msg_0df4a91bd001oZ2S56ixEb1n9R','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452603497,1790452603498,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4a9a6c001Zr2b1V94v3qQh2','msg_0df4a91bd001oZ2S56ixEb1n9R','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452603500,1790452610560,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Interesting findings:\n\n1. `tcp 127.0.0.1:9050` — Tor SOCKS is LISTENING! So the user DID install tor, or at least it''s running. Wait, earlier I checked and tor was NOT installed. Let me recheck. Actually the earlier check showed \"tor NOT INSTALLED\" and no tor service. But now 127.0.0.1:9050 is listening. Hmm — maybe the user installed it between turns? Or the check was wrong.\n\nWait, the `ss` output shows the process column is empty for 9050 (no `users:((...))`), which means it may be owned by another user or I can''t see it. Actually `ss -p` as non-root can''t show other users'' processes. So 9050 is listening but I can''t see who owns it.\n\nSo the user likely installed tor between turns. Let me verify.\n\n2. `*:1716` kdeconnectd TCP — reachable from LAN\n3. `0.0.0.0:5353` and `[::]:5353` — mDNS, reachable from LAN (avahi)\n4. `*:1716` UDP — kdeconnectd\n\n5. firewalld public zone: services = `dhcpv6-client ssh`, no ports, no rich rules.\n   - `ssh` is allowed but sshd is NOT running — a useless hole (but if sshd ever starts, it''s exposed)\n   - `dhcpv6-client` allowed\n   - Note: `--list-rich-rules` returned nothing, and no target output. Interesting.\n\nSo currently firewalld is blocking kdeconnectd (1716) and mDNS (5353) since neither is in the services list. Good.\n\nBut wait — is the default zone policy \"drop\" for unlisted? Yes, firewalld zones reject unlisted by default.\n\nSo the current state is actually reasonably locked down already. The remaining issues:\n- `ssh` service is unnecessarily open (sshd not running, but the hole exists)\n- firewalld''s public zone also allows some ICMP by default (needed for PMTU)\n- IPv6 NDP is allowed by default\n- The kdeconnectd and mDNS sockets are bound to * but blocked by firewalld\n\nWhat the user wants: revoke ALL incoming from outside, keep only needed wifi.\n\nNeeded for wifi:\n- DHCPv4 client (UDP 68→67) — firewalld handles this via `dhcpv6-client` for v6, and v4 DHCP is generally allowed in the default zone implicitly. Let me verify: firewalld allows DHCP for the zone it''s in. Actually `dhcpv6-client` is an explicit service; DHCPv4 is typically allowed by default in firewalld zones.\n- ICMP / ICMPv6 (PMTU, NDP) — critical, must keep\n- DNS responses (established) — fine\n- mDNS (5353) — for anonymity, block. Also not needed.\n- kdeconnectd (1716) — block unless on trusted home network\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Remove `ssh` from the public zone (nothing needs it; sshd is disabled)\n2. Keep `dhcpv6-client` (needed for IPv6 addressing)\n3. Ensure ICMP is allowed (PMTU — blocking it breaks HTTPS and IPv6 entirely)\n4. Add a `block` policy or rely on default-deny (firewalld already default-denies)\n5. Optional: add a `trusted` zone for home wifi that allows kdeconnectd, and keep public strict\n\nLet me also verify tor state since 9050 is listening.\n\nLet me check tor.\n\nAlso — important: I should double check whether firewalld is actually enforcing, and test from an external perspective. I can''t easily test externally. But I can check the ruleset.\n\nLet me verify tor and the current ICMP settings.\n\nLet me be thorough about what firewalld allows by default in a zone.\n\nLet me check.","time":{"start":1790452603500,"end":1790452610559}}');
