INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4b786d001Fd9jQMdjWHFfGY','msg_0df4b73f4001oM3slqXobp0qk9','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452660334,1790452662534,'{"type":"text","text":"Good news: your system is **already** nearly fully locked down. firewalld default-denies unlisted traffic, so `kdeconnectd` (1716) and mDNS (5353) are already blocked despite their sockets being bound to `*`.\n\nThe real finding is a standing hole:","time":{"start":1790452660333,"end":1790452662533}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4b7a59001GU8m3hPUGYa4xv','msg_0df4b73f4001oM3slqXobp0qk9','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452660825,1790452662536,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_tsgut6diczwj_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cat <<''EOF''\n  THE ONLY GENUINE HOLE:  <service name=\"ssh\"/>  in the public zone\n\n  sshd is disabled and not listening, so there is no live risk today.\n  But the door is propped open — anyone who enables ssh later, or an\n  update that starts it, is remotely reachable with no further action.\n\n  Also: tor 0.4.9.13-1 is already installed, active, enabled, and bound to\n  127.0.0.1:9050. That is loopback-only, so it is NOT an inbound surface\n  and there is nothing to revoke. No browser is installed yet, so nothing\n  is currently using the SOCKS port.\nEOF"},"output":"  THE ONLY GENUINE HOLE:  <service name=\"ssh\"/>  in the public zone\n\n  sshd is disabled and not listening, so there is no live risk today.\n  But the door is propped open — anyone who enables ssh later, or an\n  update that starts it, is remotely reachable with no further action.\n\n  Also: tor 0.4.9.13-1 is already installed, active, enabled, and bound to\n  127.0.0.1:9050. That is loopback-only, so it is NOT an inbound surface\n  and there is nothing to revoke. No browser is installed yet, so nothing\n  is currently using the SOCKS port.\n","metadata":{"output":"  THE ONLY GENUINE HOLE:  <service name=\"ssh\"/>  in the public zone\n\n  sshd is disabled and not listening, so there is no live risk today.\n  But the door is propped open — anyone who enables ssh later, or an\n  update that starts it, is remotely reachable with no further action.\n\n  Also: tor 0.4.9.13-1 is already installed, active, enabled, and bound to\n  127.0.0.1:9050. That is loopback-only, so it is NOT an inbound surface\n  and there is nothing to revoke. No browser is installed yet, so nothing\n  is currently using the SOCKS port.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cat <<''EOF''\n  THE ONLY GENUINE HOLE:  <service name=\"ssh\"/>  in the public zone\n\n  sshd is disabled and not listening, so there is no live risk today.\n  But the door is propped open — anyone who enables ssh later, or an\n  update that starts it, is remotely reachable with no further action.\n\n  Also: tor 0.4.9.13-1 is already installed, active, enabled, and bound to\n  127.0.0.1:9050. That is loopback-only, so it is NOT an inbound surface\n  and there is nothing to revoke. No browser is installed yet, so nothing\n  is currently using the SOCKS port.\nEOF","time":{"start":1790452662523,"end":1790452662536}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4b8109001PGn3M36ME3hRxo','msg_0df4b73f4001oM3slqXobp0qk9','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452662538,1790452662538,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":60539,"input":158,"output":243,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":60138}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4b8694001KwJuk5Y8nDyoZ0','msg_0df4b8122001J3W4MOceqT56CO','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452663956,1790452663957,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4b8696001YtYeVTs5xxVa2W','msg_0df4b8122001J3W4MOceqT56CO','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452663958,1790452671490,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now let me be clear about what to change, and the important thing about keeping ICMP unblocked.\n\nThe commands:\n1. Remove ssh from public zone (permanent + runtime)\n2. Keep dhcpv6-client\n3. Keep ICMP unblocked (CRITICAL)\n4. Optionally add a trusted zone for home network if they want KDE Connect\n\nLet me give the exact commands. And I need to be careful: the \"needed wifi communications\" are DHCPv4, DHCPv6, DNS, ICMP/ICMPv6, and the return traffic on established connections.\n\nImportant: DHCPv4 — firewalld allows it implicitly in the default zone? Let me think. Actually in firewalld, the `dhcpv6-client` service is explicit. For DHCPv4, firewalld''s default zone typically allows it because the zone includes an implicit rule for DHCP... Actually no. firewalld zones by default accept all traffic in the sense of \"reject unlisted inbound\", but DHCP broadcast responses come from the DHCP server. Hmm.\n\nActually, in firewalld, the `public` zone XML has:\n```xml\n<service name=\"dhcpv6-client\"/>\n```\nAnd for DHCPv4, firewalld''s built-in behavior: the `default` zone (and most zones) allow DHCP via the fact that firewalld''s default policy for a zone without explicit ingress rules is to reject unsolicited inbound, BUT DHCP uses broadcast to 255.255.255.255 which... \n\nLet me not speculate. The safest approach: add `dhcp` service explicitly if worried, but verify empirically that wifi still works after changes. Actually the user is currently connected and gets DHCP. If I remove only `ssh`, DHCPv4 keeps working because it''s currently working with `dhcpv6-client` + `ssh` allowed — removing ssh doesn''t touch DHCP.\n\nWait — is DHCPv4 explicitly allowed? The zone has only `dhcpv6-client` and `ssh`. And the user has a working wifi connection with an IPv4 address (10.245.106.176). So DHCPv4 must be working through some implicit allowance. Removing `ssh` won''t change that.\n\nSo the change is safe: remove ssh only.\n\nLet me also add explicit insurance so a future `firewall-cmd --add-service=ssh` doesn''t silently re-open. Best: use firewalld''s `icmp-block` to leave alone, and add a rich rule or use the `block` zone.\n\nActually, a more durable guard: set firewalld to drop everything not explicitly allowed — which it already does. The main durable thing is: remove ssh from the *source file* I pushed, so a rebuild doesn''t reintroduce it.\n\nSo plan:\n1. `firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --remove-service=ssh` + runtime\n2. Update `~/Projects/zen-hardening/firewalld-workstation.conf` to drop the ssh line\n3. Re-push\n4. Verify wifi still has connectivity\n\nAnd ask about KDE Connect / trusted zone.\n\nLet me give them the commands to run, and offer the trusted-zone option.\n\nLet me be concise now — I''ve been verbose. Let me give the commands and the one question.","time":{"start":1790452663958,"end":1790452671488}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df4ba403001sjvJkAzUH1Hus3','msg_0df4b8122001J3W4MOceqT56CO','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790452671491,1790452676305,'{"type":"text","text":"## What to run\n\nOnly one change is needed, and it''s the removal of that hole:\n\n```bash\nsudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --remove-service=ssh\nsudo firewall-cmd --reload\n```\n\nVerify:\n```bash\nfirewall-cmd --zone=public --list-services      # expect: dhcpv6-client only\nfirewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --list-services\n```\n\n**Then delete it from the source file too**, or a rebuild puts it right back:\n```bash\nsed -i ''/<service name=\"ssh\"\\/>/d'' ~/Projects/zen-hardening/firewalld-workstation.conf\n```\n\n## What I am deliberately *not* telling you to block\n\nYour instinct to \"block all inbound\" has one trap: **do not block ICMP.**\n\nfirewalld currently has nothing ICMP-blocked, which is correct. `packet-too-big` (PMTU) and `v6-neighbour-solicit` (IPv6 NDP) must keep passing or:\n- IPv6 stops working entirely\n- Large HTTPS downloads hang and stall on a laptop with a small MSS path\n\nBlocking ICMP to \"look locked down\" is the single most common way people break their own networking while believing they secured it.\n\n## WiFi stays up\n\n`dhcpv6-client` remains, and IPv4 DHCP is currently working via firewalld''s implicit allowance — neither is affected by removing `ssh`. Your connection won''t drop.\n\n## One decision for you\n\n`kdeconnectd` (1716) is blocked now, and I recommend keeping it that way **on this network**. But that means no phone-to-laptop file transfer or clipboard unless you allow it somewhere. Options:\n\n1. **Leave blocked everywhere** — most secure, lose KDE Connect\n2. **Add a `trusted` zone for your home SSID only** — KDE Connect works at home, still blocked on coffee-shop wifi. Needs your home network name.\n\nAlso still open from earlier: no browser is installed, so tor''s SOCKS port is running with nothing using it. Want me to install `torbrowser-launcher`? That''s the piece that actually gives you anonymous browsing — the daemon alone does not.","time":{"start":1790452671491,"end":1790452676303}}');
