INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df54c2970012pxxf4DYwZ3izU','msg_0df54ba40001Rzk4dap8Vz6TsI','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453269143,1790453274189,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Critical findings — it did NOT work as intended:\n\n1. `firewall-cmd --state` = running ✓\n2. **BUT `nft list tables | grep -c firewalld` = 0** — no firewalld nft tables! So the firewall is STILL NOT ENFORCING. This is the same critical state as before.\n3. `default zone: public` — NOT workstation. So step 4''s `--set-default-zone=workstation` didn''t take effect, or was rolled back.\n4. `INVALID_ZONE: workstation` — **the workstation zone doesn''t exist!** So the `cp` to /etc/firewalld/zones/workstation.xml either didn''t happen or the file isn''t there.\n5. `no zone` for wlo0 — the interface isn''t bound to any zone.\n\nWait — but `--state` says running. And there are 0 firewalld tables. That''s contradictory-ish: running but not enforcing.\n\nAlso note: wifi is on `wlo1` (not wlo0). Earlier commands used wlo0. Let me re-check: the default route says `dev wlo1`. So the interface is wlo1. My commands used wlo0 — that was a mistake in my instructions!\n\nActually earlier I used `firewall-cmd --get-zone-of-interface=wlo0` → \"no zone\". And the active connection is \"USL - Public\" bound to wlo1.\n\nSo my instructions had `wlo0` but the actual interface is `wlo1`. Hmm, but `nmcli con mod \"USL - Public\" connection.zone workstation` should work on the connection regardless of interface name.\n\nBut `workstation` zone doesn''t exist at all (INVALID_ZONE). So the `cp` didn''t happen, OR the reload dropped it.\n\nLet me check if the file is in /etc.\n\nLet me be careful and check:\n- does /etc/firewalld/zones/workstation.xml exist?\n- is /etc/firewalld/zones/public.xml gone?\n- what does firewall-offline-cmd say?\n\nThe 0 nft tables is the alarming part. Let me investigate why firewalld is \"running\" with no tables.\n\nPossible: firewalld with nftables backend... in firewalld 2.x the tables might be named differently, not \"firewalld\". Let me check what nft tables exist at all. My grep for \"firewalld\" might be wrong! In firewalld 2.x, the nftables tables are `inet firewalld` and `inet firewalld_filter`... let me actually list them.\n\nThat''s likely my error again — grepping for \"firewalld\" in table names. Let me list ALL nft tables.\n\nLet me check.","time":{"start":1790453269143,"end":1790453274189}}');
