INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5bde14001u331ZbexiFNAXJ','msg_0df5bd713001XbvTYRc5cP41CJ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453734932,1790453738105,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"\"No such file or directory\" for a file I just wrote and verified exists. This is a classic symptom: the file has a CRLF line ending or a bad shebang, causing \"No such file or directory\" — BUT the error says `bash: <file>: No such file or directory` which is bash''s message when the file doesn''t exist.\n\nWait — I explicitly invoked `sudo bash <file>`. If bash can''t find the file, it means the path is wrong or the file isn''t there.\n\nBut I verified with `chmod +x` and `bash -n` that it exists and is valid. Let me re-verify it exists RIGHT NOW.\n\nPossibilities:\n1. The file was deleted somehow\n2. The path is different\n3. Something cleaned it\n\nLet me check immediately.\n\nAlso the classic \"No such file or directory\" for a script that EXISTS is a CRLF/encoding issue in the shebang — but that error appears as `/path/to/script: bash: /path/to/script: /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter` or similar. When you run `bash script` directly, a shebang issue wouldn''t matter.\n\nSince the error is `bash: /home/.../apply-firewall.sh: No such file or directory` — bash itself couldn''t open the file. So the file genuinely isn''t at that path.\n\nLet me check.","time":{"start":1790453734932,"end":1790453738104}}');
