INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e0216001YMwbwDpY250uS0','msg_0df5df38f0014TzPVw3XC2xGH5','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453875222,1790453876245,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Very important finding: `fail2ban-coverage.md` says **three of four jails don''t work**, and `sddm` and `sudo` are \"structurally dead\" because `filter = pam-generic` doesn''t match.\n\nThis is a critical finding I need to read fully. Let me read the rest.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of thing that matters — the user thinks they have brute-force protection and they don''t.\n\nLet me read the full doc.","time":{"start":1790453875222,"end":1790453876244}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e061600115uqUf1iDtaNA7','msg_0df5df38f0014TzPVw3XC2xGH5','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453876246,1790453876464,'{"type":"text","text":"This is important. Hermes found that **`sddm` and `sudo` can never ban anything** — the `pam-generic` filter doesn''t match. Reading the full analysis:","time":{"start":1790453876246,"end":1790453876463}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e06e1001epuR2t4d6Wrueh','msg_0df5df38f0014TzPVw3XC2xGH5','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453876449,1790453876471,'{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_function_ogk1qhh9awa2_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/fail2ban-coverage.md"},"output":"<path>/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/fail2ban-coverage.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # fail2ban coverage — what is actually enforced, and what is not\n2: \n3: Verified on this machine, 2026-09-26. Read this before assuming you are\n4: protected. Three of the four jails in `shield-kitty.conf` do not work, and\n5: cannot, for structural reasons that no amount of configuration will fix.\n6: \n7: ## Summary\n8: \n9: | Jail | Enabled | Actually bans? | Why |\n10: |---|---|---|---|\n11: | `sshd` | yes | **yes, if sshd is running** | sshd is currently `inactive` — 0 listeners on :22. The jail is correct and dormant. |\n12: | `recidive` | yes | yes, once another jail bans something | Re-bans repeat offenders. Needs at least one working jail. |\n13: | `sddm` | yes | **NO — structurally dead** | See below. |\n14: | `sudo` | yes | **NO — structurally dead** | See below. |\n15: \n16: ## Why sddm and sudo can never ban anything\n17: \n18: Both jails use `filter = pam-generic`. Its failregex is:\n19: \n20: ```\n21: failregex = ^ruser=<F-ALT_USER>(?:\\S*|.*?)</F-ALT_USER> rhost=<HOST>...\n22: ```\n23: \n24: The filter requires a literal `rhost=<IP>` field to extract an address to ban.\n25: Tested with `fail2ban-regex`:\n26: \n27: ```\n28: remote line (rhost=203.0.113.5)  -> 1 matched\n29: local  line (rhost=)             -> 0 matched, 1 missed\n30: ```\n31: \n32: A failure at the SDDM greeter is by definition local: someone physically at\n33: your keyboard. There is no IP to ban, because there is no network involved. The\n34: same is true of a bad `sudo` password — it comes from a local tty.\n35: \n36: **This is not a misconfiguration to be repaired.** IP banning is the wrong tool\n37: for both cases, and no filter rewrite makes it the right one. A person standing\n38: at your laptop cannot be banned by IP because they are not coming from an IP.\n39: \n40: ## What actually protects those two cases\n41: \n42: - **`sudo`** — `pam_faillock` with `deny = 10`. Enforced per account. Note that\n43:   if `admin_group` is configured, admin accounts are exempt by design.\n44: - **`sddm` / the physical greeter** — **nothing automated**. This is a real gap\n45:   and no config in this repo closes it. The mitigations that do apply:\n46:   - Strong greeter password.\n47:   - `deny = 10` in faillock applies to the account being guessed, so an\n48:     attacker gets 10 tries per account per `unlock_time`, not unlimited.\n49:   - Automatic screen lock, and physically shutting the lid when away.\n50: \n51: If you want the greeter case covered by something automated, the honest options\n52: are a camera or a physical presence sensor — not a log filter.\n53: \n54: ## Why sshd works but currently does nothing\n55: \n56: The `sshd` jail''s filter is correct (2 of 4 synthetic attack lines matched; the\n57: other 2 were PAM-format sudo/console lines that belong to the other jail).\n58: But `sshd.service` is `inactive` and nothing listens on port 22, so there is no\n59: sshd traffic to match. The jail exists so that protection is already in place if\n60: sshd is ever enabled — it is not evidence of current protection.\n61: \n62: If you enable sshd, the jail will start working immediately with no config\n63: change.\n64: \n65: ## The permanent-ban trap\n66: \n67: All jails use `bantime = -1` — permanent, never expiring. `ignoreip` covers\n68: loopback and RFC1918, so on your current network (10.245.106.176) you cannot be\n69: banned.\n70: \n71: **The moment you connect from a public address — tethering, cafe wifi, airport\n72: — you are no longer in `ignoreip`, and four wrong sudo passwords would ban your\n73: own address on every port, permanently.**\n74: \n75: This is why the `sudo`/`sddm` jails being dead is, right now, the reason you\n76: have not locked yourself out.\n77: \n78: Escape hatch:\n79: \n80: ```bash\n81: sudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/unban-self.sh --list    # is my address banned?\n82: sudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/unban-self.sh          # clear bans on my addresses\n83: ```\n84: \n85: ## Verification commands used\n86: \n87: ```bash\n88: systemctl is-active sshd                       # inactive\n89: ss -ltn | grep -c '':22 ''                        # 0\n90: fail2ban-regex /tmp/remote.log /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/pam-generic.conf   # 1 matched\n91: fail2ban-regex /tmp/local.log  /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/pam-generic.conf   # 0 matched\n92: fail2ban-client -t                              # configuration test is successful\n93: ```\n94: \n95: `fail2ban-client status` needs root (socket is 0700), so jail state cannot be\n96: read unprivileged.\n\n(End of file - total 96 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# fail2ban coverage — what is actually enforced, and what is not\n\nVerified on this machine, 2026-09-26. Read this before assuming you are\nprotected. Three of the four jails in `shield-kitty.conf` do not work, and\ncannot, for structural reasons that no amount of configuration will fix.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Jail | Enabled | Actually bans? | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `sshd` | yes | **yes, if sshd is running** | sshd is currently `inactive` — 0 listeners on :22. The jail is correct and dormant. |\n| `recidive` | yes | yes, once another jail bans something | Re-bans repeat offenders. Needs at least one working jail. |\n| `sddm` | yes | **NO — structurally dead** | See below. |\n| `sudo` | yes | **NO — structurally dead** | See below. |\n\n## Why sddm and sudo can never ban anything\n\nBoth jails use `filter = pam-generic`. Its failregex is:\n\n```","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/fail2ban-coverage.md","text":"# fail2ban coverage — what is actually enforced, and what is not\n\nVerified on this machine, 2026-09-26. Read this before assuming you are\nprotected. Three of the four jails in `shield-kitty.conf` do not work, and\ncannot, for structural reasons that no amount of configuration will fix.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Jail | Enabled | Actually bans? | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `sshd` | yes | **yes, if sshd is running** | sshd is currently `inactive` — 0 listeners on :22. The jail is correct and dormant. |\n| `recidive` | yes | yes, once another jail bans something | Re-bans repeat offenders. Needs at least one working jail. |\n| `sddm` | yes | **NO — structurally dead** | See below. |\n| `sudo` | yes | **NO — structurally dead** | See below. |\n\n## Why sddm and sudo can never ban anything\n\nBoth jails use `filter = pam-generic`. Its failregex is:\n\n```\nfailregex = ^ruser=<F-ALT_USER>(?:\\S*|.*?)</F-ALT_USER> rhost=<HOST>...\n```\n\nThe filter requires a literal `rhost=<IP>` field to extract an address to ban.\nTested with `fail2ban-regex`:\n\n```\nremote line (rhost=203.0.113.5)  -> 1 matched\nlocal  line (rhost=)             -> 0 matched, 1 missed\n```\n\nA failure at the SDDM greeter is by definition local: someone physically at\nyour keyboard. There is no IP to ban, because there is no network involved. The\nsame is true of a bad `sudo` password — it comes from a local tty.\n\n**This is not a misconfiguration to be repaired.** IP banning is the wrong tool\nfor both cases, and no filter rewrite makes it the right one. A person standing\nat your laptop cannot be banned by IP because they are not coming from an IP.\n\n## What actually protects those two cases\n\n- **`sudo`** — `pam_faillock` with `deny = 10`. Enforced per account. Note that\n  if `admin_group` is configured, admin accounts are exempt by design.\n- **`sddm` / the physical greeter** — **nothing automated**. This is a real gap\n  and no config in this repo closes it. The mitigations that do apply:\n  - Strong greeter password.\n  - `deny = 10` in faillock applies to the account being guessed, so an\n    attacker gets 10 tries per account per `unlock_time`, not unlimited.\n  - Automatic screen lock, and physically shutting the lid when away.\n\nIf you want the greeter case covered by something automated, the honest options\nare a camera or a physical presence sensor — not a log filter.\n\n## Why sshd works but currently does nothing\n\nThe `sshd` jail''s filter is correct (2 of 4 synthetic attack lines matched; the\nother 2 were PAM-format sudo/console lines that belong to the other jail).\nBut `sshd.service` is `inactive` and nothing listens on port 22, so there is no\nsshd traffic to match. The jail exists so that protection is already in place if\nsshd is ever enabled — it is not evidence of current protection.\n\nIf you enable sshd, the jail will start working immediately with no config\nchange.\n\n## The permanent-ban trap\n\nAll jails use `bantime = -1` — permanent, never expiring. `ignoreip` covers\nloopback and RFC1918, so on your current network (10.245.106.176) you cannot be\nbanned.\n\n**The moment you connect from a public address — tethering, cafe wifi, airport\n— you are no longer in `ignoreip`, and four wrong sudo passwords would ban your\nown address on every port, permanently.**\n\nThis is why the `sudo`/`sddm` jails being dead is, right now, the reason you\nhave not locked yourself out.\n\nEscape hatch:\n\n```bash\nsudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/unban-self.sh --list    # is my address banned?\nsudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/unban-self.sh          # clear bans on my addresses\n```\n\n## Verification commands used\n\n```bash\nsystemctl is-active sshd                       # inactive\nss -ltn | grep -c '':22 ''                        # 0\nfail2ban-regex /tmp/remote.log /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/pam-generic.conf   # 1 matched\nfail2ban-regex /tmp/local.log  /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/pam-generic.conf   # 0 matched\nfail2ban-client -t                              # configuration test is successful\n```\n\n`fail2ban-client status` needs root (socket is 0700), so jail state cannot be\nread unprivileged.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":96,"totalLines":96,"truncated":false}},"title":"home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/fail2ban-coverage.md","time":{"start":1790453876461,"end":1790453876470}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e06f9001xwam6C6LGiS4z3','msg_0df5df38f0014TzPVw3XC2xGH5','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453876473,1790453876474,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":106272,"input":338,"output":80,"reasoning":96,"cache":{"write":0,"read":105758}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e0fbf001PEweJLkXb5azEh','msg_0df5e071c001ZNXFB55T406h8a','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453878719,1790453878720,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e0fc2001q0dwmnEWTT8k2L','msg_0df5e071c001ZNXFB55T406h8a','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453878722,1790453881846,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"This is a genuinely excellent piece of analysis by Hermes, and it corrects a fundamental misunderstanding I had. Let me be honest about this.\n\nI built the `sddm` and `sudo` jails and told the user they would protect them. They''re **structurally incapable** of working:\n- SDDM greeter failures are LOCAL — someone at your keyboard. There''s no IP to ban.\n- sudo failures are LOCAL — same.\n\nThe `pam-generic` filter requires `rhost=<IP>`, which doesn''t exist for local console events.\n\nI got this fundamentally wrong. I told the user \"permanent lockout\" protection was in place for sddm/sudo. It isn''t. And it can''t be, because IP banning is the wrong tool for local threats.\n\nI should own this clearly. This is a real error in my earlier advice.\n\nLet me push the configs and be honest.\n\nKey points:\n1. My `sddm`/`sudo` jails never worked and never will. My fault.\n2. The actual protections: faillock `deny=10` for sudo; nothing for the greeter.\n3. The \"permanent ban trap\" — my `bantime=-1` design was dangerous, and the only thing preventing a self-lockout is that those jails are dead.\n4. `unban-self.sh` is a good escape hatch.\n\nI should also reconsider: should I remove the dead `sddm`/`sudo` jails? They cost nothing but add confusion. And `bantime=-1` on `recidive` + `sshd` is a real risk if the user ever enables sshd from a public IP.\n\nActually, important: now that the firewall is strict and `ssh` is removed from the zone, the `sshd` jail is moot unless sshd is enabled. And `recidive` will re-ban.\n\nLet me push and then give an honest summary with recommendations.\n\nLet me push first.","time":{"start":1790453878722,"end":1790453881845}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e1bf70015PVjf7t0pditkr','msg_0df5e071c001ZNXFB55T406h8a','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453881847,1790453884955,'{"type":"text","text":"Pushing. But I have to correct something significant first.","time":{"start":1790453881847,"end":1790453884955}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e1d4a001SDK51WlunsKiFf','msg_0df5e071c001ZNXFB55T406h8a','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453882186,1790453886797,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_g98tbzv0ex52_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/opencode/hss\ngit add -A\ngit -c user.name=opencode -c user.email=opencode@localhost commit -q -m \"Replace broken public.xml override with validated workstation zone\n\nThe firewalld-workstation.conf approach was wrong on two counts and is\nremoved. A zone file cannot subtract an inherited service: <service>\naccepts only a name attribute, there is no remove-service element in\nfirewalld 2.5.2, and an /etc file sharing a built-in zone''s name is\nMERGED rather than substituted. Overriding ''public'' can therefore never\nproduce an empty allowlist.\n\nworkstation.xml is a uniquely-named zone, so it inherits nothing and\nstarts empty. Validated against firewalld 2.5.2''s own zone_reader before\ninstalling. Result: all inbound denied except dhcpv6-client. ssh, mdns,\nsamba and kdeconnectd are all unreachable.\n\napply-firewall.sh installs and activates it, works around an Arch\npackaging bug (fail2ban.service declares PartOf=firewalld.service, which\npropagates restarts bidirectionally and makes ''systemctl restart\nfirewalld'' fail with conflicting jobs), and refuses to report success\nunless nft rules are confirmed loaded. firewalld --state reports\n''running'' even while enforcing nothing, so it is not a valid check.\n\nfail2ban-coverage.md documents that the sddm and sudo jails can never\nban anything: pam-generic requires an rhost=<IP> field, and both are\nlocal console events with no IP. unban-self.sh is the escape hatch for\nthe bantime=-1 self-lockout trap.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\ngit push -q origin HEAD 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /'' && echo \"  [✓] pushed\" || echo \"  [✗] failed\""},"output":"  [✓] pushed\n","metadata":{"output":"  [✓] pushed\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /tmp/opencode/hss\ngit add -A\ngit -c user.name=opencode -c user.email=opencode@localhost commit -q -m \"Replace broken public.xml override with validated workstation zone\n\nThe firewalld-workstation.conf approach was wrong on two counts and is\nremoved. A zone file cannot subtract an inherited service: <service>\naccepts only a name attribute, there is no remove-service element in\nfirewalld 2.5.2, and an /etc file sharing a built-in zone''s name is\nMERGED rather than substituted. Overriding ''public'' can therefore never\nproduce an empty allowlist.\n\nworkstation.xml is a uniquely-named zone, so it inherits nothing and\nstarts empty. Validated against firewalld 2.5.2''s own zone_reader before\ninstalling. Result: all inbound denied except dhcpv6-client. ssh, mdns,\nsamba and kdeconnectd are all unreachable.\n\napply-firewall.sh installs and activates it, works around an Arch\npackaging bug (fail2ban.service declares PartOf=firewalld.service, which\npropagates restarts bidirectionally and makes ''systemctl restart\nfirewalld'' fail with conflicting jobs), and refuses to report success\nunless nft rules are confirmed loaded. firewalld --state reports\n''running'' even while enforcing nothing, so it is not a valid check.\n\nfail2ban-coverage.md documents that the sddm and sudo jails can never\nban anything: pam-generic requires an rhost=<IP> field, and both are\nlocal console events with no IP. unban-self.sh is the escape hatch for\nthe bantime=-1 self-lockout trap.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\ngit push -q origin HEAD 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /'' && echo \"  [✓] pushed\" || echo \"  [✗] failed\"","time":{"start":1790453884953,"end":1790453886797}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e2f4f001M6Ymx9Pmqybxa3','msg_0df5e071c001ZNXFB55T406h8a','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453886799,1790453886799,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":108478,"input":1329,"output":477,"reasoning":402,"cache":{"write":0,"read":106270}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e36e0001JwqEmjvh4J6qcV','msg_0df5e2f6f0017qLKUJu9up31h8','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453888736,1790453888737,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df5e36e2001j9euVoRkNXf26u','msg_0df5e2f6f0017qLKUJu9up31h8','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790453888738,1790453894165,'{"type":"text","text":"Pushed. Now the correction.\n\n## I was wrong about sddm and sudo protection\n\nI built those two jails and told you they gave you permanent brute-force lockout. They never worked and **cannot** work. Hermes caught it with `fail2ban-regex`:\n\n`pam-generic` requires a literal `rhost=<IP>` field to extract an address to ban. An SDDM greeter failure is someone standing at your keyboard. A bad `sudo` password comes from a local tty. Neither has an IP, because neither involves a network.\n\n```\nremote line (rhost=203.0.113.5)  -> 1 matched\nlocal  line (rhost=)             -> 0 matched\n```\n\nIP banning is the wrong tool for a local threat. No filter rewrite fixes that.\n\n## What actually protects you\n\n| Threat | Protection |\n|---|---|\n| `sudo` brute force | `pam_faillock deny=10` — the only thing working |\n| SDDM greeter | **nothing automated** — real gap |\n| SSH | jail is correct, but `sshd` is inactive and the firewall now blocks it anyway |\n| Remote (any) | strict `workstation` zone: all inbound denied |\n\nFor the greeter, the honest mitigations are a strong password, `deny=10` capping attempts at 10 per `unlock_time`, auto screen lock, and closing the lid. A camera or presence sensor, not a log filter.\n\n## The trap in my own design\n\nEvery jail uses `bantime = -1` — permanent, never expiring. `ignoreip` covers RFC1918, so on `10.245.106.176` you''re safe. **The moment you tether or join cafe wifi, your address is no longer ignored, and four wrong sudo passwords would permanently ban you on every port.**\n\nThe only reason you haven''t locked yourself out is that the `sudo`/`sddm` jails are dead. That''s an accident, not a safeguard.\n\n`unban-self.sh` is a solid escape hatch and I reviewed it — root-gated, IP-validated before any command, fixed-string matching, only touches addresses you currently hold. It correctly refuses to offer an unprivileged `--list`, since the socket is `0700` and an empty result would falsely read as \"nobody is banned.\"\n\n## Recommendation\n\nSince `ssh` is now firewalled off and `sshd` is inactive, the `sshd` and `recidive` jails are dormant but not harmless — if you ever enable sshd from a public address, `recidive` will make any ban permanent. I''d drop `bantime = -1` to a long-but-finite value like `1d`, and delete the two structurally-dead jails rather than leave them implying protection that doesn''t exist.\n\nWant me to make that change? It means editing `shield-kitty-fail2ban.local` and re-testing.","time":{"start":1790453888738,"end":1790453894165}}');
