INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df6114e5001jA3pyZ470MmIDy','msg_0df610646001oGoHtrzk7sngnE','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790454076645,1790454078248,'{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed on disk — this matches the \"1 jail\" you saw:\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `sshd` enabled (line 67) | `true` |\n| `recidive` enabled (line 92) | `false` — bootstrap order preserved |\n| `bantime` × 3 | `86400` — no `-1` anywhere |\n| `sddm`/`sudo` sections | 0 — removed |\n\nNow enable recidive:\n\n```bash\nsudo sed -i ''s/^enabled  = false/enabled  = true/'' /etc/fail2ban/jail.d/shield-kitty.conf\nsudo fail2ban-client -t\n```\n\nMust print `configuration test is successful`. If not, stop — `recidive` reads `/var/log/fail2ban.log` and a bad path aborts the whole startup.\n\nThen:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl stop fail2ban\nsudo systemctl restart fail2ban\nf2bready\n```\n\nExpect **2 jails**: `recidive`, `sshd`. If you haven''t defined `f2bready` yet, use:\n\n```fish\nwhile not sudo fail2ban-client status 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"Jail list\"\n    sleep 0.5\nend\nsudo fail2ban-client status\n```\n\nWorth noting: the jail order in the output is alphabetical, so `recidive` then `sshd` is expected, not a sign the sed hit the wrong section.","time":{"start":1790454076645,"end":1790454078248}}');
