INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df812f0d001JwajgkzQrPc5pA','msg_0df8110b8001ekEm41XpjtW94M','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790456180493,1790456189093,'{"type":"text","text":"Agents are **still writing** — `zen-hardening` committed 67s ago, 17 agent processes live. I won''t edit files they''re writing.\n\n## Audit findings so far (read-only, safe to run concurrently)\n\n**Boot readiness — clean**\n- No `pacman` db lock, **fully up to date**, no held-back packages → no partial-upgrade boot-breaker\n- No queued systemd jobs; no failed units\n- ESP mounted, `fsck` passed at boot; all `fstab` devices resolve\n- `dracut 111` is the initramfs generator (not `mkinitcpio`) — normal for current Garuda; initramfs newer than kernel\n- Boot 49.5s total, graphical target 2.8s into userspace\n- No `.pacnew`/`.pacsave` config drift\n\n**Real issues found**\n\n1. **Shield Kitty will not survive your reboot.** It''s running as a bare process (PID 287282) with *no systemd unit installed anywhere* — the units exist only in the repo. A Hermes handoff also confirms the device-blacklist is `NOT INSTALLED`. Earlier in this session it was reported \"active\"; that was the process, not a unit. Needs installing.\n\n2. **`zen-hardening` recidive regression — just introduced by another agent.** A commit 67s ago set `recidive enabled=true` in the *repo*. My `bootstrap.sh` only ever *enables* it when `/var/log/fail2ban.log` exists; it never disables it. A fresh install would therefore copy `enabled = true` with no log file — and a missing log aborts **every** jail at startup. That breaks fail2ban on a clean rebuild.\n\n3. **Memory pressure:** 5.5Gi of 7.4Gi used, 2.9Gi in zram swap. Expected with 17 agents; should drop once they exit, but worth re-checking before you reboot.\n\n**Clean / by design**\n- `kdeconnectd` and mDNS bind all interfaces, but `workstation` is `target: default` with only `dhcpv6-client` + ICMP allowed → rejected. Matches the earlier decision to block KDE Connect.\n- 3 coredumps were `alert.py --demo` test runs, not the watcher\n- `bch-faucet` `.env` missing, but its unit is `not-found` → not a boot issue\n- `dmesg` unreadable because our own `kernel.dmesg_restrict=1` is active — expected, but it means SMART/dmesg checks need root\n\nI need a decision before touching anything, because \"finished\" is ambiguous here:","time":{"start":1790456180493,"end":1790456189092}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df8131c70019AcfYf45W0LaYM','msg_0df812b70001Ykh39nqMzr5Zjo','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790456181191,1790456181192,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df8131c9001Lk8o2P8QxfHUiH','msg_0df812b70001Ykh39nqMzr5Zjo','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790456181193,1790456186788,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have accurate facts:\n\n1. `kdeconnectd` runs as a **user session autostart** process (pid 1535, user livings, PPID 1185), via `app-org.kde.kdeconnect.daemon@autostart.service` — NOT a system service. That''s why `systemctl is-active kdeconnectd` says inactive/not-found. It''s a KDE autostart app.\n\n2. **The workstation zone is bound to `lo wlo1`** — so all traffic on wlo1 goes through the workstation zone. And the zone has:\n   - services: dhcpv6-client ONLY (no kdeconnectd, no mdns)\n   - ports: NONE\n   - rich rules: NONE\n\n   But `sources:` is EMPTY. And no ports/services for kdeconnect. So **port 1716 should be BLOCKED by the workstation zone default policy** (since no service/port allows it).\n\n   Let me verify the zone''s default target/policy. `target: default` — and for a zone with no explicit policy shown, the default is usually DROP for firewalld zones... actually firewalld zones default to `target: default` which rejects unsolicited inbound. Let me verify the actual policy.\n\n   Actually the listing doesn''t show a \"policy\" line. Firewalld''s default zone policy is to DROP unmatched traffic. So 1716 should be blocked inbound from the LAN.\n\n   So my earlier report that \"kdeconnectd is LAN-exposed on *:1716 with no jail — the only real remote surface, unmonitored\" was PARTIALLY WRONG. The process listens on all interfaces, but the firewall zone (workstation, bound to wlo1) has no rule allowing 1716, so inbound from LAN should be dropped. I should correct this.\n\n   Let me verify by checking the zone policy explicitly and testing.\n\nThis is an important correction to make honestly. Let me verify the firewall actually blocks it.\n\nLet me check the zone target policy and confirm.\n\nLet me test: `firewall-cmd --zone=workstation --get-target` and check if there''s a default policy. Also can I test connectivity? I could try connecting from... I only have the local machine. I could check `firewall-cmd --get-default-zone` and the zone policy.\n\nLet me verify properly.\n\nGiven firewalld''s design, a zone with `target: default` and no explicit policy rejects inbound by default. Let me confirm via `--info-zone` which shows policy.\n\nLet me run that.\n\nThen move to building arpwatch integration.\n\nLet me be efficient.","time":{"start":1790456181193,"end":1790456186787}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df8141d0001IkJqgnYbn3Tk0B','msg_0df8110b8001ekEm41XpjtW94M','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790456185297,1790456257466,'{"type":"tool","tool":"question","callID":"call_function_6xn73c2wk8wj_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"questions":[{"header":"Wait for agents?","question":"Several of these processes are persistent infrastructure (`hermes_cli.main gateway`, the bare `opencode` TUI sessions) that will never exit, so I can''t use process count alone. Repos are still being written to, though. How should I proceed?","options":[{"label":"Wait for repo activity to go quiet (Recommended)","description":"I poll until no repo has been committed to for ~5 minutes, then fix the Shield Kitty units and the recidive regression. Safest — avoids conflicting with in-flight writes."},{"label":"Proceed now, only touching other agents'' repos","description":"I fix Shield Kitty unit installation now but leave zen-hardening alone until it goes quiet, since that repo is actively being written."},{"label":"Proceed now with everything","description":"I fix both issues immediately. Risk: overwriting an agent''s in-flight commit to zen-hardening, which is where the recidive regression lives."}]},{"header":"Root-only checks","question":"Several diagnostics need root (nft rule dump, NVMe SMART, btrfs scrub status, dmesg). I cannot authenticate. How do you want to handle these?","options":[{"label":"Give me a command list to run (Recommended)","description":"I write out the exact read-only commands; you paste the output back and I interpret it. Nothing is changed."},{"label":"Skip root-only checks","description":"Rely on the unprivileged results only. Boot readiness is already strongly evidenced without them."},{"label":"Add NOPASSWD for specific read-only commands","description":"I create a tightly scoped sudoers drop-in for only these audit commands. Note this adds a sudo surface, which cuts against the current hardening."}]}]},"output":"User has answered your questions: \"Several of these processes are persistent infrastructure (`hermes_cli.main gateway`, the bare `opencode` TUI sessions) that will never exit, so I can''t use process count alone. Repos are still being written to, though. How should I proceed?\"=\"Wait for repo activity to go quiet (Recommended)\", \"Several diagnostics need root (nft rule dump, NVMe SMART, btrfs scrub status, dmesg). I cannot authenticate. How do you want to handle these?\"=\"Add NOPASSWD for specific read-only commands\". You can now continue with the user''s answers in mind.","metadata":{"answers":[["Wait for repo activity to go quiet (Recommended)"],["Add NOPASSWD for specific read-only commands"]],"truncated":false},"title":"Asked 2 questions","time":{"start":1790456189090,"end":1790456257464}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df8147a5001rQPYhLQ63vR5RU','msg_0df812b70001Ykh39nqMzr5Zjo','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790456186789,1790456188077,'{"type":"text","text":"Important correction to what I told you earlier. Let me verify the firewall actually blocks it:","time":{"start":1790456186789,"end":1790456188077}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df814903001kXhvaHrLhhSlbN','msg_0df812b70001Ykh39nqMzr5Zjo','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790456187140,1790456192951,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_ejqb6xxudt9t_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== workstation zone policy (determines if 1716 is reachable) ===\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=workstation --info-zone=workstation 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"policy|target|interfaces|services|ports\" | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho; echo \"=== does the zone allow kdeconnect/mdns at all? ===\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=workstation --service=kdeconnectd --query-port=1716/tcp 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\nfirewall-cmd --zone=workstation --query-port=1716/tcp 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  1716/tcp: /''\nfirewall-cmd --zone=workstation --query-port=5353/udp 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  5353/udp: /''"},"output":"=== workstation zone policy (determines if 1716 is reachable) ===\n\n=== does the zone allow kdeconnect/mdns at all? ===\n  usage: ''firewall-cmd --help'' for usage information or see firewall-cmd(1) man page\n  Option can be used only with --permanent.\nsed: -e expression #1, char 12: unknown option to ''s''\nsed: -e expression #1, char 12: unknown option to ''s''\n","metadata":{"output":"=== workstation zone policy (determines if 1716 is reachable) ===\n\n=== does the zone allow kdeconnect/mdns at all? ===\n  usage: ''firewall-cmd --help'' for usage information or see firewall-cmd(1) man page\n  Option can be used only with --permanent.\nsed: -e expression #1, char 12: unknown option to ''s''\nsed: -e expression #1, char 12: unknown option to ''s''\n","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== workstation zone policy (determines if 1716 is reachable) ===\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=workstation --info-zone=workstation 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"policy|target|interfaces|services|ports\" | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho; echo \"=== does the zone allow kdeconnect/mdns at all? ===\"\nfirewall-cmd --zone=workstation --service=kdeconnectd --query-port=1716/tcp 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\nfirewall-cmd --zone=workstation --query-port=1716/tcp 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  1716/tcp: /''\nfirewall-cmd --zone=workstation --query-port=5353/udp 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  5353/udp: /''","time":{"start":1790456188073,"end":1790456192951}}}');
