INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df8e5513001q13c55Rf62M3IQ','msg_0df8e495f001fm892AAX3d05pb','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457042195,1790457044854,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants the commands to run. Let me give them clearly and concisely.\n\nTwo commands: dry-run first, then real install. Plus verification commands.\n\nLet me give exactly what''s needed, clean and minimal, with what to look for.\n\nLet me be concise — the user just wants the commands.\n\nCommands:\n1. Dry run\n2. Real install\n3. Verify status\n4. Watch the log\n\nLet me also mention after install, verify. And note the service may need a real event to appear.\n\nLet me present them cleanly.\n\nOne consideration: the previously-deployed things aren''t installed. But the user asked specifically for the arpwatch commands. Let me give those, and maybe briefly note the others still pending. But keep focused — they asked for the commands.\n\nLet me give the arpwatch commands primarily.\n\nLet me double-check the verification commands that work without root vs need root.\n\n- `systemctl status arpwatch-spoof` — needs no root to read status usually\n- `tail -f /var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log` — log is livings:livings 0640, so livings can read it. Good.\n- `journalctl -u arpwatch-spoof -f` — works without root\n\nLet me give the commands.","time":{"start":1790457042195,"end":1790457044853}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df8e5f77001bphpf1jYP2akZX','msg_0df8e495f001fm892AAX3d05pb','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457044855,1790457047313,'{"type":"text","text":"```\nsudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh --dry-run\nsudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n```\n\nThe dry-run should now get past step 1 and print `home: /home/livings (from SUDO_USER=livings)`. If it still says `hook not executable`, stop and send me the output.\n\nThen verify it actually came up:\n\n```\nsystemctl status arpwatch-spoof --no-pager\n```\n\n```\njournalctl -u arpwatch-spoof -f\n```\n\nWatch the event log (owned by you, so no sudo needed):\n\n```\ntail -f /var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log\n```\n\nTo fire a test spoof alert without waiting for a real event:\n\n```\n~/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh --selftest\n```\n\nThat only reports config. To push an actual alert through the whole chain, run the hook directly with a fake flip-flop — it will notify you and write one `FLIP-FLOP` line to the log:\n\n```\n~/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh 10.0.0.5 aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:01 10.0.0.5 aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:02\n```\n\nOne caveat: `arpwatch` only reports hosts it sees on `wlo1`, so the log may stay quiet until there''s normal LAN traffic. The manual command above proves the chain regardless.","time":{"start":1790457044855,"end":1790457047312}}');
