       [-n net[/width]] [-xnet[/width]] [-r file]\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero systemd[1]: arpwatch-spoof.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=1/FAILURE\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero systemd[1]: arpwatch-spoof.service: Failed withresult ''exit-code''.\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero systemd[1]: arpwatch-spoof.service: Scheduled restart job immediately on client request, restart counter is at 1.\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero systemd[1]: Started ARP spoof watcher (arpwatchdatafile collector).\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero arpwatch[315374]: Version 3.9\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero arpwatch[315374]: usage: arpwatch [-CdFNpqsvzZ][-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface]\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero arpwatch[315374]:          [-P pidfile] [-w watcher@email] [-W watchee@email]\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero arpwatch[315374]:          [-n net[/width]] [-xnet[/width]] [-r file]\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero systemd[1]: arpwatch-spoof.service: Main process exited, code=exited, status=1/FAILURE\nSep 26 14:16:10 zero systemd[1]: arpwatch-spoof.service: Failed withresult ''exit-code''. "}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df942b7a00158tDiC14rYqP5x','msg_0df941d1e001rTcjtmjVNXRTuT','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457424762,1790457424763,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df942b7c001lL4Va1bO1LR1Hc','msg_0df941d1e001rTcjtmjVNXRTuT','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457424765,1790457427939,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Still failing with the same usage message. So the flags are STILL wrong. The unit installed is the NEW one (`-F -i wlo1 -f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat -s -q -N -z`) and the ExecStart in the status output confirms it. But arpwatch still prints usage and exits 1.\n\nSo which flag is invalid? Let me test the flags directly. I need root for the raw socket, but the usage error happens during argument parsing — BEFORE any socket access. So I can test as non-root and see whether it complains about args or about permissions.\n\nLet me test each flag combination as non-root and see what it says.\n\nLet me run arpwatch with the flags and observe.\n\nNote: arpwatch prints \"Version 3.9\" then usage. That happens on getopt error. Let me find which flag.\n\nPossible issues:\n- `-q` might not be valid in combination? No, it''s listed.\n- The flags listed: [-CdFNpqsvzZ] — so -C -d -F -N -p -q -s -v -z -Z are valid single letters.\n- -D arpdir, -f datafile, -i interface, -P pidfile, -w, -W, -n, -x, -r\n\nAll of -F -i -f -s -q -N -z appear valid. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the issue is that `-i` requires the interface to exist AND... no, that wouldn''t print usage.\n\nLet me actually run it and see. As non-root it should get past parsing and fail on the socket. Let me test.\n\nLet me test incrementally.","time":{"start":1790457424764,"end":1790457427938}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df9437e4001EHe1knvejaU6l6','msg_0df941d1e001rTcjtmjVNXRTuT','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457427940,1790457429485,'{"type":"text","text":"Still the usage error — so one of those flags is still wrong. The usage message prints *before* any socket access, so I can test this without root:","time":{"start":1790457427940,"end":1790457429484}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df9439170018dEIV4x6C2bu51','msg_0df941d1e001rTcjtmjVNXRTuT','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457428248,1790457429529,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_uz8dnep7gral_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== full unit command, as non-root (parse errors show before socket errors) ===\"\n/usr/sbin/arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat -s -q -N -z 2>&1 | head -5 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== bisect: which flag breaks it? ===\"\nfor combo in \"-F\" \"-F -i wlo1\" \"-F -f /tmp/t.dat\" \"-F -s\" \"-F -q\" \"-F -N\" \"-F -z\" \"-F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat\" \"-F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat -s -q -N -z\"; do\n  printf \"  %-38s \" \"$combo\"\n  out=$(/usr/sbin/arpwatch $combo 2>&1 | head -2 | tr ''\\n'' '' '')\n  case \"$out\" in\n    *usage*) echo \"USAGE ERROR\";;\n    *) echo \"parsed OK -> ${out:0:60}\";;\n  esac\ndone"},"output":"=== full unit command, as non-root (parse errors show before socket errors) ===\n  Version 3.9\n  usage: arpwatch [-CdFNpqsvzZ] [-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface]\n  \t [-P pidfile] [-w watcher@email] [-W watchee@email]\n  \t [-n net[/width]] [-x net[/width]] [-r file]\n\n=== bisect: which flag breaks it? ===\n  -F                                     parsed OK -> \n  -F -i wlo1                             parsed OK -> \n  -F -f /tmp/t.dat                       parsed OK -> \n  -F -s                                  parsed OK -> \n  -F -q                                  USAGE ERROR\n  -F -N                                  parsed OK -> \n  -F -z                                  parsed OK -> \n  -F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat               parsed OK -> \n  -F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat -s -q -N -z   USAGE ERROR\n","metadata":{"output":"=== full unit command, as non-root (parse errors show before socket errors) ===\n  Version 3.9\n  usage: arpwatch [-CdFNpqsvzZ] [-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface]\n  \t [-P pidfile] [-w watcher@email] [-W watchee@email]\n  \t [-n net[/width]] [-x net[/width]] [-r file]\n\n=== bisect: which flag breaks it? ===\n  -F                                     parsed OK -> \n  -F -i wlo1                             parsed OK -> \n  -F -f /tmp/t.dat                       parsed OK -> \n  -F -s                                  parsed OK -> \n  -F -q                                  USAGE ERROR\n  -F -N                                  parsed OK -> \n  -F -z                                  parsed OK -> \n  -F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat               parsed OK -> \n  -F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat -s -q -N -z   USAGE ERROR\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== full unit command, as non-root (parse errors show before socket errors) ===\"\n/usr/sbin/arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat -s -q -N -z 2>&1 | head -5 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== bisect: which flag breaks it? ===\"\nfor combo in \"-F\" \"-F -i wlo1\" \"-F -f /tmp/t.dat\" \"-F -s\" \"-F -q\" \"-F -N\" \"-F -z\" \"-F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat\" \"-F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat -s -q -N -z\"; do\n  printf \"  %-38s \" \"$combo\"\n  out=$(/usr/sbin/arpwatch $combo 2>&1 | head -2 | tr ''\\n'' '' '')\n  case \"$out\" in\n    *usage*) echo \"USAGE ERROR\";;\n    *) echo \"parsed OK -> ${out:0:60}\";;\n  esac\ndone","time":{"start":1790457429480,"end":1790457429528}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df943e1b001WFheHwxMsHKd4l','msg_0df941d1e001rTcjtmjVNXRTuT','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457429532,1790457429532,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":134034,"input":990,"output":297,"reasoning":356,"cache":{"write":0,"read":132391}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df944914001OsSxy4JAy1uMzZ','msg_0df943e2f001vHmADjOc89wxQU','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457432340,1790457432342,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df9449180015FCdGa1e9prefq','msg_0df943e2f001vHmADjOc89wxQU','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457432345,1790457435414,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Found it: **`-q` is the bad flag.** The man page lists `-q` in the synopsis `[-CdFNpqsvzZ]`, but the OPTIONS section says:\n\n\"The -q flag suppresses reports being logged or printed to stderr.\"\n\nYet the binary rejects `-q`. Interesting — the man page and binary disagree. The synopsis includes it but getopt rejects it. Possibly the synopsis is a typo in the man page, or `-q` requires something.\n\nLet me check: maybe the man page''s synopsis `[-CdFNpqsvzZ]` should be read as individual flags, and `-q` was documented in OPTIONS but not actually implemented in the getopt string.\n\nSo the fix: remove `-q`.\n\nLet me verify the combination without -q parses OK.\n\nLet me test.\n\nAlso note: without -q, arpwatch will log reports to syslog. That''s fine (we do our own detection); it''s just extra syslog noise. Alternatively `-d` sends reports to stderr instead of syslog and inhibits forking — but -d also inhibits backgrounding which we don''t want with -F... Actually -F prevents forking, -d inhibits forking AND sends to stderr. Using -d with -F: -F stays foreground, -d sends reports to stderr (journald). That could be useful. But simplest: drop -q.\n\nLet me confirm `-F -i wlo1 -f ... -s -N -z` parses, then check whether it gets further (needs root for socket).\n\nLet me verify and fix.","time":{"start":1790457432345,"end":1790457435413}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df9455170018ubGENBNZchQou','msg_0df943e2f001vHmADjOc89wxQU','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457435415,1790457436136,'{"type":"text","text":"Found it: **`-q` is rejected by the binary**, even though the man page lists it in the synopsis. The docs and the build disagree. Let me confirm:","time":{"start":1790457435415,"end":1790457436135}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df9455e3001EW2rkjdz78vLi0','msg_0df943e2f001vHmADjOc89wxQU','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457435619,1790457436172,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_yo7sag5d56gr_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== -q alone ===\"\n/usr/sbin/arpwatch -q 2>&1 | head -2 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho \"=== is -q really in the getopt string? try each synopsis letter ===\"\nfor f in C d F N p q s v z Z; do\n  printf \"  -%s  \" \"$f\"\n  /usr/sbin/arpwatch -$f 2>&1 | head -2 | grep -q usage && echo \"REJECTED\" || echo \"accepted\"\ndone"},"output":"=== -q alone ===\n  Version 3.9\n  usage: arpwatch [-CdFNpqsvzZ] [-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface]\n=== is -q really in the getopt string? try each synopsis letter ===\n  -C  accepted\n  -d  accepted\n  -F  accepted\n  -N  accepted\n  -p  accepted\n  -q  REJECTED\n  -s  accepted\n  -v  accepted\n  -z  accepted\n  -Z  accepted\n","metadata":{"output":"=== -q alone ===\n  Version 3.9\n  usage: arpwatch [-CdFNpqsvzZ] [-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface]\n=== is -q really in the getopt string? try each synopsis letter ===\n  -C  accepted\n  -d  accepted\n  -F  accepted\n  -N  accepted\n  -p  accepted\n  -q  REJECTED\n  -s  accepted\n  -v  accepted\n  -z  accepted\n  -Z  accepted\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== -q alone ===\"\n/usr/sbin/arpwatch -q 2>&1 | head -2 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho \"=== is -q really in the getopt string? try each synopsis letter ===\"\nfor f in C d F N p q s v z Z; do\n  printf \"  -%s  \" \"$f\"\n  /usr/sbin/arpwatch -$f 2>&1 | head -2 | grep -q usage && echo \"REJECTED\" || echo \"accepted\"\ndone","time":{"start":1790457436131,"end":1790457436171}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df94580d001VFkA6ko5BvNNob','msg_0df943e2f001vHmADjOc89wxQU','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457436173,1790457436173,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":134766,"input":238,"output":176,"reasoning":320,"cache":{"write":0,"read":134032}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df94637a001BhVsoOw9HfgMy7','msg_0df945822001nDRJ2oKsVOIcqI','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457439098,1790457439098,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df94637b0010TgP5HF6oHJlyk','msg_0df945822001nDRJ2oKsVOIcqI','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457439099,1790457441837,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: `-q` is the ONLY letter from the man page synopsis that is rejected. It''s a documentation bug in arpwatch 3.9''s man page.\n\nSo I need to remove `-q` from the unit. Let me verify the corrected command parses and gets to the socket stage.\n\nLet me test the full command without -q as non-root — it should parse and then fail on the raw socket (permission), which proves parsing is fine.\n\nLet me verify.\n\nThen decide: without -q, arpwatch logs reports to syslog. Since -s suppresses email and we do our own detection, the syslog reports are just extra noise in the journal. Options:\n- Accept the syslog noise\n- Use `-d` which sends reports to stderr instead of syslog (and inhibits forking). Combined with -F... -d inhibits forking into background, sends reports to stderr. That would put them in the journal. Not obviously better.\n- Just accept it. arpwatch reports are actually useful (they include \"flip flop\" messages!).\n\nActually — the syslog reports are VALUABLE. arpwatch''s own \"flip flop\" detection is authoritative. Since 3.9 can''t exec a hook, we could ALSO parse its syslog output. But my datafile scanner already covers it.\n\nLet me keep it simple: remove -q, accept syslog reports (they go to the journal, which is fine and gives an audit trail).\n\nLet me verify the corrected invocation and update the unit.","time":{"start":1790457439099,"end":1790457441836}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df946e2e001PrQjXfTJgXz7RR','msg_0df945822001nDRJ2oKsVOIcqI','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457441838,1790457442590,'{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed: `-q` is the **only** letter from the man page''s synopsis that the binary rejects — a documentation bug in arpwatch 3.9. Let me verify the corrected command:","time":{"start":1790457441838,"end":1790457442589}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0df946e30001MCLK267EPmSR6k','msg_0df945822001nDRJ2oKsVOIcqI','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790457441840,1790457442603,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_f8rem5iclayt_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== corrected command, no -q (as non-root: should PARSE, then fail only on the raw socket) ===\"\n/usr/sbin/arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat -s -N -z 2>&1 | head -4 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"  ^ if that says permission/pcap rather than ''usage'', parsing is fixed\""},"output":"=== corrected command, no -q (as non-root: should PARSE, then fail only on the raw socket) ===\n\n  ^ if that says permission/pcap rather than ''usage'', parsing is fixed\n","metadata":{"output":"=== corrected command, no -q (as non-root: should PARSE, then fail only on the raw socket) ===\n\n  ^ if that says permission/pcap rather than ''usage'', parsing is fixed\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== corrected command, no -q (as non-root: should PARSE, then fail only on the raw socket) ===\"\n/usr/sbin/arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /tmp/t.dat -s -N -z 2>&1 | head -4 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"  ^ if that says permission/pcap rather than ''usage'', parsing is fixed\"","time":{"start":1790457442583,"end":1790457442603}}}');
