with ''timeout 3'', treating exit 124 as success (''still running'' == flags parsed); the real signal is absence of a usage message, valid under both root and unprivileged. Probe now uses a temp datafile. All 3 paths tested. commit 54050ec\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd ~/Projects/buildlog\nbuildlog fix \"install-arpwatch-spoof.sh appeared frozen on ''already installed''. Cause: my new parse-check ran ''arpwatch ARGS'' in the foreground; under sudo that succeeds, opens the raw socket and NEVER RETURNS (-F is what keeps it foreground). The check had only ever been tested unprivileged, where arpwatch fails on the socket and exits at once -- the single case that returns. Fixed with ''timeout 3'', treating exit 124 as success (''still running'' == flags parsed); the real signal is absence of a usage message, valid under both root and unprivileged. Probe now uses a temp datafile. All 3 paths tested. commit 54050ec\" 2>&1 | tail -1","time":{"start":1790457735758,"end":1790457735778}}}');
