INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa13b85001Ocg2MwTOQ5GUqt','msg_0dfa13338001k51mhSsBnX8dLB','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458280838,1790458280839,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa13b88001zofG6EsjKaLB5u','msg_0dfa13338001k51mhSsBnX8dLB','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458280840,1790458285144,'{"type":"text","text":"Good news and bad news in that output.\n\n**Good:** the service is genuinely healthy now — `listening on wlo1`, and the timer is cycling (last 1m2s ago, next in 57s).\n\n**Bad:** `Active: active (running)` was hiding two real defects, which I''ve now fixed (`fafed7f`).\n\n1. **The scan service had no `User=`, so it ran as root.** It resolves `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` from its own uid → `/run/user/0`, a bus that doesn''t exist. Notifications would have failed *silently* while the scan reported healthy — the worst failure mode, because the log claims success. Now `User=livings` with the bus variables set explicitly.\n\n2. **The installer was truncating your datafile on every run.** It did `install /dev/null arp.dat` unconditionally, which wipes the file. That''s also why `arp.dat` looked missing to me. It was destroying collected addresses while the live collector was appending to them. Now it''s only created when absent.\n\nThat second one also revealed a third: `/var/lib/arpwatch` was `0750 root:root`, which locks `livings` out of the directory entirely no matter what the datafile''s own mode says. Now `0755`, with `spoof-state` created owned by `livings` so the scanner can rewrite its dedup state.\n\nReinstall:\n\n```\nsudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n```\n\nThen this — I need this one, since `/var/lib/arpwatch` is root-only and I can''t read it:\n\n```\nsudo ls -la /var/lib/arpwatch/ && sudo wc -l /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat\n```\n\nI want to confirm it''s actually *gaining entries* over time. A collector that runs but writes nothing looks identical to a healthy one in `systemctl status`, which is exactly what just fooled me.\n\nTo answer your question directly: don''t paste everything. Journal and `/var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log` are both readable by me, and the one thing I genuinely can''t see is that root-only directory.","time":{"start":1790458280840,"end":1790458285143}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa140050013YDLDmBl6GsCp4','msg_0dfa12dee0012LFgwb7Fk4W3aH','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458281989,1790458281990,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa14007001R3aI0HFMQG9As5','msg_0dfa12dee0012LFgwb7Fk4W3aH','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458281991,1790458284936,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now clear:\n\n1. **Units are in `~/.config/systemd/user/`, not `/etc/systemd/user`.** So they were installed by an agent (not my script), OR my script... no, my script installs to /etc/systemd/user. Since /etc/systemd/user is empty of these and ~/.config/systemd/user has them, an agent installed them as user units in the home dir. Either way: units ARE installed, active, enabled, linger=yes. Good — the reboot-persistence goal is met. My script apparently wasn''t the one that did it (or the user didn''t run it).\n\n2. **Only ONE real watcher process** (PID 287282). The other 3 \"matches\" are opencode agent processes whose command-line text contains the string \"shield-kitty-watch.py\" from the Hermes handoff prompt. So there''s no double-alert problem. My pgrep was over-counting. Good.\n\n   But note: PID 287282''s PPID is 1185 — not systemd user manager. So this is still the manually started process? Or is 1185 the systemd --user manager? Let me check. Actually if the unit is active, systemd would have started its own. Let me verify PID 287282 is the systemd-started one by checking its cgroup.\n\n3. Audit helpers NOT installed.\n\n4. Sudoers fix — unknown, need to check.\n\nSo the real state: the Shield Kitty goal appears MET (units active, enabled, linger). But I should verify the running process belongs to systemd, not a stray.\n\nLet me check the cgroup of 287282 and the sudoers state.\n\nLet me run those checks.","time":{"start":1790458281991,"end":1790458284934}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa14b8a0018K76bI1kjFPVjM','msg_0dfa12dee0012LFgwb7Fk4W3aH','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458284938,1790458287399,'{"type":"text","text":"Correction to my own alarm: there is only **one** real watcher (PID 287282). The other three \"matches\" are opencode agent processes whose command lines merely *contain* the string `shield-kitty-watch.py` from the Hermes handoff text. No double-alert risk — `pgrep -fc` over-counted.\n\nAlso: the units are in `~/.config/systemd/user/`, not `/etc/systemd/user/`, so an agent installed them, not my script. Verifying the running process actually belongs to systemd:","time":{"start":1790458284938,"end":1790458287398}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa14c5a001V4wshZQD6TPs4A','msg_0dfa13338001k51mhSsBnX8dLB','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458285146,1790458285147,'{"reason":"stop","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":47836,"input":259,"output":456,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":47121}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa15052001HeWyL1sApul6xU','msg_0dfa12dee0012LFgwb7Fk4W3aH','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458286162,1790458382389,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_j0lct1yjvzkv_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ is PID 287282 owned by systemd, or a stray? ═══\"\ncat /proc/287282/cgroup 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"  if the path contains ''systemd/user'' -> systemd owns it (good)\"\necho \"  if it shows the session scope or a bare user.slice -> stray process\"\necho\necho \"  ppid 1185 is: $(ps -o comm= -p 1185 2>/dev/null)\""},"output":"═══ is PID 287282 owned by systemd, or a stray? ═══\n  0::/user.slice/user-1000.slice/user@1000.service/app.slice/shield-kitty-watch.service\n\n  if the path contains ''systemd/user'' -> systemd owns it (good)\n  if it shows the session scope or a bare user.slice -> stray process\n\n  ppid 1185 is: systemd\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ is PID 287282 owned by systemd, or a stray? ═══\n  0::/user.slice/user-1000.slice/user@1000.service/app.slice/shield-kitty-watch.service\n\n  if the path contains ''systemd/user'' -> systemd owns it (good)\n  if it shows the session scope or a bare user.slice -> stray process\n\n  ppid 1185 is: systemd\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ is PID 287282 owned by systemd, or a stray? ═══\"\ncat /proc/287282/cgroup 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"  if the path contains ''systemd/user'' -> systemd owns it (good)\"\necho \"  if it shows the session scope or a bare user.slice -> stray process\"\necho\necho \"  ppid 1185 is: $(ps -o comm= -p 1185 2>/dev/null)\"","time":{"start":1790458286984,"end":1790458382388}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa1538a001dWu9CIKPFZ2v6h','msg_0dfa12dee0012LFgwb7Fk4W3aH','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458286986,1790458287408,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_j0lct1yjvzkv_2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ 3. SUDOERS STATE (did the repair run?) ═══\"\necho \"  --- can I see it unprivileged? ---\"\nls -1 /etc/sudoers.d 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/    /''\necho\necho \"  --- quarantine dir created? ---\"\nls -d /root/sudoers-quarantine-* 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/    /''"},"output":"═══ 3. SUDOERS STATE (did the repair run?) ═══\n  --- can I see it unprivileged? ---\n    ls: cannot open directory ''/etc/sudoers.d'': Permission denied\n\n  --- quarantine dir created? ---\n    ls: cannot access ''/root/sudoers-quarantine-*'': No such file or directory\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ 3. SUDOERS STATE (did the repair run?) ═══\n  --- can I see it unprivileged? ---\n    ls: cannot open directory ''/etc/sudoers.d'': Permission denied\n\n  --- quarantine dir created? ---\n    ls: cannot access ''/root/sudoers-quarantine-*'': No such file or directory\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ 3. SUDOERS STATE (did the repair run?) ═══\"\necho \"  --- can I see it unprivileged? ---\"\nls -1 /etc/sudoers.d 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/    /''\necho\necho \"  --- quarantine dir created? ---\"\nls -d /root/sudoers-quarantine-* 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/    /''","time":{"start":1790458287394,"end":1790458287408}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa1ffdf0028ddZ1Jr9QrjNdZ','msg_0dfa1ffdf001RyXgt6DxSMfhBV','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458331105,1790458331106,'{"type":"text","text":"sudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n=== arpwatch spoof detection installer ===\ninterface : wlo1\nhome      : /home/livings (from SUDO_USER=livings)\ndatabase  : /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat\nlog       : /var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log\n[1/6] checking prerequisites\nwlo1 present\nhook executable\n[2/6] installing arpwatch\nalready installed\nflags parse OK: arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat-s -N -z (probe exited 1, unprivileged socket denial)\n[3/6] preparing state\nexisting /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat preserved (0\nexisting /var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log preserved\n[4/6] installing units\ninstalled arpwatch-spoof.service\ninstalled arpwatch-spoof-scan.service\ninstalled arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer\n[5/6] checking scanner prerequisites\nscanner executable\n=== arpwatch-spoof-scan selftest ===\ndatafile : /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat\nstate    : /var/lib/arpwatch/spoof-state\nalert    : /home/livings/Projects/shield-kitty/shield-kitty-alert.py\nignored  : 127.0.0.0/8,0.0.0.0/8,169.254.0.0/16,fe80::/10\n\ndatafile readable, 0 entries\n\nuse ./selftest-data.sh to run the full detection suite offline\n[6/6] enabling\n\n[+] Installed. Verify:\nsystemctl status arpwatch-spoof\ntail -f /var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log\n\nA flip-flop raises a desktop notification. New hosts are logged only.\nIf the first real event shows the wrong fields, arpwatch''s argument\norder differs from what the hook assumes -- the raw args are logged,\nso adjust and re-run.  should i run the next command or wait?"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa215ee00104aOxpNT8VuB4j','msg_0dfa1fff4001JFaGvhPN0qFdfw','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458336750,1790458336751,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa215f0001xAX4ouCF2Vnbq8','msg_0dfa1fff4001JFaGvhPN0qFdfw','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458336752,1790458349961,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user asks: should I run the next command or wait?\n\nKey observation from the output: `existing /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat preserved (0` — truncated, and 0 entries. So the datafile has 0 entries. That''s the concern.\n\nAlso note the message got mangled: `arp.dat-s -N -z` — missing space. That''s a display bug in my echo (the `-f $DB -s` concatenation in the ARPWATCH_ARGS echo). Actually looking at it: `flags parse OK: arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat-s -N -z` — the space between `$DB` and `-s` was lost. That''s because... hmm, in the message I do `echo \"    flags parse OK: arpwatch $ARPWATCH_ARGS ...\"`. ARPWATCH_ARGS is a string with spaces. It shouldn''t lose a space.\n\nWait — maybe it''s terminal line-wrapping artifact. But more likely: the `-f $DB -s` ... Hmm. Actually maybe it''s just copy-paste mangling from the terminal.\n\nActually, I bet the real issue: `ARPWATCH_ARGS=\"-F -i $IFACE -f $DB -s -N -z\"`. That''s fine. The display shows `arp.dat-s`. Odd. Could be the paste.\n\nLet me not over-focus; but I should verify the actual unit''s ExecStart is correct on disk (I can read /etc/systemd/system/arpwatch-spoof.service? It''s root-owned but world-readable typically 0644). Let me check.\n\nThe CRITICAL question: datafile has 0 entries. The collector is running and \"listening on wlo1\" but has written nothing.\n\nWhy 0 entries? Possibilities:\n1. arpwatch only writes to the datafile when it sees a NEW mac→ip mapping. On a /8 with -N (no bogon) — wait, does -N suppress datafile writes? No, -N suppresses bogon *reports*, not data collection.\n2. Actually, arpwatch writes the datafile when it learns a new address. If the network is quiet or all hosts are already known... but it''s a fresh empty file, so every host seen should be a new entry.\n3. Maybe the file was just truncated by the previous install and arpwatch has the old fd / or it appends only on change.\n4. Most likely: arpwatch needs to actually see ARP traffic. On a switched Wi-Fi network, you only see ARP for your own network. It should at least see the gateway.\n5. OR: arpwatch caches its state in the datafile only, and with `-f` pointing to a file that didn''t exist when it started, it may hold the file open and write at exit/periodically.\n\nLet me answer the user''s direct question first: they should WAIT a bit, and I should investigate the 0-entry issue.\n\nActually the honest answer: yes, run the ls/wc command I asked for — but the installer already told us it''s 0 entries. So more waiting + checking is the right move, but I should first figure out whether 0 is expected or a bug.\n\nLet me check:\n- The installed unit''s ExecStart on disk (readable?)\n- Whether arpwatch is actually writing (strace needs root; can''t)\n- The datafile mtime\n\nLet me check what I can read.\n\nLet me tell the user: yes, run the check command. But also: 0 entries is the thing to investigate. Let me look at whether the file is being written at all.\n\nImportant: I should check if arpwatch actually appends. One known behavior: arpwatch 3.9 writes to the datafile only when it detects a new mac/ip pair, and it may buffer. Let me check the file mtime — if it''s recent and still 0 bytes, arpwatch isn''t writing.\n\nLet me look at what I can.\n\nAlso that mangled line: `existing /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat preserved (0` — the closing paren and \"entries)\" is missing. My echo was:\n```\necho \"    existing $DB preserved ($(wc -l <\"$DB\" 2>/dev/null || echo ''?'' entries)\"\n```\nBug! The `||` inside command substitution: `wc -l <\"$DB\" 2>/dev/null || echo ''?'' entries` — the `entries` is OUTSIDE the quotes so it''s a separate word appended after `?`. So it echoes `0 entries`? Actually `echo ''?'' entries` → prints \"? entries\". Hmm, that would give \"0 entries)\" which is right-ish.\n\nBut the output shows `(0` then newline. So the subshell output was just \"0\"? That means... the paste got cut. Or the `entries` word was parsed oddly.\n\nActually wait: `$(wc -l <\"$DB\" 2>/dev/null || echo ''?'' entries)` — this is inside `echo \"...\"`. The `$( )` contains `wc ... || echo ''?'' entries`. That should work.\n\nHmm, but there''s a real bug: this is a `sudo` script and `$DB` is root-owned 0644, readable. wc should work.\n\nThe displayed output is likely mangled by the terminal/paste. But the \"0\" is clear: 0 entries.\n\nLet me focus on the real question: is 0 entries correct?\n\nLet me investigate on my side what I can, then give a clear recommendation.\n\nLet me check the installed unit file and whether I can see anything.\n\nLet me run checks.\n\nI should answer concisely: \"Yes, run it — but the more important finding is 0 entries.\"\n\nLet me investigate first.","time":{"start":1790458336752,"end":1790458349958}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa2498a001pdBCYlHBt3LoN3','msg_0dfa1fff4001JFaGvhPN0qFdfw','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458349963,1790458351831,'{"type":"text","text":"Yes, run it — but the installer already answered it, and the answer is the real problem: **`(0` entries**. The datafile exists but is empty.\n\nAlso, I spotted a display bug in my own installer: the line reads `arp.dat-s -N -z` (missing space) and `preserved (0` (truncated). Let me check whether those are real or just paste artifacts:","time":{"start":1790458349963,"end":1790458351830}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa24cb9001M0K3bCSpy0xtZC','msg_0dfa1fff4001JFaGvhPN0qFdfw','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458350777,1790458351853,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_arb25kf506yg_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== installed unit: is ExecStart correct on disk? (readable?) ===\"\nsudo -n true 2>/dev/null && echo \"  have passwordless sudo\" || echo \"  no passwordless sudo (expected)\"\nfor f in /etc/systemd/system/arpwatch-spoof.service; do\n  if [ -r \"$f\" ]; then echo \"  READABLE\"; grep \"^ExecStart\" \"$f\" | sed ''s/^/    /''; else echo \"  not readable without sudo\"; fi\ndone\necho\necho \"=== the exact echo lines in my installer (source of the odd output) ===\"\ngrep -n ''flags parse OK'' ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''\ngrep -n ''preserved'' ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''"},"output":"=== installed unit: is ExecStart correct on disk? (readable?) ===\n  no passwordless sudo (expected)\n  READABLE\n    ExecStart=/usr/sbin/arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat -s -N -z\n\n=== the exact echo lines in my installer (source of the odd output) ===\n  101:        echo \"    flags parse OK: arpwatch $ARPWATCH_ARGS (still running when the probe timed out)\"\n  103:        echo \"    flags parse OK: arpwatch $ARPWATCH_ARGS (probe exited $probe_rc, unprivileged socket denial)\"\n  118:    echo \"    existing $DB preserved ($(wc -l <\"$DB\" 2>/dev/null || echo ''?'' entries)\"\n  130:    echo \"    existing $LOG preserved\"\n","metadata":{"output":"=== installed unit: is ExecStart correct on disk? (readable?) ===\n  no passwordless sudo (expected)\n  READABLE\n    ExecStart=/usr/sbin/arpwatch -F -i wlo1 -f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat -s -N -z\n\n=== the exact echo lines in my installer (source of the odd output) ===\n  101:        echo \"    flags parse OK: arpwatch $ARPWATCH_ARGS (still running when the probe timed out)\"\n  103:        echo \"    flags parse OK: arpwatch $ARPWATCH_ARGS (probe exited $probe_rc, unprivileged socket denial)\"\n  118:    echo \"    existing $DB preserved ($(wc -l <\"$DB\" 2>/dev/null || echo ''?'' entries)\"\n  130:    echo \"    existing $LOG preserved\"\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== installed unit: is ExecStart correct on disk? (readable?) ===\"\nsudo -n true 2>/dev/null && echo \"  have passwordless sudo\" || echo \"  no passwordless sudo (expected)\"\nfor f in /etc/systemd/system/arpwatch-spoof.service; do\n  if [ -r \"$f\" ]; then echo \"  READABLE\"; grep \"^ExecStart\" \"$f\" | sed ''s/^/    /''; else echo \"  not readable without sudo\"; fi\ndone\necho\necho \"=== the exact echo lines in my installer (source of the odd output) ===\"\ngrep -n ''flags parse OK'' ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''\ngrep -n ''preserved'' ~/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''","time":{"start":1790458351825,"end":1790458351852}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa250ee001Hl5MU6nWFZYoEo','msg_0dfa1fff4001JFaGvhPN0qFdfw','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458351854,1790458351855,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":49811,"input":461,"output":306,"reasoning":1210,"cache":{"write":0,"read":47834}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa25933001sjxW5KdwULxh8f','msg_0dfa25105001wUTpltyEmZo37B','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458353972,1790458353973,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa25936001h53aN8BAVXJx32','msg_0dfa25105001wUTpltyEmZo37B','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458353974,1790458373207,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Good:\n- The installed ExecStart is CORRECT: `-f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat -s -N -z` with proper space. So `arp.dat-s` was a paste artifact.\n- Line 118: `echo \"    existing $DB preserved ($(wc -l <\"$DB\" 2>/dev/null || echo ''?'' entries)\"` — the `entries` is a bare word after `echo ''?''`, so `echo ''?'' entries` → prints `? entries`. That works but is sloppy. Actually wait, if wc succeeds it prints just the number, so output = \"0\" and then `)` — we got \"(0\" then cut. So \"entries\" is missing in output, meaning the `||` branch wasn''t taken and wc printed \"0\". Then `)` should follow. So \"(0)\" — the user pasted \"(0\" and it got cut. Fine, paste artifact.\n\nBut it IS a latent bug: if wc fails, it prints \"? entries\" — fine. Actually no bug, just sloppy quoting. The bigger issue: it doesn''t say \"entries\" on success. Let me fix that for clarity.\n\nNow the REAL issue: 0 entries in the datafile.\n\nWhy? Let me think about arpwatch 3.9 behavior.\n\narpwatch maintains a database file. With `-f datafile`, it reads existing entries and appends new ones. It writes an entry when it sees a new MAC/IP binding.\n\nOn this host: the laptop is on a /8 WLAN. arpwatch on wlo1 should see:\n- The gateway''s ARP (10.128.128.128)\n- Other hosts on the same subnet — but on a switched network, only broadcast ARP reaches the host.\n\nSo at minimum the gateway should appear. Unless the gateway was already learned before the datafile was created and arpwatch doesn''t re-log it.\n\nKey insight: arpwatch only records a mac→ip pair the FIRST time it sees it. If the datafile was truncated/created empty AFTER arpwatch already had state, or if the ARP cache is warm, arpwatch may not see \"new\" bindings.\n\nBut more importantly: does arpwatch write to the datafile continuously, or only on new bindings? Only on new bindings. So a quiet network = empty file.\n\nAlso, does arpwatch re-append entries it reads at startup? Some implementations rewrite the whole DB. Here 0 entries after ~a minute of running.\n\nAnother possibility: `-N` (no bogon). Does -N suppress datafile writes for bogon addresses? Possibly! arpwatch 3.9''s -N might skip *processing* of bogon addresses entirely, not just reporting. On a /8 where the local subnet is 10.197.250.0/24-ish but the address space is /8, the gateway 10.128.128.128 might be considered a \"bogon\" relative to the interface''s configured prefix (10.197.250.246/8 → actually with /8 the prefix is 10.0.0.0/8 so 10.128.128.128 is NOT a bogon).\n\nHmm. Let me think about what -N does: \"Don''t report bogon addresses.\" arpwatch defines bogon as addresses not on the same network as the interface. With a /8 mask, 10.128.128.128 IS on 10.0.0.0/8 → not a bogon. So -N shouldn''t block it.\n\nSo why 0 entries?\n\nPossibility: arpwatch needs actual ARP traffic. On a switched WLAN, the laptop only ARPs for the gateway when its cache expires. If the ARP cache is warm, there''s no ARP traffic at all. arpwatch listens passively — no traffic = no entries. Over time (cache expiry, DHCP renew, new hosts), entries will appear.\n\nThat''s the most likely benign explanation: passive listener + warm ARP cache + switched network = nothing to record yet.\n\nHow to test decisively? Generate ARP traffic. Options:\n- `ping` the gateway (forces ARP resolution if cache is cold) — but cache is warm.\n- Flush the ARP cache: `sudo ip neigh flush all` then ping gateway → forces ARP request/reply → arpwatch sees it → should write an entry.\n\nThat''s the decisive test! And it also proves the whole pipeline.\n\nBut careful: flushing the ARP cache is safe (it just re-resolves). Low risk.\n\nAlso: arpwatch might need `-p` off (promiscuous). Default is promiscuous on, which on a switched network still doesn''t help much.\n\nLet me propose:\n```\nsudo ip neigh flush all\nping -c1 10.128.128.128\nsudo wc -l /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat\n```\n\nActually simpler and non-disruptive: `ping -c 3 <gateway>` a few times won''t force ARP if cached. Flushing does.\n\nAlternative that doesn''t touch the system: arpwatch may respond to any ARP. We could just wait — DHCP renewals and neighbors will generate traffic.\n\nLet me also consider: maybe arpwatch needs the `-n` option (netmask) or the datafile gets written only on exit with a flush. Let me check if there''s a known behavior: arpwatch writes the datafile on each new binding immediately (it calls save_db). So no flush needed.\n\nLet me verify my claim about arpwatch being passive and needing ARP traffic. I can test this myself in a limited way? I''d need root to open the raw socket. Can''t.\n\nBut I CAN test the scanner against the real (empty) datafile — already did via --selftest (it said \"datafile readable, 0 entries\").\n\nSo my recommendation: run the decisive test — flush ARP cache + ping gateway, then check the datafile.\n\nLet me give that, plus explain the two paste artifacts are cosmetic.\n\nLet me also fix the two cosmetic installer issues:\n1. line 118: add \"entries\" suffix\n2. The `arp.dat-s` — confirmed a paste artifact since disk has the right value. Nothing to fix. But why did it happen? Possibly the terminal. Not a code bug. Actually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider. In the parse-check I build the probe command separately (not from ARPWATCH_ARGS), and print ARPWATCH_ARGS. ARGWATCH_ARGS has proper spaces. So the display is fine. Paste artifact. Good.\n\nLet me fix line 118 for clarity and commit, then give the test.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether -N could be suppressing. Let me test what arpwatch considers. I can''t easily.\n\nBut here''s a thought: I should double-check whether the datafile is even being opened/appended by checking its mtime and size. The user can do that. Let me fold it into the command.\n\nLet me be concise now. The user asked a simple question: run next command or wait?\n\nAnswer: run the decisive test (don''t just wait) — here''s the command. And explain 0 entries is expected-ish for a passive listener but we must prove it.\n\nLet me fix the cosmetic label and commit quickly, then answer.\n\nLet me do the edit.","time":{"start":1790458353974,"end":1790458373204}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa2a459001CY97jRvLi6PVF0','msg_0dfa25105001wUTpltyEmZo37B','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458373210,1790458374231,'{"type":"text","text":"Two of those are paste artifacts — the installed unit on disk is correct: `-f /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat -s -N -z`, with the proper space. The `(0` is my sloppy quoting on line 118. Let me fix that label:","time":{"start":1790458373209,"end":1790458374231}}');
