INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa7c2be001Qx7PoR7erLW3gp','msg_0dfa7bc48001flwJ4SjHFYwHtv','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458708670,1790458708672,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa7c2c1001aaazCul80OSgox','msg_0dfa7bc48001flwJ4SjHFYwHtv','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458708673,1790458713049,'{"type":"text","text":"Please paste what that command prints. The possible outcomes:\n\n- **Dialog appears, lists the file** → `pkexec` works, the restore path just needs re-running\n- **Dialog appears, \"No such file or directory\"** → the quarantine copy is gone; I''ll have `pkexec` write the stock rule directly\n- **\"Error executing command as another user: Not authorized\"** → polkit is refusing; we''ll need the recovery route instead\n\nI held off on the fix until I know which, because each has a different command and I''d rather not have you paste another password into a script that can''t work.\n\n## Meanwhile, to be clear about the risk\n\nThis is a sudo-policy problem, **not** a boot problem. Your system will boot fine and everything else is intact:\n\n- Firewall strict, `wlo1` in `workstation`, only `dhcpv6-client`\n- fail2ban active, recidive true\n- Hostname `zero`, MAC randomized (`ea:bd:c8:4d:53:a8`)\n- Shield Kitty units active/enabled with `linger=yes` — survives reboot\n- No pacman lock, 0 updates, 0 failed units, 0 queued jobs\n\nNothing about the reboot depends on sudo working. The only thing you cannot currently do is install packages or edit root-owned config, which is an inconvenience rather than a risk to the machine.\n\nIf polkit also refuses, the fallback is booting a live USB and running the restore from there — say so and I''ll walk you through it. But try `pkexec` first; on a KDE session with a running polkit agent it normally just works.\n\nI''d also rather not run another sudoers-touching script until this is closed. `fix-sudoers-permissions.sh` needs a hard guarantee that it never leaves the invoking user without sudo, and it doesn''t have that yet.","time":{"start":1790458708673,"end":1790458713046}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa7c2ce001f4TVd7bAhZDUlX','msg_0dfa7bdd90010TEEcYwzp6BIzM','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458708686,1790458708687,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa7c2d1001kDLXeqF2Zpi5LG','msg_0dfa7bdd90010TEEcYwzp6BIzM','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458708689,1790458723558,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have important info:\n\n`pam_faillock.so authfail` with `[default=die]` — this is the standard Fedora/Arch pattern. When faillock locks the account, it returns an error → default=die → auth fails → sudo cannot authenticate.\n\nThe user IS in wheel.\n\n`/var/run/sudo` is not readable — normal (0700 root).\n\n`/etc/sudoers.d` is drwxr-x--- root:root — that''s actually the Arch default? Let me think. Arch''s sudo package creates /etc/sudoers.d with 0750 root:root. Yes, I believe that''s default on Arch. So not a change.\n\nSo the most likely cause: **the account got faillocked**. The `[default=die]` on authfail means once locked, sudo dies immediately.\n\nHow could they get locked? 10 failed attempts. Did something cause repeated auth failures?\n\nOH WAIT. I think I see it. My installer''s parse-check:\n\n```\nif probe_out=\"$(timeout 3 arpwatch ...)\"\n```\nNo auth there.\n\nHmm, but what about the nested sudo calls? My installer calls `sudo install ...`, `sudo systemctl ...`. Those don''t prompt.\n\nWhat could cause repeated auth failures...\n\nActually — think about `pam_faillock.so preauth` + `authfail` + `authsucc` and the deploy script. The deploy script was NOT run. So no admin_group. Default faillock config: deny=10? Actually the LIVE config has deny10 unlock30 per earlier notes. So 10 failures within the window → lock for 30 min (unlock_time 30).\n\nThe user ran `sudo install-arpwatch-spoof.sh` multiple times. Between them I made changes. If a typo in MY script caused a wrong password prompt... no, sudo wouldn''t re-prompt inside a script.\n\nHmm — but actually there''s a classic: if `sudo` is invoked and the user mistypes, it counts. But the user says it''s broken now.\n\nThe key diagnostic: the user needs to tell me the actual error. Common sudo errors:\n- \"sudo: 3 incorrect password attempts\" → faillock\n- \"user is not in the sudoers file\" → wheel rule missing\n- \"sudo: unable to resolve host\" → hostname issue\n- PAM errors\n\nI should ask for the exact message. That''s the responsible move rather than guessing.\n\nBut I can also give them a path forward:\n- If faillock: wait 30 min (unlock_time=30), or reboot? A reboot doesn''t clear faillock (it''s on disk). Actually faillock state is in /var/lib/faillock/ — survives reboot. So they''d wait 30 min or need root another way.\n- If it''s the \"not in sudoers\" issue, that''s serious.\n\nIMPORTANT: If faillock locked them, they need a recovery path. The recovery is: wait unlock_time (30 min), or boot to a rescue/live environment.\n\nGiven the earlier finding: unlock_time = 30 (minutes), deny = 10. So a 30-minute wait clears it.\n\nBut hold on — is that right? `unlock_time 30` in pam_faillock means 30 SECONDS or 30 minutes? In pam_faillock, `unlock_time` is in SECONDS by default. Earlier notes said \"unlock30\" meaning unlock_time=30 → 30 seconds! That''s very short.\n\nHmm, earlier summary said \"live config deny10 unlock30\". In pam_faillock, unlock_time is specified in seconds. So 30 seconds. That would mean locks clear in 30s — so a persistent lock is unlikely to be the cause.\n\nWait, but earlier I noted \"unlock_time 30 remains, no permanent 0\" and the deploy script had `--reset`. And I noted a recovery command `sudo faillock --user livings --reset`. If unlock_time=30 seconds, locks self-clear quickly.\n\nHmm, so faillock is less likely to be a persistent cause.\n\nLet me reconsider. What else?\n\nCritical: I should check the sudoers wheel rule. I can read /etc/sudoers (it''s 0440 root:root — NOT readable by me!). `-r--r----- 1 root root` → mode 440? The listing shows `-r--r-----` = 0440. So I can''t read it. Interesting: Arch default is 0440.\n\nAnd /etc/sudoers.d is 0750 root:root.\n\nSo I can''t read either sudo config. I can only ask.\n\nLet me be honest and efficient: ask for the exact error message, and give the most likely causes with the fix for each. Also offer a recovery path that doesn''t need sudo if needed.\n\nLet me also check one more thing I CAN read: whether the recent arpwatch install corrupted anything, and whether there''s a systemd unit failing. Also check if `su` is available as an alternative.\n\nActually, one more important check: PAM. If pam_faillock is denying, the user would see it at the sudo password prompt. Let me read the full pam_faillock line args — the earlier notes said the live config has \"audit=silent malformed\" and deny10/unlock30. Let me see the actual args.\n\nFrom the grep, line 3/8/11 show only `preauth`, `authfail`, `authsucc` — the args (deny=, unlock_time=) must be elsewhere. In the standard Arch file, there''s usually a commented block or the args are on those lines. The grep showed no deny=/unlock_time= args! So pam_faillock is using DEFAULTS.\n\npam_faillock defaults: deny=3? Actually pam_faillock default deny is 3 (or 5). unlock_time default 600 seconds? Let me recall: pam_faillock defaults are deny=3, unlock_time=600? Hmm. Actually the man page: `deny` default 3, `unlock_time` default 600 (seconds)? I''m not certain.\n\nLet me look at the actual file to see if there are deny/unlock_time args anywhere.\n\nLet me grep the whole file for the args and also check /etc/security/faillock.conf (the faillock.conf I edited!).\n\nIMPORTANT: I edited `~/Projects/zen-hardening/faillock.conf` but the LIVE one is `/etc/security/faillock.conf`. If faillock.conf in /etc/security has `deny` and `unlock_time` settings, those apply. pam_faillock reads /etc/security/faillock.conf by default.\n\nBut wait — does pam_faillock read /etc/security/faillock.conf? Yes, pam_faillock.so reads /etc/security/faillock.conf.\n\nBut earlier notes said the live config has the args in the PAM lines (\"live config deny10 unlock30 audit=silent malformed\"). The grep now shows no args. Hmm, the earlier note may have been about a different file or about my own conf file.\n\nLet me just read /etc/security/faillock.conf — that''s readable probably (0644).\n\nLet me check it. This is the most likely culprit for a sudo lockout.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nAlso, I must be careful: if faillock is denying them, I should give a recovery path. And critically — I should NOT have deployed anything. I didn''t. Good.\n\nLet me check /etc/security/faillock.conf.","time":{"start":1790458708689,"end":1790458723555}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa7d3dd001WO08POIX0UGHyi','msg_0dfa7bc48001flwJ4SjHFYwHtv','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458713053,1790458713054,'{"reason":"stop","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":115226,"input":33,"output":388,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":114805}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa7fce8001D5D3VIfUTF360U','msg_0dfa7bdd90010TEEcYwzp6BIzM','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458723561,1790458724962,'{"type":"text","text":"`pam_faillock` is live with `[default=die]` on authfail — that''s the one thing here that can hard-deny `sudo`, and it has **no `deny=`/`unlock_time=` args on those lines**, so it''s using defaults. Let me check the config file it actually reads:","time":{"start":1790458723560,"end":1790458724961}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa7ff24001OW1ZC0PKzreTdO','msg_0dfa7bdd90010TEEcYwzp6BIzM','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458724133,1790458724973,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_2xs33fz54bhw_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== /etc/security/faillock.conf (what pam_faillock.so reads) ===\"\nif [ -r /etc/security/faillock.conf ]; then grep -vE ''^\\s*#|^\\s*$'' /etc/security/faillock.conf | sed ''s/^/  /''; echo \"  --- (uncommented lines above) ---\"; else echo \"  NOT READABLE\"; fi\necho\necho \"=== full pam_faillock stanza with all args ===\"\nsed -n ''1,15p'' /etc/pam.d/system-auth | grep -n \"faillock\" | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== sddm-autologin also has it? ===\"\ngrep -n \"faillock\" /etc/pam.d/sddm-autologin 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''"},"output":"=== /etc/security/faillock.conf (what pam_faillock.so reads) ===\n  deny = 10\n  unlock_time = 30\n  audit = silent\n  --- (uncommented lines above) ---\n\n=== full pam_faillock stanza with all args ===\n  3:auth       required                    pam_faillock.so      preauth\n  8:auth       [default=die]               pam_faillock.so      authfail\n  11:auth       required                    pam_faillock.so      authsucc\n  12:# If you drop the above call to pam_faillock.so the lock will be done also\n\n=== sddm-autologin also has it? ===\n  3:auth        required    pam_faillock.so preauth\n","metadata":{"output":"=== /etc/security/faillock.conf (what pam_faillock.so reads) ===\n  deny = 10\n  unlock_time = 30\n  audit = silent\n  --- (uncommented lines above) ---\n\n=== full pam_faillock stanza with all args ===\n  3:auth       required                    pam_faillock.so      preauth\n  8:auth       [default=die]               pam_faillock.so      authfail\n  11:auth       required                    pam_faillock.so      authsucc\n  12:# If you drop the above call to pam_faillock.so the lock will be done also\n\n=== sddm-autologin also has it? ===\n  3:auth        required    pam_faillock.so preauth\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== /etc/security/faillock.conf (what pam_faillock.so reads) ===\"\nif [ -r /etc/security/faillock.conf ]; then grep -vE ''^\\s*#|^\\s*$'' /etc/security/faillock.conf | sed ''s/^/  /''; echo \"  --- (uncommented lines above) ---\"; else echo \"  NOT READABLE\"; fi\necho\necho \"=== full pam_faillock stanza with all args ===\"\nsed -n ''1,15p'' /etc/pam.d/system-auth | grep -n \"faillock\" | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== sddm-autologin also has it? ===\"\ngrep -n \"faillock\" /etc/pam.d/sddm-autologin 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/  /''","time":{"start":1790458724956,"end":1790458724972}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa8026e001eLqMYqNf0WgDOL','msg_0dfa7bdd90010TEEcYwzp6BIzM','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458724974,1790458724975,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":67922,"input":196,"output":282,"reasoning":1548,"cache":{"write":0,"read":65896}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa807b0001Cbm49uuZi2TU8N','msg_0dfa802970010qjq6xhFHoS8ZS','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458726320,1790458726321,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa807b2001IHHH3v9G3arnDc','msg_0dfa802970010qjq6xhFHoS8ZS','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458726322,1790458732736,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"So the live config:\n- deny = 10\n- unlock_time = 30 (SECONDS in pam_faillock!)\n- audit = silent\n\npam_faillock''s `unlock_time` is in SECONDS. So 30 seconds. That means a lock clears in 30 seconds. So a *persistent* lockout is very unlikely.\n\nWait — is unlock_time in seconds? Yes, pam_faillock''s unlock_time is in seconds. So 30s. Locks clear almost immediately. So faillock is NOT a plausible cause of a *persistent* sudo failure.\n\nHmm. But if the user is retrying repeatedly, they could re-lock every 30s while typing. deny=10 means 10 failures needed. If they typed wrong 10 times in 30s, lock for 30s. But they''d get in after 30s.\n\nSo likely NOT faillock.\n\nSo what else? I genuinely don''t know without the error message.\n\nLet me reconsider what I did that could break sudo:\n1. The installer runs `sudo` commands nested inside a sudo-invoked script. Nested sudo is generally fine.\n2. `install -d -m 0755 /var/lib/arpwatch` — harmless.\n3. `systemctl daemon-reload`, `enable --now` — harmless.\n4. Nothing touched /etc/sudoers, /etc/pam.d, or /etc/security.\n\nIMPORTANT: My deploy-faillock-admin.sh was NOT run (enroll.lock absent, no shield-admin group presumably). So I didn''t change PAM.\n\nSo most likely cause is unrelated to my work: e.g., the wheel sudoers rule, or a PAM config change, or the user hit deny=10 and is in a retry loop, or something else entirely.\n\nWait — actually, there''s a REALLY important possibility I should check. `pam_faillock.so preauth` is in `/etc/pam.d/sddm-autologin`. If the faillock state got weird... no.\n\nAnother strong possibility: **the user is being asked for a password and doesn''t realize the password prompt isn''t sudo''s**. Or `sudo` is prompting but they typed the wrong thing.\n\nHonestly: I need the error message. Let me ask, but also give a quick decision tree and a no-sudo recovery path.\n\nLet me check a few more things that are readable and could matter:\n- Is there a /etc/sudoers.d file that might contain something? Not readable (0750).\n- Hmm, but I could check the FILE NAMES if the dir were readable. It''s not.\n\nLet me check whether the system has any recent pam changes: stat /etc/pam.d/system-auth mtime. If it changed today, someone/something modified it.\n\nLet me check mtimes of relevant files — that tells me if something changed today vs months ago.\n\nThat''s genuinely useful: if /etc/pam.d/system-auth was modified today, that''s a clue.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nLet me stat:\n- /etc/pam.d/system-auth\n- /etc/security/faillock.conf\n- /etc/sudoers\n- /etc/pam.d/sudo (if exists)\n- /etc/pam.d/sudo-local\n\nAnd also check faillock''s on-disk state file if readable (/var/lib/faillock/ by user).\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790458726322,"end":1790458732734}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa820c1001Mt32exocuK7qS9','msg_0dfa802970010qjq6xhFHoS8ZS','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458732737,1790458734075,'{"type":"text","text":"`unlock_time = 30` in pam_faillock is **seconds**, and `deny = 10` — so any lock clears in 30 seconds. That makes a *persistent* faillock lockout unlikely. Let me check whether anything actually changed today:","time":{"start":1790458732737,"end":1790458734074}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa822be001Qr74r52k1AK3a9','msg_0dfa802970010qjq6xhFHoS8ZS','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458733247,1790458734088,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_2ohl2cbdznnk_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"=== mtimes: has anything in the auth path changed today? ===\"\nfor f in /etc/pam.d/system-auth /etc/pam.d/sudo /etc/security/faillock.conf /etc/sudoers /etc/login.defs; do\n  stat -c ''  %y  %n'' \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || echo \"  (absent)   $f\"\ndone\necho\necho \"=== faillock on-disk tally (readable?) ===\"\nls -la /var/lib/faillock/ 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== today is: ===\"\ndate ''+  %Y-%m-%d''"},"output":"=== mtimes: has anything in the auth path changed today? ===\n  2026-06-15 21:40:03.000000000 -0700  /etc/pam.d/system-auth\n  2026-06-16 02:31:42.000000000 -0700  /etc/pam.d/sudo\n  2026-09-26 12:31:51.757786861 -0700  /etc/security/faillock.conf\n  2026-06-16 02:31:42.000000000 -0700  /etc/sudoers\n  2026-07-30 03:04:45.000000000 -0700  /etc/login.defs\n\n=== faillock on-disk tally (readable?) ===\n  ls: cannot access ''/var/lib/faillock/'': No such file or directory\n\n=== today is: ===\n  2026-09-26\n","metadata":{"output":"=== mtimes: has anything in the auth path changed today? ===\n  2026-06-15 21:40:03.000000000 -0700  /etc/pam.d/system-auth\n  2026-06-16 02:31:42.000000000 -0700  /etc/pam.d/sudo\n  2026-09-26 12:31:51.757786861 -0700  /etc/security/faillock.conf\n  2026-06-16 02:31:42.000000000 -0700  /etc/sudoers\n  2026-07-30 03:04:45.000000000 -0700  /etc/login.defs\n\n=== faillock on-disk tally (readable?) ===\n  ls: cannot access ''/var/lib/faillock/'': No such file or directory\n\n=== today is: ===\n  2026-09-26\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"=== mtimes: has anything in the auth path changed today? ===\"\nfor f in /etc/pam.d/system-auth /etc/pam.d/sudo /etc/security/faillock.conf /etc/sudoers /etc/login.defs; do\n  stat -c ''  %y  %n'' \"$f\" 2>/dev/null || echo \"  (absent)   $f\"\ndone\necho\necho \"=== faillock on-disk tally (readable?) ===\"\nls -la /var/lib/faillock/ 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== today is: ===\"\ndate ''+  %Y-%m-%d''","time":{"start":1790458734069,"end":1790458734088}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa8260a001NXoCwLIdQblI9Q','msg_0dfa802970010qjq6xhFHoS8ZS','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458734090,1790458734091,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":69023,"input":183,"output":230,"reasoning":690,"cache":{"write":0,"read":67920}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa82d8a0013dOycCyVzh9CCx','msg_0dfa82621001La9C7GhYsKNNFC','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458736010,1790458736010,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfa82d8b001rVpU1HPhRygf5b','msg_0dfa82621001La9C7GhYsKNNFC','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790458736011,1790458739620,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Very interesting! `/etc/security/faillock.conf` was modified TODAY at 12:31:51. That''s today. Not by me (I only edited `~/Projects/zen-hardening/faillock.conf`, a repo copy).\n\nAlso: `/var/lib/faillock/` does not exist. pam_faillock''s default tally dir is /var/lib/faillock. If it doesn''t exist, pam_faillock may fail... Actually pam_faillock creates it as root. If it doesn''t exist and can''t be created, faillock could error.\n\nHmm, but more importantly: /etc/security/faillock.conf modified today at 12:31. Who did that? Not me. The user? Or the deploy script? The deploy script wasn''t run (enroll.lock absent). But maybe the user ran parts of it?\n\nWait — earlier I noted the live config had \"audit=silent malformed\" and deny10/unlock30. And the repo copy I fixed. So the live /etc/security/faillock.conf was likely written today at 12:31 by... the user manually? Or by my earlier deploy instructions? The summary said the deploy script was prepared but not run.\n\nHmm, but the mtime says today 12:31. My session has been going on today. Possibly the user ran `deploy-faillock-admin.sh` at some point? But enroll.lock is absent, and the script''s deploy would create it.\n\nActually — maybe the user ran an earlier version of the deploy, or ran the config install step only.\n\nRegardless: /etc/security/faillock.conf was touched today, and the important question is whether its contents are valid. `audit = silent` — in pam_faillock.conf, valid options are `audit` (no value) or `silent` (no value), i.e. the syntax is:\n```\naudit\nsilent\n```\nNOT `audit = silent`. Hmm, actually pam_faillock.conf uses `option = value` format? Let me think.\n\n","time":{"start":1790458736011,"end":1790458739620}}');
