INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfab1b27001FDtDF3bnf8CXgu','msg_0dfab1196001tXPxLEY6ztPFcq','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458927911,1790458931195,'{"type":"text","text":"All three invocation paths now resolve to `livings`. Same command:\n\n```bash\npkexec bash /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/RECOVER-SUDO.sh\n```\n\nIt should print `sudo recovery for: livings`, restore the wheel rule, fix the four permission files, and install the audit helpers — in that order, verifying sudo before it installs anything else.","time":{"start":1790458927911,"end":1790458931194}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfab1d8d001FjpQphy9Q5bMtA','msg_0dfab1196001tXPxLEY6ztPFcq','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458928525,1790458932943,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_zxh9aeurj0u4_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/opencode/hss\ncp ~/Projects/zen-hardening/RECOVER-SUDO.sh config/zen-hardening/\ngit add config/zen-hardening/RECOVER-SUDO.sh\ngit -c user.name=opencode -c user.email=opencode@localhost commit -q -m \"Add RECOVER-SUDO.sh: non-destructive sudo recovery\n\nRestores sudo after the sudoers lockout on 2026-09-26 without deleting\nor moving any sudoers file. Quarantine is removed as a concept, because\nthe outage happened when a script removed the only grant that let the\naccount run it.\n\nOrdering is the fix: restore the caller''s own sudo access first, verify\nit behaviourally with an actual sudo call, and only then install the\naudit helpers. Previously two scripts each removed one of the two ways to\nrun the other, so the second failure cascaded into a lockout.\n\nResolves the invoking user from PKEXEC_UID. pkexec does not set\nSUDO_USER and USER is root under pkexec, so the first version audited\nroot and aborted with a misleading ''root is not in group wheel''. It\nfailed safe, before making changes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\ngit push -q origin HEAD 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /'' && echo \"  [✓] pushed\" || echo \"  [✗] failed\"\ngit log --oneline -1 | sed ''s/^/  /''"},"output":"  [✓] pushed\n  983bfea Add RECOVER-SUDO.sh: non-destructive sudo recovery\n","metadata":{"output":"  [✓] pushed\n  983bfea Add RECOVER-SUDO.sh: non-destructive sudo recovery\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /tmp/opencode/hss\ncp ~/Projects/zen-hardening/RECOVER-SUDO.sh config/zen-hardening/\ngit add config/zen-hardening/RECOVER-SUDO.sh\ngit -c user.name=opencode -c user.email=opencode@localhost commit -q -m \"Add RECOVER-SUDO.sh: non-destructive sudo recovery\n\nRestores sudo after the sudoers lockout on 2026-09-26 without deleting\nor moving any sudoers file. Quarantine is removed as a concept, because\nthe outage happened when a script removed the only grant that let the\naccount run it.\n\nOrdering is the fix: restore the caller''s own sudo access first, verify\nit behaviourally with an actual sudo call, and only then install the\naudit helpers. Previously two scripts each removed one of the two ways to\nrun the other, so the second failure cascaded into a lockout.\n\nResolves the invoking user from PKEXEC_UID. pkexec does not set\nSUDO_USER and USER is root under pkexec, so the first version audited\nroot and aborted with a misleading ''root is not in group wheel''. It\nfailed safe, before making changes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /''\ngit push -q origin HEAD 2>&1 | sed ''s/^/  /'' && echo \"  [✓] pushed\" || echo \"  [✗] failed\"\ngit log --oneline -1 | sed ''s/^/  /''","time":{"start":1790458931191,"end":1790458932942}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfab2ed1001noUENVckx6twLW','msg_0dfab1196001tXPxLEY6ztPFcq','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458932945,1790458932945,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":120193,"input":94,"output":455,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":119644}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfab378c001rF7U5V1KP1CxWI','msg_0dfab2f0b001ylTAItoH0bo6yZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458935181,1790458935182,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfab3790001nj4FpbwKUwsOm5','msg_0dfab2f0b001ylTAItoH0bo6yZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458935184,1790458940714,'{"type":"text","text":"`983bfea` pushed.\n\n## Where this actually stands\n\nThe lockout is a self-inflicted script bug, now fixed and superseded by a script that can''t cause it — it deletes nothing, restores your access first, and verifies with a real `sudo` call before doing anything else.\n\nI''ve cost you six failed attempts. Worth naming why, since the pattern is consistent enough to act on: every failure this session was a config value that was **accepted without complaint, reported success, and did something other than what was asked** — `mac-address-mode`, `mac-address-randomization 1`, sudoers `\"\"`, the trailing comma, `visudo -c` exit codes, and now `SUDO_USER` under pkexec. In this codebase I should verify runtime behaviour, not trust that a script printed `[ok]`.\n\n## Once sudo is back\n\n```bash\nsudo -l | tail -3\nsudo audit-nft\n```\n\nThen I''d suggest rebooting. The Shield Kitty persistence fix has been verified in the running system, but not across an actual boot cycle, and that was the original point of the exercise.\n\nNothing about the reboot depends on any of this. Firewall strict, fail2ban active, hostname `zero`, MAC randomized, Shield Kitty units enabled with `linger`, no pacman lock, 0 updates, 0 failed units, 0 queued jobs.\n\n## One thing I''d change about how we work\n\nI''ve been handing you scripts that mutate `/etc/sudoers.d` — the one directory where a wrong guess locks you out of your own machine — and asking you to run them blind. That''s the wrong risk profile regardless of how carefully I test them. If you want, the remaining sudoers work can be done by writing the file contents out for you to inspect first, or via `pkexec` one-liners you can read in full before running. Say which and I''ll switch to that.\n\nStill open regardless: **Tor Browser isn''t installed**, so Tor is running with no client and anonymity is not actually in place. And **the token should be revoked on GitHub** — shredding the local copy never invalidated it server-side.","time":{"start":1790458935184,"end":1790458940711}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfab4d2c001IH3NoHOEe8Yy2a','msg_0dfab2f0b001ylTAItoH0bo6yZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458940717,1790458940717,'{"reason":"stop","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":120676,"input":43,"output":442,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":120191}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfabf4f3002iBztQCMbujeY69','msg_0dfabf4f3001aiSWmaoJq5VhS6','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458983671,1790458983671,'{"type":"text","text":"pkexec bash /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/RECOVER-SUDO.sh\n==============================================\nsudo recovery for: livings\n==============================================\n[ok]   livings is in wheel\n\n-- restoring sudo access --\n[ok]   restored 10-installer from /root/sudoers-quarantine-20260926-143446\ncontents: %wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL\n\n-- fixing permissions (no file is deleted or moved) --\n[ok]   editor: 440 -> 0440\n[ok]   insults: 440 -> 0440\n[ok]   pwfeedback: 440 -> 0440\n[ok]   opencode: 440 -> 0440\n\n-- validating --\n[ok]   visudo -c: parsed OK\n\n-- behavioural check --\n[ok]   wheel rule present and livings is in wheel -- sudo will prompt for a password\n\n-- audit helpers --\n[ok]   audit-readonly installed and parses\n[ok]   audit helpers work without a password\n\n==============================================\nfinal state\n==============================================\n/etc/sudoers: parsed OK\n/etc/sudoers.d/10-installer: parsed OK\n/etc/sudoers.d/audit-readonly: parsed OK\n/etc/sudoers.d/editor: parsed OK\n/etc/sudoers.d/insults: parsed OK\n/etc/sudoers.d/opencode: parsed OK\n/etc/sudoers.d/pwfeedback: parsed OK\n\ntotal 20\n-r--r----- 1 root root  21 Sep 26 14:42 10-installer\n-r--r----- 1 root root 241 Sep 26 14:42 audit-readonly\n-r--r----- 1 root root  31 Sep 26 09:40 editor\n-r--r----- 1 root root  17 Sep 26 09:40 insults\n-r--r----- 1 root root   0 Sep 26 11:55 opencode\n-r--r----- 1 root root  20 Sep 26 09:40 pwfeedback\n\nNow test from YOUR shell:\nsudo -l | tail -3\nsudo audit-nft\n=================  is the output should i run that next command?"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfabfcc8001HBlcRhl6S6Q11Y','msg_0dfabf514001lnUOuDIfoAf7Ux','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458985672,1790458985673,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfabfcca001QoyWT1hsXmi7Bd','msg_0dfabf514001lnUOuDIfoAf7Ux','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458985674,1790458990068,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The recovery worked. All files parsed OK, audit helpers work.\n\nOne oddity: the permissions show `-r--r-----` which is 0440. Good. But the script reported \"440 -> 0440\" — meaning the original was `440` (i.e., 0440 without leading zero, stat -c%a gives \"440\"). And it chmod''d to 0440 → same thing. So they were already fine; the \"440 -> 0440\" is just a leading-zero cosmetic difference. Fine.\n\nSo sudo should work now. Let me verify independently rather than trust.\n\nLet me verify:\n1. sudo -l works\n2. audit-nft works\n3. Full security state re-verified\n4. Then the root-only diagnostics I never got to run (SMART, btrfs scrub, dmesg, nft rules)\n\nYes, they should run the test commands — but actually I can verify sudo myself now if the NOPASSWD helpers work. Let me just verify directly.\n\nLet me run the verification, including the long-deferred root-only diagnostics.\n\nLet me do that — this is the payoff.\n\nLet me run:\n- sudo -n audit-nft (firewall nft rules)\n- sudo -n audit-btrfs (scrub, subvolumes, device stats)\n- sudo -n audit-smart (nvme health)\n- sudo -n audit-dmesg (kernel errors)\n- sudo -n audit-fail2ban (jails)\n- sudo -n audit-firewall\n\nAnd confirm sudo itself works for normal use — but `sudo -n true` isn''t whitelisted, so it needs a password. I can''t test that without the user. But `sudo -l` with password... the user should run it. However, since audit-readonly is NOPASSWD, I can at least confirm the wheel rule is present (already shown parsed OK).\n\nLet me run the diagnostics now.\n\nLet me batch them.\n\nPriority: complete the audit that was interrupted.\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790458985674,"end":1790458990067}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfac0df6001psPfM3D1lGzBXF','msg_0dfabf514001lnUOuDIfoAf7Ux','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790458990070,1790458990803,'{"type":"text","text":"Yes — but I can verify it myself now, and more importantly I can finally run the root-only diagnostics that were blocked since the beginning of this audit.","time":{"start":1790458990070,"end":1790458990803}}');
