INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfad2aa1001MKDj36FwBIq7HY','msg_0dfad1920001UXXtM2VdCGVyKL','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459062945,1790459062945,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":131291,"input":448,"output":181,"reasoning":166,"cache":{"write":0,"read":130496}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfad3390001Ir89HAM1MXukJM','msg_0dfad2aea001MBhW7i1nill9oK','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459065233,1790459065234,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfad3393001MckiBnK8ELYSqY','msg_0dfad2aea001MBhW7i1nill9oK','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459065235,1790459070965,'{"type":"text","text":"## Audit complete: 27/27, safe to reboot\n\nThe one \"failure\" was my own grep. **All five btrfs error counters are 0** — no corruption, no I/O errors.\n\n### Two real issues found and fixed\n\n**1. Shield Kitty wouldn''t have survived your reboot.** It was a bare process with no systemd unit anywhere. Its units use `%h` in `ExecStart`, which in a *system* unit expands to root''s home — so installing them as system units would have produced units that can never start. Installed as user units with `linger=yes`, so monitoring now starts at boot without a login.\n\n**2. `bootstrap.sh` couldn''t handle the recidive change.** Another agent set `recidive enabled=true` in the source; my script only ever *enabled* it, never disabled it. A fresh install would have copied `enabled=true` with no `/var/log/fail2ban.log` — and a jail pointing at a missing log aborts **every** fail2ban jail. Now set in both directions, scoped to `[recidive]` via awk, since `enabled = true` also appears under `[sshd]` and a blanket sed would have silently disabled sshd.\n\n### Confirmed by direct inspection, not assumption\n\n- **Firewall genuinely enforces** — the nft chain terminates in `reject with icmpx admin-prohibited`, so `kdeconnectd:1716` and `mDNS:5353` are blocked despite being bound\n- **fail2ban** — 2 jails, correct journal filter, recidive consistent between source and live\n- **Storage** — scrub reports no errors, 456GB free\n- **Kernel** — only benign probes; no btrfs warnings, no OOM, no hardware faults\n- **arpwatch** was crash-looping (27 restarts) but another agent fixed it; now listening, DB in the right place. The `chdir` warning is cosmetic.\n\n### Debts I owe you\n\nYou spent six failed attempts on scripts I wrote. Every failure shared one shape: a value that was **accepted silently, printed `[ok]`, and did something other than what was asked** — `mac-address-mode`, `mac-address-randomization 1`, sudoers `\"\"`, a trailing comma, `visudo -c` exit codes, and `SUDO_USER` under pkexec. The worst was structural: `fix-sudoers-permissions.sh` removed the only rule granting you sudo. That''s why `RECOVER-SUDO.sh` deletes nothing and verifies access before touching anything.\n\nIf you want to keep going on this repo, I''d suggest changing how sudoers work gets done: write the file contents out for you to read before anything is applied. Happy to switch to that.\n\n### After you reboot\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --user is-active shield-kitty-watch.service evidence-seal.timer\nsudo audit-nft\n```\n\nStill genuinely open, and unrelated to boot: **Tor Browser is not installed** — Tor is running with no client, so you have firewall hardening but no anonymity. And **the GitHub token was never revoked server-side**; local shredding never invalidated it.","time":{"start":1790459065235,"end":1790459070965}}');
