INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfae7a62001wD2ls6X72q1eCZ','msg_0dfae7408001Pwzrr9Aojaf9cH','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459148898,1790459173776,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user wants me to audit everything I built in this session for bugs. Let me be thorough and honest.\n\nWhat I built/changed in this session:\n1. `arpwatch-spoof.service` — collector unit, several revisions\n2. `arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh` — new scanner (rewrote detection)\n3. `arpwatch-spoof-scan.service` — new scan oneshot (revised: root + explicit bus)\n4. `arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer` — new timer\n5. `arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh` — kept as manual test path (was it modified? I rewrote it earlier in the session per summary — it was \"rewritten\" with BASH_SOURCE session bootstrap. Actually per the summary it was fixed in b39490a. In THIS session, did I modify it? The summary said the old hook was replaced. Let me check its state.)\n6. `install-arpwatch-spoof.sh` — heavily rewritten\n7. `faillock.conf` (repo) — earlier session? The summary says it was modified and committed 819c363. That was before this session''s arpwatch work but within the \"conversation\".\n8. `deploy-faillock-admin.sh` — committed 819c363\n9. buildlog entries\n\nThe user says \"everything you built in this instance\" — so the arpwatch work primarily, plus possibly the faillock work.\n\nLet me do a genuine audit. Let me read all the files and look for real bugs.\n\nKey things to check:\n- The scan unit runs as root with `ProtectHome=read-only` and executes /home/livings/... — read-only allows exec? ProtectHome=read-only makes /home read-only but still accessible (readable). Executing a script from a read-only mount is fine.\n- `NoNewPrivileges=true` on the scan unit running as root — does that break the HUP signal? NoNewPrivileges prevents gaining privileges via setuid, but sending signals to a same-uid process is fine. Both run as root. OK.\n- BUT: `ProtectSystem=full` + `ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/arpwatch /var/log`. The scanner writes to /var/lib/arpwatch/spoof-state. OK.\n- The scanner reads /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat. OK.\n- The alert script is at /home/livings/Projects/shield-kitty/shield-kitty-alert.py — with ProtectHome=read-only, readable. OK.\n- Does shield-kitty-alert.py need to WRITE anything? Let me check. It may write a log. If it writes to /var/log or its own dir, ProtectHome=read-only would block it. Need to check.\n- The scan unit''s ExecStartPre uses /bin/sh -c with `$(cat ...)`. Fine.\n- PIDFILE=/run/arpwatch-spoof.pid — but the collector unit has ProtectSystem=full (not strict), so /run is writable? ProtectSystem=full makes /usr, /boot, /efi, /etc read-only. /run is NOT read-only under =full. Under =strict, /run is read-only too? Actually systemd docs: \"full: /usr, /boot, /efi and /etc are read-only\"; \"strict: the entire file system hierarchy is mounted read-only, except for API file systems /proc, /sys, /dev\". /run is on the root fs, so under strict it WOULD be read-only. We use full, so /run is writable. OK.\n\nBUT: PrivateTmp=true is on both. Not an issue.\n\n- Potential bug: the collector has `ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/arpwatch` but now also writes to /run (pidfile) and /var/lib/arpwatch (datafile). Under ProtectSystem=full, /run is writable, /var is writable except... wait, ProtectSystem=full doesn''t make /var read-only. Let me re-check: systemd ProtectSystem=full mounts /usr, /boot, /efi, /etc read-only. /var is NOT included. So ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/arpwatch is redundant but harmless.\n\nHmm, actually I should double check: some docs say full = \"/usr, /boot, /etc\" read-only. Yes. So /var and /run are writable. Fine.\n\n- Timer: OnUnitActiveSec=120. Need to check the timer file content. Let me read it.\n- Does the timer have Persistent=true? And does the scan service have Requires=arpwatch-spoof.service — if the collector is stopped, the scan fails. That''s intended.\n- The scanner''s dedup state: if arpwatch''s datafile gets truncated/rotated, the state would be stale. Minor.\n- IMPORTANT BUG CANDIDATE: the scanner compares the datafile''s accumulated history. arpwatch''s arp.dat accumulates ALL bindings forever. If an IP legitimately changes MAC (DHCP reassign to a different device), the datafile will contain BOTH the old and new MAC for that IP permanently → permanent \"spoof\" alert, forever. That''s a significant design flaw! The datafile is a historical log, not current state. So my \"same IP, different MAC\" detection over the whole file will produce false positives whenever a device''s MAC changes (which is normal on a big DHCP /8).\n\nThat''s a REAL bug worth flagging. The scanner should only consider RECENT entries (e.g., the last timestamp window) or treat arp.dat as current-state-only.\n\nActually, let me think about arpwatch''s semantics: arp.dat is the mac/ip database. When arpwatch sees a mac/ip pair for the first time, it appends. It does NOT remove old entries when a mac changes... Actually, arpwatch DOES update: when a MAC changes IP, it records the new binding and the old one remains in the file? The man page says the file contains mac/ip pairs seen. Classic arpwatch keeps the full history in arp.dat.\n\nHmm, actually classic arpwatch''s arp.dat is a flat list of all bindings. It grows forever. So yes, my detection over the whole file yields permanent false positives on MAC changes.\n\nThis is the most important finding. I should fix it: restrict analysis to a time window, or better — only consider the most recent entry per IP, or entries within the last N minutes.\n\nGiven the datafile has a timestamp field (seconds since epoch), I can filter to a recent window. But arpwatch only writes every 15 min, and only appends NEW pairs. So \"recent\" = appended in the last flush. Filtering by timestamp window is reasonable: e.g., only consider pairs seen in the last 24h? Still accumulates over days.\n\nBetter approach: track per-IP the set of MACs seen in the last WINDOW (e.g., 15 min or 1 hour), which detects an active conflict but ignores historical changes. Since arpwatch only appends, a MAC change shows as two entries with different timestamps; the older one falls out of the window.\n\nThat''s a sound fix. Let me implement: use the timestamp field, only consider entries newer than (now - WINDOW).\n\nWait, but there''s a subtlety: if a spoofer appears, arpwatch appends the new mac/ip pair once. After the window passes, it leaves the window and the alert clears. That''s correct behavior (attack ended).\n\nLet me check whether the scanner currently uses the timestamp at all. Per the summary: reads \"MAC IP timestamp [hostname]\". It probably ignores the timestamp.\n\nSo BUG: no time filtering → permanent false positives on legitimate MAC changes. This is a genuine, important bug.\n\nLet me verify by reading the scanner.\n\nOther potential bugs:\n- The scanner ignores 0.0.0.0/8, 127/8, 169.254/16. Good.\n- Does it handle the `-C` compact MAC format (0:8:e1:1:2:d6)? We don''t use -C, so MACs are colon-separated full form. But if the datafile had compact form, the distinct-MAC comparison still works. Fine.\n- Case-insensitive MAC comparison: I fixed that with a distinct-value set. But is the set case-insensitive? If arp.dat has AA:BB and aa:bb, a naive set would treat them as distinct. My fix used a set of values — but are they normalized to lowercase? Let me check. The test \"same MAC diff case -> 0\" passed, so presumably normalized. Let me verify.\n\n- The `anomal$(...)` grammar: \"anomaly\"/\"anomalies\" — the code does `echo y` or `echo ies` appended to \"anomal\" → \"anomaly\"/\"anomalies\". Correct.\n\n- Installer: `ARPWATCH_PROBE_DB=\"$(mktemp -u)/probe.dat\"` then `mkdir -p $(dirname ...)`. mktemp -u returns something like /tmp/tmp.XXXX. Then probe.dat is /tmp/tmp.XXXX/probe.dat. mkdir -p creates the dir. Then `rm -rf $(dirname)` at the end. But the mkdir is OUTSIDE the dry-run guard! So in --dry-run mode, it creates a real temp dir. Minor, but it means dry-run has side effects (a temp dir). Should be cleaned up. It is cleaned at the end. Acceptable but let me check the dry-run path actually reaches the rm.\n\n- Installer: does it still reference HOOK? It requires the hook to be executable as a prerequisite (\"hook executable\") even though the service no longer uses it. That''s intentional (kept as manual test path) but the message is confusing. Fine.\n\n- The installer''s dry-run: `run` function echoes \"[dry-run] sudo ...\". The probe is NOT wrapped in `run` — it actually executes arpwatch even in dry-run. That''s intentional (the probe must run), and it writes to a temp path. OK. But in dry-run it still ran the probe (we saw \"flags parse OK\" in the dry run). Good.\n\n- Potential issue: the probe writes a pidfile at the temp path; under root it''s created. Then `rm -rf $(dirname \"$ARPWATCH_PROBE_DB\")` removes the temp dir but NOT the pidfile if it''s in a different temp dir! ARPWATCH_PROBE_PID=\"$(mktemp -u)/probe.pid\" — a SEPARATE mktemp -u call → different directory! So the pidfile leaks in its own temp dir. That''s a real (minor) bug: orphaned temp dir per install run.\n\nLet me fix: use the same mktemp dir for both.\n\n- Also `mktemp -u` is insecure (race) but fine for a probe path. Could use mktemp -d. Better: `PROBE_DIR=\"$(mktemp -d)\"`. Cleaner and secure. But mktemp -d creates it immediately, so the mkdir -p becomes unnecessary. Let me do that.\n\n- Another: the installer writes units via sed replacing /home/livings with TARGET_HOME. The scan unit contains /run/user/1000 and User=livings hardcoded. If TARGET_USER isn''t livings, the scan unit would be wrong. The sed only rewrites paths, not User=/Environment= values. Since this is a single-user machine, it''s fine, but it''s a latent inconsistency. Worth noting; the header uses SUDO_USER. Low priority but let me check whether the installer even uses TARGET_HOME consistently vs hardcoded livings.\n\nActually the units have hardcoded /home/livings AND User=livings AND /run/user/1000. The installer''s sed rewrites /home/livings → TARGET_HOME. If run as a different user, User= and /run/user/1000 would still say livings. Inconsistent. For a single-user box it''s acceptable, but I should either fix or document. Let me note it and fix the sed to also handle User= and the runtime dir, deriving UID from the target user. That''s more correct.\n\nLet me do that — derive the target UID and rewrite User=/Group=/run/user/<uid>.\n\n- The `arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh` manual test path: does it still work? It''s used to prove the notify chain. Let me test it.\n\n- Also: does `arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh` still reference the old exec-hook argv assumptions? It''s now only a manual tool, so argv handling should be documented. Let me check it runs.\n\nLet me now actually read the files and run tests rather than speculate.\n\nPlan:\n1. Read the scanner fully, check timestamp usage and MAC normalization\n2. Read the timer\n3. Read the installer for the temp-dir leak and the sed/TARGET_USER consistency\n4. Test the alert script\n5. Fix the real bugs\n6. Re-run the full test suite\n7. Commit + buildlog\n\nLet me start reading.\n\nLet me be efficient with parallel reads.\n\nLet me read the scanner and timer.","time":{"start":1790459148898,"end":1790459173774}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfae7f92001rFQza7R1JFOIt1','msg_0dfae77a4001I2XZEbH6dZ59ij','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459150226,1790459150228,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfae7f950016ncV2WzBqxUMTI','msg_0dfae77a4001I2XZEbH6dZ59ij','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459150229,1790459156235,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"So:\n- No browser installed at all\n- To revoke the token they need a browser\n- GitHub warns against logging in over Tor, so Tor Browser is the wrong choice for that\n- Firefox and Chromium are available in official repos\n\nPlan:\n1. Install Tor Browser (for anonymity browsing) — `sudo pacman -S torbrowser-launcher`\n2. Install a clearnet browser temporarily to revoke the token — `sudo pacman -S firefox`\n3. Revoke the token at github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens\n4. Optionally remove Firefox afterwards, or keep it\n\nActually, thinking about it more: the user is doing a \"secure/anonymous\" setup. Do they want a clearnet browser at all? The safest approach:\n- Revoke the token using a browser, then remove that browser if they don''t want clearnet browsing\n- Use Tor Browser for actual browsing\n\nBut there''s a subtlety: revoking a token requires being logged into GitHub, which is a clearnet identity-linked action anyway. That''s fine — it''s an administrative action, and GitHub login over Tor would likely trigger fraud protection.\n\nAlternative without any browser: use `curl` with the token to delete itself via the API! That''s actually possible:\n```\ncurl -X DELETE -H \"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\" https://api.github.com/applications/.../token\n```\nBut I shredded the token, so I can''t.\n\nHmm — but is the token in any snapshot? The snapshots are of `@` (root subvolume), not `@home`, so no.\n\nSo the token is unrecoverable locally → must be revoked via web UI → needs a browser.\n\nLet me present the plan clearly:\n\nStep 1: install both packages\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S torbrowser-launcher firefox\n```\n\nStep 2: Revoke the token in Firefox (clearnet), at:\n- https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens (fine-grained)\n- or https://github.com/settings/tokens (classic)\n\nStep 3: Verify revocation.\n\nThen optionally remove firefox if they don''t want a clearnet browser.\n\nLet me also mention: while logged in to GitHub, they could check the token''s scopes and last-used date to see what it accessed.\n\nLet me give this clearly and concisely.\n\nLet me write the final response with the commands.\n\nI should be decisive and clear. Let me write it.\n\nLet me also note: installing firefox is a reasonable permanent addition anyway (a normal browser for clearnet admin tasks), but if they want strict anonymity they should keep profiles separate. I''ll mention briefly.\n\nLet me finalize.\n\nI won''t run the install (needs sudo + it''s a system change they should initiate). Let me give the command.\n\nActually — I could offer to run it via pkexec? No, pacman via pkexec is messy. Let them run it with sudo, which now works.\n\nLet me give the command.","time":{"start":1790459150229,"end":1790459156234}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfae970c001LZCMUA24G5ko73','msg_0dfae77a4001I2XZEbH6dZ59ij','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459156237,1790459160976,'{"type":"text","text":"Both problems are solvable, but the token one has a catch I hadn''t accounted for: **revoking it needs a browser, and you have none.** GitHub also warns against logging in over Tor, so Tor Browser is the wrong tool for it.\n\n## 1. Tor Browser\n\nStraightforward — official repo, signature-validated:\n\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S torbrowser-launcher\n```\n\nKeep the `tor` daemon running as-is (`127.0.0.1:9050`). Tor Browser runs its own independent Tor instance with its own circuits, which is the correct design — don''t point it at the system daemon.\n\n## 2. GitHub token\n\nNeeds a clearnet browser for the one-off task:\n\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S firefox\n```\n\nThen, **over your normal connection, not Tor**:\n\n1. Go to `https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens`\n2. If nothing is listed, check the classic tokens at `https://github.com/settings/tokens`\n3. **Delete** the token\n4. While you''re there, check its scopes and last-used date — that tells you exactly what was accessible with it\n\nThen `sudo pacman -Rns firefox` if you''d rather not keep a clearnet browser on the machine.\n\n## Why this can''t be automated\n\nI shredded the token file, so I can''t revoke it via the API. And the btrfs snapshots are of `@` (the root subvolume), not `@home` — so the plaintext isn''t recoverable from them either. That was the right call for local hygiene, but it does mean revocation has to happen in a browser.\n\nWorth being clear about the actual exposure: a still-live token on GitHub''s side is only exploitable by someone who already has it. The realistic risk is low since it was mode `0600` and never committed. But leaving a live credential for something you no longer use is worth closing.\n\n## One note on your repo config\n\n`chaotic-aur` is enabled in `/etc/pacman.conf` alongside the official repos. It''s signed and widely used, so not a problem per se — but it does mean some of your packages come from a third party rather than Arch. Given you''re building a verified baseline, worth knowing where the trust boundary sits. `torbrowser-launcher` and `firefox` both come from `extra`, so neither of these two installs touches chaotic-aur.","time":{"start":1790459156237,"end":1790459160974}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaea9930013bAZqdqFX74xn0','msg_0dfae77a4001I2XZEbH6dZ59ij','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459160980,1790459160981,'{"reason":"stop","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":136063,"input":101,"output":502,"reasoning":608,"cache":{"write":0,"read":134852}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaedb91001CPo4eiK5W6y8mF','msg_0dfae7408001Pwzrr9Aojaf9cH','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459173777,1790459174062,'{"type":"text","text":"Good — let me audit this properly rather than assume. First, the files I wrote:","time":{"start":1790459173777,"end":1790459174062}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaedc5c001eeEZR0AWKeFGVP','msg_0dfae7408001Pwzrr9Aojaf9cH','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459173981,1790459174073,'{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_function_dhbvcn37395x_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh"},"output":"<path>/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: #!/bin/bash\n2: # arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh — detect ARP spoofing from arpwatch''s datafile.\n3: #\n4: # WHY THIS EXISTS INSTEAD OF AN EXEC HOOK\n5: #\n6: # arpwatch 3.9 (the build in Arch ''extra'') has no exec hook. The original LBL\n7: # arpwatch took -a /path/to/prog, but 3.9 only offers -f datafile, -w/-W for\n8: # email, and syslog. The valid flags are:\n9: #\n10: #     [-CdFNpqsvzZ] [-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface] [-P pidfile]\n11: #     [-w watcher@email] [-W watchee@email] [-n net[/width]] [-x net[/width]]\n12: #     [-r file]\n13: #\n14: # So detection reads the datafile instead. Format, from arpwatch(8):\n15: #\n16: #     <mac> <ip> <unix-seconds> [hostname]\n17: #\n18: # A spoof signature is one IP claimed by more than one MAC. The previous\n19: # approach also assumed a different interface syntax; 3.9 wants -i INTERFACE,\n20: # not a positional argument, which is why the service exited 1 in a restart\n21: # loop.\n22: #\n23: # NO AUTO-BAN. The 1-day ban stays with fail2ban, which keys on real\n24: # connection and auth attempts. On a DHCP /8, a re-used address is routine and\n25: # Android/Windows per-SSID MAC randomisation is common; banning for 24h would\n26: # cut off colleagues and phones, not attackers. This notifies and logs.\n27: #\n28: # Alerts are deduplicated against a state file: a condition that is still true\n29: # on the next scan does not re-notify, so one persistent anomaly produces one\n30: # alert rather than one every interval.\n31: \n32: set -uo pipefail\n33: \n34: DATAFILE=\"${BDE_ARP_DATAFILE:-/var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat}\"\n35: STATE=\"${BDE_ARP_STATE:-/var/lib/arpwatch/spoof-state}\"\n36: LOG=\"${BDE_ARPWATCH_LOG:-/var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log}\"\n37: \n38: HERE=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\n39: ALERT=\"${BDE_ALERT:-$(dirname \"$HERE\")/shield-kitty/shield-kitty-alert.py}\"\n40: \n41: # Reconstruct the graphical session so notify-send works. The scanner runs from\n42: # a system unit, so it inherits none of DISPLAY/DBUS and every alert would fail\n43: # silently. See the same logic in arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh.\n44: bootstrap_session_env() {\n45:     local uid=\"${EUID:-$UID}\" rt\n46:     rt=\"${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$uid}\"\n47:     if [ -z \"${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:-}\" ] && [ -S \"$rt/bus\" ]; then\n48:         export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=\"$rt\"\n49:         export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=\"unix:path=$rt/bus\"\n50:     fi\n51:     if [ -z \"${WAYLAND_DISPLAY:-}\" ]; then\n52:         local w\n53:         for w in \"$rt\"/wayland-*; do\n54:             [ -S \"$w\" ] || continue\n55:             export WAYLAND_DISPLAY=\"${w##*/}\"\n56:             break\n57:         done\n58:     fi\n59: }\n60: \n61: log() { printf ''%s %s\\n'' \"$(date -Is)\" \"$*\" >>\"$LOG\" 2>/dev/null || \\\n62:         printf ''%s %s\\n'' \"$(date -Is)\" \"$*\" >&2; }\n63: \n64: # Addresses that are supposed to move or be shared, and must never be reported.\n65: # Comma-separated CIDRs or bare IPs.\n66: IGNORE_CIDRS=\"${BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE:-127.0.0.0/8,0.0.0.0/8,169.254.0.0/16,fe80::/10}\"\n67: \n68: in_ignore() {\n69:     local ip=\"$1\" c\n70:     for c in ${IGNORE_CIDRS//,/ }; do\n71:         if [[ \"$c\" == */* ]]; then\n72:             [ \"$(python3 -c ''\n73: import ipaddress, sys\n74: try:\n75:     a = ipaddress.ip_address(sys.argv[1]); n = ipaddress.ip_network(sys.argv[2])\n76:     print(\"y\" if a in n else \"n\")\n77: except ValueError:\n78:     print(\"n\")'' \"$ip\" \"$c\")\" = \"y\" ] && return 0\n79:         else\n80:             [ \"$ip\" = \"$c\" ] && return 0\n81:         fi\n82:     done\n83:     return 1\n84: }\n85: \n86: selftest() {\n87:     echo \"=== arpwatch-spoof-scan selftest ===\"\n88:     echo \"  datafile : $DATAFILE\"\n89:     echo \"  state    : $STATE\"\n90:     echo \"  alert    : $ALERT\"\n91:     echo \"  ignored  : $IGNORE_CIDRS\"\n92:     echo\n93:     if [ -r \"$DATAFILE\" ]; then\n94:         echo \"  datafile readable, $(wc -l < \"$DATAFILE\") entries\"\n95:     else\n96:         echo \"  datafile NOT readable yet (arpwatch has not written it)\"\n97:     fi\n98:     echo\n99:     echo \"  use ./selftest-data.sh to run the full detection suite offline\"\n100: }\n101: \n102: if [ \"${1:-}\" = \"--selftest\" ]; then selftest; exit 0; fi\n103: \n104: [ -r \"$DATAFILE\" ] || { log \"datafile $DATAFILE not readable; nothing to scan\"; exit 0; }\n105: \n106: # Build the current anomaly set: \"ip mac1,mac2,...\" for every IP with more than\n107: # one DISTINCT MAC.\n108: #\n109: # Distinctness matters. Testing for a comma is not enough: arpwatch can list the\n110: # same hardware address twice with different capitalisation, and joining those\n111: # yields \"mac,mac\" -- a comma, but still one device. Counting unique values is\n112: # the only way to avoid alerting on a formatting difference.\n113: current=\"$(\n114:     awk ''NF >= 2 {\n115:             ip = $2\n116:             mac = tolower($1)\n117:             if (!(ip in seen)) { seen[ip] = mac; order[ip] = 1; next }\n118:             n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n119:             for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) if (arr[i] == mac) next\n120:             seen[ip] = seen[ip] \" \" mac\n121:          }\n122:          END {\n123:             for (ip in order) {\n124:                 n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n125:                 if (n < 2) continue\n126:                 s = arr[1]\n127:                 for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) s = s \",\" arr[i]\n128:                 print ip \" \" s\n129:             }\n130:          }'' \"$DATAFILE\" 2>/dev/null | sort\n131: )\"\n132: \n133: if [ -z \"$current\" ]; then\n134:     # Clear the state, not just skip. Leaving it stale means a spoof that is\n135:     # resolved and later returns would be silently swallowed as \"already seen\".\n136:     [ -s \"$STATE\" ] && { : >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not clear state file $STATE\"; }\n137:     log \"scan: no anomalies\"\n138:     exit 0\n139: fi\n140: \n141: previous=\"\"\n142: [ -r \"$STATE\" ] && previous=\"$(cat \"$STATE\")\"\n143: \n144: # Only alert on what is newly anomalous. A condition carried over from the last\n145: # scan has already been reported.\n146: newly=\"\"\n147: while read -r ip macs; do\n148:     [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n149:     in_ignore \"$ip\" && { log \"scan: $ip ($macs) ignored by BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE\"; continue; }\n150:     if ! printf ''%s\\n'' \"$previous\" | grep -qxF \"$ip $macs\"; then\n151:         newly+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''\n152:     else\n153:         log \"scan: $ip ($macs) persists, not re-alerting\"\n154:     fi\n155: done <<<\"$current\"\n156: \n157: printf ''%s'' \"$current\" >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not write state file $STATE\"\n158: \n159: [ -z \"$newly\" ] && { log \"scan: no new anomalies\"; exit 0; }\n160: \n161: count=$(printf ''%s'' \"$newly\" | grep -c .)\n162: bootstrap_session_env\n163: while read -r ip macs; do\n164:     [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n165:     n=$(awk -F, ''{print NF}'' <<<\"$macs\")\n166:     log \"SPOOF $ip claimed by $n MACs: $macs\"\n167:     payload=$(python3 - \"$ip\" \"$macs\" \"$n\" <<''PY''\n168: import json, sys\n169: ip, macs, n = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]\n170: print(json.dumps({\n171:     \"severity\": \"critical\",\n172:     \"events\": [{\n173:         \"label\": \"ARP spoof: {} claimed by {} MACs\".format(ip, n),\n174:         \"count\": 1,\n175:         \"sample\": \"MACs: {}. Not auto-banned: DHCP re-use and MAC randomisation \"\n176:                   \"look identical. Verify, then ban via the 86400s fail2ban path \"\n177:                   \"if real.\".format(macs),\n178:     }],\n179: }))\n180: PY\n181: )\n182:     [ -f \"$ALERT\" ] || { log \"alert script missing at $ALERT\"; continue; }\n183:     out=\"$(python3 \"$ALERT\" \"$payload\" 2>&1)\" || log \"alert delivery failed\"\n184:     [ -n \"$out\" ] && log \"alert: $out\"\n185: done <<<\"$newly\"\n186: \n187: log \"scan: $count new anomal$( [ \"$count\" = 1 ] && echo y || echo ies)\"\n\n(End of file - total 187 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"#!/bin/bash\n# arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh — detect ARP spoofing from arpwatch''s datafile.\n#\n# WHY THIS EXISTS INSTEAD OF AN EXEC HOOK\n#\n# arpwatch 3.9 (the build in Arch ''extra'') has no exec hook. The original LBL\n# arpwatch took -a /path/to/prog, but 3.9 only offers -f datafile, -w/-W for\n# email, and syslog. The valid flags are:\n#\n#     [-CdFNpqsvzZ] [-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface] [-P pidfile]\n#     [-w watcher@email] [-W watchee@email] [-n net[/width]] [-x net[/width]]\n#     [-r file]\n#\n# So detection reads the datafile instead. Format, from arpwatch(8):\n#\n#     <mac> <ip> <unix-seconds> [hostname]\n#\n# A spoof signature is one IP claimed by more than one MAC. The previous\n# approach also assumed a different interface syntax; 3.9 wants -i INTERFACE,\n# not a positional argument, which is why the service exited 1 in a restart","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh","text":"#!/bin/bash\n# arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh — detect ARP spoofing from arpwatch''s datafile.\n#\n# WHY THIS EXISTS INSTEAD OF AN EXEC HOOK\n#\n# arpwatch 3.9 (the build in Arch ''extra'') has no exec hook. The original LBL\n# arpwatch took -a /path/to/prog, but 3.9 only offers -f datafile, -w/-W for\n# email, and syslog. The valid flags are:\n#\n#     [-CdFNpqsvzZ] [-D arpdir] [-f datafile] [-i interface] [-P pidfile]\n#     [-w watcher@email] [-W watchee@email] [-n net[/width]] [-x net[/width]]\n#     [-r file]\n#\n# So detection reads the datafile instead. Format, from arpwatch(8):\n#\n#     <mac> <ip> <unix-seconds> [hostname]\n#\n# A spoof signature is one IP claimed by more than one MAC. The previous\n# approach also assumed a different interface syntax; 3.9 wants -i INTERFACE,\n# not a positional argument, which is why the service exited 1 in a restart\n# loop.\n#\n# NO AUTO-BAN. The 1-day ban stays with fail2ban, which keys on real\n# connection and auth attempts. On a DHCP /8, a re-used address is routine and\n# Android/Windows per-SSID MAC randomisation is common; banning for 24h would\n# cut off colleagues and phones, not attackers. This notifies and logs.\n#\n# Alerts are deduplicated against a state file: a condition that is still true\n# on the next scan does not re-notify, so one persistent anomaly produces one\n# alert rather than one every interval.\n\nset -uo pipefail\n\nDATAFILE=\"${BDE_ARP_DATAFILE:-/var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat}\"\nSTATE=\"${BDE_ARP_STATE:-/var/lib/arpwatch/spoof-state}\"\nLOG=\"${BDE_ARPWATCH_LOG:-/var/log/arpwatch-spoof.log}\"\n\nHERE=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\nALERT=\"${BDE_ALERT:-$(dirname \"$HERE\")/shield-kitty/shield-kitty-alert.py}\"\n\n# Reconstruct the graphical session so notify-send works. The scanner runs from\n# a system unit, so it inherits none of DISPLAY/DBUS and every alert would fail\n# silently. See the same logic in arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh.\nbootstrap_session_env() {\n    local uid=\"${EUID:-$UID}\" rt\n    rt=\"${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$uid}\"\n    if [ -z \"${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:-}\" ] && [ -S \"$rt/bus\" ]; then\n        export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=\"$rt\"\n        export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=\"unix:path=$rt/bus\"\n    fi\n    if [ -z \"${WAYLAND_DISPLAY:-}\" ]; then\n        local w\n        for w in \"$rt\"/wayland-*; do\n            [ -S \"$w\" ] || continue\n            export WAYLAND_DISPLAY=\"${w##*/}\"\n            break\n        done\n    fi\n}\n\nlog() { printf ''%s %s\\n'' \"$(date -Is)\" \"$*\" >>\"$LOG\" 2>/dev/null || \\\n        printf ''%s %s\\n'' \"$(date -Is)\" \"$*\" >&2; }\n\n# Addresses that are supposed to move or be shared, and must never be reported.\n# Comma-separated CIDRs or bare IPs.\nIGNORE_CIDRS=\"${BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE:-127.0.0.0/8,0.0.0.0/8,169.254.0.0/16,fe80::/10}\"\n\nin_ignore() {\n    local ip=\"$1\" c\n    for c in ${IGNORE_CIDRS//,/ }; do\n        if [[ \"$c\" == */* ]]; then\n            [ \"$(python3 -c ''\nimport ipaddress, sys\ntry:\n    a = ipaddress.ip_address(sys.argv[1]); n = ipaddress.ip_network(sys.argv[2])\n    print(\"y\" if a in n else \"n\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"n\")'' \"$ip\" \"$c\")\" = \"y\" ] && return 0\n        else\n            [ \"$ip\" = \"$c\" ] && return 0\n        fi\n    done\n    return 1\n}\n\nselftest() {\n    echo \"=== arpwatch-spoof-scan selftest ===\"\n    echo \"  datafile : $DATAFILE\"\n    echo \"  state    : $STATE\"\n    echo \"  alert    : $ALERT\"\n    echo \"  ignored  : $IGNORE_CIDRS\"\n    echo\n    if [ -r \"$DATAFILE\" ]; then\n        echo \"  datafile readable, $(wc -l < \"$DATAFILE\") entries\"\n    else\n        echo \"  datafile NOT readable yet (arpwatch has not written it)\"\n    fi\n    echo\n    echo \"  use ./selftest-data.sh to run the full detection suite offline\"\n}\n\nif [ \"${1:-}\" = \"--selftest\" ]; then selftest; exit 0; fi\n\n[ -r \"$DATAFILE\" ] || { log \"datafile $DATAFILE not readable; nothing to scan\"; exit 0; }\n\n# Build the current anomaly set: \"ip mac1,mac2,...\" for every IP with more than\n# one DISTINCT MAC.\n#\n# Distinctness matters. Testing for a comma is not enough: arpwatch can list the\n# same hardware address twice with different capitalisation, and joining those\n# yields \"mac,mac\" -- a comma, but still one device. Counting unique values is\n# the only way to avoid alerting on a formatting difference.\ncurrent=\"$(\n    awk ''NF >= 2 {\n            ip = $2\n            mac = tolower($1)\n            if (!(ip in seen)) { seen[ip] = mac; order[ip] = 1; next }\n            n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n            for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) if (arr[i] == mac) next\n            seen[ip] = seen[ip] \" \" mac\n         }\n         END {\n            for (ip in order) {\n                n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n                if (n < 2) continue\n                s = arr[1]\n                for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) s = s \",\" arr[i]\n                print ip \" \" s\n            }\n         }'' \"$DATAFILE\" 2>/dev/null | sort\n)\"\n\nif [ -z \"$current\" ]; then\n    # Clear the state, not just skip. Leaving it stale means a spoof that is\n    # resolved and later returns would be silently swallowed as \"already seen\".\n    [ -s \"$STATE\" ] && { : >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not clear state file $STATE\"; }\n    log \"scan: no anomalies\"\n    exit 0\nfi\n\nprevious=\"\"\n[ -r \"$STATE\" ] && previous=\"$(cat \"$STATE\")\"\n\n# Only alert on what is newly anomalous. A condition carried over from the last\n# scan has already been reported.\nnewly=\"\"\nwhile read -r ip macs; do\n    [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n    in_ignore \"$ip\" && { log \"scan: $ip ($macs) ignored by BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE\"; continue; }\n    if ! printf ''%s\\n'' \"$previous\" | grep -qxF \"$ip $macs\"; then\n        newly+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''\n    else\n        log \"scan: $ip ($macs) persists, not re-alerting\"\n    fi\ndone <<<\"$current\"\n\nprintf ''%s'' \"$current\" >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not write state file $STATE\"\n\n[ -z \"$newly\" ] && { log \"scan: no new anomalies\"; exit 0; }\n\ncount=$(printf ''%s'' \"$newly\" | grep -c .)\nbootstrap_session_env\nwhile read -r ip macs; do\n    [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n    n=$(awk -F, ''{print NF}'' <<<\"$macs\")\n    log \"SPOOF $ip claimed by $n MACs: $macs\"\n    payload=$(python3 - \"$ip\" \"$macs\" \"$n\" <<''PY''\nimport json, sys\nip, macs, n = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3]\nprint(json.dumps({\n    \"severity\": \"critical\",\n    \"events\": [{\n        \"label\": \"ARP spoof: {} claimed by {} MACs\".format(ip, n),\n        \"count\": 1,\n        \"sample\": \"MACs: {}. Not auto-banned: DHCP re-use and MAC randomisation \"\n                  \"look identical. Verify, then ban via the 86400s fail2ban path \"\n                  \"if real.\".format(macs),\n    }],\n}))\nPY\n)\n    [ -f \"$ALERT\" ] || { log \"alert script missing at $ALERT\"; continue; }\n    out=\"$(python3 \"$ALERT\" \"$payload\" 2>&1)\" || log \"alert delivery failed\"\n    [ -n \"$out\" ] && log \"alert: $out\"\ndone <<<\"$newly\"\n\nlog \"scan: $count new anomal$( [ \"$count\" = 1 ] && echo y || echo ies)\"","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":187,"totalLines":187,"truncated":false}},"title":"home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh","time":{"start":1790459174059,"end":1790459174071}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaedcbc001ecZcLtulHK9iAg','msg_0dfae7408001Pwzrr9Aojaf9cH','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459174076,1790459174077,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":72549,"input":34,"output":62,"reasoning":2756,"cache":{"write":0,"read":69697}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaee374001EEVfoLm0N2fx2E','msg_0dfaedcd3001h4APBSDKdDBiOl','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459175797,1790459175797,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaee376001Pc86vlpHWuSGC7','msg_0dfaedcd3001h4APBSDKdDBiOl','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459175798,1790459195692,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Now I can see the critical bug clearly:\n\n**Line 113-131: the awk ignores the timestamp entirely.** It scans the ENTIRE datafile and flags any IP that has ever had more than one MAC.\n\n`arp.dat` is an append-only historical log. arpwatch only appends a binding the FIRST time it sees a mac/ip pair, and it never removes old rows. So:\n\n- A laptop gets 10.197.250.100 today with MAC A\n- Tomorrow the DHCP lease moves 10.197.250.100 to a different laptop with MAC B\n- arp.dat now has BOTH rows forever\n- My scanner concludes \"10.197.250.100 claimed by 2 MACs\" — permanently\n- It alerts once (dedup), then stays \"persists, not re-alerting\" forever\n\nOn a large enterprise DHCP /8 with hundreds of devices, this WILL fire, and once it fires the entry never clears. The dedup hides the repetition but the alert is a permanent false positive.\n\nWorse: it pollutes `current`, which is written to STATE. So a stale anomaly never leaves the state.\n\nThis is the most serious bug in the whole thing. On this exact machine (a /8 with constant DHCP churn) it''s a near-certain false positive.\n\n**THE FIX:** use the timestamp field. Only consider bindings within a recent window. arpwatch appends with a unix timestamp, so a MAC *change* shows as two rows with different timestamps. If I only count MACs seen within the last N (e.g., 30 min), then:\n- Historical MAC change: old row falls out of the window → single MAC → no alert\n- Active spoof: both MACs are being seen NOW → both in window → alert\n\nWindow choice: arpwatch flushes every 15 min, and the scan HUPs for a fresh flush. A window of ~30-60 min is safe: long enough that a genuine concurrent conflict overlaps, short enough that a stale binding ages out. But careful — arpwatch only appends a pair ONCE (first sighting). If a spoofer appears, the row is written once with that timestamp. If the spoof lasts 5 minutes, the row exists with a timestamp from that window. Since we only re-append never, the row''s timestamp is fixed at first sighting. So a window means: the pair must have been FIRST SEEN within the last WINDOW. For an ongoing spoof where the attacker keeps sending ARP with the same mac/ip, arpwatch won''t append again (already known) → after the window passes, the alert clears even though the spoof continues. That''s a false negative.\n\nHmm. That''s the trade-off. arpwatch''s datafile can''t distinguish \"still spoofing\" from \"seen once long ago\".\n\nWhich error is worse? A permanent false positive that trains the user to ignore alerts (bad), vs a spoof that clears from view after the window while continuing (also bad, but the attack is noisy in other ways and arpwatch''s own flip-flop reports go to the journal).\n\nGiven this machine''s DHCP reality, false positives are near-certain and would destroy trust in the alert. A window is the right call. And I should be explicit about the trade-off in comments.\n\nBetter design: combine both signals.\n- Alert on a MAC conflict among RECENT entries (window), so old changes age out.\n- That catches the realistic attack (attacker actively ARPing with a different MAC → gets appended → alert within the window).\n\nAlso: since arpwatch flushes every 15 min and we HUP each scan, a window of ~45-60 min gives 3-4 scans of visibility per appended pair. An attacker spoofing continuously gets ONE append, so we see it for up to 60 min. Reasonable.\n\nLet me also handle: if the same IP legitimately flaps between two MACs right now (e.g., a device with two NICs, or a phone switching between a real MAC and a randomized MAC) → false positive. That''s inherent; the alert text already says \"verify\".\n\nSecond bug to check: line 117 `if (!(ip in seen)) { seen[ip] = mac; order[ip] = 1; next }` — fine.\n\nLine 119: `for (i=1;i<=n;i++) if (arr[i]==mac) next` — `next` inside a for loop in awk skips to the next RECORD, not the next iteration. That''s actually the intent here (skip this record entirely if the MAC is already known). Correct, though subtle. It works because if the mac is already present, we don''t want to append. Good.\n\nBut careful: if the mac is already present, `next` skips the record. If NOT present, the for loop completes without next, and we append. Correct.\n\nThird: `sort` on the output — sorts \"ip mac1,mac2\" lines lexicographically. Deterministic. Good.\n\nNow other checks:\n- Line 150: `grep -qxF \"$ip $macs\"` — exact fixed-string line match. Good.\n- Line 157: writes `current` (ALL anomalies including ignored ones? No — `current` is computed before the ignore filter. The ignore filter happens in the `while read` loop at 147-155, which only adds to `newly`. But `current` written to STATE includes IGNORED anomalies! So an ignored IP''s entry goes into the state file. Then next scan, `previous` contains it, and the loop skips it via in_ignore anyway. Minor inconsistency but not a bug per se. Actually it IS slightly wrong: the state should reflect what we''ve alerted on. If BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE is added later, previously-ignored entries in state would... still be ignored. Fine. Low priority. But cleaner to write only non-ignored anomalies to state. Let me fix for correctness: build the state from the non-ignored set.\n\nActually there''s a subtle bug: if an IP is in `current` but ignored, it gets written to STATE. Later, if the user removes it from IGNORE_CIDRS, the next scan sees it in `previous` and treats it as \"persists, not re-alerting\" → NEVER alerts. That''s a real (if narrow) bug. Fix: exclude ignored entries from the state.\n\n- Line 104: if datafile unreadable → exit 0. Fine.\n- Line 136: `[ -s \"$STATE\" ] && { : >\"$STATE\" ... }` — clears state when no anomalies. Good.\n- Line 161: `count=$(printf ''%s'' \"$newly\" | grep -c .)` — counts lines. Fine.\n- Line 165: `n=$(awk -F, ''{print NF}'' <<<\"$macs\")` — counts MACs by commas. Since macs is built as comma-joined distinct MACs, correct.\n- Line 183: `python3 \"$ALERT\" \"$payload\"` — payload could be large with many MACs; command-line arg limit is fine.\n- Line 175: the sample text mentions \"86400s fail2ban path\" — accurate.\n- `bootstrap_session_env` uses EUID — but the unit now sets XDG_RUNTIME_DIR explicitly, so the `if [ -z DBUS ]` guard means it won''t override. Good. But note: if DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS is already set (by the unit), it skips setting both. Fine.\n- Line 45-46: `local uid=\"${EUID:-$UID}\"` — fine.\n- Wayland detection: needed for notify-send? With DBus address set, notify-send works without WAYLAND_DISPLAY. Harmless.\n- `in_ignore` spawns python3 per IP per CIDR — for a large datafile with many anomaly IPs this is slow-ish, but `current` is usually small. Acceptable. Though with the window fix, `current` will be smaller. Fine.\n- `set -uo pipefail` — no `-e`. Intentional (don''t die on grep returning 1). Good.\n- Line 149: `in_ignore \"$ip\" && { log ...; continue; }` — with `set -u` fine.\n\nNow let me check the OTHER files: timer, installer temp dir leak, TARGET_USER consistency, alert script.\n\nLet me read them.\n\nLet me also verify: does the timer have the right settings?\n\nLet me read timer + installer relevant parts + alert script.\n\nLet me do parallel reads.\n\nPriority bugs to fix:\n1. **No time window → permanent false positives on legitimate MAC changes** (critical)\n2. **Ignored entries written to state → can never alert later** (minor but real)\n3. **Installer leaks a temp dir for the probe pidfile** (minor)\n4. **Hardcoded livings/1000 in units vs TARGET_USER in installer** (latent inconsistency)\n\nLet me read the remaining files then fix all.\n\nLet me read the timer and the installer''s relevant sections.","time":{"start":1790459175798,"end":1790459195690}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf312e001zluLDKw9XhEhmL','msg_0dfaedcd3001h4APBSDKdDBiOl','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459195694,1790459196726,'{"type":"text","text":"Found a serious one. Let me check the rest before fixing:","time":{"start":1790459195694,"end":1790459196725}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf3131001hWOrgHP5SPF26W','msg_0dfaedcd3001h4APBSDKdDBiOl','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459195697,1790459196739,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_4iv32rqnosaq_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd ~/Projects/zen-hardening\necho \"=== timer ===\"; cat arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== installer: TARGET_USER / hardcoded identity ===\"\ngrep -nE \"TARGET_USER|TARGET_HOME|sed -e|SUDO_USER|id -un\" install-arpwatch-spoof.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== units: hardcoded identity that sed would NOT rewrite ===\"\ngrep -nE \"^(User|Group)=|run/user\" arpwatch-spoof-scan.service | sed ''s/^/  /''"},"output":"=== timer ===\n  [Unit]\n  Description=Scan for ARP spoofing every 2 minutes\n  Requires=arpwatch-spoof.service\n  After=arpwatch-spoof.service\n  \n  [Timer]\n  OnBootSec=2min\n  OnUnitActiveSec=2min\n  AccuracySec=15s\n  Unit=arpwatch-spoof-scan.service\n  \n  [Install]\n  WantedBy=timers.target\n\n=== installer: TARGET_USER / hardcoded identity ===\n  26:# reports the hook as missing. SUDO_USER names whoever actually invoked it.\n  27:SUDO_USER_NAME=\"${SUDO_USER:-}\"\n  28:if [ -n \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" ]; then\n  29:    TARGET_HOME=\"$(getent passwd \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" | cut -d: -f6)\"\n  31:    TARGET_HOME=\"$HOME\"\n  33:[ -n \"$TARGET_HOME\" ] && [ -d \"$TARGET_HOME\" ] || {\n  34:    echo \"ERROR: cannot resolve the invoking user''s home (got ''$TARGET_HOME'')\" >&2\n  37:HOOK=\"$TARGET_HOME/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh\"\n  38:SCAN=\"$TARGET_HOME/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\"\n  60:echo \"  home      : $TARGET_HOME (from SUDO_USER=${SUDO_USER:-none})\"\n  140:    sed -e \"s#/home/livings#$TARGET_HOME#g\" \"$HERE/$u\" > \"$UNIT_TMP\"\n  142:        sed -i \"/^\\[Service\\]/a Environment=HOME=$TARGET_HOME\" \"$UNIT_TMP\"\n\n=== units: hardcoded identity that sed would NOT rewrite ===\n  21:# /run/user/0 -- a bus that does not exist. Notifications failed silently while\n  25:Environment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000\n  26:Environment=DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/1000/bus\n","metadata":{"output":"=== timer ===\n  [Unit]\n  Description=Scan for ARP spoofing every 2 minutes\n  Requires=arpwatch-spoof.service\n  After=arpwatch-spoof.service\n  \n  [Timer]\n  OnBootSec=2min\n  OnUnitActiveSec=2min\n  AccuracySec=15s\n  Unit=arpwatch-spoof-scan.service\n  \n  [Install]\n  WantedBy=timers.target\n\n=== installer: TARGET_USER / hardcoded identity ===\n  26:# reports the hook as missing. SUDO_USER names whoever actually invoked it.\n  27:SUDO_USER_NAME=\"${SUDO_USER:-}\"\n  28:if [ -n \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" ]; then\n  29:    TARGET_HOME=\"$(getent passwd \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" | cut -d: -f6)\"\n  31:    TARGET_HOME=\"$HOME\"\n  33:[ -n \"$TARGET_HOME\" ] && [ -d \"$TARGET_HOME\" ] || {\n  34:    echo \"ERROR: cannot resolve the invoking user''s home (got ''$TARGET_HOME'')\" >&2\n  37:HOOK=\"$TARGET_HOME/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh\"\n  38:SCAN=\"$TARGET_HOME/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\"\n  60:echo \"  home      : $TARGET_HOME (from SUDO_USER=${SUDO_USER:-none})\"\n  140:    sed -e \"s#/home/livings#$TARGET_HOME#g\" \"$HERE/$u\" > \"$UNIT_TMP\"\n  142:        sed -i \"/^\\[Service\\]/a Environment=HOME=$TARGET_HOME\" \"$UNIT_TMP\"\n\n=== units: hardcoded identity that sed would NOT rewrite ===\n  21:# /run/user/0 -- a bus that does not exist. Notifications failed silently while\n  25:Environment=XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000\n  26:Environment=DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/1000/bus\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd ~/Projects/zen-hardening\necho \"=== timer ===\"; cat arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== installer: TARGET_USER / hardcoded identity ===\"\ngrep -nE \"TARGET_USER|TARGET_HOME|sed -e|SUDO_USER|id -un\" install-arpwatch-spoof.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== units: hardcoded identity that sed would NOT rewrite ===\"\ngrep -nE \"^(User|Group)=|run/user\" arpwatch-spoof-scan.service | sed ''s/^/  /''","time":{"start":1790459196720,"end":1790459196739}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf3545001IyKy7w86O7rfry','msg_0dfaedcd3001h4APBSDKdDBiOl','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459196741,1790459196741,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":77162,"input":3838,"output":174,"reasoning":1889,"cache":{"write":0,"read":71261}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf3b91001cO3DBHJywAF8z7','msg_0dfaf3559001qDQc6EtZ7oGbSx','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459198353,1790459198354,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf3b94001b3ptbxJ1XKYrgE','msg_0dfaf3559001qDQc6EtZ7oGbSx','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459198356,1790459203473,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed several issues:\n\n1. **CRITICAL: no time window in the scanner.** arp.dat is append-only history; any legitimate MAC change (DHCP reassignment) creates a permanent \"spoof\" that never clears. On this /8 that''s near-certain.\n\n2. **Ignored entries written to STATE** → if BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE changes, previously-ignored entries are \"persists\" and never alert.\n\n3. **Units hardcode /run/user/1000 but the installer only rewrites /home/livings.** The installer''s sed would rewrite paths but NOT `/run/user/1000`. So for a different user the bus path would be wrong. Also `User=livings` — wait, I removed User= from the scan unit (it''s root now). Let me check: the grep for `^(User|Group)=` returned nothing, so no User= line. Good — the scan unit runs as root and relies on the explicit env. But the env hardcodes /run/user/1000 while the installer only rewrites /home/livings. Inconsistent for other users. For this single-user box it''s fine but it''s a latent bug and the installer *pretends* to be user-aware (it resolves SUDO_USER) while silently leaving 1000 baked in. Worth fixing for consistency.\n\n4. Installer: `sed -i \"/^\\[Service\\]/a Environment=HOME=$TARGET_HOME\"` only if `^Environment=HOME=` not present. Both units have Environment=HOME=/home/livings, which the first sed rewrites to TARGET_HOME. So the fallback rarely fires. OK.\n\n5. Timer: `OnUnitActiveSec=2min` — with the HUP forcing a flush, 2 min is fine.\n\nNow let me also check the alert script (arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh) still works, and check for the temp dir leak in the installer.\n\nLet me look at the installer''s probe temp handling and the alert script.\n\nThen fix everything.\n\nLet me read those.\n\nLet me check the probe temp lines.\n\nI already know:\n```\nARPWATCH_PROBE_DB=\"$(mktemp -u)/probe.dat\"\nARPWATCH_PROBE_PID=\"$(mktemp -u)/probe.pid\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$ARPWATCH_PROBE_DB\")\"\n...\nrm -rf \"$(dirname \"$ARPWATCH_PROBE_DB\")\"\n```\nTwo separate mktemp -u → two different dirs. The pidfile dir is never removed → leak. And under root, arpwatch creates the pidfile, leaving an orphan /tmp/tmp.XXXX dir each run. Confirmed bug.\n\nAlso `mktemp -u` is a race (TOCTOU) — should use `mktemp -d`.\n\nLet me verify the leak exists in practice and check the alert script.\n\nLet me run checks.","time":{"start":1790459198356,"end":1790459203472}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf4f93001aXWB7IrLzh5mqY','msg_0dfaf3559001qDQc6EtZ7oGbSx','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459203475,1790459204366,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_j574qo3adew4_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd ~/Projects/zen-hardening\necho \"=== installer probe temp handling (leak?) ===\"\ngrep -n \"mktemp\\|rm -rf\" install-arpwatch-spoof.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== orphan temp dirs from the runs so far? ===\"\nls -d /tmp/tmp.* 2>/dev/null | head -10 | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  none\"\necho \"  count: $(ls -d /tmp/tmp.* 2>/dev/null | wc -l)\"\necho\necho \"=== manual test hook still functional? ===\"\nls -l arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''\nhead -3 arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''"},"output":"=== installer probe temp handling (leak?) ===\n  52:ARPWATCH_PROBE_DB=\"$(mktemp -u)/probe.dat\"\n  53:ARPWATCH_PROBE_PID=\"$(mktemp -u)/probe.pid\"\n  107:    rm -rf \"$(dirname \"$ARPWATCH_PROBE_DB\")\"\n  138:UNIT_TMP=\"$(mktemp)\"\n\n=== orphan temp dirs from the runs so far? ===\n  count: 0\n\n=== manual test hook still functional? ===\n  -rwxr-xr-x 1 livings livings 11489 Sep 26 14:13 arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh\n  #!/bin/bash\n  # arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh — arpwatch(8) exec hook for local spoof detection.\n  #\n","metadata":{"output":"=== installer probe temp handling (leak?) ===\n  52:ARPWATCH_PROBE_DB=\"$(mktemp -u)/probe.dat\"\n  53:ARPWATCH_PROBE_PID=\"$(mktemp -u)/probe.pid\"\n  107:    rm -rf \"$(dirname \"$ARPWATCH_PROBE_DB\")\"\n  138:UNIT_TMP=\"$(mktemp)\"\n\n=== orphan temp dirs from the runs so far? ===\n  count: 0\n\n=== manual test hook still functional? ===\n  -rwxr-xr-x 1 livings livings 11489 Sep 26 14:13 arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh\n  #!/bin/bash\n  # arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh — arpwatch(8) exec hook for local spoof detection.\n  #\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd ~/Projects/zen-hardening\necho \"=== installer probe temp handling (leak?) ===\"\ngrep -n \"mktemp\\|rm -rf\" install-arpwatch-spoof.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''\necho\necho \"=== orphan temp dirs from the runs so far? ===\"\nls -d /tmp/tmp.* 2>/dev/null | head -10 | sed ''s/^/  /'' || echo \"  none\"\necho \"  count: $(ls -d /tmp/tmp.* 2>/dev/null | wc -l)\"\necho\necho \"=== manual test hook still functional? ===\"\nls -l arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''\nhead -3 arpwatch-spoof-alert.sh | sed ''s/^/  /''","time":{"start":1790459204349,"end":1790459204365}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf53110014VE0OMsxL4hx2a','msg_0dfaf3559001qDQc6EtZ7oGbSx','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459204369,1790459204370,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":78423,"input":465,"output":203,"reasoning":595,"cache":{"write":0,"read":77160}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf5897001t5hZAKHxInVqTW','msg_0dfaf5327001wPk1F0ionbBxoN','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459205783,1790459205784,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf5899001E8uKEMz2iIK41V','msg_0dfaf5327001wPk1F0ionbBxoN','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459205785,1790459213885,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"No orphan dirs yet because the probe ran unprivileged (dry-run) so arpwatch exited before writing the pidfile. Under root (real install) it WOULD create the pidfile dir and leak. So the bug is latent — confirmed by reasoning, not yet observed.\n\nNow let me fix the bugs. Priority:\n\n1. **CRITICAL: time window** — the scanner treats arp.dat as current state, but it''s append-only history. Legit MAC changes produce permanent false positives.\n\n2. **Ignored entries in STATE** → never alert if the ignore list changes.\n\n3. **Installer temp dir leak** under root (two mktemp -u calls).\n\n4. **/run/user/1000 hardcoded** while installer claims user-awareness.\n\nLet me implement the time-window fix carefully.\n\nDesign:\n- Add `WINDOW_SECS` (default e.g. 3600 = 60 min), env-overridable `BDE_SPOOF_WINDOW`.\n- In the awk, only consider rows whose timestamp >= now - WINDOW.\n- Timestamp field is $3 (unix seconds). Some rows may lack it? Format is 3-4 fields, so $3 always exists. But be defensive: if $3 isn''t numeric, treat as recent (or skip?). Better: treat non-numeric as \"include\" to avoid missing an active spoof due to parse issues. Hmm, that reintroduces false positives for malformed rows. Let me treat non-numeric as recent (include) — safer for detection, and malformed rows shouldn''t exist.\n\nActually, careful: `arpwatch -C` compact format changes MAC but timestamp is still field 3. Fine.\n\nImplementation in awk: pass now and cutoff via -v.\n\n```awk\nBEGIN { cutoff = systime() - window }\nNF >= 3 && ($3 + 0) >= cutoff { ... }\n```\nUse systime() so tests are deterministic relative to now. But for TESTING I need to control the clock. If I use systime(), my test fixtures must use current timestamps. That''s fine — I''ll generate fixtures with `date +%s`.\n\nBetter for testability: allow BDE_SPOOF_NOW override for tests. Let me add `BDE_SPOOF_NOW` env (defaults to systime()) passed as -v now=... That makes tests deterministic.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nAlso: the `sort` and output building stays the same.\n\nLet me also fix the state to exclude ignored entries.\n\nRestructure:\n```bash\n# build current (recent-window anomalies)\n# then filter ignored ones into `current` and the rest into `newly`\nalerts_now=\"\"   # non-ignored anomalies -> goes to STATE\nnewly=\"\"\nwhile read -r ip macs; do\n    [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n    if in_ignore \"$ip\"; then log ...; continue; fi\n    alerts_now+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''\n    if ! grep -qxF \"$ip $macs\" <<<\"$previous\"; then newly+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''; else log persists; fi\ndone <<<\"$current\"\nprintf ''%s'' \"$alerts_now\" > \"$STATE\"\n```\nAnd the \"no anomalies → clear state\" branch: if current is empty. But if current is non-empty yet all ignored, alerts_now is empty → state should be cleared too. Let me handle: if alerts_now empty → clear state (truncate) and exit.\n\nLet me restructure cleanly.\n\nLet me write the new scanner sections.\n\nLet me be careful to keep it working with `set -uo pipefail`.\n\nLet me implement.\n\nWindow default: what''s sensible? arpwatch flushes every 15 min; we HUP each scan (every 2 min). A pair is appended once. For an active spoof, the attacker keeps sending ARP with the same mac/ip — arpwatch won''t re-append. So visibility is bounded by the window from FIRST sighting. 60 min seems a reasonable balance: catches realistic attacks, lets stale DHCP changes age out in an hour.\n\nLet me set default 3600 and document the trade-off.\n\nLet me now make the edits.\n\nLet me do the awk + window first.","time":{"start":1790459205785,"end":1790459213884}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf783f0010qFtAlMPRYyf39','msg_0dfaf5327001wPk1F0ionbBxoN','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459213887,1790459226726,'{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed 4 bugs. The critical one: **the scanner treats `arp.dat` as current state, but it''s append-only history.** arpwatch only ever *adds* a mac/ip row, so one legitimate DHCP reassignment leaves two MACs on that IP **forever**:\n\n```\n10.197.250.100  aa:bb:...  (laptop A, yesterday)\n10.197.250.100  cc:dd:...  (laptop B, today)\n```\n\nThat alerts once, then \"persists\" indefinitely. On a /8 with constant DHCP churn this is a near-certain false positive — and it never clears. Let me fix it with a time window:","time":{"start":1790459213887,"end":1790459226725}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfaf7ff6001i4elOzD4CKISlM','msg_0dfaf5327001wPk1F0ionbBxoN','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459215862,1790459226737,'{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_function_lxpkrfxu9nh7_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh","newString":"# Build the current anomaly set: \"ip mac1,mac2,...\" for every IP with more than\n# one DISTINCT MAC.\n#\n# THE WINDOW IS ESSENTIAL, NOT COSMETIC.\n#\n# arp.dat is APPEND-ONLY HISTORY, not current state. arpwatch adds a row the\n# first time it sees a mac/ip pair and never removes one. So a single ordinary\n# DHCP reassignment leaves this forever:\n#\n#     10.197.250.100  aa:bb:cc:00:00:01   <- laptop A, yesterday\n#     10.197.250.100  cc:dd:ee:00:00:02   <- laptop B, today\n#\n# Reading the whole file calls that a permanent spoof. It alerts once, then\n# \"persists\" on every scan, and because the state is never cleared the alert can\n# never go away. On a /8 with constant lease churn that is guaranteed to fire on\n# innocent devices, and a monitor that cries wolf gets ignored.\n#\n# So only bindings FIRST SEEN within WINDOW seconds count. A historical change\n# ages out; an attack in progress is inside the window and is reported.\n#\n# The trade-off, stated plainly: arpwatch does not re-append a pair it already\n# knows, so a spoof that is *continuing* is only visible for WINDOW seconds after\n# its first sighting, then ages out like anything else. There is no way to tell\n# \"still spoofing\" from \"seen once\" using the datafile alone. The window is set\n# generously to favour catching attacks over suppressing noise, and arpwatch''s\n# own flip-flop reports keep going to the journal regardless.\n#\n# BDE_SPOOF_NOW exists so tests can pin the clock; production leaves it unset.\n#\n# Distinctness matters. Testing for a comma is not enough: arpwatch can list the\n# same hardware address twice with different capitalisation, and joining those\n# yields \"mac,mac\" -- a comma, but still one device. Counting unique values is\n# the only way to avoid alerting on a formatting difference.\ncurrent=\"$(\n    awk -v now=\"${BDE_SPOOF_NOW:-$(date +%s)}\" -v window=\"$WINDOW\" ''\n    function kept(ts) {\n        # Unparseable timestamp: keep the row rather than risk missing a live\n        # attack over a formatting surprise.\n        if (ts !~ /^[0-9]+$/) return 1\n        return (ts + 0) >= (now - window)\n    }\n    NF >= 3 && kept($3) {\n            ip = $2\n            mac = tolower($1)\n            if (!(ip in seen)) { seen[ip] = mac; order[ip] = 1; next }\n            n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n            for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) if (arr[i] == mac) next\n            seen[ip] = seen[ip] \" \" mac\n         }\n         END {\n            for (ip in order) {\n                n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n                if (n < 2) continue\n                s = arr[1]\n                for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) s = s \",\" arr[i]\n                print ip \" \" s\n            }\n         }'' \"$DATAFILE\" 2>/dev/null | sort\n)\"\n\n# Every IP currently flagged as spoofed, whether or not it alerted.\ncurrent=\"\"\nalerting=\"\"\nwhile read -r ip macs; do\n    [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n    if in_ignore \"$ip\"; then\n        log \"scan: $ip ($macs) ignored by BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE\"\n        continue\n    fi\n    alerting+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''\ndone <<<\"$all\"\n\n# State holds exactly what we are currently reporting on. Ignored addresses are\n# excluded on purpose: if one was recorded while ignored and later removed from\n# BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE, matching against state would call it \"persists\" and it would\n# never alert at all.\nif [ -z \"$alerting\" ]; then\n    # Clear the state, not just skip. Leaving it stale means a spoof that is\n    # resolved and later returns would be silently swallowed as \"already seen\".\n    [ -s \"$STATE\" ] && { : >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not clear state file $STATE\"; }\n    log \"scan: no anomalies\"\n    exit 0\nfi\n\nprevious=\"\"\n[ -r \"$STATE\" ] && previous=\"$(cat \"$STATE\")\"\n\n# Only alert on what is newly anomalous. A condition carried over from the last\n# scan has already been reported.\nnewly=\"\"\nwhile read -r ip macs; do\n    [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n    if ! printf ''%s\\n'' \"$previous\" | grep -qxF \"$ip $macs\"; then\n        newly+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''\n    else\n        log \"scan: $ip ($macs) persists, not re-alerting\"\n    fi\ndone <<<\"$alerting\"\n\nprintf ''%s'' \"$alerting\" >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not write state file $STATE\"","oldString":"# Build the current anomaly set: \"ip mac1,mac2,...\" for every IP with more than\n# one DISTINCT MAC.\n#\n# Distinctness matters. Testing for a comma is not enough: arpwatch can list the\n# same hardware address twice with different capitalisation, and joining those\n# yields \"mac,mac\" -- a comma, but still one device. Counting unique values is\n# the only way to avoid alerting on a formatting difference.\ncurrent=\"$(\n    awk ''NF >= 2 {\n            ip = $2\n            mac = tolower($1)\n            if (!(ip in seen)) { seen[ip] = mac; order[ip] = 1; next }\n            n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n            for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) if (arr[i] == mac) next\n            seen[ip] = seen[ip] \" \" mac\n         }\n         END {\n            for (ip in order) {\n                n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n                if (n < 2) continue\n                s = arr[1]\n                for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) s = s \",\" arr[i]\n                print ip \" \" s\n            }\n         }'' \"$DATAFILE\" 2>/dev/null | sort\n)\"\n\nif [ -z \"$current\" ]; then\n    # Clear the state, not just skip. Leaving it stale means a spoof that is\n    # resolved and later returns would be silently swallowed as \"already seen\".\n    [ -s \"$STATE\" ] && { : >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not clear state file $STATE\"; }\n    log \"scan: no anomalies\"\n    exit 0\nfi\n\nprevious=\"\"\n[ -r \"$STATE\" ] && previous=\"$(cat \"$STATE\")\"\n\n# Only alert on what is newly anomalous. A condition carried over from the last\n# scan has already been reported.\nnewly=\"\"\nwhile read -r ip macs; do\n    [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n    in_ignore \"$ip\" && { log \"scan: $ip ($macs) ignored by BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE\"; continue; }\n    if ! printf ''%s\\n'' \"$previous\" | grep -qxF \"$ip $macs\"; then\n        newly+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''\n    else\n        log \"scan: $ip ($macs) persists, not re-alerting\"\n    fi\ndone <<<\"$current\"\n\nprintf ''%s'' \"$current\" >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not write state file $STATE\""},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\n===================================================================\n--- /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\n+++ /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\n@@ -105,14 +105,47 @@\n \n # Build the current anomaly set: \"ip mac1,mac2,...\" for every IP with more than\n # one DISTINCT MAC.\n #\n+# THE WINDOW IS ESSENTIAL, NOT COSMETIC.\n+#\n+# arp.dat is APPEND-ONLY HISTORY, not current state. arpwatch adds a row the\n+# first time it sees a mac/ip pair and never removes one. So a single ordinary\n+# DHCP reassignment leaves this forever:\n+#\n+#     10.197.250.100  aa:bb:cc:00:00:01   <- laptop A, yesterday\n+#     10.197.250.100  cc:dd:ee:00:00:02   <- laptop B, today\n+#\n+# Reading the whole file calls that a permanent spoof. It alerts once, then\n+# \"persists\" on every scan, and because the state is never cleared the alert can\n+# never go away. On a /8 with constant lease churn that is guaranteed to fire on\n+# innocent devices, and a monitor that cries wolf gets ignored.\n+#\n+# So only bindings FIRST SEEN within WINDOW seconds count. A historical change\n+# ages out; an attack in progress is inside the window and is reported.\n+#\n+# The trade-off, stated plainly: arpwatch does not re-append a pair it already\n+# knows, so a spoof that is *continuing* is only visible for WINDOW seconds after\n+# its first sighting, then ages out like anything else. There is no way to tell\n+# \"still spoofing\" from \"seen once\" using the datafile alone. The window is set\n+# generously to favour catching attacks over suppressing noise, and arpwatch''s\n+# own flip-flop reports keep going to the journal regardless.\n+#\n+# BDE_SPOOF_NOW exists so tests can pin the clock; production leaves it unset.\n+#\n # Distinctness matters. Testing for a comma is not enough: arpwatch can list the\n # same hardware address twice with different capitalisation, and joining those\n # yields \"mac,mac\" -- a comma, but still one device. Counting unique values is\n # the only way to avoid alerting on a formatting difference.\n current=\"$(\n-    awk ''NF >= 2 {\n+    awk -v now=\"${BDE_SPOOF_NOW:-$(date +%s)}\" -v window=\"$WINDOW\" ''\n+    function kept(ts) {\n+        # Unparseable timestamp: keep the row rather than risk missing a live\n+        # attack over a formatting surprise.\n+        if (ts !~ /^[0-9]+$/) return 1\n+        return (ts + 0) >= (now - window)\n+    }\n+    NF >= 3 && kept($3) {\n             ip = $2\n             mac = tolower($1)\n             if (!(ip in seen)) { seen[ip] = mac; order[ip] = 1; next }\n             n = split(seen[ip], arr, \" \")\n@@ -129,9 +162,25 @@\n             }\n          }'' \"$DATAFILE\" 2>/dev/null | sort\n )\"\n \n-if [ -z \"$current\" ]; then\n+# Every IP currently flagged as spoofed, whether or not it alerted.\n+current=\"\"\n+alerting=\"\"\n+while read -r ip macs; do\n+    [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n+    if in_ignore \"$ip\"; then\n+        log \"scan: $ip ($macs) ignored by BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE\"\n+        continue\n+    fi\n+    alerting+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''\n+done <<<\"$all\"\n+\n+# State holds exactly what we are currently reporting on. Ignored addresses are\n+# excluded on purpose: if one was recorded while ignored and later removed from\n+# BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE, matching against state would call it \"persists\" and it would\n+# never alert at all.\n+if [ -z \"$alerting\" ]; then\n     # Clear the state, not just skip. Leaving it stale means a spoof that is\n     # resolved and later returns would be silently swallowed as \"already seen\".\n     [ -s \"$STATE\" ] && { : >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not clear state file $STATE\"; }\n     log \"scan: no anomalies\"\n@@ -145,17 +194,16 @@\n # scan has already been reported.\n newly=\"\"\n while read -r ip macs; do\n     [ -n \"$ip\" ] || continue\n-    in_ignore \"$ip\" && { log \"scan: $ip ($macs) ignored by BDE_SPOOF_IGNORE\"; continue; }\n     if ! printf ''%s\\n'' \"$previous\" | grep -qxF \"$ip $macs\"; then\n         newly+=\"$ip $macs\"$''\\n''\n     else\n         log \"scan: $ip ($macs) persists, not re-alerting\"\n     fi\n-done <<<\"$current\"\n+done <<<\"$alerting\"\n \n-printf ''%s'' \"$current\" >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not write state file $STATE\"\n+printf ''%s'' \"$alerting\" >\"$STATE\" 2>/dev/null || log \"could not write state file $STATE\"\n \n [ -z \"$newly\" ] && { log \"scan: no new anomalies\"; exit 0; }\n \n count=$(printf ''%s'' \"$newly\" | grep -c .)\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh","patch":"Index: /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\n===================================================================\n--- /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\n+++ /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\n@@ -105,14 +105,47 @@\n \n # Build the current anomaly set: \"ip mac1,mac2,...\" for every IP with more than\n # one DISTINCT MAC.\n #\n+# THE WINDOW IS ESSENTIAL, NOT COSMETIC.\n+#\n+# arp.dat is APPEND-ONLY HISTORY, not current state. arpwatch adds a row the\