INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb0fd94001rVwymSi7BCaOqz','msg_0dfb0f461001Z0RWfN069sihil','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459313556,1790459313557,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb0fd96001MJ1jpEvUCu8dGT','msg_0dfb0f461001Z0RWfN069sihil','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459313558,1790459315626,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"OK so:\n- There are NO systemd units for shield-kitty (list-unit-files returned nothing) — it''s run as a plain process (PID 287282 `/usr/bin/python3 /home/livings/Projects/shield-kitty/shield-kitty-watch.py`), not a systemd service. So `shield-kitty-watch.service` doesn''t exist — my earlier `systemctl is-active shield-kitty-watch` said \"inactive\" because the unit doesn''t exist, not because the watcher is stopped.\n\nThe watcher IS running (PID 287282).\n\n- `shield-kitty-alert.py` uses notify-send with an icon. It returned rc=0, no output → the notification path works (notify-send succeeded silently).\n\nSo notifications are fine. The \"inactive\" was a false alarm from checking a non-existent unit name.\n\nLet me verify with --test-notify to be sure, and confirm the watcher process.\n\nLet me do a definitive notification test and ask the user if they saw it.\n\nLet me run --test-notify.\n\nThen finish the audit: commit the fixes, run buildlog, and summarize.\n\nLet me run the test-notify.","time":{"start":1790459313558,"end":1790459315624}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb0ff0a001edIkZsc4mUukBq','msg_0dfb0f04f001ZVSKUAmNylCiiT','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459313931,1790459313931,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":139446,"input":80,"output":485,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":138881}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb104c5001o0qpQPVeBB0vEl','msg_0dfb0ff50001NwDNTmj42KSoUH','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459315397,1790459315398,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb104c7001oiy6XThNQ8kBXi','msg_0dfb0ff50001NwDNTmj42KSoUH','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459315399,1790459320735,'{"type":"text","text":"Work is finished and pushed. `47601c0`, seven commits this session.\n\n## Reboot checklist\n\n**Before** — nothing pending, all 27 checks green. Two optional installs if you want them:\n\n```bash\nsudo pacman -S torbrowser-launcher   # official repo, signature-validated\nsudo pacman -S firefox               # only to revoke the GitHub token\n```\n\n**After** — this is the actual test, since Shield Kitty has never been verified across a real boot:\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --user is-active shield-kitty-watch.service evidence-seal.timer\nsudo audit-nft\n```\n\nExpect `active`/`active`, and a `reject with icmpx admin-prohibited` at the end of the nft chain. If Shield Kitty shows `inactive` after reboot, `linger` isn''t doing its job and I''ll fix that.\n\n**Then remove the temporary root surface** — that NOPASSWD entry was scoped to the audit and shouldn''t outlive the audit:\n\n```bash\nsudo bash /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-audit-readonly.sh --remove\n```\n\n## What the audit found\n\nTwo real defects, both fixed:\n\n- **Shield Kitty had no systemd unit anywhere** — a bare process that would have silently stopped existing. Its units also use `%h`, which in a *system* unit expands to root''s home, so installing them as system units would have produced units that can never start. Installed as user units with `linger`.\n- **`bootstrap.sh` couldn''t handle the recidive change** — it only ever enabled the flag, so a fresh install would have shipped `enabled=true` with no `/var/log/fail2ban.log`, and a jail pointing at a missing log aborts *every* fail2ban jail. Now set both ways, scoped to `[recidive]` because `enabled = true` also appears under `[sshd]`.\n\nConfirmed healthy by direct inspection: nft chain terminates in reject, btrfs scrub clean with all error counters at 0, no OOM or hardware faults, ESP `fsck`-clean, initramfs newer than kernel, 0 pending updates.\n\n## Still open, none of it urgent\n\n- **Revoke the GitHub token.** Needs a browser; GitHub warns against doing it over Tor.\n- **`lifsea-security-suite` vs `hermes-security-scripts`.** Documented in the README as an open decision rather than one I made for you. They''ll drift until you pick a canonical repo.\n- **`machine-id` still unique** — I won''t change it without you deciding, since it breaks services that bind to it.\n- **SDDM greeter** has no automated brute-force protection. Structural, not fixable with config.\n\nOne thing worth carrying forward: every failure this session had the same shape — a value accepted silently, printing `[ok]`, doing the opposite of what was asked. Seven instances, including one that locked you out of sudo. That''s why the README leads with \"verify behaviour, not configuration,\" and why I''d treat any `[ok]` from these scripts as unproven until something independent confirms it.","time":{"start":1790459315399,"end":1790459320733}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb105ab001gI8OdGn6UXm5pB','msg_0dfb0f461001Z0RWfN069sihil','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459315628,1790459316430,'{"type":"text","text":"False alarm on my part — there is no `shield-kitty-watch` *unit*; the watcher runs as a plain process (PID 287282) and it **is** running. The notifier uses `notify-send` and returned rc=0. Let me confirm with its own test:","time":{"start":1790459315627,"end":1790459316430}}');
