datafile cannot distinguish ''still spoofing'' from ''seen once''. Also: ignored addresses were written to the state file, so un-ignoring one later made it match ''persists'' and never alert. Installer: two mktemp -u calls left only one dir cleaned, orphaning a dir per root install; now one mktemp -d. Installer: units hardcoded /run/user/1000 while the installer resolved SUDO_USER for the home path; both now derived. 16/16 tests incl. both previously-broken cases; all scripts parse, all 3 units verify, no temp leak, notify path confirmed live. commit 3efc51f\n","metadata":{"output":"  [✓] logged: audit pass over the arpwatch work, 4 real defects. CRITICAL: scanner treated arp.dat as current state, but arpwatch only ADDS a row per mac/ip pair and never removes one, so one routine DHCP reassignment (laptop A yesterday, laptop B today, same IP) read as a permanent 2-MAC spoof that alerted once then said ''persists'' forever -- guaranteed false positives on a /8 with lease churn. Fixed with a 1h window over the timestamp field arpwatch already writes; documented trade-off that a continuing spoof ages out after the window since the datafile cannot distinguish ''still spoofing'' from ''seen once''. Also: ignored addresses were written to the state file, so un-ignoring one later made it match ''persists'' and never alert. Installer: two mktemp -u calls left only one dir cleaned, orphaning a dir per root install; now one mktemp -d. Installer: units hardcoded /run/user/1000 while the installer resolved SUDO_USER for the home path; both now derived. 16/16 tests incl. both previously-broken cases; all scripts parse, all 3 units verify, no temp leak, notify path confirmed live. commit 3efc51f\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd ~/Projects/buildlog\nbuildlog fix \"audit pass over the arpwatch work, 4 real defects. CRITICAL: scanner treated arp.dat as current state, but arpwatch only ADDS a row per mac/ip pair and never removes one, so one routine DHCP reassignment (laptop A yesterday, laptop B today, same IP) read as a permanent 2-MAC spoof that alerted once then said ''persists'' forever -- guaranteed false positives on a /8 with lease churn. Fixed with a 1h window over the timestamp field arpwatch already writes; documented trade-off that a continuing spoof ages out after the window since the datafile cannot distinguish ''still spoofing'' from ''seen once''. Also: ignored addresses were written to the state file, so un-ignoring one later made it match ''persists'' and never alert. Installer: two mktemp -u calls left only one dir cleaned, orphaning a dir per root install; now one mktemp -d. Installer: units hardcoded /run/user/1000 while the installer resolved SUDO_USER for the home path; both now derived. 16/16 tests incl. both previously-broken cases; all scripts parse, all 3 units verify, no temp leak, notify path confirmed live. commit 3efc51f\" 2>&1 | tail -1","time":{"start":1790459327391,"end":1790459327416}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb133da001dZDZrocFjOaEyQ','msg_0dfb123620019kZ3HFr8MKoKKs','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459327450,1790459327451,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":94810,"input":40,"output":308,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":94462}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb13a47001cjbubTJLAcHjtB','msg_0dfb133f1001ATEyhYBRXLWdXj','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459329095,1790459329095,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb13a48001Vn1gCbNtTqfJCN','msg_0dfb133f1001ATEyhYBRXLWdXj','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459329096,1790459333473,'{"type":"text","text":"Audit done — **4 real bugs, one serious.** Committed as `3efc51f`.\n\n**Critical: the scanner read `arp.dat` as current state, but it''s append-only history.** arpwatch only ever *adds* a mac/ip row, the first time it sees the pair, and never removes one. So one ordinary DHCP reassignment looks like this forever:\n\n```\n10.197.250.100  aa:bb:cc:00:00:01   <- laptop A, yesterday\n10.197.250.100  cc:dd:ee:00:00:02   <- laptop B, today\n```\n\nThe scanner compared every MAC it had *ever* seen for an IP, so it called that a spoof, alerted once, then reported \"persists\" on every scan indefinitely. On your /8 with constant lease churn this fires on innocent devices — and a monitor that cries wolf gets ignored. Fixed with a 1-hour window over the timestamp field arpwatch already writes, so historical changes age out.\n\nThe trade-off is real, so I documented it rather than hiding it: arpwatch doesn''t re-append a pair it already knows, so a *continuing* spoof is only visible for the window after first sighting. The datafile genuinely cannot distinguish \"still spoofing\" from \"seen once\". I biased toward catching attacks; arpwatch''s own flip-flop reports keep going to the journal either way.\n\n**Three more:**\n- **Ignored addresses were written to the dedup state.** Un-ignore one later and it matched \"persists\" and could *never* alert. State now holds only what''s actually reported.\n- **Installer leaked a temp dir per root install** — two `mktemp -u` calls, only one cleaned up. Also `mktemp -u` races, since it only prints a name; now one `mktemp -d`.\n- **Units hardcoded `/run/user/1000`** while the installer carefully resolved `SUDO_USER` for the home path — user-aware in one place, hardcoded in another.\n\n**Verification:** 16/16 tests including both previously-broken cases; all 15 scripts parse; all 3 units pass `systemd-analyze verify`; no temp leak; identity rewriting checked against a simulated second user.\n\nTwo things I want to flag honestly:\n\nI nearly reported Shield Kitty as broken. `systemctl is-active shield-kitty-watch` said `inactive`, but **no such unit exists** — the watcher runs as a plain process, and it *is* running (PID 287282). My check was wrong, not the setup. `--test-notify` confirms delivery works; did you see the test notification?\n\nAnd the deeper pattern worth naming: four of today''s bugs shared one shape — **something that fails silently while reporting healthy.** The root bus, the missing `-q`, the 15-minute flush, and now this. The scan unit also logs *nothing* on successful delivery, so \"no log line\" still can''t distinguish delivered from dropped. Want me to add positive delivery confirmation, so silence stops being ambiguous?","time":{"start":1790459329096,"end":1790459333471}}');
