INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb22397001DrvZuQHAqJpA21','msg_0dfb21dff0019qvKCzDW7FkoYh','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459388823,1790459392095,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The user says \"open the token interface\". This is ambiguous. I have no idea what \"token interface\" means in this context. Let me think.\n\nPossible interpretations:\n1. A TUI/token-based interface for something I built\n2. They want to open some \"token interface\" in opencode\n3. Something related to the shield-kitty / zen-hardening work?\n\nThere''s no \"token interface\" in opencode that I know of. This might be a mis-sentence, or they might mean something specific to their setup.\n\nI should not guess wildly. I should ask for clarification. The user asked me to \"stop and ask for clarification if unsure how to proceed\" earlier — that guidance still applies.\n\nLet me consider: is there anything in the repos named \"token\"? Let me quickly check if there''s a \"token\" related file in their projects, which would make the request interpretable. That''s a cheap, useful check before asking.\n\nLet me search for \"token\" in the projects.\n\nActually, let me be careful about spending too much effort. A quick grep is cheap and could disambiguate.\n\nLet me check.\n\nAlso could they mean an API token interface? Or a \"tokens\" display in opencode (like /status or context usage)?\n\nLet me do a quick search and then ask.\n\nLet me search for files/scripts with \"token\" in the name in their Projects.","time":{"start":1790459388823,"end":1790459392094}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb2259b001hNcRBkCVGzeuSm','msg_0dfb119ee001exia2qbr2XX89K','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459389339,1790459408487,'{"type":"text","text":"## Objective\n- Finish and independently verify the Garuda/Arch hardening, anonymity, rebuild, and reboot-readiness stack; push completed tooling to private repositories.\n- After the core stack survives reboot, resume remaining Void/security-tool, ISO, and emulator work.\n\n## Important Details\n- User `livings` runs Garuda Linux, Plasma/Wayland, fish, and belongs to `wheel`; use fish syntax for shell snippets.\n- Never grant blanket `NOPASSWD: ALL`. Temporary NOPASSWD is limited to fixed, root-owned, argument-ignoring wrappers under `/usr/local/sbin/`.\n- Verify runtime behavior, not parser output or `[ok]`; this session repeatedly found accepted settings that did the opposite of intent.\n- User will reboot manually only after a final go/no-go audit.\n- Other OpenCode/Hermes instances were still writing. Avoid overwriting their files; wait for repo activity to quiet before final integration.\n- `hermes-security-scripts` and new `~/Projects/lifsea-security-suite` overlap; user chose to document their relationship rather than select a canonical repo yet.\n- No direct messaging channel exists to dispatch Hermes.\n- Current source fail2ban config intentionally has `recidive enabled = true`; this is valid because `/var/log/fail2ban.log` exists. `bootstrap.sh` now scopes recidive changes to `[recidive]` and disables it when the log is absent.\n- Keep `workstation` strict; do not allow KDE Connect/mDNS globally. Do not block PMTU or IPv6 NDP.\n- `machine-id`, DMI, Ethernet MAC policy, SDDM protection, and transparent Tor remain explicit decisions/deferred work.\n- Shield Kitty units must remain user units because their `ExecStart` uses `%h`; a system unit would resolve paths under `/root`.\n- `/etc/sudoers.d/opencode` is an empty `0440` file; `/root/sudoers-quarantine-20260926-143446/10-installer` is inactive retained evidence.\n- Do not run `fix-sudoers-permissions.sh` again until it gains a hard “never leave the invoking user without sudo” guard.\n- Audit helpers are temporary; remove after diagnostics with `sudo bash /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-audit-readonly.sh --remove`.\n\n## Work State\n### Completed\n- Hostname is `zero`; `/etc/hostname`, `/etc/hosts`, and runtime agree; DHCP hostname suppression is enabled.\n- NetworkManager MAC randomization is fixed and live:\n  - property `802-11-wireless.mac-address-randomization=always`\n  - current `wlo1` MAC `ea:bd:c8:4d:53:a8`\n  - locally administered, not hardware MAC `f4:a4:75:e8:00:b2`\n- `bootstrap.sh` is idempotent, supports `--check`, verifies live nft enforcement, hostname, fail2ban, and MAC state; current baseline reports `0 checks failed`.\n- `set-home-trusted-zone.sh` now discovers `wlo1` dynamically instead of hardcoding `wlo0`.\n- Firewall is enforced:\n  - firewalld active/enabled\n  - default zone `workstation`, `wlo1` bound\n  - only `dhcpv6-client`; ICMP/IPv6-ICMP allowed without blocking critical types\n  - nft `filter_INPUT` ends in `reject with icmpx admin-prohibited`\n  - globally bound KDE Connect `1716` and mDNS `5353` are therefore blocked\n- Fail2ban is active/enabled with `sshd` and `recidive`, `bantime = 86400`, `zone = workstation`; SSH journal filter is correct and no startup errors remain.\n- Tor is active/enabled, bootstrapped 100%, and SOCKS is loopback-only on `127.0.0.1:9050`.\n- Shield Kitty persistence is fixed:\n  - units under `/home/livings/.config/systemd/user/`\n  - `shield-kitty-watch.service` and `evidence-seal.timer` active/enabled\n  - linger enabled\n  - watcher cgroup is `.../shield-kitty-watch.service`, parented by systemd\n- Shield Kitty coredumps were `alert.py --demo` test runs, not the active watcher; the Xorg coredump was at startup and non-blocking.\n- Boot-readiness checks passed:\n  - no `/var/lib/pacman/db.lck`\n  - zero pending updates, no ignored packages\n  - no `.pacnew`/`.pacsave`\n  - zero failed units and queued jobs\n  - ESP UUID `F54B-53CA` mounted and boot-time fsck clean\n  - dracut `111`; `initramfs-linux-zen.img` newer than `vmlinuz-linux-zen`\n  - running kernel `7.2.7-zen1-1-zen`\n  - boot 49.552s total; graphical target 2.818s into userspace\n- Storage is healthy: ~456.86 GiB free, btrfs scrub found no errors, read/write/flush/corruption counters all zero.\n- Kernel diagnostics found no btrfs warnings, OOM kills, MCE, thermal, PCIe, or hardware faults; only benign TDX, touchscreen, and ACPI capability messages.\n- Sudo lockout was fully recovered:\n  - `/etc/sudoers.d/10-installer` restored as `%wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL`, mode `0440`\n  - `livings` remains in `wheel`\n  - `visudo -c` clean\n  - `editor`, `insults`, `opencode`, and `pwfeedback` are `0440`\n- Scoped passwordless audit helpers are installed and functional:\n  - `/usr/local/sbin/audit-nft`\n  - `/usr/local/sbin/audit-btrfs`\n  - `/usr/local/sbin/audit-smart`\n  - `/usr/local/sbin/audit-dmesg`\n  - `/usr/local/sbin/audit-firewall`\n  - `/usr/local/sbin/audit-fail2ban`\n  - `/usr/local/sbin/audit-journal-err`\n- `/home/livings/.config/gh/token.clean` was shredded with three random passes plus zero pass and verified absent; SSH push still works.\n- Latest pushed `hermes-security-scripts` commit is `983bfea`; relevant fixes include `fbc8523`, `02f1bf3`, and `983bfea`.\n- Private repos also contain Shield Kitty, fail2ban, bootstrap, business monitoring/debugging, antimalware, and audit tooling.\n\n### Active\n- Deploy the corrected comma-aware `audit-fail2ban` parser; source was edited in `install-audit-readonly.sh`, but the installed wrapper is still the old version.\n- Finish remaining diagnostics: final current-boot journal summary, package integrity, SUID/world-writable checks, GPU/current display errors, memory after agents exit, and NVMe health.\n- `nvme-cli` is absent, so `audit-smart` cannot read NVMe SMART/error logs.\n- Wait for other agents/repo activity to go quiet, inspect `~/Projects/zen-hardening` dirty files, commit only reviewed files, and push.\n- Document the relationship between `hermes-security-scripts` and `~/Projects/lifsea-security-suite` to prevent divergence.\n- Run the end-to-end fail2ban ban test using `192.0.2.66`, verify its rich rule in `workstation`, then unban it.\n- Add a reviewed systemd drop-in removing fail2ban’s harmful `PartOf=firewalld.service` propagation while retaining `After=firewalld.service`.\n- Install and launch Tor Browser; the Tor daemon alone currently provides no anonymity.\n- Decide whether to revoke the shredded GitHub token server-side.\n- Decide on Ethernet MAC randomization, `machine-id`, DMI, SDDM screen-lock/lid mitigations, and Shield Kitty device-blacklist installation.\n- Remaining ecosystem work: fresh `void-bin` builds; review/install `bde-security-biometrics`, `lin-antimalware-tools`, `local-emergency-patrol`, `zen-healer`, and `secure-tactical-systems`.\n\n### Blocked\n- Actual reboot survival cannot be proven until the user performs the manual reboot and post-boot checks.\n- SDDM local-greeter brute force cannot be solved with fail2ban IP bans because no remote `rhost` exists.\n- Secure `home-net` activation requires control of the future network’s WPA credentials and DHCP.\n- Transparent Tor proxying remains deferred due firewalld/fail2ban conflicts and DNS/connectivity leak risk.\n- Ephemeral `machine-id` requires an explicit compatibility decision.\n- `zen-healer` remains unsafe due hardcoded `sin`, privileged daemon, `shell=True`, service restarts, Snapper rollback, and journal deletion.\n- `zen-suite` ISO creation uses `pacstrap`; it is not a disk installer.\n- Xbox/Xbox 360 native emulation remains unavailable; console emulation requires legally dumped firmware.\n\n## Next Move\n1. Re-run `sudo bash /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-audit-readonly.sh`, then verify `sudo audit-fail2ban` reports both `recidive` and `sshd` without a trailing-comma jail error.\n2. Run the remaining root diagnostics; install `nvme-cli` with user approval if SMART data is required, then execute `sudo audit-smart` and `sudo audit-journal-err`.\n3. Wait for agent repo activity to quiet, commit only reviewed files, push the latest parser/recovery changes, and document the `lifsea-security-suite` relationship.\n4. Perform the final pre-reboot sweep: normal `sudo -l`, bootstrap check, no pacman lock/updates/failed units/jobs, MAC randomized, Shield Kitty user units active/enabled, firewall/fail2ban/Tor healthy.\n5. Have the user reboot, then verify `systemctl --user is-active shield-kitty-watch.service evidence-seal.timer`, firewall, fail2ban, Tor, hostname, MAC, and network connectivity.\n6. After reboot, install Tor Browser, run the fail2ban ban/unban test, add the systemd dependency fix, and resume remaining tooling work.\n\n## Relevant Files\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/bootstrap.sh`: idempotent security baseline and live-state verification.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/set-anon-identity.sh`: hostname, DHCP hostname, and NM MAC randomization; modern value is `always`.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/set-home-trusted-zone.sh`: deferred home profile with dynamic interface discovery.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/workstation.xml`: strict firewalld zone source.\n- `/etc/firewalld/zones/workstation.xml`: live strict zone.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/shield-kitty-fail2ban.local`: current source has `recidive enabled = true`.\n- `/etc/fail2ban/jail.d/shield-kitty.conf`: live two-jail configuration.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/apply-firewall.sh`: firewall installer and firewalld/fail2ban restart sequencing workaround.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/unban-self.sh`: root-gated self-ban escape hatch.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/fail2ban-coverage.md`: documents unusable local sudo/SDDM jails.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-audit-readonly.sh`: temporary scoped audit-helper installer; latest source fix handles comma-separated jail lists.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/RECOVER-SUDO.sh`: non-destructive sudo recovery using `PKEXEC_UID`; currently successful.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/fix-sudoers-permissions.sh`: corrected classifier, but do not run until it guarantees sudo continuity.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-shield-kitty-units.sh`: user-unit installer with linger and duplicate-process checks.\n- `/home/livings/.config/systemd/user/shield-kitty-watch.service`: active user watcher unit.\n- `/home/livings/.config/systemd/user/evidence-seal.service`: active evidence sealer.\n- `/home/livings/.config/systemd/user/evidence-seal.timer`: active hourly timer.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/shield-kitty/shield-kitty-watch.py`: systemd-owned intrusion watcher.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/shield-kitty/device-blacklist/`: reviewed but not root-installed udev enforcement.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/shield-kitty-forensics/evidence.py`: evidence-chain implementation.\n- `/home/livings/.local/share/shield-kitty/evidence/chain.jsonl`: evidence chain.\n- `/home/livings/.config/shield-kitty/evidence.key`: mode-600 evidence key.\n- `/etc/sudoers.d/10-installer`: restored stock `%wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL` rule.\n- `/etc/sudoers.d/audit-readonly`: valid narrow NOPASSWD wrapper list.\n- `/root/sudoers-quarantine-20260926-143446/10-installer`: inactive retained quarantine copy.\n- `/usr/local/sbin/audit-*`: seven installed audit wrappers; `audit-fail2ban` still needs redeployment.\n- `/home/livings/.config/gh/token.clean`: shredded and absent.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening`: active local source, previously dirty from parallel agents.\n- `/tmp/opencode/hss`: private consolidated repository checkout used for pushes.\n- `git@github.com:intricos777-dot/hermes-security-scripts.git`: primary pushed security repository.\n- `/home/livings/Projects/lifsea-security-suite`: second consolidating repository awaiting relationship documentation.\n- `/tmp/hermes-handoff.md`: prior Shield Kitty handoff.","time":{"start":1790459389339,"end":1790459408484}}');
