INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb2e592001omClL6k3WLvE6U','msg_0dfb2df5a001FWA1R8c5MhRW6U','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459438483,1790459438484,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb2e595001cAOGfIc78P0bjt','msg_0dfb2df5a001FWA1R8c5MhRW6U','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459438485,1790459440379,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: `systemctl enable --now arpwatch-spoof.service` will not restart the already-running old process. The new ExecStart (with -D and -P) would only take effect on next boot or manual restart. So:\n- The pidfile /run/arpwatch-spoof.pid would never be created\n- The scanner''s ExecStartPre HUP would silently do nothing\n- We''d be back to scanning a 15-minute-stale file — the exact bug we fixed\n\nThat''s a real bug. Let me fix: use `enable` + `restart` for the collector.\n\nAlso the closing message references the exec hook (\"If the first real event shows the wrong fields, arpwatch''s argument order differs from what the hook assumes\") — that''s stale, since 3.9 has no exec hook and we use the datafile scanner. Misleading. Let me fix that too.\n\nLet me make both fixes.\n\nLet me edit.","time":{"start":1790459438485,"end":1790459440378}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb2e860001c7iTMzNqLPWff7','msg_0dfb2e2b8001DGhTv2luqy6vhq','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459439201,1790459439204,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb2e866001J7hxO9PMgLn1Nl','msg_0dfb2e2b8001DGhTv2luqy6vhq','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459439206,1790459449134,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: `SUDO_USER_NAME=\"${SUDO_USER:-livings}\"` — hardcoded `livings` fallback. Under pkexec it resolves to `livings` (correct by luck!) but the home path issue in the other two is real.\n\nWait: `install-audit-readonly.sh` → `livings` (hardcoded), so it actually WORKS under pkexec but only by luck of the hardcoded name. Non-portable but not broken on this machine.\n\n`install-shield-kitty-units.sh` → `ACCOUNT=livings` (correct by luck) but `SRC_WATCH=\"$HOME/Projects/...\"` = `/root/Projects/...` (broken).\n\n`install-arpwatch-spoof.sh` → genuinely broken under pkexec.\n\nSo the highest-value fix is:\n1. `install-arpwatch-spoof.sh` — real breakage, installs persistent broken units\n2. `install-shield-kitty-units.sh` — `$HOME` under pkexec breaks source lookup\n3. All three — hardcoded `livings` fallback is non-portable\n\nLet me write a single canonical resolution block and apply it to all three. That''s the right fix: one correct pattern, consistently.\n\nLet me implement. I''ll create the canonical block and patch each script.\n\nThe canonical block:\n```bash\n# Resolve the REAL invoking user and their home. Resolution order matters:\n#   PKEXEC_UID : pkexec does NOT set SUDO_USER and sets USER=root\n#   SUDO_USER  : set by sudo\n#   logname    : already root, but a session user exists\n# Hardcoding a name here is what previously made these scripts audit or\n# rewrite the wrong account under pkexec.\nresolve_invoker() {\n    if [ -n \"${PKEXEC_UID:-}\" ]; then\n        _u=$(getent passwd \"$PKEXEC_UID\" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f1)\n    elif [ -n \"${SUDO_USER:-}\" ]; then\n        _u=\"$SUDO_USER\"\n    else\n        _u=$(logname 2>/dev/null || true)\n    [ -n \"${_u:-}\" ] && [ \"$_u\" != root ] || _u=$(id -un)\n    printf ''%s'' \"$_u\"\n}\n```\n\nHmm, that''s getting complex. Let me keep it simpler and match RECOVER-SUDO.sh''s proven pattern (which worked in production):\n\n```bash\nif [ -n \"${PKEXEC_UID:-}\" ]; then\n  ACCOUNT=$(getent passwd \"$PKEXEC_UID\" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f1)\nelif [ -n \"${SUDO_USER:-}\" ]; then\n  ACCOUNT=\"$SUDO_USER\"\nelse\n  ACCOUNT=\"${USER:-$(logname 2>/dev/null)}\"\nfi\n[ -n \"$ACCOUNT\" ] && [ \"$ACCOUNT\" != root ] || ACCOUNT=$(logname 2>/dev/null || id -un)\n```\n\nThe last line is the key safety: if we somehow still resolved to root, fall back to the console user via logname. That prevents ever targeting root''s home.\n\nAnd home: `TARGET_HOME=\"$(getent passwd \"$ACCOUNT\" | cut -d: -f6)\"`\n\nLet me apply this to the three scripts.\n\nFor `install-arpwatch-spoof.sh`, replace lines 25-42 (the resolution block).\nFor `install-shield-kitty-units.sh`, replace line 32-35 region and add home resolution.\nFor `install-audit-readonly.sh`, replace line 44.\n\nLet me do the edits.\n\nLet me read the exact text to replace.\n\nFor install-arpwatch-spoof.sh, the block is (from the sed output, lines 25-42 area). Let me get exact line numbers: the sed showed line 20 = PIDFILE, so:\nline 25 = `SUDO_USER_NAME=\"${SUDO_USER:-}\"`\nline 26 = `if [ -n \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" ]; then`\n...\n\nLet me just use edit with the exact old string I saw.\n\nThe old string in install-arpwatch-spoof.sh:\n```\n# Resolve the REAL user''s home. Under sudo, $HOME is /root, so every path into\n# the user''s own ~/Projects silently becomes /root/Projects/... and the script\n# reports the hook as missing. SUDO_USER names whoever actually invoked it.\nSUDO_USER_NAME=\"${SUDO_USER:-}\"\nif [ -n \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" ]; then\n    TARGET_HOME=\"$(getent passwd \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" | cut -d: -f6)\"\nelse\n    TARGET_HOME=\"$HOME\"\nfi\n[ -n \"$TARGET_HOME\" ] && [ -d \"$TARGET_HOME\" ] || {\n    echo \"ERROR: cannot resolve the invoking user''s home (got ''$TARGET_HOME'')\" >&2\n    exit 1\n}\n```\n\nAnd the TARGET_USER line:\n```\nTARGET_USER=\"${SUDO_USER_NAME:-$(stat -c %U \"$TARGET_HOME\")}\"\nTARGET_UID=\"$(id -u \"$TARGET_USER\" 2>/dev/null || echo 1000)\"\n```\n\nLet me replace the first block with a correct one that handles PKEXEC_UID.\n\nNew:\n```\n# Resolve the REAL invoking user and their home. Two separate traps here:\n#\n#  1. Under sudo, $HOME is /root, so every path into the user''s own ~/Projects\n#     silently becomes /root/Projects/... and the hook reads as missing.\n#  2. pkexec does NOT set SUDO_USER at all, so a SUDO_USER-only script resolves\n#     to root and installs units pointing at /run/user/0 and /root/Projects/...\n#     Those units are broken permanently, which is worse than a clean failure.\n#\n# PKEXEC_UID is the reliable signal under pkexec. Never fall back to a\n# hardcoded account name, and never accept \"root\" as the invoker.\nif [ -n \"${PKEXEC_UID:-}\" ]; then\n    SUDO_USER_NAME=\"$(getent passwd \"$PKEXEC_UID\" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f1)\"\nelif [ -n \"${SUDO_USER:-}\" ]; then\n    SUDO_USER_NAME=\"$SUDO_USER\"\nelse\n    SUDO_USER_NAME=\"$(logname 2>/dev/null || true)\"\nfi\nif [ -z \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" ] || [ \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" = root ]; then\n    SUDO_USER_NAME=\"$(logname 2>/dev/null || id -un)\"\nfi\nTARGET_HOME=\"$(getent passwd \"$SUDO_USER_NAME\" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f6)\"\n[ -n \"$TARGET_HOME\" ] && [ -d \"$TARGET_HOME\" ] || {\n    echo \"ERROR: cannot resolve the invoking user''s home (got ''$TARGET_HOME'' for ''$SUDO_USER_NAME'')\" >&2\n    exit 1\n}\n```\n\nAnd the TARGET_USER/UID lines can stay since SUDO_USER_NAME is now always set. But `stat -c %U \"$TARGET_HOME\"` fallback is now unnecessary. Let me simplify to just use SUDO_USER_NAME.\n\nLet me make the edits.\n\nLet me do install-arpwatch-spoof.sh first.","time":{"start":1790459439206,"end":1790459449132}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb2ecfc001MUVVdQ1mz0TFyK','msg_0dfb2df5a001FWA1R8c5MhRW6U','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459440380,1790459443406,'{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed — a real bug. `enable --now` leaves the old process running, so `-P` never takes effect, the pidfile is never created, and the scanner''s HUP silently does nothing. That''s the exact stale-data bug we just fixed, coming back.","time":{"start":1790459440380,"end":1790459443406}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb2edfb00188LXnmZKL178WY','msg_0dfb2df5a001FWA1R8c5MhRW6U','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459440635,1790459443413,'{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_function_bcbg9z9p3wen_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh","newString":"echo \"[6/6] enabling\"\nrun sudo systemctl daemon-reload\n\n# enable + restart, NOT `enable --now`.\n#\n# `enable --now` only starts a unit if it is not already running. The previous\n# collector was started with the old ExecStart, so it would have kept running\n# with the old flags: no -D (so ethercodes.dat still unreadable) and no -P (so\n# /run/arpwatch-spoof.pid is never created). The scan unit then SIGHUPs a\n# nonexistent pidfile, silently does nothing, and goes back to scanning a file\n# arpwatch only refreshes every 15 minutes -- the exact bug this was meant to\n# fix, reintroduced by the installer.\nrun sudo systemctl enable arpwatch-spoof.service\nrun sudo systemctl restart arpwatch-spoof.service\n\n# The collector writes the pidfile the scan unit signals. If it is missing the\n# scan will quietly read stale data, so make that loud instead.\nif [ \"$DRY\" = 0 ]; then\n    sleep 1\n    if [ -s \"$PIDFILE\" ]; then\n        echo \"    collector pid $(cat \"$PIDFILE\") -- scan unit can force a flush\"\n    else\n        echo \"    WARNING: $PIDFILE missing; the scan timer cannot force arpwatch\" >&2\n        echo \"             to flush arp.dat and will read stale data.\" >&2\n    fi\nfi\n\nrun sudo systemctl enable --now arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer","oldString":"echo \"[6/6] enabling\"\nrun sudo systemctl daemon-reload\nrun sudo systemctl enable --now arpwatch-spoof.service\nrun sudo systemctl enable --now arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n===================================================================\n--- /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n+++ /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n@@ -167,9 +167,33 @@\n run \"$SCAN\" --selftest || true\n \n echo \"[6/6] enabling\"\n run sudo systemctl daemon-reload\n-run sudo systemctl enable --now arpwatch-spoof.service\n+\n+# enable + restart, NOT `enable --now`.\n+#\n+# `enable --now` only starts a unit if it is not already running. The previous\n+# collector was started with the old ExecStart, so it would have kept running\n+# with the old flags: no -D (so ethercodes.dat still unreadable) and no -P (so\n+# /run/arpwatch-spoof.pid is never created). The scan unit then SIGHUPs a\n+# nonexistent pidfile, silently does nothing, and goes back to scanning a file\n+# arpwatch only refreshes every 15 minutes -- the exact bug this was meant to\n+# fix, reintroduced by the installer.\n+run sudo systemctl enable arpwatch-spoof.service\n+run sudo systemctl restart arpwatch-spoof.service\n+\n+# The collector writes the pidfile the scan unit signals. If it is missing the\n+# scan will quietly read stale data, so make that loud instead.\n+if [ \"$DRY\" = 0 ]; then\n+    sleep 1\n+    if [ -s \"$PIDFILE\" ]; then\n+        echo \"    collector pid $(cat \"$PIDFILE\") -- scan unit can force a flush\"\n+    else\n+        echo \"    WARNING: $PIDFILE missing; the scan timer cannot force arpwatch\" >&2\n+        echo \"             to flush arp.dat and will read stale data.\" >&2\n+    fi\n+fi\n+\n run sudo systemctl enable --now arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer\n \n echo\n if [ \"$DRY\" = 1 ]; then\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh","patch":"Index: /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n===================================================================\n--- /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n+++ /home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh\n@@ -167,9 +167,33 @@\n run \"$SCAN\" --selftest || true\n \n echo \"[6/6] enabling\"\n run sudo systemctl daemon-reload\n-run sudo systemctl enable --now arpwatch-spoof.service\n+\n+# enable + restart, NOT `enable --now`.\n+#\n+# `enable --now` only starts a unit if it is not already running. The previous\n+# collector was started with the old ExecStart, so it would have kept running\n+# with the old flags: no -D (so ethercodes.dat still unreadable) and no -P (so\n+# /run/arpwatch-spoof.pid is never created). The scan unit then SIGHUPs a\n+# nonexistent pidfile, silently does nothing, and goes back to scanning a file\n+# arpwatch only refreshes every 15 minutes -- the exact bug this was meant to\n+# fix, reintroduced by the installer.\n+run sudo systemctl enable arpwatch-spoof.service\n+run sudo systemctl restart arpwatch-spoof.service\n+\n+# The collector writes the pidfile the scan unit signals. If it is missing the\n+# scan will quietly read stale data, so make that loud instead.\n+if [ \"$DRY\" = 0 ]; then\n+    sleep 1\n+    if [ -s \"$PIDFILE\" ]; then\n+        echo \"    collector pid $(cat \"$PIDFILE\") -- scan unit can force a flush\"\n+    else\n+        echo \"    WARNING: $PIDFILE missing; the scan timer cannot force arpwatch\" >&2\n+        echo \"             to flush arp.dat and will read stale data.\" >&2\n+    fi\n+fi\n+\n run sudo systemctl enable --now arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer\n \n echo\n if [ \"$DRY\" = 1 ]; then\n","additions":25,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/install-arpwatch-spoof.sh","time":{"start":1790459443403,"end":1790459443412}}}');
