INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb4c5fe001d2z9mOx8sHFaOe','msg_0dfb4c079001aZsSx9l5R7h53S','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459561470,1790459568738,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Work is safe locally. One unpushed commit, 4 files, clean working tree.\n\nNow I need to stop and ask the user, because:\n1. The push requires their GitHub access\n2. I genuinely can''t proceed on this without them\n\nBut wait — the user said \"Continue if you have next steps, or stop and ask for clarification if you are unsure how to proceed.\"\n\nThis is a case where I should stop and ask. The push blocker needs their input.\n\nBut let me also consider: is there anything else productive I can do that doesn''t need the push? \n\nThe live system is fine. The scripts are fixed and committed locally. The reboot is theirs.\n\nActually, one thing: I should also flag that the audit-readonly helper and other live state is unaffected by the push failure. The push is only about the backup/canonical repo. So the system is ready to reboot regardless.\n\nAlso worth noting: the fact that the SSH key stopped working mid-session is itself a security signal worth mentioning — someone/something removed it. Combined with the token shredding earlier, it''s worth the user checking their GitHub account security log.\n\nThat''s actually valuable context: if a token was revoked AND an SSH key removed, that pattern suggests either the user doing hygiene or a compromise. Let me flag it.\n\nLet me ask the user with the question tool about how to proceed on the push.\n\nActually, let me present the situation clearly and ask. Let me use the question tool since there''s a genuine decision.\n\nOptions for the push:\n1. User re-adds the SSH key to GitHub (they''d need to do it via browser — but no browser installed, and the token is shredded)\n2. User creates a new SSH key and adds it\n3. Skip pushing; the local repo is the record\n4. Investigate the GitHub account first (check security log)\n\nGiven no browser is installed, option 1/2 requires a browser. That''s a chicken-and-egg.\n\nHmm, this is actually a real problem: no browser + no working git credentials = can''t do anything on GitHub.\n\nLet me lay this out and ask.\n\nLet me write the summary and ask the question.\n\nI should be honest and concise. Let me present:\n1. The push blocker\n2. That the system is ready to reboot regardless\n3. The security question (why did the key stop working?)\n4. Ask how to proceed\n\nLet me use the question tool.\n\nActually, let me first double check one thing — is it possible the repo is fine but the user lost access to that specific repo? \"Permission denied (publickey)\" is key-level, not repo-level. So it''s the key.\n\nLet me ask the user.\n\nI''ll present the question about how to handle GitHub access.\n\nLet me keep it tight.","time":{"start":1790459561470,"end":1790459568737}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb4e26400156fCbYLaMuGGgE','msg_0dfb4c079001aZsSx9l5R7h53S','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459568740,1790459578067,'{"type":"text","text":"Work is safe — one unpushed commit, 4 files, clean tree. But I''ve hit a genuine blocker that needs your input, and it has a security angle worth flagging.\n\n## What happened\n\nMy push failed, and **my own verification lied about it**. I wrote `git push ... | sed && echo \"[✓] pushed\"` — `sed` exits 0, which masked git''s exit 128. I reported success on a failed push. That''s the exact failure mode I''ve been criticising all session, and I did it in the same breath as describing it.\n\n## The actual blocker\n\n```\nOffering public key: SHA256:dzuWC4iX1T0PAmPnWyYQpsw2qfZxRYLD/U9y2Nqca+4\nPermission denied (publickey).\n```\n\n- GitHub over HTTPS: **200 OK** — network and firewall are fine\n- SSH handshake: completes, then rejects the key\n- Only one key exists on disk, same fingerprint the agent held\n\nSo that key authenticated minutes ago and no longer does. Nothing I changed touches SSH — but combined with the token being revoked, **two GitHub credentials disappearing in one session is a pattern, not a coincidence.**\n\nPlease check your GitHub account activity log and SSH keys page. If you didn''t remove that key, treat it as a compromise and rotate everything.\n\n## Why I can''t just fix it\n\nYou''ve shredded the token, the SSH key is rejected, and you have **no browser installed**. That''s a closed loop — every recovery path needs one credential you no longer have and one tool you don''t have. I''d rather stop and ask than start generating keys at you.\n\n## Reboot is unaffected\n\nThis is only the backup repo. The live system is fully configured and all 27 checks pass, so **you can reboot whenever you like.** The unpushed commit is a backup-repo concern, not a system one.\n\nNothing is lost: `d72d480` is committed locally with the four script fixes.","time":{"start":1790459568740,"end":1790459578067}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb4f602001x95CV337qrYYCu','msg_0dfb4c079001aZsSx9l5R7h53S','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790459573763,1790459675653,'{"type":"tool","tool":"question","callID":"call_function_5ez7e09hnync_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"questions":[{"header":"GitHub key rejected","question":"How do you want to handle the rejected GitHub SSH key?","multiple":false,"options":[{"label":"I''ll check my account first","description":"You verify whether you removed the key, and whether the token revocation was you. Safest before regenerating anything."},{"label":"Generate a new SSH key","description":"I create a fresh ed25519 key with a passphrase and give you the one-line command to add it to GitHub. Needs a browser or gh CLI to register."},{"label":"Leave it local for now","description":"Skip the push. d72d480 stays in /tmp/opencode/hss, and the live system is unaffected. You can sync after you sort out access."},{"label":"Diagnose further first","description":"I investigate whether the block is account-wide, repo-specific, or a GitHub-side rate limit before you touch anything."}]},{"header":"After the reboot","question":"Anything you want done before you reboot?","multiple":true,"options":[{"label":"Nothing, go ahead","description":"You''ve got the post-reboot checklist from earlier. I''ll wait."},{"label":"Remove the audit NOPASSWD surface first","description":"Run install-audit-readonly.sh --remove now, so the temporary root access is gone before you reboot. You''d verify the reboot with plain sudo instead."},{"label":"Fix the pipestatus pattern in my workflow","description":"Make future push/verification steps use PIPESTATUS so a failure can never be reported as a success again."}]}]},"output":"User has answered your questions: \"How do you want to handle the rejected GitHub SSH key?\"=\"i destroyed it if we need another i can make one but we need to delete that token so open my firedragon browser to that page\", \"Anything you want done before you reboot?\"=\"Remove the audit NOPASSWD surface first, Fix the pipestatus pattern in my workflow\". You can now continue with the user''s answers in mind.","metadata":{"answers":[["i destroyed it if we need another i can make one but we need to delete that token so open my firedragon browser to that page"],["Remove the audit NOPASSWD surface first","Fix the pipestatus pattern in my workflow"]],"truncated":false},"title":"Asked 2 questions","time":{"start":1790459578064,"end":1790459675652}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb51ecc001uzJ1KvHxtMV9yT','msg_0dfb51ecb001c7EKU3rUrg1ejA','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459584205,1790459584207,'{"type":"text","text":"sudo systemctl start arpwatch-spoof-scan.service && sudo systemctl status arpwatch-spoof-scan.service --no-pager\nsudo cat /var/lib/arpwatch/arp.dat\n○ arpwatch-spoof-scan.service - Scan arpwatch datafile for ARP spoofing\nLoaded: loaded (/etc/systemd/system/arpwatch-spoof-scan.service; static)\nActive: inactive (dead) since Sat 2026-09-26 14:52:45 MST; 19msago\nInvocation: f366bb46281a4c47bbdb6cfa37e9f83d\nTriggeredBy: ● arpwatch-spoof-scan.timer\nDocs: file:///root/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh\nProcess: 334082 ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c kill -HUP \"$(cat /run/arpwatch-spoof.pid 2>/dev/null)\" 2>/dev/null; sleep 1 (code=exited, status=0/SUCCESS)\nProcess: 334086 ExecStart=/home/livings/Projects/zen-hardening/arpwatch-spoof-scan.sh (code=exited, status=0/SUCCESS)\nMain PID: 334086 (code=exited, status=0/SUCCESS)\nMem peak: 3.5M\nCPU: 49ms\n\nSep 26 14:52:44 zero systemd[1]: Starting Scan arpwatch datafile…...\nSep 26 14:52:45 zero systemd[1]: arpwatch-spoof-scan.service: De…ly.\nSep 26 14:52:45 zero systemd[1]: Finished Scan arpwatch datafile…ng.\nHint: Some lines were ellipsized, use -l to show in full.\ne4:55:a8:e:ae:9c        10.128.128.128  1790459530      _gateway\nea:bd:c8:4d:53:a8       10.197.250.246  1790459530      zero  was the output did it work?"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb524cf001KR6FTD1yFVQ5G8','msg_0dfb51ee50012Crcv3YG0vwoFa','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459585743,1790459585744,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfb524d1001FswHfuNPFJKvK1','msg_0dfb51ee50012Crcv3YG0vwoFa','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790459585746,1790459591689,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Excellent! It worked:\n- `ExecStartPre` succeeded (status=0) — the HUP worked\n- `ExecStart` succeeded (status=0)\n- `arp.dat` now has 2 real entries:\n  - `e4:55:a8:e:ae:9c  10.128.128.128  1790459530  _gateway`\n  - `ea:bd:c8:4d:53:a8  10.197.250.246  1790459530  zero`\n\nBoth with hostname resolution working (via -D /var/lib/arpwatch, PTR lookups) — `_gateway` and `zero` (the machine''s own name).\n\n`Active: inactive (dead)` is CORRECT for a Type=oneshot service that completed successfully. Not an error.\n\nSo: yes, it worked.\n\nLet me verify the timestamp: 1790459530. Let me check what date that is. It should be around now (2026-09-26). Let me verify — 1790459530 seconds since epoch. Let me compute: that''s roughly 2026-09-26. Good.\n\nLet me verify the entries make sense:\n- Gateway 10.128.128.128 with MAC e4:55:a8:e:ae:9c — real gateway\n- Own IP 10.197.250.246 with MAC ea:bd:c8:4d:53:a8 — the laptop''s own address\n\nOnly 2 entries because the network is quiet/switched — only broadcast ARP is visible. That''s expected on WLAN.\n\nLet me confirm the timestamp decodes to now, and confirm the scan logged properly. Let me check the log.\n\nAlso: hostname resolution working (`_gateway`, `zero`) is a good sign that -D/ethercodes/PTR is functioning. Actually the hostname comes from PTR records, not ethercodes.dat. ethercodes.dat is for vendor lookup in reports. But PTR resolution working is good.\n\nLet me verify the timestamp and check the scan log, and confirm no anomalies.\n\nLet me also double check: is the gateway MAC vendor resolvable via ethercodes.dat? That would confirm -D works. I could check with the ethercodes.dat file. Let me test: look up e4:55:a8 in /var/lib/arpwatch/ethercodes.dat. But /var/lib/arpwatch is root-only... I can''t read ethercodes.dat (dir is 0755 now after install, so I CAN read it! The installer set 0755). Let me check.\n\nLet me verify:\n1. timestamp → date\n2. scan log tail\n3. ethercodes lookup for the gateway MAC (proves -D works)\n\nThat would be a nice confirmation that the -D fix works.\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790459585745,"end":1790459591687}}');
