INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbbe015001wfdWCynIwpj8J7','msg_0dfbbd95b001UZ4xewf3K706Qb','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460026902,1790460026902,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbbe019001wzExHIl72sBkUC','msg_0dfbbd95b001UZ4xewf3K706Qb','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460026905,1790460043573,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Very important findings:\n\n1. **GRUB EFI entry is Boot0002 → `\\EFI\\Garuda\\grubx64.efi` on partition c6edeec0-3238-4f23-8e93-eda7dc35fe1a**. But `lsblk` showed the ESP PARTUUID as `c12a7328-f81f-11d2-ba4b-00a0c93ec93b` (that''s the standard EFI System Partition GUID, not a disk GUID). The efibootmgr entry references `HD(1,GPT,c6edeec0-3238-4f23-8e93-eda7dc35fe1a,...)` which is a **disk GUID** style reference with partition 1.\n\nWait — the ESP''s PARTUUID per lsblk is c12a7328... (ESP type GUID shown as PARTTYPE). lsblk''s PARTLABEL was empty and PARTTYPE = c12a7328-f81f-11d2-ba4b-00a0c93ec93b which is the EFI System Partition type GUID.\n\nThe efibootmgr HD() entry uses c6edeec0-3238-4f23-8e93-eda7dc35fe1a which would be the **disk** PARTUUID (first partition). So the NVRAM points to disk GUID c6edeec0... partition 1, file \\EFI\\Garuda\\grubx64.efi.\n\nSo I need to verify /dev/nvme0n1p1''s PARTUUID (the partition''s UUID) — and the disk''s ID. Let me check `blkid` / `lsblk -o PARTUUID`.\n\nIf the NVRAM references disk GUID c6edeec0 but the actual disk PARTUUID differs, the entry is stale → firmware falls through to Boot0005 (UEFI OS → \\EFI\\BOOT\\BOOTX64.EFI) as fallback.\n\nBootOrder: 0002,0004,0003,0005,0000,0001,0006,0007,0008. So Garuda (0002) is first. If its file is missing, it''d fall to PXE (0004), PXE (0003), then UEFI OS (0005 = \\EFI\\BOOT\\BOOTX64.EFI), then kali, BlackArch, CD/DVD, then network. That''s a slow boot path but could still work if BOOTX64.EFI exists.\n\nThis is a REAL risk: 14.961s firmware time is quite long, which is consistent with the firmware trying entries and failing/timing out.\n\n2. **Two stale boot entries: `kali` and `BlackArch_Linux`** — pointing to `VenHw(99e275e7-...)` (a QEMU/virtio vendor hardware path) which is a leftover from when the disk was used as a VM guest. Harmless but worth noting.\n\n3. **Snapshots: 11 snapshots**, IDs 5, 28-37, under `.snapshots/` which is a subvolume of the ROOT subvol (top level 256 = @). So these are snapshots of the ROOT subvolume only. `/home` (@home, ID 257) has NO snapshots.\n\nImportant: `.snapshots` is top level 256 → inside `@`. So snapshots are of `@` (root). They do NOT include /home.\n\n4. gen 79 for snapshot 5 (very old), gen 398-629 for 28-37.\n\nNow the key question for \"boot errors in snapshots\": these are Timeshift-style snapshots. `grub-btrfs.cfg` is present, so GRUB can boot them.\n\nCritical checks for each snapshot:\n- Does it have a valid fstab with resolvable UUIDs?\n- Kernel: /boot is NOT a subvolume (it''s on the ESP? no, /boot is on the btrfs root? Let me check). Actually /boot is on the btrfs @ subvolume (not separate), since lsblk showed only /boot/efi and the btrfs. So /boot IS part of the root subvolume → each snapshot has its OWN /boot!\n\nThat''s crucial. If /boot is in the root subvolume, then each snapshot has its own kernel+initramfs. An old snapshot may have an old kernel whose modules are gone (since /usr/lib/modules is in the snapshot too, so it should be self-consistent). Actually if the whole root subvol is snapshotted, then /boot and /usr/lib/modules are consistent within the snapshot. Good.\n\nBut: a snapshot taken before a kernel update has an old kernel. If that kernel still exists in the snapshot, it boots. GRUB menuentries via grub-btrfs point to the snapshot''s /boot. So they should work.\n\nRisks per snapshot:\n- fstab referencing UUIDs that changed\n- Missing /etc/fstab\n- Kernel modules mismatch\n- Broken initramfs\n\nLet me check each snapshot''s fstab and /boot contents.\n\nWhere are the snapshots on the filesystem? `.snapshots/5/snapshot` relative to the top-level (id 5). The top-level 5 is the btrfs filesystem root. Mounted subvols: @ → /, @home → /home, etc. So `.snapshots` is at... top level 256 is @, and .snapshots has top level 256, so `.snapshots` is a subvolume of `@`. So .snapshots lives at `/.snapshots` on the root filesystem!\n\nSo snapshot 5 is at /.snapshots/5/snapshot. Let me verify and read from there.\n\nLet me check readability and content.\n\nLet me now do the per-snapshot analysis.\n\nLet me check:\n1. Each snapshot''s /etc/fstab — do UUIDs resolve?\n2. Each snapshot''s /boot — kernel + initramfs present?\n3. Each snapshot''s /etc/mkinitcpio.d or dracut config\n4. Whether /etc/sudoers in old snapshots has issues (the sudoers incident)\n5. Snapshot dates (from their content or metadata)\n\nLet me go.\n\nAlso important: the `@home` has no snapshots — so a rollback of root won''t roll back /home. That''s an asymmetry worth flagging: if a package update breaks something, rolling back root but not home could leave inconsistent state. Actually it means /home keeps its state which is usually desirable, but worth noting.\n\nLet me examine.\n\nLet me first find the snapshot mount path and check readability.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nAlso I should double check: is /boot a separate subvolume? lsblk showed btrfs mounted at /var/log (the last one). Let me check `findmnt /boot`. Earlier fstab showed no separate /boot entry, so /boot is part of @. Confirmed by absence in fstab.\n\nSo each snapshot includes its own /boot. Let me verify that each snapshot has a kernel.\n\nLet me run the analysis.\n\nLet me be careful about reading snapshot /etc/fstab — need read permission. Snapshots are root-owned 755 typically, and /etc/fstab is 644, so readable.\n\nLet me go.\n\nLet me write a comprehensive per-snapshot check.\n\nLet me also get snapshot dates. Timeshift stores info in /.snapshots/N/info? Or btrfs subvolume show has creation time. I can use `btrfs subvolume show` but needs root. Alternative: read the snapshot''s /etc/... mtime, or check /.snapshots/N/ directory mtime.\n\nLet me use directory mtimes via stat (readable).\n\nLet me do it.\n\nLet me write the command.\n\nFor UUID validation I need blkid (works as user? blkid usually needs root for cached probing, but `blkid -U` may work as user... earlier `blkid -U` worked in my fstab check as user! Yes, it printed [ok] for the ESP UUID. So blkid -U works as user.)\n\nGood, so I can validate fstab UUIDs in each snapshot without root.\n\nLet me go.\n\nLet me run the per-snapshot check.\n\nI''ll do it in one comprehensive command.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nNote: I should also check the ESP partition UUID to assess the NVRAM reference validity.\n\nLet me include that.\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790460026905,"end":1790460043572}}');
