INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbc5601001dGhanxUYV2qaeY','msg_0dfbc4eb0001XPKsxY7WvV7EjW','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460057089,1790460057090,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbc5603001LK6ZBkPFoSa5E6','msg_0dfbc4eb0001XPKsxY7WvV7EjW','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460057091,1790460063832,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"All 11 snapshots show KERNEL/MODS MISMATCH. Let me investigate — is this real, or is my extraction wrong?\n\nThe kernel filename is `vmlinuz-linux-zen` (no version suffix, since Arch uses that convention). So `sed ''s/.*vmlinuz-//''` gives `linux-zen`, not a version. And modules dir gives `7.2.7-zen1-1-zen`. So they ALWAYS mismatch because of naming convention, not because of a real problem.\n\nThat''s my bug again — Arch/Arch-based (Garuda/zen) uses unversioned `/boot/vmlinuz-linux-zen`. So I can''t compare filenames. I need to compare properly: check if the modules dir version matches the running/expected kernel, or better, read the kernel version from the image.\n\nLet me verify: is this a real mismatch or naming artifact? Let me check what vmlinuz files exist in a snapshot and whether there''s a version marker.\n\nAlso all dates are 2026-09-26 (today) — because snapshot dir mtime is the snapshot time, all created today. Hmm, but gen 79 for snapshot 5 suggests it''s old. Actually the mtime of the snapshot directory reflects when the snapshot was taken. All today. But wait — earlier btrfs showed gen numbers varying (79, 398-629), which reflects transaction history, not necessarily creation time.\n\nLet me get real dates. Timeshift stores them. Let me check /.snapshots/N/ for info files, and use `btrfs subvolume show` (needs root) for creation time. Alternatively check the snapshot''s /etc/... or use stat on /.snapshots/N (parent dir mtime = creation).\n\nActually the mtime I used was on the `snapshot` subdirectory itself. For a read-only snapshot, mtime = when the snapshot was created. All showing 2026-09-26 means all created today. That''s plausible if the user made them today, OR if snapper/timeshift restored mtimes.\n\nLet me get precise creation times with btrfs (root via audit-btrfs wrapper? The wrapper runs `btrfs subvolume list` etc. — I can''t pass args since wrappers ignore args).\n\nLet me instead check the parent directory /.snapshots/N mtime, and look for info files.\n\nLet me fix the kernel/modules comparison properly and get real dates.\n\nFor kernel version: I can extract from the snapshot''s /boot/vmlinuz-* filename (unversioned) — so instead check whether /usr/lib/modules has exactly one dir and whether the initramfs was built for it. Better approach: check if the snapshot''s /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img is newer than its vmlinuz, and whether modules dir count matches expectations.\n\nActually the most meaningful check: does the snapshot''s /boot contain a kernel that matches its /usr/lib/modules? Since Arch names the image unversioned, the mapping is implicit — the installed kernel package version determines both. If they were captured atomically (same snapshot), they''re consistent by construction.\n\nSo the real risk isn''t mismatch — it''s:\n1. Missing files\n2. fstab UUID rot\n3. A kernel in /boot whose modules were removed (only matters for the LIVE system, not snapshots, since snapshots are atomic)\n\nLet me verify the naming artifact and check properly.\n\nLet me look at actual filenames.\n\nLet me also get real snapshot dates.\n\nLet me run.","time":{"start":1790460057091,"end":1790460063830}}');
