INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbcd19d001xTMAaO4sGaCvQC','msg_0dfbcbafe001Y4SPqXeVK8mtjZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460088733,1790460088735,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbcd1a0001yOpHg2WGG4hvCo','msg_0dfbcbafe001Y4SPqXeVK8mtjZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460088736,1790460097129,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"My P1 check is garbage — the `/0` and `/a]` are parsing artifacts from systemctl show output (e.g., `{ path=... ; argv[]=... }`), my grep -oE ''/[^ ;{}]+'' picked up fragments. So those 33 \"missing\" are false positives. Except possibly `garuda-pacman-lock.service /var/lib/pacman/db.lck` which is legitimately a file that doesn''t exist (it''s a lock file, created at runtime — that''s correct behavior, not a bug).\n\nI need to redo P1 properly. Let me extract ExecStart paths correctly.\n\nBetter approach: use `systemctl show -p ExecStart` and parse `path=` field, or use `systemctl cat` and parse ExecStart lines. Or simplest: check the binary via `systemctl show --value -p ExecStart` with proper parsing of `{ path=/usr/bin/foo ; argv[]=... }`.\n\nLet me redo it properly.\n\nP2: fstab is complete — all btrfs subvol mounts present, ESP present, tmpfs /tmp. No crypttab (LUKS handled via kernel cmdline, which is normal for a single LUKS root). Good.\n\nP3: snapper-cleanup.timer active, last run 2min ago succeeded, no errors. snapper-timeline.timer inactive — meaning no automatic timeline snapshots. Notable: snapshots are being created by something (Garuda?) but the timeline timer is off, so snapper isn''t auto-snapshotting. Not a boot risk.\n\nWait — but snapshots ARE being created (11 today, last at 14:47). And timeshift is not installed. So what creates them? Maybe Garuda-assistant, or a manual `snapper create`. Since snapper-timeline.timer is inactive, they''re manual. Fine.\n\nImportant: snapper-cleanup.timer is active. If snapper''s config has limits, cleanup could DELETE snapshots. Also, a known issue: snapper-cleanup can fail if the btrfs volume is read-only or if /.snapshots isn''t a proper snapper subvolume config. It ran fine 2 min ago.\n\nNow the critical remaining item: GRUB integration. Do the snapshots actually appear in the GRUB menu? grub-btrfs.cfg is present. But is it wired into grub.cfg? And does it correctly find /.snapshots?\n\nLet me check that, plus redo P1 properly.\n\nAlso I should check: is /.snapshots listed in snapper config with the right SUBVOLUME and FSTYPE? A misconfigured snapper could cause cleanup to fail on next boot.\n\nAnd check for a real boot risk: `grub-btrfs` needs the btrfs module and correct GRUB_DEVICE. Let me check grub.cfg includes grub-btrfs.cfg.\n\nLet me also verify the initramfs actually contains what''s needed (btrfs, luks, nvme) — a missing module in initramfs = boot failure after kernel update. Let me check with lsinitcpio (needs root for the file, but /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img is 0644 readable).\n\nThat''s a genuinely valuable predictive check. Let me do it.\n\nLet me run:\n1. Redo P1 correctly\n2. GRUB snapshot integration\n3. initramfs contents (lsinitcpio) — critical for boot\n4. snapper config validity\n\nLet me go.\n\nlsinitcpio /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img | grep -E ''btrfs|luks|crypt|nvme'' — check the storage modules are present.\n\nLet me run.\n\nThis is the most important remaining check: if the initramfs lacks btrfs/crypt modules, the next reboot fails. Let me verify.\n\nLet me do it.","time":{"start":1790460088736,"end":1790460097128}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbcf26a001wXQvZkpZodYoxx','msg_0dfbcbafe001Y4SPqXeVK8mtjZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460097130,1790460102464,'{"type":"text","text":"My P1 check was garbage — `/0` and `/a]` are artifacts of me regex-parsing `systemctl show` output, not real paths. Redoing it properly, plus the checks that actually predict boot failure:","time":{"start":1790460097130,"end":1790460102463}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbcf533001DyHFjXLEnTKkgF','msg_0dfbcbafe001Y4SPqXeVK8mtjZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460097844,1790460099795,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_26aujj18a8d5_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ P1-redo: enabled units, real ExecStart paths only ═══\"\nbad=0\nfor u in $(systemctl list-unit-files --state=enabled --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk ''{print $1}''); do\n  paths=$(systemctl show \"$u\" -p ExecStart --value 2>/dev/null | grep -oE ''path=[^ ;}]+'' | cut -d= -f2- | sort -u)\n  for p in $paths; do\n    [ -z \"$p\" ] && continue\n    case \"$p\" in -*) continue ;; esac\n    [ -e \"$p\" ] || { printf \"      [MISSING] %-40s %s\\n\" \"$u\" \"$p\"; bad=$((bad+1)); }\n  done\ndone\necho \"      missing ExecStart targets: $bad\"\necho\necho \"  (db.lck under garuda-pacman-lock is correct: a lock file, absent until acquired)\""},"output":"═══ P1-redo: enabled units, real ExecStart paths only ═══\n      [MISSING] garuda-pacman-lock.service               rm\n      missing ExecStart targets: 1\n\n  (db.lck under garuda-pacman-lock is correct: a lock file, absent until acquired)\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ P1-redo: enabled units, real ExecStart paths only ═══\n      [MISSING] garuda-pacman-lock.service               rm\n      missing ExecStart targets: 1\n\n  (db.lck under garuda-pacman-lock is correct: a lock file, absent until acquired)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ P1-redo: enabled units, real ExecStart paths only ═══\"\nbad=0\nfor u in $(systemctl list-unit-files --state=enabled --no-legend 2>/dev/null | awk ''{print $1}''); do\n  paths=$(systemctl show \"$u\" -p ExecStart --value 2>/dev/null | grep -oE ''path=[^ ;}]+'' | cut -d= -f2- | sort -u)\n  for p in $paths; do\n    [ -z \"$p\" ] && continue\n    case \"$p\" in -*) continue ;; esac\n    [ -e \"$p\" ] || { printf \"      [MISSING] %-40s %s\\n\" \"$u\" \"$p\"; bad=$((bad+1)); }\n  done\ndone\necho \"      missing ExecStart targets: $bad\"\necho\necho \"  (db.lck under garuda-pacman-lock is correct: a lock file, absent until acquired)\"","time":{"start":1790460099281,"end":1790460099795}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbcfad6001rAkQhzIfUfLXxq','msg_0dfbcbafe001Y4SPqXeVK8mtjZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460099286,1790460102480,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_26aujj18a8d5_2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ P4: initramfs has the modules a cold boot needs? (top boot-failure cause) ═══\"\nimg=/boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img\necho \"  image: $img  ($(stat -c %s \"$img\" 2>/dev/null | numfmt --to=iec 2>/dev/null))\"\necho \"  storage/crypto modules present:\"\nlsinitcpio \"$img\" 2>/dev/null | grep -oE ''(btrfs|crypt_blk|dm_crypt|dm_mod|luks2|nvme|ext4|xfs|volume_key|serio_i8042|hid_|xhci_pci|ahci|ata_piix)[^/]*\\.ko[^ ]*'' \\\n  | sed ''s/\\.ko.*/.ko/'' | sort -u | tr ''\\n'' '' '' | fold -sw 100 -s | sed ''s/^/      /''\necho\nfor m in btrfs dm_crypt luks2 nvme; do\n  if lsinitcpio \"$img\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"$m\"; then echo \"      [ok]   $m\"; else echo \"      [MISS] $m  <-- cold boot would FAIL\"; fi\ndone\necho\necho \"  initramfs newer than kernel? $([ \"$img\" -nt /boot/vmlinuz-linux-zen ] && echo yes || echo ''NO -- stale initramfs'')\"\necho \"  hook that emits the crypt root cmdline:\"\nlsinitcpio -p \"$img\" 2>/dev/null | grep -E ''encrypt|luks|crypt'' | sed ''s/^/      /'' | head"},"output":"═══ P4: initramfs has the modules a cold boot needs? (top boot-failure cause) ═══\n  image: /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img  (127M)\n  storage/crypto modules present:\n\n      [MISS] btrfs  <-- cold boot would FAIL\n      [MISS] dm_crypt  <-- cold boot would FAIL\n      [MISS] luks2  <-- cold boot would FAIL\n      [MISS] nvme  <-- cold boot would FAIL\n\n  initramfs newer than kernel? yes\n  hook that emits the crypt root cmdline:\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ P4: initramfs has the modules a cold boot needs? (top boot-failure cause) ═══\n  image: /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img  (127M)\n  storage/crypto modules present:\n\n      [MISS] btrfs  <-- cold boot would FAIL\n      [MISS] dm_crypt  <-- cold boot would FAIL\n      [MISS] luks2  <-- cold boot would FAIL\n      [MISS] nvme  <-- cold boot would FAIL\n\n  initramfs newer than kernel? yes\n  hook that emits the crypt root cmdline:\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ P4: initramfs has the modules a cold boot needs? (top boot-failure cause) ═══\"\nimg=/boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img\necho \"  image: $img  ($(stat -c %s \"$img\" 2>/dev/null | numfmt --to=iec 2>/dev/null))\"\necho \"  storage/crypto modules present:\"\nlsinitcpio \"$img\" 2>/dev/null | grep -oE ''(btrfs|crypt_blk|dm_crypt|dm_mod|luks2|nvme|ext4|xfs|volume_key|serio_i8042|hid_|xhci_pci|ahci|ata_piix)[^/]*\\.ko[^ ]*'' \\\n  | sed ''s/\\.ko.*/.ko/'' | sort -u | tr ''\\n'' '' '' | fold -sw 100 -s | sed ''s/^/      /''\necho\nfor m in btrfs dm_crypt luks2 nvme; do\n  if lsinitcpio \"$img\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q \"$m\"; then echo \"      [ok]   $m\"; else echo \"      [MISS] $m  <-- cold boot would FAIL\"; fi\ndone\necho\necho \"  initramfs newer than kernel? $([ \"$img\" -nt /boot/vmlinuz-linux-zen ] && echo yes || echo ''NO -- stale initramfs'')\"\necho \"  hook that emits the crypt root cmdline:\"\nlsinitcpio -p \"$img\" 2>/dev/null | grep -E ''encrypt|luks|crypt'' | sed ''s/^/      /'' | head","time":{"start":1790460102459,"end":1790460102479}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbd0751001SxbkDQJuvpJSpq','msg_0dfbcbafe001Y4SPqXeVK8mtjZ','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460102481,1790460102482,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":89252,"input":1163,"output":676,"reasoning":782,"cache":{"write":0,"read":86631}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbd0cdc001ViebK5jAyz9cYN','msg_0dfbd07a0001Yn76lGwiyJl4mV','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460103900,1790460103901,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbd0cdf001eNP3ydpYdqqvym','msg_0dfbd07a0001Yn76lGwiyJl4mV','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460103903,1790460108341,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"P4 says all modules MISSING. But the system boots fine! So this is almost certainly a false result — `lsinitcpio` may not be able to read the image as non-root, or the image is compressed in a way lsinitcpio can''t parse, or dracut format (this is dracut 111, not mkinitcpio!).\n\nEarlier summary said \"dracut 111\". So it''s dracut, not mkinitcpio. `lsinitcpio` is for mkinitcpio images. For dracut, the tool is `lsinitrd` or `lsinitrd`/`dracut-c decompress`.\n\nThe image is 127M which is large — consistent with dracut (mkinitcpio images are usually smaller, though 127M is plausible for a big one).\n\nSo my P4 used the wrong tool. Let me verify: is this dracut or mkinitcpio? Check for /etc/mkinitcpio.d and /etc/dracut.conf.d.\n\nLet me redo with the right tool.\n\nAlso, if dracut: the modules are in the initramfs; check with `lsinitrd`.\n\nLet me check which initramfs generator is in use, then use the right inspection tool.\n\nAlso note: garuda-pacman-lock.service shows \"rm\" as ExecStart — that''s `ExecStart=rm ...`? Let me check. \"rm\" as a bare command — path resolution would be /usr/bin/rm. My parser took `path=rm`? Actually it printed `rm` as missing. Let me look at the actual unit. It probably has `ExecStart=/usr/bin/rm -f /var/lib/pacman/db.lck` and my regex `path=` picked something odd. Let me check that unit directly.\n\nLet me verify both.\n\nLet me run.\n\nI need to be careful — I keep producing false negatives. Let me be rigorous: determine the initramfs generator first.","time":{"start":1790460103903,"end":1790460108340}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbd1e36001jyoNa8vETf529l','msg_0dfbd07a0001Yn76lGwiyJl4mV','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460108343,1790460109909,'{"type":"text","text":"P4 is a false alarm — I used `lsinitcpio` (mkinitcpio) on what''s likely a **dracut** image, and the system boots fine. Confirming the generator and using the right tool:","time":{"start":1790460108342,"end":1790460109909}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbd1f35001DsgpgyZN9zJfjJ','msg_0dfbd07a0001Yn76lGwiyJl4mV','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460108597,1790460109950,'{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_function_z9tbeulrvud2_1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"echo \"═══ which initramfs generator is actually in use? ═══\"\nls -d /etc/dracut.conf.d /etc/mkinitcpio.d /etc/mkinitcpio.conf /etc/dracut.conf 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/      /''\necho \"  initramfs generator packages:\"\npacman -Qq 2>/dev/null | grep -E ''^(dracut|mkinitcpio)'' | sed ''s/^/      /''\necho \"  mkinitcpio hooks dir: $(ls -d /etc/mkinitcpio.d 2>/dev/null || echo none)\"\necho\necho \"  image magic (what format?):\"\nfile -b /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/      /''\necho\necho \"  garuda-pacman-lock.service actual content:\"\nsystemctl cat garuda-pacman-lock.service 2>/dev/null | grep -E ''ExecStart|Description'' | sed ''s/^/      /''"},"output":"═══ which initramfs generator is actually in use? ═══\n      /etc/dracut.conf\n      /etc/dracut.conf.d\n  initramfs generator packages:\n      dracut\n  mkinitcpio hooks dir: none\n\n  image magic (what format?):\n      regular file, no read permission\n\n  garuda-pacman-lock.service actual content:\n      Description=Pacman lock cleaner\n      ExecStart=rm -f /var/lib/pacman/db.lck\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ which initramfs generator is actually in use? ═══\n      /etc/dracut.conf\n      /etc/dracut.conf.d\n  initramfs generator packages:\n      dracut\n  mkinitcpio hooks dir: none\n\n  image magic (what format?):\n      regular file, no read permission\n\n  garuda-pacman-lock.service actual content:\n      Description=Pacman lock cleaner\n      ExecStart=rm -f /var/lib/pacman/db.lck\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ which initramfs generator is actually in use? ═══\"\nls -d /etc/dracut.conf.d /etc/mkinitcpio.d /etc/mkinitcpio.conf /etc/dracut.conf 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/      /''\necho \"  initramfs generator packages:\"\npacman -Qq 2>/dev/null | grep -E ''^(dracut|mkinitcpio)'' | sed ''s/^/      /''\necho \"  mkinitcpio hooks dir: $(ls -d /etc/mkinitcpio.d 2>/dev/null || echo none)\"\necho\necho \"  image magic (what format?):\"\nfile -b /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/      /''\necho\necho \"  garuda-pacman-lock.service actual content:\"\nsystemctl cat garuda-pacman-lock.service 2>/dev/null | grep -E ''ExecStart|Description'' | sed ''s/^/      /''","time":{"start":1790460109903,"end":1790460109949}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbd2481001VzMtzt3riqyV76','msg_0dfbd07a0001Yn76lGwiyJl4mV','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460109953,1790460109953,'{"reason":"tool-calls","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":90191,"input":220,"output":301,"reasoning":420,"cache":{"write":0,"read":89250}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbd2b36001jJGPjow8fv3F0N','msg_0dfbd24d1001G6XeeCXvuAR7GC','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460111670,1790460111670,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbd2b37001XjlQM14Gd03Sr4','msg_0dfbd24d1001G6XeeCXvuAR7GC','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460111671,1790460119302,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"Confirmed: dracut, not mkinitcpio. So `lsinitcpio` is the wrong tool — that''s why everything showed MISSING. False alarm, as I suspected.\n\nAnd `garuda-pacman-lock.service` has `ExecStart=rm -f /var/lib/pacman/db.lck` — a bare `rm`, relying on PATH. My parser reported \"rm\" as missing because it''s not an absolute path. Bare command names in ExecStart are technically an error in systemd (it requires absolute paths since v240), BUT systemd actually tolerates it in some cases by searching PATH... Actually, systemd requires the executable to be an absolute path in ExecStart; a bare name is rejected. Hmm, but the unit is enabled and presumably works.\n\nWait — systemd docs: \"the first item must be either an absolute path to an executable or a simple file name without any slashes... If it''s a simple file name, it''s searched in a fixed set of directories.\" Actually systemd does support simple names searched in $PATH-like default dirs (/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin). Let me not overstate. systemd will resolve `rm` via a default search path. It works.\n\nActually, systemd''s documented behavior: \"the command line accepts either an absolute path to the executable or a simple file name without any slashes. ... it''s recommended to use absolute paths.\" So bare names work.\n\nSo that''s not a bug either. My P1''s single \"missing\" was `rm` because I only tested `-e \"rm\"` relative to cwd.\n\nSo P1 is actually clean: 0 real missing targets.\n\nNow the file permission issue: `file -b /boot/initramfs-linux-zen.img` → \"regular file, no read permission\". So I can''t read the initramfs as livings. Need root.\n\nSo to verify initramfs contents I need root. Let me use the dracut tool if available and ask user, OR note it.\n\nActually — I have the audit wrappers. None inspect initramfs. So I need the user to run it, OR I can note that the initramfs was verified fresh earlier (newer than kernel) and dracut config is what matters.\n\nLet me check /etc/dracut.conf contents (readable?) for correctness — that''s the config that determines whether the initramfs can boot.\n\nLet me check dracut.conf and dracut.conf.d.\n\nKey: `hostonly`, `add_drivers`, and whether btrfs/crypt are included. If hostonly=\"yes\" (default), the initramfs only includes what''s on the current machine — which is fine.\n\nLet me read the dracut config. And check the kernel cmdline from GRUB to confirm the root= parameter matches (that''s what the initramfs uses to find root).\n\nActually a REALLY important predictive check I CAN do without root: read GRUB''s grub.cfg to see the kernel command line, and confirm `root=UUID=...` or `rd.luks.uuid=...` matches the actual disk. If GRUB''s cmdline references a stale UUID, boot fails.\n\nLet me check /boot/grub/grub.cfg — is it readable? It''s in @ subvolume root, 0644 probably. Let me try.\n\nThat''s the highest-value remaining check. Let me do it.\n\nLet me read grub.cfg kernel lines.\n\nLet me also read dracut.conf.\n\nLet me run.\n\nAlso check mkinitcpio/dracut cmdline: root=UUID, and whether `cryptdevice` or `rd.luks.uuid` is present for LUKS.\n\nLet me go.\n\nNote: /boot/grub/grub.cfg is root-owned but likely world-readable (0644). Let me try.\n\nLet me run these checks.\n\nAlso I should check the ESP grubx64.efi existence — needs root. Flag it.\n\nLet me do the GRUB cmdline check now.","time":{"start":1790460111671,"end":1790460119301}}');
