INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbe2337001ZATvuMElKXZDr9','msg_0dfbe1e15001P75NMuQchzLqEb','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460175160,1790460175161,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbe233a001BsiqqYiNVAjXTz','msg_0dfbe1e15001P75NMuQchzLqEb','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460175162,1790460178436,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The script is read-only (only comment lines matched the mutating-verb grep). \n\nNow let me present the report. Let me be honest and organized.\n\nSummary of what I verified vs what needs root.\n\nLet me write the final report concisely but completely.\n\nKey points:\n- 11 snapshots, all bootable in principle\n- Current boot clean\n- 1 critical unknown: ESP EFI file existence\n- 2 stale NVRAM entries\n- 3 false alarms I created and corrected\n\nLet me present.\n\nLet me be honest about my repeated false-negative errors in this session: the sed kernel/mods, the lsinitcpio on dracut, the PIPESTATUS, the PARTUUID, the crypttab grep -c, the P1 regex. That''s a lot. I should acknowledge the pattern honestly rather than hide it — the user needs to know my check tooling is unreliable and why I''m giving them a script to run themselves.\n\nLet me count: in this turn alone I made 5 false-negative/false-positive errors:\n1. PARTUUID whole-disk check → false MISMATCH\n2. kernel/mods sed → false MISMATCH on all 11 snapshots\n3. P1 regex → 33 false MISSING\n4. lsinitcpio on dracut → 4 false MISS\n5. grep -vc crypttab → false \"no crypttab\"\n6. snapshot sudoers.d → false \"MISSING\" (inconclusive, correctly labeled)\n\nThat''s a genuinely high error rate and I should own it clearly. The user asked me to check for boot errors; I generated a lot of fake ones.\n\nLet me report honestly.\n\nLet me structure:\n1. Verdict\n2. What''s verified clean\n3. The one real risk (ESP, unverified)\n4. Real non-boot findings\n5. What I got wrong in this check\n6. Next action\n\nLet me write it.\n\nKeep it tight but complete.","time":{"start":1790460175162,"end":1790460178435}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbe3006001k3WJwBgSrS9NYv','msg_0dfbe1e15001P75NMuQchzLqEb','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460178438,1790460186252,'{"type":"text","text":"## Verdict: no boot errors found — but one thing I could not verify, and it''s the one that matters\n\n### Verified clean\n\n**Current boot:** 0 failed units, 0 queued jobs, no pending pacman lock, 0 updates. Boot 49.5s (firmware 15.0 / loader 21.2 / kernel 2.8 / initrd 7.6 / userspace 3.0). Coredumps are 3× `alert.py --demo` plus one Xorg at startup — all accounted for.\n\n**Boot chain:** every `fstab` UUID resolves, all 7 btrfs subvolumes mount, ESP at `F54B-53CA` with 299M free, one kernel (`7.2.7-zen1-1-zen`) matching the sole `/usr/lib/modules` dir — no orphans. Initramfs is newer than the kernel. **0 enabled units with a missing ExecStart target.**\n\n**LUKS:** auto-unlocks unattended in ~4s via `/crypto_keyfile.bin` (aes-xts-plain64, 512-bit). No passphrase prompt after a power cut.\n\n**The authoritative cmdline matches the hardware** — `root=UUID=51bd7935…` and `rd.luks.uuid=8c39203b…` both resolve to `nvme0n1p2`. NVRAM `Boot0002` targets PARTUUID `c6edeec0…`, which is exactly `nvme0n1p1`.\n\n**All 11 snapshots** (IDs 5, 28–37): `fstab` present with all UUIDs resolving, kernel + initramfs + modules present, microcode and `grub` present. `snapper-cleanup.timer` ran clean 2 minutes ago with zero errors. `grub-btrfs.cfg` carries 22 subvolume references.\n\n### The real remaining risk\n\n**I cannot confirm `\\EFI\\Garuda\\grubx64.efi` still exists on the ESP.** NVRAM points at it and it worked at last boot, but the ESP is mounted `dmask=0077` — root-only — so an empty `ls` told me nothing, and the same applies to the `\\EFI\\BOOT\\BOOTX64.EFI` fallback.\n\nThis matters more than anything else I checked. If that file is gone, the firmware walks its BootOrder: two PXE entries, then the fallback, then stale `kali` and `BlackArch` entries, then the CD-ROM. Worst case is a PXE boot or no boot at all, and a **21.2s loader time** is consistent with firmware already probing through entries.\n\nSo I wrote a read-only script to close that gap and the other four:\n\n```bash\nsudo bash /tmp/opencode/boot-check-root.sh\n```\n\nVerified read-only — the only mutating verbs in it are in comments. It checks ESP contents, whether `grub.cfg` actually sources `grub-btrfs` (snapshot bootability), `lsinitrd` module presence, whether any snapshot holds the broken sudoers state, and `crypttab`.\n\n### Genuine non-boot findings\n\n- **Two stale NVRAM entries:** `kali` and `BlackArch_Linux`, both on a `VenHw(99e275e7…)` QEMU path — leftovers from when this disk was a VM. Harmless, but they''d be tried if Garuda ever failed.\n- **`@home` has no snapshots.** All 11 cover only `@`. Rolling back root without home can leave user files referencing removed packages. Not boot-breaking, but it''s an asymmetry.\n- `snapper-timeline.timer` is inactive, so nothing snapshots automatically.\n\n### I have to flag my own work here\n\nI generated **six false findings** in this check and had to retract every one:\n\n| My claim | Reality |\n|---|---|\n| `PARTUUID` MISMATCH | Whole disks have no PARTUUID; the partition matches |\n| KERNEL/MODS MISMATCH ×11 | My `sed` on an unversioned Arch image name |\n| 33 missing ExecStart targets | Regex artifacts `/0`, `/a]` |\n| btrfs/dm_crypt/luks2/nvme MISSING | I ran `lsinitcpio` (mkinitcpio) on a **dracut** image |\n| \"no crypttab\" | `grep -c` returns exit 1 on zero, so `\\|\\|` fired |\n| 10-installer MISSING in all snapshots | `0750` root dir; I couldn''t traverse it |\n\nEvery one was a false negative or false positive in *my checking*, not a fault in your system. That''s a bad hit rate on read-only diagnostics, and it''s the same failure mode as the `| sed && echo ok` bug — the tool asserting something it hasn''t actually established. Given that, I''d trust the root script''s output over my analysis above, and treat any finding I report without direct evidence as unproven.","time":{"start":1790460178438,"end":1790460186251}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbe4e8e001v14KYgm3n44X1P','msg_0dfbe1e15001P75NMuQchzLqEb','ses_f211dfcd4ffevS1Yoddwab036g',1790460186254,1790460186255,'{"reason":"stop","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":100870,"input":108,"output":1056,"reasoning":402,"cache":{"write":0,"read":99304}},"cost":0}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfbee9ea001CuufaqrEvExlwt','msg_0dfbee9e9001KZBs2UckF7f33n','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790460226028,1790460226028,'{"type":"text","text":"sudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/deploy-fail2ban-ignoreip.sh 2>/dev/null; sudo ~/Projects/zen-hardening/deploy-faillock-admin.sh\n=== 1/6  backing up /etc/security/faillock.conf ===\n[ok] backup written (see ''ls /etc/security/faillock.conf.orig.*'')\n=== 2/6  group shield-admin ===\n[ok] created system group shield-admin\n=== 3/6  enrolling livings ===\n[ok] processed\n=== 4/6  verifying the enrolment took effect ===\n[ok] livings is a member of shield-admin\n=== 5/6  installing faillock.conf ===\n[ok] installed /etc/security/faillock.conf\n[ok] config names admin_group = shield-admin\n=== 6/6  closing enrollment ===\nenrollment locked: only livings may be a member of shield-admin\n[ok] enrollment locked to livings\n\n=== faillock admin exemption ===\nconfig admin_group: admin_group = shield-admin\ngroup shield-admin: shield-admin:x:952:livings\nenrollment: LOCKED (only livings may be a member)\n\n=== is the exemption actually in effect? ===\n[ok] livings is in shield-admin and the config names it -> NEVER locked out\n\n=== next step ===\n\nGroup membership is read at process start, so the exemption becomes\neffective for NEW logins. Log out and back in (or reboot) before testing.\n\nDo NOT test by typing a wrong password 11 times. To confirm the exemption\nsafely, check that your account has no faillock tally to begin with:\n\nsudo faillock --user livings\n\nIf you ever DO get locked out, recovery is one root command:\n\nsudo faillock --user livings --reset\n\nunlock_time is 30s, not 0, so a normal lockout also clears on its own.  did it work?"}');
