INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfc26ea6001mML7UkEieaTDDE','msg_0dfc25fff0017Bu0m9z4KC9z2Q','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790460456614,1790460456615,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfc26eb9001PB54MoXY8e9yk5','msg_0dfc25fff0017Bu0m9z4KC9z2Q','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790460456633,1790460471390,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"\nThe file content is correct! The GROUPS array is properly declared with the strings. So why does bash -c with the same content produce numbers?\n\nWait — in my `bash -c` test I only had 2 entries but got 6 numeric elements. And in the standalone test, the array had 6 elements: 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967.\n\nHmm, that''s really strange. Let me reconsider — maybe the issue is that `GROUPS` conflicts with something. \n\nOH WAIT. I think I finally get it. The numbers 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967 — these could be the result of the array being assigned in a context where the strings got word-split and arithmetic-expanded.\n\nActually, no. Let me reconsider the `bash -c` test. I wrote:\n```\nbash -c ''\nset -euo pipefail\nZONE=\"workstation\"\nGROUPS=(\n  \"VNC/RFB remote desktop   |x11vnc,tigervnc,realvnc            |5900-5910 5800-5810 5500\"\n  \"Windows RDP              |xrdp,freerdp                                             |3389\"\n)\necho \"declare -p:\"\ndeclare -p GROUPS\n''\n```\n\nThe `bash -c ''...''` — single quotes. Inside, the GROUPS array. But `declare -p GROUPS` showed 6 numeric elements.\n\nHmm, 6 elements from 2 strings? That doesn''t add up either.\n\nUnless... the `bash -c` string is being processed by the OUTER bash before reaching the inner bash. The outer bash sees `bash -c ''...''` with single quotes, so no expansion. But wait — the tool runs via bash, and my command had the array inside single quotes.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that these numbers ARE from a different source entirely. Let me check: is `GROUPS` perhaps a special array? \n\nActually — I bet the problem is much simpler and I''m overthinking. Let me check if there''s a `GROUPS` environment variable or if the shell has something weird.\n\nLet me just run a minimal test: create a file with the array and source it.\n\nActually, the KEY insight: in my `bash -c` test, I used single quotes around the whole script. Inside single quotes, `GROUPS=( ... )` with newlines should be fine. But the result was numeric.\n\nWait — could it be that `set -euo pipefail` plus something... no.\n\nLet me try a completely minimal reproduction.\n\nActually, you know what — let me reconsider whether the numbers 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967 could be PIDs from the `<<<` herestring or process substitution in the SCRIPT (not the bash -c test). In the script run, `desc=1000` etc. \n\nHmm, but the bash -c test also gave numbers.\n\nLet me just do a clean minimal test.\n\nLet me write a small file and run it.\n\nLet me test with a file instead of -c.\n\nLet me run:\n```\ncat > /tmp/t.sh <<''EOF''\nGROUPS=( \"a|b|c\" \"d|e|f\" )\ndeclare -p GROUPS\nEOF\nbash /tmp/t.sh\n```\n\nLet me see what that gives.\n\nActually, I suspect the issue might be related to how the tool wraps my command. Let me test with a heredoc-written file.\n\nLet me run it.","time":{"start":1790460456633,"end":1790460471389}}');
