   now:   graphical.target reached after 2.818s in userspace.\n","metadata":{"output":"═══ DID ANY REBOOT OCCUR? Checking the authoritative record ═══\n  current time:  2026-09-26 15:07:59\n  boot time:     2026-09-26 10:48:01\n  uptime:        up 4 hours, 19 minutes\n  -> 10:48:01 + 4h19m = 10:48 start, still running. No new boot.\n\n  last reboot/shutdown records:\n\n  journal: any shutdown/reboot marker this boot?\n      matches: 0\n\n  boot_id file mtime (changes only on boot):\n      2026-09-26 14:49:42.829853987 -0700  /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id\n\n  systemd-analyze numbers vs the pre-reboot reading:\n      before: 14.961 + 21.243 + 2.759 + 7.550 + 3.038 = 49.552\n      now:   Startup finished in 14.961s (firmware) + 21.243s (loader) + 2.759s (kernel) + 7.550s (initrd) + 3.038s (userspace) = 49.552s \n      now:   graphical.target reached after 2.818s in userspace.\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"echo \"═══ DID ANY REBOOT OCCUR? Checking the authoritative record ═══\"\necho \"  current time:  $(date ''+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S'')\"\necho \"  boot time:     $(uptime -s)\"\necho \"  uptime:        $(uptime -p)\"\necho \"  -> 10:48:01 + 4h19m = $(date -d \"$(uptime -s)\" +%H:%M) start, still running. No new boot.\"\necho\necho \"  last reboot/shutdown records:\"\nlast -r 2>/dev/null | head -5 | sed ''s/^/      /'' || echo \"      (none recorded)\"\necho\necho \"  journal: any shutdown/reboot marker this boot?\"\njournalctl -b --no-pager 2>/dev/null | grep -icE ''systemd-shutdown|Rebooting|reboot.target'' | sed ''s/^/      matches: /''\necho\necho \"  boot_id file mtime (changes only on boot):\"\nstat -c ''      %y  %n'' /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id 2>/dev/null\necho\necho \"  systemd-analyze numbers vs the pre-reboot reading:\"\necho \"      before: 14.961 + 21.243 + 2.759 + 7.550 + 3.038 = 49.552\"\nsystemd-analyze time 2>/dev/null | sed ''s/^/      now:   /''","time":{"start":1790460479126,"end":1790460479211}}}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfc2c688001r5kpf3SmPNBYSP','msg_0dfc2b908001H36W1rXc0C10v3','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790460479113,1790460479113,'{"type":"step-start"}');
INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0dfc2c68a001JNzsHv7e5H5bZQ','msg_0dfc2b908001H36W1rXc0C10v3','ses_f20e5cfc2ffeSFa4odnoYLiUOk',1790460479114,1790460488059,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"\nEven a minimal file gives numbers! `GROUPS=( \"VNC remote |x11vnc |5900-5910 5500\" \"RDP |xrdp |3389\" )` gives 6 numeric elements: 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967.\n\nThis is bizarre. 6 elements from 2 strings. The values look like PIDs.\n\nWait — 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967. These are increasing-ish. Could they be PIDs spawned?\n\nOH. I think I finally understand. The `|` character! Even inside double quotes in an array... no, that can''t be.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Actually — could `GROUPS` be a reserved/special variable name in this bash? Let me test with a different name.\n\nActually, let me test the SAME content but with a different array name.\n\nLet me test `MYGROUPS` instead of `GROUPS`.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the numbers. 1000, 983, 989, 992, 998, 967. If I sort: 967, 983, 989, 992, 998, 1000. These look like a sequence of PIDs. \n\nHmm, what if the `|` in the strings is being interpreted as a pipe at SOME level, and the array assignment is capturing command output?\n\nActually — here''s a thought: what if `bash /tmp/t.sh` is being run, but the heredoc content is being mangled? Let me check what /tmp/t.sh actually contains.\n\nLet me cat /tmp/t.sh.\n\nLet me check the actual file content.\n\nActually, the heredoc `<<''EOF''` should be literal. But let me verify.\n\nLet me cat the file.\n\nLet me run `cat -A /tmp/t.sh` to see exact bytes.\n\nLet me do that.\n","time":{"start":1790460479114,"end":1790460488058}}');
