INSERT INTO part VALUES('prt_0e995c416001rBM7DndWD5vHge','msg_0e9959532001KkxLhmAF2luaX8','ses_f16732810ffehZhBAPc6TQNXa2',1790625301526,1790625318796,'{"type":"reasoning","text":"The first grep across the whole home timed out (bad — I should avoid full-fs grep with `--include=\"*\"`). Fine.\n\nLet me focus. The deliverable:\n\n1. **Check Lia.CLI + older corrupted versions for memories and data** — I''ve found:\n   - Working repo `/home/livings/Projects/lia-cli` (has memory vault, legal reports, git history with extra commits)\n   - Inventory clone `/home/livings/Projects/_inventory/lia-cli` (older, behind — includes `keygen key.txt` file! a key file, which is a secret — careful)\n   - `lia-vault.json` in voynich-occult-corpus/data/memories (2026-09-03, 1 entry)\n   - `hermes-pending-memories.json` same dir\n   - other lia bridges: lia-groq-bridge, living-sin-models/lia-sync.py, metals-alloy-engineering/lia_memory_bridge.py, nanite-systems/lia_nanite_bridge.py\n   - hermes state.db message noting `lia-cli` is corrupted source (truncated imports) — older version. Need to find that corrupted version. Possibly in git history of lia-cli (earlier commits) or the inventory clone. Let me check the git history source at commit 39f544b etc. Also `_inventory/lia-cli` has \"keygen key.txt\" — weird file.\n\nLet me look for corrupted lia source: check `lia_legal_bridge.py` and src files for truncation, check git log for src, check `lia-groq-bridge`.\n\n2. **Pull all user data (4 months, usernames)** — sources:\n   - hermes: memories, .hermes_history, logs, pastes, state.db (sessions/messages), curator backups, terminal-sessions, cron executions.db, kanban.db, shared-state.db, vault (encrypted — has vault.key; but pushing keys is bad), auth.json (secrets — exclude), config.yaml   - opencode: opencode.db (sessions/messages), log/\n   - shell: fish_history, konsole profiles, recently-used.xbel\n   - lia data   - buildlog (repo of logs), sin-archive-2026, blood-build-backup\n   - Desktop notes?\n\n3. **Push to private repo thru hermes**:\n   - gh is authenticated with repo scope → can create private repo & push via HTTPS.\n   - hermes sync pushes its own hermes-cli repo (working copy at _inventory/hermes-cli with origin https://github.com/intricos777-dot/hermes-cli).\n   - \"thru hermes\" likely means using hermes'' sync mechanism (GitHub backchannel). Simplest robust approach: create new private repo (e.g., `user-data-archive-2026-09`), init local export dir as git repo, add remote, push. Then optionally wire into hermes sync? Let me read hermes_cli gitbus.py/sources.py/sync command to see how sync works — maybe sync supports arbitrary repos or is hardwired to hermes-cli repo.\n\nAlso secrets concern: The bundle will contain auth.json (API tokens), vault.key, keygen key.txt, gh token (keyring, not on disk), fish history may contain secrets, _secrets.json audit. Per user''s USER.md: they authorize push of data but expect security caution; MEMORY.md says security work pushed as private repo by default. User explicitly said \"push all of the data to a private repo\". I''ll exclude live credentials (auth.json, vault.key, nous_auth.json, keygen key.txt, .ssh keys, gh hosts.yml) and note that plainly at the end — or include? \"all of the data\" — but pushing API tokens to GitHub even private is a real risk. Best practice: exclude secrets, state it clearly. The user prefers being told plainly what wasn''t run/done. I''ll exclude secrets and report.\n\nActually — let me reconsider: the user''s standing convention says push security/business work as private repo by default. They asked to push \"all of the data\". I''ll stage everything except credential material, and list exactly which files I withheld, offering to include them if they want. That respects \"expects unrun/partial work stated plainly\".\n\nLet me now:\n1. Read hermes sync source to understand push path.\n2. Check the corrupted lia source (find it).\n3. Survey remaining data sources (fi